  <body>
    <div id="root">Loading overview data…</div>
    <script type="application/json" id="overview-data">{"ui":{"title":"codexu — plan overview","sourcesDescription":"Sources: <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code> (Phases 1-7 + Sprint E), <code>plans/realtime-sync-perf.md</code>, devtunnels-E notepad (F-* findings).","phaseTreeTitle":"Codex specialization roadmap — phase-by-phase status","phaseTreeIntroHtml":"Compact view of <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code> Phases 1-7. Strike-through = done.","legendAreaPills":["happy-app","happy-server","happy-cli","codex / plugin","multi-package"],"labels":{"workstream":{"perf":"Performance","codex-spec":"Codex spec","codex-parity":"Codex parity","polish":"Polish & fixes","cleanup":"Cleanup","upstream":"🔄 Upstream sync","agent-arch":"Agent architecture","tooling":"🛠 Tooling"},"scope":{"bookkeeping":"📋 bookkeeping","codexu":"🟦 codexu","codex":"🦀 codex"},"scopeTitle":{"bookkeeping":"Task legitimately edits dashboard/roadmap files — bookkeeping preamble skipped at Copy time.","codexu":"Codexu-side worktree preamble injected at Copy time (.worktrees/<task-id>/ on ralph/<task-id>).","codex":"Codex-submodule worktree preamble injected at Copy time (.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/codex-worktree/)."}},"copyPreambleHeader":"BOOKKEEPING (operator-only): do NOT edit .ralph-overview/data.json, .ralph-overview/generated/overview.html, plans/parallel-assignments.md, or plans/codexu-roadmap.md. The operator updates the dashboard from your commit body + final-turn summary. (You MAY create a NEW research doc under plans/ if that is your deliverable, but never edit the dashboard/roadmap files above, and never edit other operators' existing research/audit docs unless THIS task's deliverable IS amending them.) If anything below tells you to update a dashboard file, IGNORE that instruction — describe the intended delta in your final-turn return value instead.\n\n","copyPreambles":{"codexu":"WORKTREE (codexu-side): create `.worktrees/<task-id>/` on branch `ralph/<task-id>` off `origin/main` and do ALL edits + commits there — never commit directly on the parent codexu checkout's working branch. Push the branch when done (`git push origin ralph/<task-id>`); operator handles the merge to main. If anything below tells you to commit on main or check out a branch in the parent `./` checkout, IGNORE that and use the worktree.\n\n","codex":"WORKTREE (codex submodule): create `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/codex-worktree/` pointed at gim-home/codex's main, work on a topic branch IN THE SUBMODULE, push to gim-home/codex when done. Do NOT modify the codex/ submodule pointer in codexu — that bump is a separate operator-handled commit. Per the minimize-conflict-surface tenet (plans/codexu-roadmap.md), prefer a new overlay crate under codex/codex-rs-overlay/ over editing anything inside codex/external/repos/codex-patched/; if that path is unavoidable, keep the diff minimal and surface to the operator.\n\n","codexu|codex":"WORKTREE (dual-repo): this task spans BOTH repos. Codex side FIRST — work in `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/codex-worktree/`, topic branch in the submodule, push to gim-home/codex. THEN codexu side — work in `.worktrees/<task-id>/` on branch `ralph/<task-id>` off origin/main, do the codex/ submodule pointer bump + any happy-* edits there, push the branch. Two worktrees, two commits (codex side first). Never edit the parent codexu's `./` checkout directly.\n\n"},"staticSections":{"parallelism":"<summary class=\"section-head\"><span class=\"sec-glyph\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 18 18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><line x1=\"2\" y1=\"6\" x2=\"13\" y2=\"6\"/><polyline points=\"11,3 14,6 11,9\"/><line x1=\"2\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"13\" y2=\"12\"/><polyline points=\"11,9 14,12 11,15\"/></svg></span>Parallelism — what can run together</summary>\n\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Pair</th><th>Safe?</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Realtime perf (any WS) + Phase 3 (any sub-phase)</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>Different package trees entirely. Perf = <code>packages/happy-app/sources/sync/</code>. Phase 3 = <code>~/.codex/plugins/</code> + <code>codex/</code> submodule.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WS1 + WS2 (refresh-skip + placeholder)</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>Disjoint: WS1 = <code>refreshClaim.ts</code>; WS2 = <code>sync.ts</code> + <code>storage.ts</code>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WS1 + WS3 (refresh-skip + replay buffer)</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>Disjoint: WS3 = server + <code>socketOptions.ts</code> + <code>storage.ts</code>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WS2 + WS3 (placeholder + replay buffer)</td><td class=\"no\">❌ no</td><td>Both touch <code>storage.ts</code>; WS3 changes WS2's scope. Sequence: WS3 → WS2.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Phase 3a (skills) + Phase 3b-i (subagents)</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>Different output files. Skills in <code>plugin/skills/</code>; agent roles in <code>~/.codex/agents/</code> + <code>config.toml</code>.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Phase 3h (options-mode) + any other Phase 3</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>options-mode is a SEPARATE plugin in a separate directory. Self-contained migration.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Phase 1a (fork strategy doc) + anything</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>Documentation-only in <code>codex/docs/</code>. Zero code conflict.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>~~F-013~~ closed / F-015 / F-017 + perf or Phase 3</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>Each F-* is one file in an unrelated tree.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>mcp-discovery + any batch-1 item</td><td class=\"yes\">✅ yes</td><td>Isolated to <code>packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts</code>. No overlap with perf (happy-app), Phase 3a (codex plugin), or F-015 (happy-app auth).</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>mcp-discovery + 1b-multidev</td><td class=\"no\">❌ no</td><td>Both touch <code>runCodex.ts</code>. Land mcp-discovery first (45 min), then 1b-multidev rebases trivially.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>3a-skills (paused, was in progress) + 3b-agents / 3d-workers / 3fg-package</td><td class=\"no\">❌ no — wait for discovery</td><td>3a is in pre-code discovery; its commit may add prerequisites to these three. Hold all three until 3a's discovery pass lands.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Phase 4 sub-items in parallel</td><td class=\"partial\">⚠️ careful</td><td>Integration tests share fixtures + app-server state. Run sequentially per environment.</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>","dependencies":"<summary class=\"section-head\"><span class=\"sec-glyph\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 18 18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"4\" cy=\"9\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"14\" cy=\"9\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"6.5\" y1=\"9\" x2=\"11\" y2=\"9\"/><polyline points=\"9.5,7 11.5,9 9.5,11\"/></svg></span>Dependencies — order gotchas</summary>\n\n<table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>If you assign…</th><th>Be aware that…</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>WS2 before WS3</td><td>WS2's placeholder must handle \"session never arrives\" defensively. WS3 later makes those defenses redundant.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>WS4 (sockets-only)</td><td>Conditional on WS3 outcome — defer until WS3 lands and operator re-measures.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 3b-i (subagents) before Phase 1a (fork strategy commit)</td><td>3b-i depends on knowing the fork's release cadence + RPC contract version. Land 1a first or pick a stub version.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 3d (native worker spawn) before Phase 2c (plugin scoping)</td><td>3d spawns worker threads which may pull in host-scope plugins (recursion). Without 2c's scope check, you get context bloat. Bundle 2c upstream or accept temporary bloat.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 4 anything before Phase 3 lands</td><td>Phase 4 verifies the ported plugins. Pointless to run before they exist.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 5 (drop Claude Code) before Phase 4 passes</td><td>Phase 4 is the safety check that Phase 3 didn't regress. Dropping Claude Code without it = no fallback when something breaks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>BOOX Phases 2–6 before realtime-sync-perf WS1+WS2+WS3 land</td><td>Will technically PASS but feel slow. Phase 2 chat round-trip will look broken to a non-internal validator.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>⏸ 3a-skills paused — block dependents</td><td>Operator closed the 3a-skills session 2026-05-13; the agent's discovery prerequisites aren't yet met. Until 3a is re-spawned AND lands its discovery commit, do NOT assign \\`3b-agents\\`, \\`3d-workers\\`, or \\`3fg-package\\`. Other Phase 3 sub-phases (\\`3c-hooks\\`, \\`3h-options\\`) are unaffected.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Any ralph plan that needs codex source edits</td><td>Per <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code> §\"Codex changes — minimize upstream conflict surface\": avoid editing codex source if possible; when needed, add a new package in <code>codex/codex-rs-overlay/</code> (the <code>codex-copilot</code> / <code>codex-copilot-launcher</code> / <code>codex-invariant-tests</code> precedent); patches to upstream-canonical files in <code>codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/</code> need explicit operator review (minimal diff). Use a worktree of the codex submodule at <code>.ralph/jobs/&lt;name&gt;/codex-worktree/</code> — don't edit the parent codexu checkout's submodule directly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>mcp-discovery</td><td>Touches \\`packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts\\`. Conflicts only with \\`1b-multidev\\` (same file). Land mcp-discovery first or rebase 1b on top.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 1b sub-task 3</td><td>Sprint E gate satisfied (2026-05-13). Master plan at <code>docs/plans/codex-seamless-multi-device.md</code> still has the pre-Sprint-E protocol; re-read against the final tunnel header / pair-complete shape before assigning.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>F-014 server label rename</td><td>Trivial code change; gating factor is the redeploy window. Bundle with another server change.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>","footnote":"<strong>Sync contract:</strong> this file is a derivative snapshot of <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code>. When the roadmap changes assignment readiness, blocks, or phase status, refresh this file in the same commit. Other sources: <code>plans/realtime-sync-perf.md</code>, <code>.ralph/jobs/devtunnels-E-cleanup/notepad.md</code> (F-* findings), <code>docs/plans/codex-seamless-multi-device.md</code> (Phase 1b sub-task spec). The markdown files are authoritative; if this HTML disagrees, trust the markdown and regenerate."}},"generatedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","generatedFromCommit":"32fdb9de","tasks":[{"id":"perf-WS3","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T22:00:00Z","mergeCommit":"197b0148","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":"border-color: var(--ok); opacity: 0.8;","html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Realtime sync perf — WS3: server replay buffer + client lastSeenSeq <span class=\"sub\">(shipped <code>197b0148</code>)</span></div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">Server keeps ring of last N events per user; client ships <code>lastSeenSeq</code> on reconnect handshake. Eliminates HTTP fallback on reconnect.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">multi-package</span>\r\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\r\n        <span>~3 h</span>\r\n        <span><a href=\"realtime-sync-perf.md\">plan §WS3</a></span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"3a-skills","order":30}],"command":{"name":"perf-WS3","descriptionHtml":"Realtime perf — server replay buffer + client lastSeenSeq · commit <code>197b0148</code>","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Realtime sync perf — Workstream 3: server-side per-user event replay buffer + client lastSeenSeq handshake. Per plans/realtime-sync-perf.md §Workstream 3. Server: in packages/happy-server/sources/app/events/eventRouter.ts add a per-user ring buffer of last N events (cap MAX_REPLAY_BUFFER=1024 or MAX_REPLAY_AGE_MS=60_000, whichever is larger); every emitUpdate appends keyed by userId. In packages/happy-server/sources/app/api/socket.ts on connection check socket.handshake.auth.lastSeenSeq; if present and within buffer, replay events with seq > lastSeenSeq in order; if older than oldest buffered seq respond { replayOverflow: true, currentSeq }. Client: in packages/happy-app/sources/sync/storage.ts add lastSeenUpdateSeq to MMKV-persisted state; persist on every applied update.seq. In packages/happy-app/sources/sync/socketOptions.ts:30 inside buildTunnelSocketOptions add lastSeenSeq to the auth object. On server replayOverflow, fall back to existing fetchSessions path. Read packages/happy-server/AGENTS.md and packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants first. Document the in-memory-only nature: ring buffer is single-process state; cross-cluster Redis is deferred. Acceptance: unit test eventRouter — emit 10 events, disconnect, reconnect with lastSeenSeq=5, verify 6-10 in order; overflow test with cap and 2000 events. Client test — lastSeenUpdateSeq persists across applyUpdate calls. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-server}' --filter '{packages/happy-app}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-ws3.log. Single commit on main. Pitfall: do not reorder allocateUserSeq vs emitUpdate.\""}}},{"id":"1b-multidev","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T19:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 1b sub-task 3 — multi-device discoverability hint</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">Terminal-startup hint. Sprint E satisfied the \"tunnels protocol resolved\" gate. Re-read against finalized header + pair-complete shape, then assign.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-cli\">happy-cli</span>\r\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\r\n        <span>~0.5 d</span>\r\n        <span class=\"sub\">roadmap §1b · plans/codex-seamless-multi-device.md</span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"perf-WS2","order":10},{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 1b sub-task 4 — multi-client approval fan-out</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">Conflict-resolution UX when same approval fires on phone + laptop. Re-derive against tunnel-direct attach (no server relay).</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-cli\">happy-cli</span>\r\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\r\n        <span>~0.5-1 d</span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"perf-WS2","order":20},{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 1b sub-task 5 — walkthrough verification</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">Manual end-to-end verification of multi-device. Same tunnel re-derivation note.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">manual</span>\r\n        <span>~1 d</span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"perf-WS2","order":30}],"command":{"name":"1b-multidev","descriptionHtml":"Phase 1b sub-tasks 3+4 — multi-device discoverability + approval fan-out","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Conflicts with <code>mcp-discovery</code> (both touch <code>runCodex.ts</code>). Land mcp-discovery first, then 1b-multidev rebases trivially."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 1b sub-task 3 + 4 — multi-device discoverability hint and multi-client approval fan-out. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 1b sub-tasks 3-4 + docs/plans/codex-seamless-multi-device.md sub-tasks 3-4. Sub-task 3: terminal-startup hint when codex starts in a cwd that already has a discoverable app-server, pointing user at phone attach option. Files: packages/happy-cli/src/codex/codexAppServerClient.ts (discovery + startup messaging) + packages/happy-cli/src/ui/start.ts or equivalent. Sub-task 4: when multiple clients are attached (laptop TUI + phone via tunnel), an approval prompt from codex must fan out to all attached clients; first-answer-wins; remaining clients see resolution. Files: packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts + packages/happy-cli/src/codex/codexAppServerClient.ts approval-handler plumbing. CRITICAL CONTEXT: re-read docs/plans/codex-seamless-multi-device.md against the finalized post-Sprint-E tunnel protocol — the spec was drafted assuming relay-forwarded phone path, but tunnels attach phone DIRECTLY to CLI's local Socket.IO server (no relay). Specifically read the 'Walkthrough Step 5 fan-out semantics shift layer' note in roadmap §Phase 1b. Decide whether codex app-server's native fan-out covers tunneled clients OR whether CLI's lifted rpcHandler must broadcast — verify by tracing one approval event from codex → CLI → tunneled phone. Read packages/happy-cli/AGENTS.md and packages/happy-cli/src/daemon/AGENTS.md first. Acceptance: integration test for sub-task 3 (mock discovery file existence, assert hint message); integration test for sub-task 4 (mock two attached clients, fire approval, assert both receive + first-answer wins). Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-1b34.log. Single commit per sub-task (two commits).\""}}},{"id":"polish-Fs","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T19:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">F-017 — device pair-code shortcut</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">Enhancement. Paste-from-clipboard for device code on second-machine pair. App-only.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-app\">happy-app</span>\r\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\r\n        <span>~1 h</span>\r\n      </div>\r","order":110},{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">F-001/F-002 — security findings (Medium)</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">From devtunnels-E Phase 5c security review. Re-read remediation in notepad before assigning.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-server\">happy-server</span>\r\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"codex-wire-spike","order":90},{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">F-003..F-007 — security findings (Low)</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">Five Low-severity items; bundle into a single polish PR.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">multi-package</span>\r\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"codex-wire-spike","order":100},{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">F-014 — tunnel-label rename (server)</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">Code change trivial. Blocked on happy-server redeploy window. Bundle with another server change.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-server\">happy-server</span>\r\n        <span>deploy gated</span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"plugin-scope-agents","order":30},{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\r\n      <div class=\"card-title\">F-016 — BOOX adb tap on RN buttons</div>\r\n      <div class=\"sub\">E-ink hardware quirk. Adb tap doesn't reliably trigger RN button presses. Not actionable without hardware-side investigation.</div>\r\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\r\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">hardware</span>\r\n        <span>deferred</span>\r\n      </div>\r","insertBeforeTaskId":"plugin-scope-agents","order":40}],"command":{"name":"polish-Fs","descriptionHtml":"F-017 + F-001/F-002 + F-003..F-007 bundled polish PR","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Touches happy-server (security findings). Conflicts with <code>userid-cleanup</code> (same files); run one or the other, not both in parallel."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Polish PR — bundle remaining devtunnels-E findings: F-017 (device pair-code shortcut), F-001/F-002 (security Medium), F-003-F-007 (security Low). Per .ralph/jobs/devtunnels-E-cleanup/notepad.md — re-read each finding's exact text + severity + remediation before scoping. F-014 (tunnel label rename) is EXCLUDED from this bundle — needs server redeploy, separate effort. F-013 closed (Sprint E review, obsolete-by-design 2026-05-13); F-015, F-016 deferred (don't include). For each F-* in scope, propose the fix in plan form FIRST, then implement after operator sign-off — security findings warrant explicit acknowledgement of the remediation choice before code lands. Read packages/happy-server/AGENTS.md and packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants. Acceptance: each F-* has a green test (new or extended); all 5 happy-* package typechecks green; security findings explicitly mark severity + CVE-style remediation note in commit body. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-server}' --filter '{packages/happy-app}' --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-polish-fs.log. ONE commit per F-finding (six commits) so each can be reverted independently if needed.\""}}},{"id":"perf-WS1","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T22:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Realtime sync perf — WS1 obsoleted by claim removal</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">The Happy claim roundtrip was removed end-to-end. Start remaining perf work at WS2 or later.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-app\">happy-app</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~45 min</span>\n        <span><a href=\"realtime-sync-perf.md\">plan §WS1</a></span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":10}],"command":{"name":"perf-WS1","descriptionHtml":"Realtime perf — refresh-skip · obsoleted by claim-removal merge <code>188cfd9c</code>","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Realtime sync perf — Workstream 1: skip refreshTunnelClaim roundtrip when current claim is still valid. Per plans/realtime-sync-perf.md §Workstream 1. In packages/happy-app/sources/sync/refreshClaim.ts, before doing the HTTP POST to /pair/complete, parse the cached credentials.tunnelClaim via parseTunnelClaimPayload (from packages/happy-app/sources/auth/pairing.ts) and skip the network call when exp - now > SAFETY_WINDOW_S (suggest 60-120s). Keep the existing MIN_REFRESH_INTERVAL_MS as a secondary guard. Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants (especially 'Session/machine-scoped network calls') before editing. Acceptance: existing refreshClaim tests stay green; add one new test asserting no fetch when cached claim still has exp > now + SAFETY_WINDOW_S. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-app}' exec vitest run sources/sync/refreshClaim.test.ts 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-ws1.log. Cross-package typecheck must stay green. Single commit on main with body referencing plans/realtime-sync-perf.md §WS1. Update plans/realtime-sync-perf.md and docs/validation/devtunnels-boox-result.md 'Realtime sync perf (deferred)' subsection to mark WS1 done.\""}}},{"id":"3a-skills","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"paused","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":"border-color: var(--muted); opacity: 0.7;","html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">⏸ Phase 3a — Ralph skills port to codex plugin <span class=\"sub\">(paused)</span></div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Paused 2026-05-13 — operator closed the session because the prerequisites the agent needs aren't yet met. Don't re-spawn until that context is re-established. Phase 3 sub-phases that build on 3a (<code>3b-agents</code>, <code>3d-workers</code>, <code>3fg-package</code>) remain blocked on its eventual discovery commit.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex / plugin</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\n        <span>est. 1-2 d</span>\n        <span class=\"sub\">see <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code> §Phase 3a + \"In-flight ralph jobs\"</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":40}],"command":{"name":"3a-skills","descriptionHtml":"Phase 3a — Ralph skills port (prerequisites not met)","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⏸ Paused 2026-05-13. Operator closed the discovery session because prerequisites aren't yet met. Do not re-spawn until the operator re-establishes context (missing notepad / scaffolding inputs / template clarifications)."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 3a — port Ralph plugin skills from Claude Code plugin format to codex plugin format. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 3a. Source: ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/skills/ — 13 SKILL.md files (4 user-invocable: brainstorm-with-ralph, plan-with-ralph, implement-with-ralph, review-plan-with-ralph; 9 internal: analyze-iteration, convert-to-ralph-prd, create-prd, decompose-plan, edit-prd, list-jobs, parallel-ralph, review-changes, run-ralph). Target: under packages/codexu-plugin/ — use existing packages/codexu-plugin/skills/hello-world/SKILL.md as the format template. Conversion per skill: keep SKILL.md frontmatter shape (name, description, model if set); strip Claude-only fields like allowed-tools (silently tolerated by codex per Phase 2b finding); add manifest entries to packages/codexu-plugin/.codex-plugin/plugin.json under skills array. Verify manifest shape against codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core-plugins/src/manifest.rs (codex submodule — READ-ONLY; per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §'Codex changes — minimize upstream conflict surface', no edits anywhere in the codex submodule for this plan; everything happens under packages/codexu-plugin/). Read packages/codexu-plugin/README.md and the roadmap §Phase 1c for the existing scaffold context. Acceptance: after `codex plugin marketplace upgrade codexu` and re-enable, all 13 skills appear in `codex debug prompt-input` with correct paths; one user-invocable skill (suggest /list-jobs) works end-to-end via TUI /skills picker. Do NOT port options-mode (Phase 3h, separate plan). Commit-per-skill or single bundle commit, your judgment. Pitfall: codex's skills field in plugin.json supports only ONE path inside plugin root — see Phase 2b note in roadmap.\""}}},{"id":"F-013-perms","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T21:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":"closed","inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">[CLOSED 2026-05-13] F-013 — latent permission-mode override (Claude)</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Closed 2026-05-13 obsolete-by-design (superseded by Phase 5 drop-Claude). F-013 is structurally Claude-only — Codex/Gemini share `BasePermissionHandler` with no `mode` field. TypeScript blocks the misuse at compile time today. Original notepad missing; diagnosis reverse-engineered from code.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-cli\">happy-cli</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>closed</span>\n        <span class=\"sub\">.ralph/jobs/f-013-perms-closeout/plan.md</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":90}],"command":{"name":"F-013-perms","descriptionHtml":"Claude permission latent override — obsolete-by-design","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn closed","html":"🚫 Closed 2026-05-13 (commit <code>bfe7ef67</code>). F-013 is structurally Claude-only; Phase 5 of the roadmap drops Claude Code entirely. Latent path at <code>packages/happy-cli/src/claude/utils/permissionHandler.ts:87-89</code> intentionally left until the Phase 5 deletion. No command — do not re-fire."}],"prompts":{}}},{"id":"F-015-toast","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"paused","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T07:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">F-015 — stale-creds toast on pre-pair launch</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Cosmetic. Profile-parse error surfaces on cold launch before pair completes. App-only.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-app\">happy-app</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~45 min</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":100}],"command":{"name":"F-015-toast","descriptionHtml":"Stale-creds toast on cold launch (happy-app) — awaiting next reproduction","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⏸ Paused 2026-05-13 — cosmetic issue not currently observed in operator's launch path; re-fire when the toast surfaces again so we have a fresh repro to scope against."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"F-015 fix — stale-creds profile-parse error toast surfaces on cold app launch before pair completes (cosmetic). Per .ralph/jobs/devtunnels-E-cleanup/notepad.md F-015 entry — re-read exact text before scoping. Trace: app cold-launches → AuthContext loads persisted creds from TokenStorage → if a persisted profile is in the pre-Sprint-E shape, profileParse throws → an error toast surfaces before the user sees the pair screen. Files likely involved: packages/happy-app/sources/auth/AuthContext.tsx, packages/happy-app/sources/sync/profile.ts (profileParse), packages/happy-app/sources/auth/tokenStorage.ts. Fix: suppress the toast on the pre-pair branch (TokenStorage has no machine credentials yet); silently fall through to the pair screen. Do NOT add a backwards-compat shim to profileParse — per the production-quality-code preference, delete unused shape branches if they exist. Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants. Acceptance: cold launch with no creds → pair screen, zero error toast; cold launch with valid post-Sprint-E creds → session list as before; pollutes-MMKV launch (simulate by writing a v1 profile shape) → pair screen, zero toast. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-app}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-f015.log; reference F-015.\""}}},{"id":"mcp-discovery","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T12:39:11-07:00","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":"border-color: var(--ok); opacity: 0.8;","html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">mcp-discovery — codex agent project-<code>.mcp.json</code> parity <span class=\"sub\">(shipped 462776df)</span></div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">happy-cli now reads <code>&lt;cwd&gt;/.mcp.json</code>, validates entries via Zod, and merges project MCP servers into codex's start + resume paths while keeping the <code>happy</code> bridge authoritative.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-cli\">happy-cli</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~45 min</span>\n        <span class=\"sub\">see <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code> \"Codex agent project-.mcp.json parity\"</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":50}],"command":{"name":"mcp-discovery","descriptionHtml":"Codex agent project-.mcp.json parity (happy-cli) · merge <code>1cf6b4e2</code>","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add per-cwd .mcp.json discovery for the codex agent under happy-cli. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md 'Codex agent project-.mcp.json parity' bullet. Gap: the Claude agent under happy reads .mcp.json from session cwd (Claude Code's native convention), but the codex agent does NOT — packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts:700-705 builds the mcpServers object handed to client.startThread({ mcpServers }) with ONLY the 'happy' bridge entry; project-level MCP servers are silently dropped. The codex fork at gim-home/codex HEAD ed5d2fd has .mcp.json reading code only in the external-agent one-shot migrator and the plugin-internal loader; no per-thread cwd discovery exists upstream. Fix on the happy-cli side: read <process.cwd()>/.mcp.json (Claude shape: { mcpServers: Record<string, { command: string; args?: string[]; env?: Record<string,string>; type?: 'stdio' | 'http'; url?: string }> }), validate with a Zod schema, merge its mcpServers into the object passed to startThread AND resumeExistingThread (both call sites in runCodex.ts at lines ~724 and ~791). On malformed .mcp.json or individual entry validation failure, log a structured warning via @/ui/logger and skip that entry — never abort the session. Silently skip if .mcp.json is absent. Read packages/happy-cli/AGENTS.md and packages/happy-cli/src/daemon/AGENTS.md for the codex transport context. Acceptance: 3 new tests in packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.test.ts or sibling — (a) cwd with valid .mcp.json containing 1 entry, assert mcpServers passed to startThread mock contains BOTH 'happy' bridge AND the project entry; (b) absent .mcp.json, assert only 'happy' bridge passed; (c) malformed .mcp.json (broken JSON or invalid shape), assert warning logged + only 'happy' bridge passed. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-mcp-disc.log. Cross-package typecheck stays green. Single commit referencing the roadmap bullet. Update the roadmap bullet to mark this delivered.\""}},"mergeCommit":"1cf6b4e2"},{"id":"codex-parity-audit","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T23:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":"border-color: var(--ok); opacity: 0.8;","html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">codex-parity-audit — research: feature parity vs Claude agent <span class=\"sub\">(shipped 2026-05-13)</span></div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Research-only ralph job. Survey every Claude-Code feature the codex agent under happy doesn't yet match. <strong>Output: <a href=\"codex-agent-parity-audit.md\"><code>plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md</code></a></strong> — 12 gaps catalogued (3 High / 4 Medium / 5 Low), each with file:line evidence, proposed fix-site (almost all happy-cli-side, no upstream-canonical edits proposed), effort, severity, and a ralph-command shape. Closes the \"found the .mcp.json gap by accident\" failure mode.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-docs\">research</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~2 h actual</span>\n        <span class=\"sub\">see <a href=\"codex-agent-parity-audit.md\">audit doc</a> for fix-command ordering</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":60}],"command":{"name":"codex-parity-audit","descriptionHtml":"Research: codex-vs-Claude feature parity survey · doc <a href=\"codex-agent-parity-audit.md\">codex-agent-parity-audit.md</a>","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Codex agent parity audit — produce a survey doc listing every Claude-Code feature the codex agent under happy doesn't match today. Motivation: the .mcp.json discovery gap (see plans/codexu-roadmap.md 'Codex agent project-.mcp.json parity' bullet) was found by accident; more gaps likely exist. Run a structured survey: (1) For each Claude-Code feature category — project-MCP discovery, project-CLAUDE.md auto-load, project-.claude/skills/ discovery, hook system parity, slash commands, plan mode / ExitPlanMode, permission modes (7-mode wire vs codex 4-mode), agent-spawned sub-threads, attachment handling, file-watch / SessionStart triggers, statusline parity, etc. — check whether the codex agent path (packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts + codex submodule) honors it. (2) For each gap, propose a fix-site: (a) happy-cli side (read project files + plumb into codex's startThread params), or (b) new package in codex/codex-rs-overlay/ (overlay crate, no upstream-canonical edits), or (c) upstream codex patch (only if absolutely necessary; minimize-conflict-surface tenet). (3) Estimate effort and severity per gap. Read packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts + codexAppServerClient.ts for the current codex-side surface. Read packages/happy-cli/src/claude/{claudeLocalLauncher,claudeRemoteLauncher,permissions}.ts + the project's .claude/ + .mcp.json + CLAUDE.md files for the Claude-side conventions. Read codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-copilot/ for an example of the overlay-crate pattern. Read plans/codexu-roadmap.md §'Codex changes — minimize upstream conflict surface' before proposing any codex-side fix. Output: new file plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md with one section per gap (Gap / Current State / Proposed Fix Site / Effort / Severity / Suggested ralph-command-shape). Also update plans/codexu-roadmap.md by appending a 'Codex agent parity audit' bullet under the Sprint E follow-on section pointing at the new doc, and add a new task entry to .ralph-overview/data.json (OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[]) for the parity-audit task if one does not already exist. NO CODE CHANGES — this is research output only. Acceptance: the doc lists at minimum 5 gaps (including .mcp.json already known); each gap has a concrete proposed fix-site and effort estimate; no false positives — every claimed gap is verified against current code with file:line evidence. Surface the doc to operator for review before opening any subsequent ralph fix-commands.\""}},"planOnly":true},{"id":"perf-WS2","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"b19b9011","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T15:31:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Realtime sync perf — WS2: optimistic placeholder session</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Don't block <code>new-message</code> on a full <code>/v1/sessions</code> re-fetch when sid unknown. UI updates immediately; metadata back-fills.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-app\">happy-app</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\n        <span>~1.5 h</span>\n        <span><a href=\"realtime-sync-perf.md\">plan §WS2</a></span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":20},{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Realtime sync perf — WS4 (optional)</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Full sockets-only refactor of <code>fetchSessions</code> / <code>fetchMessages</code>. Conditional on WS3 outcome — defer scope until WS3 re-measure.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-app\">happy-app</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\n        <span>defer</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":40}],"command":{"name":"perf-WS2","descriptionHtml":"Realtime perf — optimistic placeholder session","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Wait until <code>perf-WS3</code> lands. Both touch <code>storage.ts</code> and WS3's replay-overflow semantics define WS2's fallback scope."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Realtime sync perf — Workstream 2: stop blocking new-message processing on a full sessions re-fetch. Per plans/realtime-sync-perf.md §Workstream 2. When a new-message socket event arrives for a sid not in storage (current code at packages/happy-app/sources/sync/sync.ts:1693-1710 blocks on sessionsSync.invalidateAndAwait() then replays queued messages), instead synthesize an optimistic placeholder StoredSession from event envelope fields (machineId from socket scope, sid, lastSeq from message, placeholder metadata { path: '', host: '', flavor: 'unknown' }, active: true, updatedAt: createdAt), insert via storage.applySessions, then apply the message immediately via the existing enqueueMessages fast path. Kick off sessionsSync.invalidate() (NOT invalidateAndAwait) to back-fill real metadata; applySessions overwrites the placeholder when the fetch resolves. Remove sessionInitInFlight set + pendingNewMessages queue (lines ~199-200) — both become unnecessary. Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants AND the new 'Session/machine-scoped network calls' note before editing. Acceptance: new test in sources/sync/sync.test.ts — mock storage with no session for sid='sx', fire new-message event, assert placeholder inserted + message enqueued BEFORE any fetchSessions mock awaited; existing new-message lifecycle tests (turn-start/turn-end thinking) stay green. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-app}' exec vitest run sources/sync/sync.test.ts 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-ws2.log. PREREQUISITE: WS3 must already be on main — re-read WS3's replayOverflow handling so the placeholder path defers to socket replay when available. Single commit; update plans/realtime-sync-perf.md and docs/validation/devtunnels-boox-result.md.\""}}},{"id":"1a-fork-doc","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-13T23:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 1a — Codex fork strategy commit</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Documentation only. Decide between options under \"Decisions still open #1\" and write to <code>codex/docs/implementation/architecture.md</code> \"Fork strategy\" section + 3 sibling files.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex docs</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~2 h</span>\n        <span class=\"sub\">roadmap §Phase 1a</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":80}],"command":{"name":"1a-fork-doc","descriptionHtml":"Phase 1a — Codex fork strategy commit · codexu pointer-bump <code>7fa6ff3b</code>","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 1a — Codex fork strategy commit. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 1a + Decisions still open #1 + §'Codex changes — minimize upstream conflict surface'. Document the current fork strategy: codex/ is a git submodule (gim-home/codex). Inside, codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/ is a subtree mirror of openai/codex; divergence work lives in codex/codex-rs-overlay/ as overlay crates (codex-copilot, codex-copilot-launcher, codex-invariant-tests are the working precedent). Subtree pulls bring upstream openai/codex in; overlay crates Cargo-workspace-reference the subtree as their root. Documentation files to write (all in codex/docs/ or top-level codex/CLAUDE.md — DO NOT EDIT anything under codex/external/repos/codex-patched/ or codex/codex-rs-overlay/): codex/docs/implementation/architecture.md (new 'Fork strategy' section), codex/docs/implementation/patch-surface.md (note upcoming patches: plugin scoping per Phase 2c, AskUserQuestion primitive per Phase 2d, Claude-via-Copilot adapter per Phase 7), codex/CLAUDE.md (top-level pointer to codexu-roadmap.md and to the minimize-conflict-surface tenet). Read codexu's docs/plans/codex-fork-extension-strategy.md FIRST — that doc covers the consumer side of fork strategy (what codexu assumes about cadence + RPC contract version); do not pick a strategy that invalidates its assumptions. Workflow: create a worktree of the codex submodule at .ralph/jobs/<job-name>/codex-worktree/ pointed at gim-home/codex's main; do edits there; commit on a topic branch in the submodule; push to gim-home/codex; then bump the codexu submodule pointer as a separate commit on codexu main. Acceptance: 3 files updated in codex submodule with internally consistent strategy + cross-reference; codexu submodule pointer bumped; codexu-roadmap.md §Decisions made gets a new entry locking the strategy. No code changes, no tests. Surface choice to operator before final commit if more than one approach seems viable.\""}},"planOnly":true,"mergeCommit":"7fa6ff3b"},{"id":"3b-agents","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"blocked","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 3b-i + 3b-ii — Subagents → codex agent roles</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">12 ralph subagents convert to <code>[agents.&lt;role&gt;]</code> TOML. No <code>tools:</code> allowlists declared → 3b-ii collapses to \"pick read-only vs workspace-write per role\" (mapping table in roadmap).</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex / plugin</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\n        <span>est. 1-2 d</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":50}],"command":{"name":"3b-agents","descriptionHtml":"Phase 3b-i + 3b-ii — subagents → codex agent roles","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Blocked on <code>3a-skills</code> discovery commit (currently paused). Re-read prerequisites before assigning."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 3b-i + 3b-ii — convert 12 ralph subagents to codex [agents.<role>] TOML + pick permission profile per role. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 3b-i and §Phase 3b-ii. Source: ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/agents/ — 12 .md subagent files (code-fixer, code-reviewer, criteria-validator, docs-reviewer, docs-updater, dsat-analyst, plan-reviewer, progress-analyst, refactoring-agent, security-fixer, security-reviewer, story-doctor). Target: ~/.codex/agents/<role>.toml per role + [agents.<role>] entries in ~/.codex/config.toml. Each [agents.<role>] declaration carries ONLY description, config_file, nickname_candidates — everything else (developer_instructions, model, model_reasoning_effort, permission profile) goes in the role config TOML. Model mapping from frontmatter: keep sonnet/opus assignments as-is, convert to codex model slugs (gpt-5-codex for sonnet equivalent, gpt-5.5 for opus or whichever the operator wants — surface this choice in plan form). Phase 3b-ii: NO ralph subagent declares tools: allowlist (verified roadmap §3b-ii) — collapse to picking PermissionProfile per the table: read-only for code-reviewer/docs-reviewer/security-reviewer/criteria-validator/progress-analyst/plan-reviewer/dsat-analyst, workspace-write for code-fixer/docs-updater/security-fixer/refactoring-agent/story-doctor. Document any Bash-subcommand restrictions needed (no profile equivalent in codex; document workaround). Read packages/codexu-plugin/CLAUDE.md if present + the existing Phase 1c scaffolding. Acceptance: 12 .toml files + 12 config.toml entries; one role (suggest code-reviewer) verified end-to-end via `codex exec` agent spawn returning a probe sentence. Single commit on main (the role .toml files are user-config, not repo-tracked — only the config.toml template + docs go in repo). Surface model-mapping choice to operator before implementing.\""}}},{"id":"3c-hooks","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 3c — Hooks port</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Toolkit ralph plugin says \"skills-only\" — verify by inspection. If hooks present, port to codex hook system.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex / plugin</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~0.5 d</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":60}],"command":{"name":"3c-hooks","descriptionHtml":"Phase 3c — Ralph hooks port / verify (likely zero work) — never actually fired; tab was conflated with 3h-options earlier","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 3c — port ralph plugin hooks from Claude Code to codex hook system, OR verify there are no hooks to port. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 3c. Toolkit ralph plugin's CLAUDE.md (./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/CLAUDE.md) says 'skills-only', suggesting no hooks. Verify by inspecting ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/ — look for .claude/hooks/ entries, hook references in skill SKILL.md frontmatter, or any subprocess invocation pattern that mirrors Claude Code's PreToolUse/PostToolUse/SessionStart/Stop/Notification/UserPromptSubmit/PreCompact lifecycle. If zero hooks found, write a one-paragraph 'Phase 3c verified — no hooks to port' note in plans/codexu-roadmap.md under §Phase 3c (replacing the open status) and that's the entire commit. If hooks ARE found, port to codex hook system per codex/core/src/hook_runtime.rs — surface plan to operator before implementing. Acceptance: roadmap §3c marked verified-no-port OR ported hooks ship with parity tests. Light single commit either way.\""}},"planOnly":true},{"id":"3d-workers","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"blocked","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 3d — Codex-based workers via native spawn</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Replace ralph's <code>codex-exec.sh</code> bash invocation with native <code>spawn-agent-role</code>. Compatibility audit (3d-i) verified in roadmap.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex / plugin</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\n        <span>est. 1-2 d</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":70}],"command":{"name":"3d-workers","descriptionHtml":"Phase 3d — codex-based workers via native spawn","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Blocked on <code>3a-skills</code> discovery (paused) AND <code>3b-agents</code> landing the agent role TOMLs."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 3d — replace ralph's codex-exec.sh subprocess pattern with codex native spawn-agent-role for codex-based workers. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 3d (+ §3d-i compatibility audit). PREREQUISITE: Phase 3b-i (I 3b-agents) must have landed the agent role TOMLs so spawn-agent-role has roles to invoke. Today: ralph orchestrator skill runs `bash → codex-exec.sh` to spawn a fresh `codex exec` for each codex-based worker (planner, reviewer, verifier) — separate process, no continuity. Target: ralph orchestrator skill calls the native spawn-agent-role tool; each worker becomes a sub-thread on the SAME app-server codexu is connected to. Compatibility constraints (audited 2026-05-02, see roadmap §3d-i): SpawnAgentArgs requires message (string) + task_name; inter-agent fast path supports text-only initial input (UserInput::Text only); fork_context field is rejected; deny_unknown_fields is strict; empty agent_type maps to default role; task_name becomes AgentPath. Files to touch: ralph orchestrator skill SCRIPT under ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/skills/run-ralph/ (or wherever the codex-exec.sh shellout lives) — replace shell invocation with codex native spawn API call. codex-exec.sh stays in tree but becomes unused for codex roles (still used by claude-exec.sh and copilot-exec.sh per Phase 3e). Read packages/codexu-plugin/CLAUDE.md and the spawn audit in roadmap. Acceptance: one orchestrator job (suggest a small 3-way review) runs end-to-end using native spawn; each worker thread visible in codexu/codex thread list (Phase 6 resumability hook); ralph .ralph/jobs/<name>/ artifacts produced as before. Test by running an existing job end-to-end via `/implement-with-ralph` against a trivial PRD. Surface to operator before merging — this changes ralph orchestration behavior.\""}}},{"id":"3fg-package","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"blocked","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 3f + 3g — Asset migration + Plugin packaging</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Port shell libs, statusline scripts; convert to <code>.codex-plugin/plugin.json</code>; publish marketplace catalog.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex / plugin</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~1 d combined</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":80}],"command":{"name":"3fg-package","descriptionHtml":"Phase 3f + 3g — asset migration + plugin packaging","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Blocked on <code>3a-skills</code> discovery commit (paused). The discovery may change the plugin.json schema expectations this command builds against."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 3f + 3g — port ralph plugin assets (shell libs, scripts) and convert plugin packaging from Claude Code format to codex format. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 3f and §Phase 3g. Phase 3f assets to port from ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/: lib/finding-merge.sh, lib/parse-not-tested-trailers.sh (shell utilities), any other lib/*.sh, statusline scripts (.ps1 + .sh) if present. Target: under packages/codexu-plugin/ — preserve directory structure. Phase 3g packaging: convert ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/.claude-plugin/plugin.json to packages/codexu-plugin/.codex-plugin/plugin.json (new manifest schema per codex/core-plugins/src/manifest.rs); update bundle layout (no agents/ directory — agent roles go in user config per Phase 3b-i); set up marketplace catalog at packages/codexu-plugin/.agents/plugins/marketplace.json (per Phase 1c finding — `codex plugin install <path>` does not exist; only `codex plugin marketplace add <SOURCE>`). Existing packages/codexu-plugin/.agents/plugins/marketplace.json already lists codexu-plugin (Phase 1c); extend or add ralph as second catalog entry, your choice. Update install instructions in ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/CLAUDE.md and (if exists) plugins/options-mode/CLAUDE.md to reflect codex marketplace add. Read packages/codexu-plugin/README.md + Phase 1c §Personal plugin scaffolding. Acceptance: `codex plugin marketplace upgrade codexu` picks up ralph plugin; ralph skills + asset references resolve from new locations; one user-invocable skill works via TUI /skills picker. Single commit or split per-asset, your judgment.\""}}},{"id":"3h-options","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T16:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":"border-color: var(--ok); opacity: 0.8;","html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 3h — options-mode plugin migration <span class=\"sub\">(shipped 2026-05-13)</span></div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Shipped <code>packages/codexu-options-mode-plugin/</code> with SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, Stop hook wiring, docs, statusline script copies, and a reproducible smoke script. Phase 3h-tail keeps statusline plugin slot + AskUserQuestion auto-intercept as engine follow-ups.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex / plugin</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\n        <span>Phase 3h done</span>\n        <span class=\"sub\">roadmap §Phase 3h</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":70}],"command":{"name":"3h-options","descriptionHtml":"Phase 3h — options-mode plugin migration · <code>packages/codexu-options-mode-plugin/</code> (14 commits merged from <code>phase-3h-options-mode-plugin</code>)","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 3h — migrate options-mode plugin from Claude Code (575 LOC of Node.js hook logic + slash command + statusline + tag-protocol enforcement) to codex plugin format. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 3h. Source: C:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/options-mode/ — 4 hook files (SessionStart, UserPromptSubmit, Stop, statusline), slash command /options-mode, PowerShell + Bash statusline scripts. What it does today: SessionStart injects rules (agent must close every turn with <options-mode>continue</options-mode> tag OR structured choice prompt); UserPromptSubmit handles /options-mode on|off|status toggle; Stop hook reads JSONL transcript, deterministically checks for closing tag, blocks turn-end if missing; statusline shows enabled/disabled state; NO LLM classification — pure tag detection. Codex hook parity (verified 2026-05-02, see roadmap §3h codex hook parity check): config/src/hook_config.rs:42 defines Stop hook kind — capabilities options-mode needs are supported. Target: new codex plugin packages/codexu-options-mode-plugin/ (or under codexu-plugin/, your choice — surface to operator). Convert Node hooks to codex hook format; convert slash command; preserve tag-protocol enforcement byte-identically. CRITICAL: the Stop hook reads codex JSONL transcript shape (NOT Claude's) — verify against codex thread JSONL format and adapt the last_assistant_message detection per roadmap note. Read C:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/options-mode/CLAUDE.md if present. Acceptance: enable plugin; agent ends turn WITHOUT tag → Stop hook returns decision:'block' with reason + continuation prompt; agent ends turn WITH <options-mode>continue</options-mode> tag → Stop allows turn end; /options-mode off → Stop becomes pass-through; statusline shows current state. Test command: integration test against codex exec session with mock transcript shapes. Single commit; reference Phase 3h.\""}},"mergeCommit":"e71497eb"},{"id":"userid-cleanup","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-25T11:00:00Z","mergeCommit":"25b9a573","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">userid-cleanup — drop multi-tenant userId scoping in happy-server</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">happy-server is embedded in the single-user daemon since Sprint A; ~140 <code>userId</code> refs across <code>packages/happy-server/sources/</code> are dead weight. Strip from request decorators, Prisma queries, eventRouter fan-out, and <code>allocateUserSeq</code>. <strong>Blocked on perf-WS3</strong> — WS3's per-user ring buffer's userId partition gets stripped as part of this commit.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-server\">happy-server</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">medium</span>\n        <span>blocked on WS3</span>\n        <span class=\"sub\">see <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code> \"Drop userId scoping\"</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":20}],"command":{"name":"userid-cleanup","descriptionHtml":"Drop multi-tenant userId scoping in happy-server (~140 refs) · unblocked (perf-WS3 shipped <code>197b0148</code>)","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Touches happy-server eventRouter + Prisma queries; conflicts with anything else mutating <code>packages/happy-server/sources/</code>. Run solo or pair only with research/codex-submodule tasks."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Drop multi-tenant userId scoping from happy-server. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md 'Drop userId scoping in happy-server' bullet. Context: happy-server is now embedded inside the per-user daemon (Sprint A createHappyServer() + dualListenerBinding); exactly ONE user per process. Multi-tenant userId scoping (~140 refs across packages/happy-server/sources/) is dead weight. Scope: remove userId from request decorators (api.ts:88, loopbackCapability.ts:34); drop userId from Prisma queries on Session / SessionMessage / Machine; simplify eventRouter (packages/happy-server/sources/app/events/eventRouter.ts) to single-user fan-out (no per-user partition); drop allocateUserSeq's userId scoping (becomes process-global seq). Keep Prisma columns themselves — schema migration is OUT OF SCOPE; only code paths change. PREREQUISITE: perf-WS3 must already be on main; WS3's per-user ring buffer's userId partition gets stripped as part of this commit (mention this in commit body referencing the WS3 commit). Read packages/happy-server/AGENTS.md before starting. Acceptance: cross-package typecheck green (happy-server + happy-cli + happy-app); all happy-server tests green; commit body lists every userId code path removed with file:line counts. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-server}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-userid-cleanup.log; cross-package: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-server}' --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' --filter '{packages/happy-app}' exec tsc --noEmit 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-userid-tc.log. Single commit on main. Pitfall: socket auth payload — clients still send a token; resolve it once at process start to the single-user identity, don't strip the auth layer.\""}}},{"id":"happy-upstream-sync","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T21:27:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"happy-upstream-sync","descriptionHtml":"Periodic — review new commits in slopus/happy since last sync","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Run the happy-upstream-sync periodic procedure. Per the skill at .agents/skills/happy-upstream-sync/SKILL.md (read end-to-end first). Anchor on the latest upstream release: gh api repos/slopus/happy/releases/latest --jq '{tag,sha:.target_commitish}'. Resume from the previous sync's upstream commit (look in docs/fork-notes.md and the 'Upstream merge YYYY-MM-DD' bullet in plans/codexu-roadmap.md — last full sync was 2026-05-03 at slopus/happy commit absorbed in our 25fe2cf3). For each commit in the range: classify cherry-pick / manual / defer / skip per the heuristics in the skill (file-location-based fast filter; happy-app/sources/sync is usually Manual; happy-server is usually Skip; happy-app/sources/components UI is usually Cherry-pick; bug-fix commits in code we still have are usually Cherry-pick; libsodium/account-auth/multi-tenant code is Skip). Apply decisions. Run cross-package typecheck + happy-app vitest (skill has the exact tee commands). Update the 4 sync-trail docs in one commit: docs/fork-notes.md, plans/codexu-roadmap.md 'Upstream merge' bullet, packages/happy-app/CHANGELOG.md if user-visible, regenerate sources/changelog/changelog.json. Final commit message: 'chore(upstream-sync): absorb slopus/happy through <tag> (<N> commits triaged)' with per-bucket counts in body. Surface to operator if cherry-pick volume is >30 (split into batches) or if any pick fails typecheck after manual conflict resolution. Pitfall: react-native-reanimated bumps break the vitest RN stub; ToolDiffView changes are likely Manual not Cherry-pick (we collapsed it post-merge). After commit, bump the lastRanAt timestamp for happy-upstream-sync in .ralph-overview/data.json (find the task entry with id='happy-upstream-sync' in OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[] and update its runs[] / periodic.lastRanAt to today's date).\""}}},{"id":"codex-upstream-rebase","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"5d26cc93,8194f011,f4551c95,03fe64287","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T21:12:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-upstream-rebase","descriptionHtml":"Periodic — rebase codex submodule on openai/codex upstream","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Run the codex-submodule upstream rebase. The procedure lives in the codex submodule itself: codex/.claude/commands/rebase-upstream.md and codex/.claude/commands/sync-upstream.md (read both end-to-end before starting). Workflow: cd into a worktree of the codex submodule at .ralph/jobs/<job-name>/codex-worktree/ pointed at gim-home/codex's main (don't edit the parent codexu checkout's submodule directly — minimize-conflict-surface tenet from plans/codexu-roadmap.md). Run the subtree pull from openai/codex per the codex submodule's sync-upstream skill. Resolve conflicts between upstream and our overlay crates (codex-rs-overlay/codex-copilot, codex-copilot-launcher, codex-invariant-tests). Run cargo build --workspace from inside codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs to verify. Commit on a topic branch IN THE SUBMODULE; push to gim-home/codex; then in this codexu repo bump the submodule pointer as a SEPARATE commit on codexu main. After commit, bump the lastRanAt timestamp for codex-upstream-rebase in .ralph-overview/data.json (find the task entry with id='codex-upstream-rebase' in OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[] and update its runs[] / periodic.lastRanAt to today's date). Surface to operator if openai/codex has a major-version upstream bump or if overlay crates need API-shape changes to match new upstream — those are escalations, not routine sync work.\""}}},{"id":"agent-view-research","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T06:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"agent-view-research","descriptionHtml":"Research Claude Code's `agent view` feature → `plans/agent-view-research.md` + 6 follow-up tasks (agent-tree-rpc, session-parent-link, mobile-tree-view, session-role-pill, spawn-from-app, agent-status-stream)","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Research Claude Code's 'agent view' feature (released recently). Output: a research doc at plans/agent-view-research.md that covers: (1) what the feature IS — how it presents agents, what UI affordances it gives, what spawning model it uses, what state it persists, what permissions / scoping it enforces; (2) where it lives in Claude Code (file paths, schema, RPC surface) — read the relevant source if accessible from C:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main; (3) how it differs from codex's existing multi_agents_v2/spawn.rs and codex's [agents.<role>] TOML world (read codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/multi_agents_v2/spawn.rs as reference — READ-ONLY per the minimize-conflict-surface tenet in plans/codexu-roadmap.md); (4) what concepts we'd want to bring to codexu: spawning model, state, persistence, communication, plugin-scoping; (5) what concepts we'd want to bring to the HAPPY/CODEXU MOBILE APP UI — today the app shows a flat list of sessions, but agent view might surface a richer model (agent roles visible per session, spawn relationships drawn as a tree, parent-child status rolled up, ability to spawn-a-named-agent from the app). Read packages/happy-app/sources/app/(app)/index.tsx for the current session-list surface and packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md for the sync invariants the app honors. Output for each finding: whether it applies to (a) codex agent runtime, (b) codexu mobile app UI, or (c) both — different fix-sites have different package targets. Output: the doc + a decomposition into follow-up ralph tasks with file:line refs and effort estimates per applicability bucket. NO CODE CHANGES — research-only. Add new task entries to plans/parallel-assignments.md and .ralph-overview/data.json (append to OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[]) for each follow-up that emerges from the research, AND set spawnedFrom='agent-view-research' on each new task's entry in .ralph-overview/data.json so the spawn relationship is captured. Cross-reference plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 6 'Long-lived teammates' since this feature likely overlaps. Surface to operator before opening any follow-up code task — the research output is itself the deliverable.\""}},"planOnly":true},{"id":"overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema","scope":"codexu|ralph-overview","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-27T17:30:00Z","mergeCommit":"ada908c6,c2a05d35","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema","descriptionHtml":"Schema cleanup consolidating 3 inter-related changes that all touch <code>types.ts</code>: (A) rename <code>OverviewTask.phase</code> → <code>OverviewTask.lifecycle</code> (decouples from RalphStage), (B) restructure <code>command.planPrompt</code> → <code>command.prompts: { brainstorm?, plan?, impl? }</code> (supports per-phase kickoff + regressions), (C) add <code>OverviewTask.initialStage?: RalphStage</code> (bookkeeper's intent for kickoff stage), (D) add <code>phase</code> field to <code>CrewSessionRef</code> (decouples from stage bucket), (E) document non-linear stage transitions including regressions (planning → brainstorming on gap-discovery; implementing → planning on plan-error). Read-side fallback (<code>lifecycle ?? phase</code>, <code>prompts.plan ?? planPrompt</code>) for 1 minor version.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Large coordinated schema change across BOTH repos (codexu + ai-developer-toolkit). ~50 task entries in .ralph-overview/data.json + types.ts + Toolbar + TaskCommand + filters + MCP tool schemas + tests + bookkeeper docs. Single-PR-per-repo. Recommend running through ralph 5-phase (brainstorm OR plan-direct → impl → 5a/5b convergence). Coordinate with active impl members — this task BLOCKS other types.ts edits while in flight."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Schema cleanup — consolidate 5 inter-related changes to the OverviewTask + RalphPipelineState + CrewSessionRef schemas. All touch types.ts; doing them as one PR per repo avoids deprecation-alias churn from sequential changes.\n\n## Change A — Rename OverviewTask.phase → OverviewTask.lifecycle\n\nThe hand-curated bookkeeper field collides with the watcher-derived RalphStage enum (both have 'plan-ready' + 'shipped' values, different meanings). Decouple:\n\n- Field: phase → lifecycle (in OverviewTask interface).\n- Values: plan-ready → tracked, shipped → merged, closed → archived.\n\n## Change B — Restructure command.planPrompt → command.prompts\n\nThe singular planPrompt field has been overloaded with /brainstorm-with-ralph and /plan-with-ralph and /implement-with-ralph content. Rename + restructure:\n\n- `command.planPrompt: string` → `command.prompts: { brainstorm?: string, plan?: string, impl?: string }`.\n- Bookkeeper authors whichever phase(s) are anticipated starting points.\n- Supports REGRESSIONS: if a planner finds a gap and the task regresses to brainstorming, the stored prompts.brainstorm is reused for the new brainstorm member.\n- Maintain back-compat alias: if `command.prompts` is absent, fall back to reading `command.planPrompt` as `prompts.plan`. Drop the alias one minor later.\n\n## Change C — Add OverviewTask.initialStage?: RalphStage\n\nThe bookkeeper's intent for where the task should kick off. Optional; auto-defaulted when absent:\n- prompts.brainstorm set + no prompts.plan → initialStage='brainstorming'\n- prompts.plan set → initialStage='planning'\n- prompts.impl set + no prompts.plan → initialStage='plan-ready' (rare; only when plan.md lives externally)\n\nThe runtime deriveNextCommand uses initialStage as the starting-point hint when no .ralph/<dirs>/ filesystem state exists yet.\n\n## Change D — Add phase field to CrewSessionRef\n\nDerived from memberName prefix (brainstorm-* / plan-* / impl-*). Decouples 'phase the member intended to run' from 'stage the task was in when discovered.' Documented in detail in the prior .ralph-overview/data.json entry for ralph-overview-crew-session-phase-field (now consolidated into this task).\n\n- buildCrewSessionRef (D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/lib/crews-cross-walk.mjs:207-228): add phase derivation from memberName.\n- types.ts CrewSessionRef interface: add phase?: 'brainstorm' | 'plan' | 'impl' | null.\n- sync-ralph-state.mjs --update-crew-session: accept optional --phase override.\n\n## Change E — Document dynamic stage transitions including regressions\n\nUpdate D:/harness-efforts/codexu/plans/codexu-roadmap.md § 'Phase discipline' and D:/harness-efforts/codexu/CLAUDE.md and D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/CLAUDE.md:\n\n- Stages form a STATE MACHINE, not a pipeline. Forward transitions: brainstorming → brainstorm-ready → planning → plan-ready → implementing → reviewing → shipped. Backward (regression) transitions: any stage → brainstorming OR planning, with reason recorded.\n- Each regression spawns a FRESH crew member of the regressed phase (per feedback_phase_discipline_separate_members). The new member reads the relevant prompts.<phase> seed from .ralph-overview/data.json.\n- Add optional `RalphPipelineState.regressionReason?: string` to record why a task regressed. Watcher detects regressions by comparing the inferred stage with the prior sidecar's stage and stamps the reason if the operator provides it via --regression-reason flag on sync-ralph-state.mjs.\n\n## Files to touch (codexu repo)\n\n1. .ralph-overview/data.json — sed-rewrite phase → lifecycle on ~50 entries; for each task, convert planPrompt → prompts.plan (preserving content); add initialStage where the bookkeeper has explicit intent (most tasks default to 'planning'). Verify zero residual 'phase:' or top-level 'planPrompt:' occurrences after.\n2. plans/codexu-roadmap.md — update Bookkeeper workflow + Phase discipline sections to reflect new field names + state-machine transitions.\n3. D:/harness-efforts/codexu/CLAUDE.md (untracked; bookkeeper operating manual) — same.\n\n## Files to touch (ai-developer-toolkit repo — ralph-overview plugin)\n\n4. tools/overview-viewer/src/types.ts — rename OverviewTask.phase → lifecycle; restructure command.planPrompt → command.prompts; add OverviewTask.initialStage; add CrewSessionRef.phase; add RalphPipelineState.regressionReason. Read-side alias support for prompts.plan ?? planPrompt.\n5. tools/overview-viewer/src/components/Toolbar.tsx — filter chip group rename (FilterAxis 'phase' → 'lifecycle'); value rename.\n6. tools/overview-viewer/src/components/TaskCommand.tsx — badge rename; render prompts.<phase> chips inline; render CrewSessionRef phase pills.\n7. tools/overview-viewer/src/utils/filters.ts — filter predicate rename.\n8. tools/overview-viewer/src/__tests__/*.test.tsx — fixture updates for renamed field + value + prompts shape + phase pills.\n9. scripts/sync-ralph-state.mjs + scripts/lib/sync-core.mjs — read sites: lifecycle ?? phase, prompts.plan ?? planPrompt; emit new shape on write.\n10. scripts/lib/crews-cross-walk.mjs — buildCrewSessionRef phase derivation (Change D).\n11. scripts/lib/derive-next-command.mjs — when state is absent (no .ralph/<dirs>/) AND task.initialStage is set, return the kickoff command from prompts.<initialStage>.\n12. scripts/lib/emit-snapshot-schema.mjs + JSON Schema output — additive fields; deprecation aliases.\n13. tools/overview-mcp/src/tools/*.ts — list_tasks / get_task / list_recommendations: surface new field names with read-side aliases for 1 minor.\n14. README.md + CLAUDE.md in the plugin — doc updates.\n15. CHANGELOG.md — v2.3.0 entry (or whatever the next minor is): describe the rename, restructure, additive fields, deprecation timeline.\n\n## Bookkeeper memory updates\n\n- C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/projects/D--harness-efforts-codexu/memory/feedback_bookkeeper_updates_overview_data.md — rename phase → lifecycle in the example + rule text.\n- C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/projects/D--harness-efforts-codexu/memory/feedback_phase_discipline_separate_members.md — add a note: regressions are valid; each regression spawns a fresh member; the regressed-to phase reuses the stored prompts.<phase> seed.\n\n## Migration (two-step deprecation)\n\nv1 of this rename (next minor; v2.3.0 candidate): ship rename + restructure + initialStage + CrewSessionRef.phase + regressionReason WITH read-side fallbacks for lifecycle ?? phase and prompts.plan ?? planPrompt. Write-side emits ONLY the new shape.\n\nv2 (next-next minor): drop the read-side fallbacks. By then all known consumers (React viewer, MCP tools, external dashboards if any) are migrated.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Zero residual 'phase:' (matching the OverviewTask shape, not the CrewSessionRef phase) in .ralph-overview/data.json after rename.\n- Zero residual top-level 'planPrompt:' occurrences in .ralph-overview/data.json after restructure.\n- React viewer renders unchanged from user POV: same chips, same badges, with renamed values + new per-phase chips on tasks that have multi-phase prompts.\n- MCP tools surface lifecycle + prompts shape; legacy field names work via alias for 1 minor.\n- New tests cover: initialStage-driven kickoff command derivation; CrewSessionRef.phase derivation from memberName; lifecycle-rename round-trip; regression-from-planning-to-brainstorming with reason recorded.\n- Bookkeeper docs explain the two axes (lifecycle vs RalphStage), per-phase prompts, regression flow, and the one-member-per-phase rule.\n- Cross-package typecheck green; vitest green in ralph-overview.\n\n## Workflow\n\nThis task is concrete enough to skip /brainstorm-with-ralph. Run /plan-with-ralph directly on this seed; produces plan.md with stories for each of the 5 changes (A-E) probably as 5-7 user stories. Phase 5a/5b convergence runs internally per the spawn-prompt invariant.\n\nVersion bump: ralph-overview v2.3.0 (minor; additive types + deprecation aliases). codexu commits to main as a single PR (the .ralph-overview/data.json rewrite + docs).\n\nBlocks other types.ts edits while in flight — coordinate with any concurrent ralph-overview impl members.\""}}},{"id":"crews-roles-and-direct-operator-channel","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","initialStage":"brainstorming","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-roles-and-direct-operator-channel","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Brainstorm-first.</strong> Redesign crews plugin roles to formalize a 3-party model: <strong>user (human operator)</strong> ↔ <strong>lead</strong> ↔ <strong>members</strong>. Today the plugin only models lead↔member; the human is an implicit out-of-band actor with no plugin awareness. Outcomes wanted: (1) explicit role title for the human (e.g. <code>operator</code>) so leads + members can refer to them consistently in prose; (2) explicit role title for the lead (e.g. <code>lead</code> — likely formalize existing usage); (3) define when the lead should escalate to user-direct-member interaction; (4) define how the user notifies the lead about an out-of-band interaction they just had with a member; (5) ideally surface user↔member direct chats in the lead's mail/history trail automatically (mirroring how progress envelopes flow) so the lead doesn't lose context.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Multiple competing approaches</strong> on each axis: <em>naming</em> (operator/principal/owner/director/human; lead/orchestrator/coordinator/scrum-master/PM), <em>direct-channel mechanism</em> (auto-detect via member's mailbox event-stream / explicit user CLI command / magic-prefix in member terminal / mailbox notification on next session-resume / nothing — trust transcripts), and <em>lead-side surface</em> (separate inbox category / merged into review-mail / dashboard-only / silent until lead asks). Brainstorm should pressure-test each. Touches plugin manifest schema (new actor role), hooks (SessionStart/PostToolUse/Stop), CLI commands, and operator-facing docs in BOTH crews CLAUDE.md and per-engine briefings. Affects every existing crew lead's mental model — coordinate with the in-flight overview-bookkeeper session before rolling out."}],"prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"Redesign the crews plugin to formalize a 3-party actor model (user ↔ lead ↔ members) with named roles and a defined user-to-member direct-interaction channel.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nToday the crews plugin coordinates 2 parties: leads and members. The human operating the workspace is OUTSIDE the plugin's model — leads relay messages to/from 'the operator' as untyped prose, members never address them directly, and the plugin's hooks/CLI commands have no concept of the human's identity or interaction surface.\n\nThis works for the common case but breaks down when:\n- A lead's session output addresses 'the operator' generically — members who read shared threads see 'lead said: ask operator about X' but have no canonical way to refer back.\n- The human wants to go DIRECTLY to a member's terminal and interact (debug something interactively, demo a UI, type a quick correction). Today this happens out-of-band: the lead never sees the interaction, the member has to ad-hoc explain it later.\n- The lead should sometimes ASK the human to take a direct action on a member (e.g. 'the member needs to see your screen' or 'this is a 5-message debugging dance, just go to their terminal'). Today there's no protocol — the lead types prose hoping the human reads it.\n- The human has interacted with the member directly and now wants the lead to know what happened. Today they have to manually re-explain to the lead.\n\n## Design axes the brainstorm must pressure-test\n\n### Axis 1 — Naming\n\nFor the LEAD's role: keep `lead` (matches existing plugin terminology) vs rename to `orchestrator` / `coordinator` / `scrum-master` / `PM`. `lead` is in active use; rename would be churn unless brainstorm finds a strong reason.\n\nFor the USER's role: `operator` (matches my bookkeeper docs) vs `principal` (auth-like) vs `owner` (overloaded) vs `director` vs `human` (too generic). Brainstorm should propose the term that least conflicts with existing plugin / Claude Code / Copilot vocabulary AND is natural in prose ('operator paused the job', 'operator confirmed the merge').\n\n### Axis 2 — Direct user↔member channel mechanism\n\nWhen the human goes to a member's terminal and types directly, how does the plugin detect + log it?\n\nA. **Nothing.** Trust transcripts. Lead reads member's lastTurnAt + transcriptPath if curious.\nB. **Auto-detect via stdin source.** Plugin hooks (PreToolUse / new) flag a turn whose first user message was typed locally (vs delivered via mailbox). Tag the manifest/mailbox-history entry with `via: 'operator-direct'`.\nC. **Explicit CLI command.** User runs `crews.js operator-message <member-name> --crew <crew> <text>` from any terminal; plugin posts as an `operator-direct` envelope into the member's inbox AND into the lead's review queue.\nD. **Magic prefix in member terminal.** User types `OPERATOR:` or similar as the first line; member's PostToolUse parses and emits an `operator-direct` log entry.\nE. **Hybrid.** B + C, with manifest field `lastOperatorDirectAt` derived from whichever fires.\n\nLens against: latency (how fast does lead know), false-positive risk (B might mis-flag), discoverability (D needs user to remember the prefix), maintenance burden.\n\n### Axis 3 — When should the lead ASK the user to do direct interaction?\n\nFormalize the escalation pattern with named triggers:\n\n- **interactive-debug-needed:** member is in a 3+ turn interactive debugging loop where the lead-relay overhead would dominate.\n- **demo-required:** the member needs to show a UI/build/output the human must visually inspect.\n- **paused-on-real-time-question:** member's kind=question requires sub-30s response cycle.\n- **transcript-too-large-to-relay:** member's context is huge and the lead can't summarize fast enough.\n\nProvide a slash command or envelope kind (e.g. `kind=escalate-to-operator`) that leads emit to BOTH the operator AND the member, signaling 'go talk directly'.\n\n### Axis 4 — How the user notifies the lead about a direct interaction\n\nAfter the user has gone direct, how do they tell the lead 'I just did X with member Y'?\n\nP. **CLI command:** `crews.js notify-lead --crew <crew> --about <member> <summary>` posts an envelope into the lead's review queue tagged `kind=operator-direct-summary`.\nQ. **Member auto-summary on next turn:** the member detects the operator-direct turn(s) just happened, summarizes in its next kind=progress envelope so the lead's normal mail-review surface picks it up.\nR. **Lead dashboard recomputes from manifest:** lead's `list-members` command shows a per-member 'lastOperatorDirectAt' column; lead reads transcript on demand.\nS. **Silent until lead reviews member transcript:** simplest; least proactive.\n\n### Axis 5 — Lead-side surface\n\nWhere/how does the lead see operator-direct events?\n\n- Separate review-mail category (`/crews:review-operator-direct`)\n- Merged into existing review-mail with a visual flag\n- Dashboard-only (list-members column)\n- Both inbox + dashboard\n- None — lead asks explicitly\n\n## Constraints / non-negotiable invariants\n\n1. Backwards compatible: existing leads + members without role-aware code keep working. New role surfaces are additive.\n2. Cross-engine: claude-code AND copilot hooks need the same surface (per the recent migration audit's lessons).\n3. Don't break the v1.6.2 listener-arm gate or v1.7.x sessionId pointer index.\n4. Bookkeeper-lead (overview-bookkeeper) should NOT need to change its operating model dramatically — this is additive UX, not a rewrite.\n5. Operator notify-CLI commands must work without requiring the operator to know which crew/lead they belong to (auto-discover from cwd, similar to crews.js's existing cwd-discovery patterns).\n\n## Reference + investigation\n\n- Current plugin manifest at `./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/.claude-plugin/plugin.json` + same path under `.copilot-plugin/`.\n- Hooks at `plugins/crews/hooks/{SessionStart,stop,pre-tool-use,post-tool-use}.js` (+ Copilot mirrors). These are where role-aware logic would live.\n- CLI at `plugins/crews/tools/crews.js` — would gain `operator-message`, `notify-lead`, or whatever the brainstorm chooses.\n- Mailbox envelope schema at `plugins/crews/hooks/lib/mailbox.js` (or wherever envelope kinds are defined) — would gain new kinds.\n- Existing `actor` concept in manifests — review whether adding a 3rd actor role (operator) makes sense or whether operator is fundamentally different (no manifest, no listener, but has identity).\n- Auto-memory `feedback_check_mailbox_after_tool_bursts` documents one ad-hoc operator-interaction pattern.\n\nLenses: feasibility (codex — what's the plumbing actually look like?), product-reality (copilot — would real operators use the CLI?), devil's-advocate (does formalizing this just add overhead operators won't bother with?), simplification (lightest mechanism that captures 80% of the value).\n\n## Deliverable\n\nAt end of brainstorm Phase 5, produce `.ralph/brainstorms/crews-roles-and-direct-operator-channel/selected-direction.md` with:\n\n- Recommended naming (lead-role term + operator-role term) with rationale.\n- Recommended direct-channel mechanism (A-E or hybrid) with rationale.\n- Recommended lead-side surface (separate category / merged / dashboard / etc).\n- Concrete escalation triggers (named, with examples) and the slash command / envelope-kind to fire them.\n- Concrete user-to-lead notify mechanism (P-S or hybrid).\n- Migration story: how existing crews v1.7.x sessions absorb the new model.\n- Acceptance criteria for eventual impl.\n- Open questions for the planner.\n\nDo NOT chain into /plan-with-ralph. The bookkeeper spawns a fresh plan-crews-roles-and-direct-operator-channel member from the selected-direction.md per phase discipline.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-overview-data-relocation-and-json-migration","scope":"ralph-overview","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T23:48:00Z","mergeCommit":"d7200aee,5f4268aa","initialStage":"planning","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-overview-data-relocation-and-json-migration","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Plan-first (design locked).</strong> Migrate ralph-overview's data layout: (1) hand-curated source moves from <code>.ralph-overview/data.json</code> to <code>.ralph-overview/data.json</code> (pure JSON, schema doc extracted to sibling <code>SCHEMA.md</code>); (2) all watcher-generated artifacts move from <code>plans/overview-*.{json,jsonl}</code> into <code>.ralph-overview/generated/*</code> (drop <code>overview-</code> prefix — parent dir conveys context); (3) viewer build artifact moves to <code>.ralph-overview/generated/overview.html</code>; (4) runtime lock + watcher-owner files move from <code>.ralph/overview-*</code> to <code>.ralph-overview/generated/.lock/*</code>; (5) <code>.ralph-overview/config.json</code> moves to <code>.ralph-overview/config.json</code>; (6) plugin's skills (<code>work-on</code>, <code>triage</code>, <code>blocker-report</code>) and viewer become JSON-aware (currently <code>parseOverviewData</code> requires the <code>.js</code> AST shape; viewer loads via <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag). Cross-repo work: plugin changes ship as a <code>ralph-overview</code> minor-version bump on <code>gim-home/ai-developer-toolkit</code>; codexu adopts as a follow-up commit. Bonus: relocating to <code>.ralph-overview/</code> with JSON-only data fixes the pre-existing <code>parseOverviewData</code> 1-statement strictness bug for free.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Cross-repo migration with sequencing risk.</strong> Plugin changes must ship before codexu adopts the new layout — otherwise consumers on the old plugin can't read the new file paths and the bookkeeper dashboard breaks mid-cutover. Plan must specify the version-bump + adoption order, and decide whether to keep the legacy <code>.ralph-overview/data.json</code> read path with a deprecation warning for one minor version or hard-cut at the bump. Touches: <code>scripts/lib/parse-overview-data.mjs</code>, <code>scripts/lib/default-config.mjs</code>, <code>scripts/lib/resolve-config.mjs</code>, <code>scripts/lib/sync-core.mjs</code>, every skill SKILL.md that hardcodes <code>.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json</code>, <code>tools/overview-viewer/</code> (the React bootstrap that currently expects <code>window.OVERVIEW_DATA</code> to exist at boot), the watcher's lock-path resolution, and codexu's <code>.ralph-overview/data.json</code> + 8+ doc references."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Migrate ralph-overview data layout to .ralph-overview/ with JSON-only source-of-truth + a generated/ subdir for all watcher output.\n\n## Design is LOCKED (do not re-litigate — plan around these)\n\nFinal target layout (codexu reference; plugin defaults should match):\n\n```\n.ralph-overview/\n├── config.json                 ← hand-curated config (was .ralph-overview/config.json)\n├── data.json                   ← hand-curated source (was .ralph-overview/data.json)\n├── SCHEMA.md                   ← extracted from the leading comment block of .ralph-overview/data.json\n└── generated/                  ← TRACKED in git, watcher writes exclusively here\n    ├── snapshot.json\n    ├── ralph-state.json\n    ├── recommendations.json\n    ├── dependency-graph.json\n    ├── activity.jsonl\n    ├── snapshot.schema.json\n    ├── overview.html           ← viewer build artifact\n    └── .lock/\n        ├── sync.lock           ← was .ralph/overview-sync.lock\n        └── watcher.owner       ← was .ralph/overview-watcher.owner\n```\n\nLocked decisions from the operator (don't re-debate):\n1. **Track `generated/` in git** — fresh clones / CI / agents get usable state without running the watcher first. Matches today's behavior (the current `plans/overview-*.json` files are tracked).\n2. **Drop the `overview-` prefix inside `generated/`** — the parent dir name already conveys the context.\n3. **`overview.html` lives at `.ralph-overview/generated/overview.html`** — sibling to the data sidecars, not under a separate `viewer/` subdir.\n4. **Lock files live at `.ralph-overview/generated/.lock/*`** — even though they're runtime coordination markers (not data), this keeps everything-state-related under one top-level subdir.\n5. **Pure JSON, not JSONC** — the schema doc currently atop `.ralph-overview/data.json` extracts to a sibling `SCHEMA.md` rather than living as `//` comments inside the data file. Pure JSON parses everywhere without parser-choice ceremony.\n\n## Cross-repo scope — plan must address both\n\n### Repo A — `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/` (the plugin)\n\nMust ship FIRST as a minor-version bump (suggested: v2.x — confirm against the plugin's existing version trajectory and CHANGELOG). Plugin changes:\n\n- **P1. Data loader accepts JSON.** `scripts/lib/parse-overview-data.mjs` currently uses `@babel/parser` and requires exactly 1 top-level statement (rejecting codexu's `globalThis.window = …` polyfill + `window.OVERVIEW_DATA = …` two-statement shape). Replace with: auto-detect by extension. For `.json`, use plain `JSON.parse`; for `.js`, keep the existing AST path for back-compat. Side-effect: fixes the pre-existing 1-statement strictness bug that currently blocks `pnpm sync-ralph-state` on codexu HEAD.\n- **P2. Viewer reads JSON via fetch bootstrap.** `tools/overview-viewer/` currently relies on `<script src=\".ralph-overview/data.json\">` setting `window.OVERVIEW_DATA` + `window.OVERVIEW_RALPH_STATE` before React mounts. Replace with a small async bootstrap that `fetch()`-es the JSON files and populates the globals (or React state) before the tree renders. Preserve all current render behavior; suspense-style loading state is acceptable.\n- **P3. Plugin skills become MCP-only or config-aware.** `skills/{work-on,triage,blocker-report}/SKILL.md` and the matching `.copilot-plugin/copilot-skills/*` Copilot mirrors currently reference `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json` literally. Decision the plan must make: do skills (a) call MCP tools exclusively and never touch files, or (b) resolve `outputs.snapshot` from `overview-config.json` first. Operator prefers (a) — MCP becomes the single read interface — but if MCP doesn't expose everything skills need, mixed-mode is acceptable for v1 with full MCP-only as a follow-up. Either way, NO hardcoded file paths in skill bodies after this lands.\n- **P4. Writer + default-config emit the new layout.** `scripts/lib/default-config.mjs` changes defaults from `plans/*` to `.ralph-overview/generated/*` with the prefix-stripped basenames. `dataFile` default becomes `.ralph-overview/data.json`. Honor existing `outputs.*` overrides in user `overview-config.json` for back-compat with old-layout consumers.\n- **P5. Lock-path resolution.** Wherever the watcher currently composes `<repoRoot>/.ralph/overview-sync.lock` and `<repoRoot>/.ralph/overview-watcher.owner` (search the plugin codebase — likely in `scripts/lib/sync-core.mjs` and `WatcherSupervisor` for the MCP side), change defaults to `<repoRoot>/.ralph-overview/generated/.lock/sync.lock` and `…/watcher.owner`. Lock creation must mkdir-p the `.lock/` path on first use. Honor existing `lockFile` config override for back-compat.\n- **P6. Tests.** Add tests for: JSON loader path; viewer bootstrap fetches and populates state correctly; default config writes to new locations; lock-path mkdir-p behavior; back-compat with old `outputs.*` overrides. Update any test that asserts `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json` to the new default.\n- **P7. CHANGELOG entry.** Document the migration, the back-compat story, the deprecation timeline (if any) for the legacy `.js` loader and `plans/` defaults.\n\n### Repo B — `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/` (this repo)\n\nAdopts the plugin's new defaults AFTER the plugin ships. Codexu changes:\n\n- **C1. Convert data file.** Strip the leading 19-line schema-doc comment + the `globalThis.window = …` polyfill + the `window.OVERVIEW_DATA =` assignment from `.ralph-overview/data.json`. Write the object literal as pure JSON to `.ralph-overview/data.json`. Move the comment block to `.ralph-overview/SCHEMA.md` (preserve all schema notes; format as proper markdown).\n- **C2. Move + rewrite config.** `.ralph-overview/config.json` → `.ralph-overview/config.json`. Update every `outputs.*` path to `.ralph-overview/generated/<prefix-stripped>.json`. Set `dataFile` to `.ralph-overview/data.json`. Update `lockFile` to `.ralph-overview/generated/.lock/sync.lock` (or just drop the override and inherit the plugin's new default).\n- **C3. Delete stale files.** After `pnpm sync-ralph-state` confirms the new layout is being regenerated correctly: `git rm` the legacy `.ralph-overview/data.json`, `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json`, `.ralph-overview/generated/ralph-state.{js,json}`, `plans/overview-recommendations.json`, `plans/overview-dependency-graph.json`, `plans/overview-activity.jsonl`, `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.schema.json`, `plans/overview.html`, `plans/overview.html.next`, and the watcher-related entries in `.ralph/`. Keep design-doc markdowns in `plans/` (overview-data-split.md, overview-viewer-polish-seed.md, overview-vite-react.md — these are human-written context).\n- **C4. Update `package.json` script paths.** The `overview`, `sync-ralph-state`, etc. scripts call into the plugin via `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` (the gitignored resolver wrapper). The wrapper itself doesn't need changes for this migration — it dispatches to the plugin's `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` which gets the new layout via the plugin update. But VERIFY the wrapper still resolves correctly after the plugin version bump.\n- **C5. Docs.** Update every reference to the old paths in AGENTS.md (multiple), `docs/fork-roadmap.md` if any, `docs/fork-notes.md` if any, `plans/overview-data-split.md`, plus the recent 'Copilot migration milestone' block (which mentions the `parseOverviewData` 1-statement bug — that bug will be FIXED by P1; update the block to reflect that). Find-and-replace `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json` → `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json`, etc.\n- **C6. Smoke-verify end-to-end.** After plugin install: run `pnpm sync-ralph-state` (must succeed and write to new locations), `pnpm overview` (must serve the viewer with data populated), `mcp__ralph-overview__overview_parallel_ready_tasks` (must return non-empty results from the new snapshot location).\n\n## Sequencing constraint\n\nPlugin (Repo A) MUST ship its minor-version bump BEFORE codexu (Repo B) adopts. During the window between plugin-ship and codexu-adopt, codexu's `pnpm sync-ralph-state` continues to work because the plugin still honors `outputs.*` overrides in the existing `.ralph-overview/config.json`. Plan must explicitly call this out so the impl member doesn't try to land both changes in one cross-repo PR.\n\n## Open questions the plan must resolve (not the operator's job — answer in the plan)\n\n1. Plugin version-bump magnitude: minor (v2.x with `dataFile` default change) or major (v3.0.0 if any consumer reads the .js form directly)?\n2. Back-compat for `parseOverviewData('.ralph-overview/data.json')`: keep the AST path with a `console.warn` deprecation for N minor versions, or hard-cut at the bump? Operator preference: keep one minor version with deprecation, then drop.\n3. MCP-only skills vs config-aware skills (P3 above) — pick one, justify, list any new MCP tools needed if MCP-only.\n4. Viewer bootstrap pattern: pre-React `await fetch + window.X = …` shim vs React-side `Suspense` + `useSWR`/`useEffect` fetch. Either is fine; pick by simplicity.\n5. Should the lock-files subdir be `.lock/` (operator-specified) or something else more conventional like `.locks/` plural? Operator picked `.lock/` so default to that unless plan finds a strong reason to deviate.\n6. Should codexu's `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` resolver wrapper be retired in favor of direct `node $CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/...` or `node ${COPILOT_HOME}/installed-plugins/...` paths in `package.json` scripts? Probably out of scope for this migration — track as a follow-up.\n\n## Reference — existing artifacts to read before planning\n\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/.ralph-overview/data.json` — the source we're migrating (~395KB, ~10K lines; mostly task entries).\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/.ralph-overview/config.json` — current config, every `outputs.*` field is here.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/lib/parse-overview-data.mjs` — the validator that currently rejects multi-statement JS.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/lib/default-config.mjs` (+ `.d.mts` + `resolve-config.mjs`) — where defaults live.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/tools/overview-viewer/` — React viewer.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/{skills,.copilot-plugin/copilot-skills}/{work-on,triage,blocker-report}/SKILL.md` — the 4 skill SKILL.md files (Claude + Copilot mirrors) hardcoding `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json`.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/CHANGELOG.md` — recent entries for sense of version-bump magnitude conventions.\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/AGENTS.md` — the 'Copilot migration milestone' block references this task's parseOverviewData follow-up at item (b).\n\n## Phase discipline + reporting\n\nThis is the PLAN phase (no brainstorm; design is locked above). Produce ONLY a plan deliverable (e.g. `.ralph/jobs/ralph-overview-data-relocation-and-json-migration/plan.md` and any stories/decomposition the plan-with-ralph skill generates). Do NOT chain into `/implement-with-ralph` — the bookkeeper will spawn a separate impl-phase member after FF'ing the plan to main.\n\nCOMMIT TARGET: lead is on `main`. Commit your plan on a fresh topic branch off main (`ralph/plan-ralph-overview-data-relocation-and-json-migration`), push to `origin`, and report commit SHA + branch in your kind=done body.\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.8.x body-canonical hard-block): final turn ends with `<|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>`. Substantive content (commit SHA, branch, plan summary, open questions, anomalies) in PROSE BODY. Summary > 200 chars + body < 50 chars triggers reject.\n\nNote any Copilot engine anomalies (hook quirks, listener issues, CLI errors) in prose — this spawn is also a smoke-test data point for the v1.8.11 caller-engine default (you should have been spawned as a Copilot member without my passing --engine; verify your manifest engine field if you can.\""}}},{"id":"codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T19:32:00Z","mergeCommit":"8dd10101","initialStage":"planning","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Plan-first.</strong> Retire codexu's gitignored <code>bin/ralph-overview.mjs</code> resolver wrapper. The wrapper is a legacy bootstrap that predates the ralph-overview plugin's modern <code>scripts/init-consumer.mjs</code> pattern (which emits <code>package.json</code> scripts that delegate directly to <code>$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/ralph-overview/bin/ralph-overview.mjs</code>). Because it's in <code>.gitignore</code>, any local-machine fix to it (e.g., the in-session 2026-05-29 Copilot-path addition) doesn't propagate to other clones. Options the plan must evaluate: (a) re-run <code>overview-init</code> to regenerate <code>package.json</code> in the modern direct-path form (works on Claude only — <code>$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT</code> is unset on Copilot CLI); (b) un-ignore <code>/bin/</code> and commit the wrapper with cross-engine support baked in; (c) push cross-engine awareness UPSTREAM to <code>scripts/init-consumer.mjs</code> so every consumer benefits without a per-consumer wrapper.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Cross-engine constraint.</strong> Copilot CLI does NOT export a <code>$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT</code> or equivalent <code>$COPILOT_PLUGIN_ROOT</code> env var (verified against the official Copilot CLI plugin reference + hooks reference docs 2026-05-29). The plugin install lives at <code>~/.copilot/installed-plugins/ai-developer-toolkit/&lt;plugin&gt;/</code> (single live copy, no versioned subdir) but consumers have no built-in env var pointing there. Any retirement option must keep <code>pnpm sync-ralph-state</code> + <code>pnpm overview</code> working under BOTH engines without per-developer shell-profile setup. Recommend option (c) — push upstream — as the architecturally cleanest answer; option (b) is the fallback if upstream is too slow."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Retire codexu's gitignored bin/ralph-overview.mjs resolver wrapper, keep cross-engine support (Claude Code + Copilot CLI) for `pnpm sync-ralph-state` / `pnpm overview`.\n\n## Background\n\nCurrent state on codexu (2026-05-29):\n- `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` is a Node resolver wrapper that locates the ralph-overview plugin install dir, then dispatches to the plugin's own `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` via spawn.\n- It is **gitignored** (`.gitignore: /bin/` covers it). Earlier this session I added Copilot install-path support (`~/.copilot/installed-plugins/ai-developer-toolkit/ralph-overview/`) to the wrapper's cascade — that local-machine patch works for THIS machine but doesn't propagate via git.\n- The ralph-overview plugin's own `scripts/init-consumer.mjs` writes consumer `package.json` scripts that point directly at `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/ralph-overview/bin/ralph-overview.mjs` — this only works in Claude Code sessions because `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` is auto-injected by Claude. Copilot CLI does NOT export an equivalent var (verified against https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-cli-reference/cli-plugin-reference and https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/hooks-reference 2026-05-29 — Copilot only exports `COPILOT_AGENT_SESSION_ID`, `COPILOT_CLI`, `COPILOT_CLI_BINARY_VERSION`, `COPILOT_LOADER_PID`, `COPILOT_RUN_APP`, plus `COPILOT_HOME` for user-config override).\n\n## Goal\n\n`pnpm sync-ralph-state` and `pnpm overview` must work on BOTH engines (Claude Code + Copilot CLI) without:\n- Per-developer shell-profile setup (`$RALPH_OVERVIEW_PLUGIN_ROOT` exported by every contributor's shell rc — operator burden too high)\n- A per-consumer gitignored wrapper that drifts out of sync across machines\n- Breaking existing Claude Code workflows that already use the direct `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` path\n\n## Three candidate paths (plan must pick + justify)\n\n### Option A — re-run `overview-init` to regenerate `package.json` scripts in the plugin's modern direct-path form\n\nWrites `\"sync-ralph-state\": \"node $CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/ralph-overview/bin/ralph-overview.mjs sync --repo .\"`. Cleanest from the plugin's POV. **Blocker:** breaks under Copilot CLI (no `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT`). Would need a parallel Copilot-friendly variant (perhaps a `npm run` cross-shell prelude). Not viable as-is.\n\n### Option B — un-ignore `/bin/` and commit the wrapper with cross-engine support\n\nLocal-to-codexu, ships today. Removes the gitignore entry on `/bin/`, commits the existing wrapper with its current cross-engine cascade (Claude install paths + the new Copilot install path I added in-session). Every machine clones a working wrapper. **Downside:** doesn't help OTHER consumers of the ralph-overview plugin who may hit the same Copilot-path gap. Pragmatic short-term fix; doesn't address the root cause.\n\n### Option C — push cross-engine awareness UPSTREAM into `scripts/init-consumer.mjs`\n\nUpdate the plugin's init flow to emit `package.json` scripts that work on BOTH engines. Three sub-strategies:\n\n   C1. Emit a TINY consumer-side wrapper at install time (essentially what codexu has, but generated + tracked by the plugin's init). Every consumer that runs `overview-init` gets a cross-engine wrapper checked in.\n   C2. Emit `package.json` scripts using a cross-shell helper (e.g. `node -e 'import(...).then(...)'` that resolves the plugin path at runtime). Ugly but no wrapper file needed.\n   C3. Emit `package.json` scripts that branch on env: `\"sync-ralph-state\": \"node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-${COPILOT_HOME:-$HOME/.copilot}/installed-plugins/ai-developer-toolkit}/ralph-overview/bin/ralph-overview.mjs sync --repo .\"`. Bash expansion works on Unix; PowerShell-only Windows users break. Need cross-shell.\n\nOption C is the right architectural answer (helps every consumer) but takes longer (plugin v-bump + adoption cycle).\n\n## Plan must specify\n\n1. **Pick A/B/C** (or hybrid) with clear rationale.\n2. **If C:** which sub-strategy (C1/C2/C3) and why; what plugin tests need adding.\n3. **Migration story:** if changing the wrapper's location or `package.json` script form, how do existing dev clones absorb the change cleanly (delete old wrapper, regenerate, etc.)?\n4. **Cross-engine smoke test:** how to verify `pnpm sync-ralph-state` works under both Claude Code and Copilot CLI before merging. Manual test plan acceptable.\n5. **Upstream-feature-request follow-up (Option D, parallel track):** draft a feature request to the GitHub Copilot CLI team proposing a `COPILOT_PLUGIN_ROOT_<KEBAB-PLUGIN-NAME>` env var or `copilot plugin path <name>` subcommand for parity with Claude Code. Not blocking this task, but a long-term cleaner solution if upstream lands it. The plan should include the draft text.\n\n## Reference\n\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/bin/ralph-overview.mjs` — current wrapper (gitignored). Includes the in-session 2026-05-29 Copilot-path addition at lines 18, 67-77, 104.\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/.gitignore` — `/bin/` line that needs to come out if Option B.\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/package.json` — current `sync-ralph-state` script calls `node bin/ralph-overview.mjs sync` (the local wrapper). Cross-reference with `overview`, `overview:dev`, `overview:build`, `sync-ralph-state:watch`.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/init-consumer.mjs` — where the plugin's modern `package.json` scripts are emitted.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/README.md` and `docs/installation.md` — how the plugin currently documents consumer setup.\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/AGENTS.md` 'Copilot migration milestone' block, follow-up (d) — describes this exact problem.\n\n## Phase discipline + reporting\n\nThis is the PLAN phase. Produce ONLY a plan deliverable (e.g. `.ralph/jobs/codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement/plan.md` and any stories the plan-with-ralph skill generates). Do NOT chain into impl.\n\nCOMMIT TARGET: lead is on `main`. Commit your plan on a fresh topic branch off main (`ralph/plan-codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement`), push to `origin`, and report commit SHA + branch in your kind=done body.\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.8.x body-canonical hard-block): final turn ends with `<|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>`. Substantive content (commit SHA, branch, recommended option + rationale, open questions, anomalies) in PROSE BODY.\""}}},{"id":"crews-stop-member-hard-terminate-no-envelope","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T22:30:00Z","mergeCommit":"f39d609a,92e4dcd6,f8cdcc90","initialStage":"planning","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-stop-member-hard-terminate-no-envelope","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Plan-first.</strong> Today's <code>stop-member</code> is a SOFT authorization: it sets <code>shutdownRequested=true</code> + appends a <code>stop-request</code> envelope to the member's mailbox, then waits for the member's next turn to read the request and emit a kind=done ack. This costs ONE extra model invocation per member shutdown (the ack turn does no useful work — just says 'ok, dying') and generates a spurious second kind=done envelope that the lead must review. For the common case — bookkeeper wants a member gone because its task is complete — this round-trip is pure overhead. The operator's intent (2026-05-29): when a member is no longer needed, the lead should HARD-TERMINATE the launcher process + wt.exe tab, NOT send a polite stop-request envelope. Plan must design: (1) hard-terminate mechanism (Stop-Process on launcher PID + identify-and-kill the wt.exe tab); (2) API shape (new <code>kill-member</code> subcommand vs <code>--hard</code> flag on existing <code>stop-member</code> vs flipping the default and adding <code>--soft</code> for the ack-flow); (3) manifest state changes (<code>listenerState</code>, <code>actorState</code>, audit-log entry); (4) cross-platform behavior (Windows wt.exe vs Mac/Linux terminal-emulator equivalents); (5) edge cases (launcher PID already dead, wt.exe tab already closed, child Copilot CLI session not under launcher's process tree).","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>CRITICAL: do NOT kill the wt.exe server process.</strong> The wt.exe server hosts ALL tabs in a Windows Terminal window (and possibly across windows) — killing it would close every tab including unrelated crews members, the bookkeeper-lead's own session, and any dev shells the operator has open. The CORRECT mechanism is to kill ONLY the per-member child process inside the spawned tab AND explicitly close the tab. <strong>Empirical 2026-05-29:</strong> killing just the child process (Copilot CLI / PowerShell host) leaves the tab open as a 'process exited' placeholder — that's UNACCEPTABLE. The plan MUST combine TWO steps: (1) kill the per-tab child process tree (Copilot CLI + parent PowerShell, walking DOWN from Copilot CLI's PID; STOP at the boundary going UP — never include wt.exe server in the kill set); (2) explicitly close the tab via <code>wt.exe --window &lt;wid&gt; close-tab --tabid &lt;tid&gt;</code> or equivalent CLI subcommand (verify against Windows Terminal docs). Requires capturing the wt window ID + tab ID at spawn time (currently NOT in the manifest — needs to be added). DO NOT rely on the user's <code>closeOnExit</code> profile setting being <code>graceful</code> — empirical evidence shows tabs persist as placeholders, suggesting the user's setting may be <code>automatic</code> or <code>never</code>. <strong>Process-tree topology:</strong> the crews launcher is a PowerShell script (<code>launcherPid</code> in the manifest). It spawns <code>wt.exe new-tab</code>, which delegates to the long-running wt.exe server — the new tab is NOT a child of the launcher script. Touches: <code>hooks/actors.js::spawnMember</code> (capture per-member child PIDs + wt window/tab ID at spawn), <code>hooks/commands/stop-member.js</code> (new hard-terminate path that kills the child tree AND closes the tab, NEVER targeting the wt.exe server), <code>tools/stop-member.js</code> (CLI mirror), tests with explicit assertions on (a) wt.exe server PID survives, AND (b) the spawned tab is no longer present in <code>wt -w &lt;wid&gt; list-tabs</code> output."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add hard-terminate capability to crews stop-member so the lead can kill a no-longer-needed member's per-tab child process WITHOUT killing the wt.exe server (which would close all sibling tabs) and WITHOUT the soft ack-and-shutdown round-trip via stop-request envelope.\n\n## Why\n\nThe current `stop-member` (in `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/commands/stop-member.js` and CLI mirror `tools/stop-member.js`) is a soft authorization:\n\n1. Sets `manifest.shutdownRequested = true` and `manifest.listenerState = exited`\n2. Appends a `stop-request` envelope to the member's mailbox\n3. The member's next turn reads the stop-request, emits a `kind=done` ack turn\n4. Member's Stop hook permits the turn to end; Copilot/Claude session may then exit\n\nObserved cost (2026-05-29 smoke-test, validated): for every clean stop, the bookkeeper-lead receives TWO `kind=done` envelopes from the same member — first the task-completion done, then the stop-acknowledgement done. The second one is pure overhead: no useful work, just 'ok, dying.' The lead must review it (mid-turn nag fires, listener re-arms, etc.) — burning lead-side model invocations too. For an autonomous bookkeeper running dozens of members per day, this is a real tax.\n\nOperator intent: when a member is no longer needed (the common case after a kind=done task report), HARD-TERMINATE the per-member child process tree AND force the tab to visually close. No envelope, no ack round-trip, no second model invocation, no orphaned 'process exited' tab placeholder. **Empirical 2026-05-29: operator observed that in prior smoke-test runs, the tab persisted open with no PowerShell inside it after termination — an empty placeholder.** That's unacceptable; the plan's solution MUST make the tab actually disappear, independent of the user's `closeOnExit` profile setting. The wt.exe server (which hosts ALL tabs in the window) MUST survive untouched.\n\n## Tab-closure acceptance criterion\n\n**The wt tab must visually disappear after hard-terminate, not persist as a 'process exited' placeholder.** Killing the child process alone is not sufficient — empirical evidence shows tabs linger. The plan MUST design a deterministic close-the-tab mechanism. Candidate strategies (plan picks one + justifies):\n\n   T1. **Explicit `wt.exe close-tab` call after kill.** `wt --window <wid> close-tab --tabid <tid>` (verify exact subcommand against Windows Terminal CLI docs). Requires capturing the wt window ID + tab ID at spawn time. Currently NOT in the manifest — needs to be added.\n   T2. **Spawn into a dedicated 'crews-member' profile with `closeOnExit: graceful` baked in.** Modify user's wt settings.json at install time. Intrusive but works for all exits. \n   T3. **UI automation (Ctrl+Shift+W keystroke to the focused tab).** Hacky, fragile, last resort.\n   T4. **Hybrid T1 + T2.** Most robust, most code.\n\n   Operator leaning: **T1** if wt CLI supports tab-id-targeted close in the user's wt version; fall back to T4 if not. Plan must verify wt CLI capabilities empirically.\n\n## CRITICAL CONSTRAINT — do NOT kill the wt.exe server\n\nThe wt.exe server is a long-running process that hosts ALL tabs in a Windows Terminal window. The bookkeeper-lead's own tab, any other crews members' tabs, the operator's dev shells — all share the same wt.exe server. **Killing wt.exe = closing every tab = catastrophic.**\n\nThe correct target is the per-tab child process tree:\n- The launcher PowerShell script (PID in `manifest.launcherPid`) — may already be exited by the time we want to kill, since its job is to invoke `wt.exe new-tab '<command>'` and exit.\n- The Copilot CLI process inside the spawned tab (NOT currently tracked in the manifest — would need to capture at spawn time, possibly via a post-spawn `Get-WmiObject Win32_Process | where CommandLine -match \"--name <member-name>\"` lookup since Copilot CLI is launched as a grand-child of the wt.exe server, not of our launcher).\n- The PowerShell that hosts the Copilot CLI process inside the tab (Copilot CLI runs inside `pwsh` or `powershell` by default in a wt tab).\n\nThe plan must:\n1. Capture per-member child process PIDs at spawn time (or shortly after) so we know what to kill later.\n2. Define a kill strategy that targets ONLY those PIDs — never the wt.exe server PID.\n3. Verify the wt.exe `closeOnExit` profile setting is `graceful` (the default) so the tab auto-closes when its child exits.\n4. Add a regression test that asserts the wt.exe server PID SURVIVES a hard-terminate (spawn member, capture wt.exe server PID via parent-of-tab-process lookup, hard-terminate the member, assert wt.exe server PID is still alive).\n\n## Design questions for the plan\n\n### Q1. API shape — three reasonable options:\n\n   A. **New `kill-member` subcommand.** Keeps `stop-member` semantics intact for back-compat. `kill-member <name> --crew <crew> [--reason <text>]` does the hard-terminate. Pro: clear semantic separation. Con: two commands for very similar intent.\n\n   B. **`--hard` flag on existing `stop-member`.** Default = soft (current behavior). `stop-member <name> --crew <crew> --hard [--reason <text>]` does hard-terminate. Pro: discoverable. Con: easy to forget the flag and pay the round-trip cost; current bookkeeper habit is to default-call stop-member.\n\n   C. **Flip the default + add `--soft` opt-in.** Default = hard-terminate. `stop-member <name> --crew <crew> [--soft] [--reason <text>]`. Pro: matches operator intent (hard is the common case). Con: breaking change for any consumer that depends on the ack-flow (does anyone?). Plan must investigate.\n\nPlan should pick one and justify. Operator leaning: **C** (flip the default), with a brief deprecation window if there are real consumers of the soft flow.\n\n### Q2. Hard-terminate mechanism — Windows specifics (tab-only, NOT wt.exe server)\n\nInvestigate (verify via experimentation):\n\n   - Does `Stop-Process -Id <launcherPid>` even matter? Probably not — by the time hard-terminate runs, the launcher PowerShell has long since exited (its job was to invoke `wt.exe new-tab '...'` and return).\n   - Does the manifest currently capture the Copilot CLI process PID inside the tab? Look at `hooks/actors.js::spawnMember` for any post-spawn PID capture. If NOT, plan must add capture — strategies:\n      - Post-spawn `Get-WmiObject Win32_Process | where CommandLine -match \"--name <safeName>\"` lookup, retried with exponential backoff until Copilot CLI process appears (typically 5-15s after spawn).\n      - Stamp a unique env var (e.g. `CREWS_MEMBER_PID_MARKER`) via the launcher's `wt.exe new-tab` command and have the spawned shell capture its own PID into the manifest on first turn.\n      - Use `COPILOT_LOADER_PID` env var (verified present in this session) — but that's set INSIDE the Copilot CLI process, not accessible from outside.\n   - Walk the process-tree parent chain: from the Copilot CLI PID, find its parent (PowerShell inside the tab). Kill PARENT to take down both. STOP walking before reaching the wt.exe server.\n   - Use `Stop-Process -Id <pid>` (graceful) first, then `-Force` if process still alive after a timeout.\n   - Verify the wt.exe `closeOnExit` setting: read `$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Packages\\Microsoft.WindowsTerminal_*\\LocalState\\settings.json` for the default profile's `closeOnExit` value. If user has set `never`, the tab will linger empty after the child exits; that's a UX nit, not a correctness issue.\n\n   Suggest an exploratory script (deliverable as part of P1 in the plan): spawn a test member with CREWS_NO_LAUNCH=0 (real wt.exe spawn), capture wt.exe server PID + member's launcher PID + Copilot CLI PID via WMI lookup, then try each kill strategy in isolation, observe what survives. The script's output becomes the empirical basis for the kill-strategy decision.\n\n### Q3. Mac / Linux behavior\n\nThe current `spawnMember` is Windows-only (errors out on non-Windows with 'spawn-member currently requires Windows'). So Q2's wt.exe-specific design is fine for v1. But the plan should sketch what the equivalent would look like on Mac (Terminal.app via osascript) and Linux (gnome-terminal / xterm via DBus or terminal-emulator CLI) so the API shape doesn't paint us into a corner.\n\n### Q4. Manifest state + audit log\n\nWhat does the manifest look like after a hard-terminate?\n\n   - `listenerState`: 'exited' (same as soft stop)\n   - `actorState`: 'cleared'? Or keep 'active' until a separate `clear-member` runs? Operator's intent — hard-terminate means 'this member is GONE' — so 'cleared' makes sense.\n   - `shutdownRequested`: true (signals 'lead authorized this')\n   - New `terminatedAt` + `terminationKind: 'hard'` + `terminationReason: <reason>` fields for audit\n   - New `terminatedPids: { launcher, copilotCli, hostShell }` for forensic record of what was killed and what survived (esp. wt.exe server PID)\n   - Log entry in the manifest's audit history (if such exists)\n\nIf actorState flips to 'cleared' automatically on hard-terminate, then `clear-member` becomes redundant for hard-terminated members — the cleanup happens in one shot. The plan should consider whether to merge `kill-member` and `clear-member` into one command, or keep them separate (clear-member also archives mailbox-history; that's a different operation).\n\n### Q5. Edge cases\n\n   - Launcher PID already dead (expected — launcher exits after invoking wt.exe new-tab): no-op for the kill; still target the Copilot CLI child + update manifest state.\n   - wt.exe tab already closed by operator (Ctrl+W or X): the Copilot CLI child is already gone; lookup returns no PID; treat as no-op + update manifest.\n   - Copilot CLI process not yet discoverable (just-spawned member): retry the WMI lookup with backoff up to N seconds; if still not found, log + soft-fail (proceed with manifest update without actually killing — the process will exit on its own eventually).\n   - Multiple PIDs under the same launcher (Copilot CLI may have forked its own children — e.g., Node language servers, child processes for shell tools): walk the process tree DOWN from Copilot CLI's PID and kill all descendants, but STOP at the boundary going UP (never include wt.exe server in the kill set).\n   - Member has unreviewed outgoing mail (kind=done not yet consumed by lead): kill anyway. The lead can still read the outbox after the process is gone (outbox is on disk).\n   - Member is currently mid-turn (active model invocation): kill anyway. Risk: leaves the model's response truncated, possibly mid-tool-call. Probably acceptable but plan should note the risk.\n   - Multiple wt.exe SERVERS running (rare — happens if user has Windows Terminal Stable + Preview both installed). Plan: find the server that owns OUR tab by walking up from Copilot CLI PID; never target any wt.exe server even if multiple are present.\n\n### Q6. AGENTS.md operating-manual update\n\nThe codexu AGENTS.md workflow diagram says:\n\n```\nlead: /crews:stop-member <name> (do NOT chain into the next phase)\n```\n\nIf Q1 picks option C (flip default), this stays correct but its semantics change — the bookkeeper should know the new default is hard. If Q1 picks A or B, update the diagram to use the new command/flag. Either way, AGENTS.md needs a line clarifying 'stop-member is now hard by default' or pointing to the new command, AND a safety note: 'hard-terminate kills only the per-tab child process tree; the wt.exe server (and all other tabs) survive untouched.'\n\n## Plan-level guidance\n\n- This is a small-surface change but with non-trivial Windows-process-tree investigation. Allocate a story for the investigation/experimentation specifically.\n- Tests: process-kill tests are hard to write portably. Suggest a `tests/stop-member-hard-terminate.test.js` that spawns a no-launch member (CREWS_NO_LAUNCH=1 skips wt.exe), simulates a child process via a sleep, kills it via the new code path, asserts manifest state + process death. Cross-platform skip if not Windows. ALSO a `tests/stop-member-hard-terminate-wt-server-survives.test.js` (Windows-only, optional in CI) that does a REAL wt.exe spawn, captures the wt.exe server PID, hard-terminates the member, and asserts the wt.exe server PID is still alive after the kill.\n- Version bump: minor (v1.9.0) if API change; patch if pure flag addition.\n- CHANGELOG: document the round-trip-cost rationale, the soft-stop deprecation timeline (if applicable), and the EXPLICIT safety guarantee that hard-terminate never touches the wt.exe server.\n\n## Reference\n\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/commands/stop-member.js` — current soft-stop implementation.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tools/stop-member.js` — CLI mirror.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/actors.js` — `spawnMember` (captures launcherPid; will need to also capture per-tab child PIDs for hard-terminate).\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/AGENTS.md` workflow diagram + duty table — references `/crews:stop-member`.\n- 2026-05-29 smoke-test seq-3 and seq-4 envelopes from `plan-ralph-overview-data-relocation-and-json-migration` — empirical evidence of the round-trip cost (two kind=done envelopes per task, second one is pure overhead).\n- Windows Terminal settings: `$env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Packages\\Microsoft.WindowsTerminal_*\\LocalState\\settings.json` — confirm default profile `closeOnExit: graceful` (or document the dependency on this setting in the CHANGELOG).\n\n## Phase discipline + reporting\n\nThis is the PLAN phase. Produce ONLY a plan deliverable (e.g. `.ralph/jobs/crews-stop-member-hard-terminate-no-envelope/plan.md` and any stories the plan-with-ralph skill generates). Do NOT chain into impl.\n\nCOMMIT TARGET: lead is on `main`. Commit your plan on a fresh topic branch off main (`ralph/plan-crews-stop-member-hard-terminate-no-envelope`), push to `origin`, and report commit SHA + branch in your kind=done body.\n\nIMPORTANT: per AGENTS.md, the lead's primary working dir stays on `main`. If `plan-with-ralph` defaults to checking out its topic branch in the primary dir (observed 2026-05-29 with `plan-ralph-overview-data-relocation-and-json-migration` — the member switched my checkout out from under me), use a worktree instead: `git worktree add ../codexu-plan-crews-hard-terminate ralph/plan-crews-stop-member-hard-terminate-no-envelope` and do your branching + committing inside that worktree. Report worktree path in your kind=done body so the lead can clean it up after FF.\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.8.x body-canonical hard-block): final turn ends with `<|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>`. Substantive content (commit SHA, branch, picked API option + rationale, process-tree investigation findings, EXPLICIT confirmation that the kill strategy targets only the per-tab child tree (never the wt.exe server), open questions for impl, anomalies) in PROSE BODY.\""}}},{"id":"crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T12:05:00Z","initialStage":"planning","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Plan-first (design locked).</strong> Move the crews outbox protocol from one-envelope-per-turn to one-row-per-report JSONL, with terminal-gated batch delivery to the lead. Surfaced 2026-05-30 by a Copilot member's multi-tag turn (3 progress emits + 1 final done) where <code>parseTurnTags</code>'s first-tag-wins logic silently dropped the terminal <code>done</code> tag, leaving the lead seeing only the first progress summary from ~18min earlier. Operator considered last-wins / terminal-preference / severity-ordered merge variants but picked the more invasive per-row-rows shape: each <code>&lt;|report ...|&gt;</code> tag writes its own outbox row; the lead's listener fires only when ≥1 terminal kind is present in the unread tail and delivers the whole batch in one shot. Strict mode for progress-only members (never deliver until terminal). Per-row <code>replyTo</code>/<code>acks</code>/<code>decisions</code>. Per-tag body slicing. Body-canonical per-row. v2.0.0 protocol bump.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>v2.0.0 protocol bump.</strong> Changes the on-disk outbox shape (envelope wrapper retired in favor of per-row JSONL rows); changes listener semantics (terminal-gated batch fire instead of any-new-row fire); touches Stop hook, listener loop, review-mail / read-member / peek-mailbox commands + CLI mirrors, body-canonical detection, force-response gates, mail-resolution credit, format-progress-tail (retired), and ~30+ test files that assume one-envelope-per-turn. Consumer-side surface: bookkeeper-lead AGENTS.md, codexu crews-plugin invariants section, any external Claude Code / Copilot CLI integrations reading the outbox directly. Plan must explicitly enumerate the breaking-change list for the CHANGELOG."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Move the crews outbox protocol from one-envelope-per-turn to one-row-per-report JSONL, with terminal-gated batch delivery to the lead.\n\n## Why\n\nEmpirically observed 2026-05-29 during smoke-test of impl-plan-with-ralph-auto-worktree: a Copilot member emitted multiple <|report kind=\\\"...\\\"|> tags in a single Stop-hook turn (3 progress emits followed by a final done emit). The current parseTurnTags (D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/mailbox.js:761) uses first-tag-wins semantics — it locks kind/summary/replyTo to the FIRST tag seen and ignores all subsequent tags. Result: the envelope was written with kind=\\\"progress\\\" and the FIRST progress summary, silently discarding the terminal kind=\\\"done\\\" with the actual ship report (commit SHAs, validation results, recommendations).\n\nSeveral attribute-merging fixes were considered (last-wins, terminal-preference, severity-ordered, body-merge variants) but all introduce different edge cases. Operator picked a more invasive but cleaner shape: every <|report|> tag becomes its own outbox row, and consumption batches the trail when at least one terminal row is present.\n\n## Locked design (do NOT re-litigate — plan around these)\n\n### D1. Outbox format\n\nEach <|report ...|> tag in a turn writes ONE row to the member's outbox JSONL. One turn → N rows. Each row carries its own seq, id, writtenAt, kind, summary, body, replyTo, decisions, acks. The legacy 'one envelope per turn' wrapper is retired; consumers operate on rows directly.\n\n### D2. Body partitioning\n\nEach row's body = the prose IMMEDIATELY ABOVE that tag — between this tag and the previous tag in the same turn (or turn start if first). The full-turn message is reconstructable by concatenation but not stored as a separate field. Each row reads as a discrete checkpoint.\n\n### D3. Delivery rule\n\nLead's listener watches member outboxes. Fires only when the unread tail contains ≥1 terminal-kind row (done/question/blocked). When it fires, delivers ALL unread rows up through the latest as one /crews:review-mail batch. If unread contains only progress rows, listener does NOT fire — keeps waiting.\n\n### D4. Progress-only members\n\nSTRICT mode: a member that only ever emits kind=progress and never reaches a terminal never triggers delivery. The lead's mailbox stays empty for that member indefinitely. Operator-acceptable; the manifest's lastKind/lastSummary still update so /list-members shows current activity. If the lead needs to peek at a progress-only trail on demand, they can run /crews:read-member <name> --all.\n\n### D5. Mail-resolution attributes (replyTo / acks / decisions)\n\nPer-row. Each row carries its own replyTo/acks/decisions. The lead's reviewed-cursor advances across the whole delivered batch and credits any row in the batch that resolved an incoming message (matches today's findCurrentDecision semantics, just iterated over multiple rows).\n\n### D6. Body-canonical hard-block\n\nApplied per terminal row (unchanged semantics, just multi-row). If a terminal row has summary > limit AND body < floor, that row is rejected at Stop-hook time. Progress rows exempt as today. A turn with multiple terminal rows where ANY one fails body-canonical → the turn fails; member must re-emit.\n\n## Surface inventory (rough — plan should refine)\n\n- hooks/mailbox.js — rewrite parseTurnTags to extract per-tag blocks with their owning body slice; new outbox-row writer that appends N rows instead of one envelope\n- hooks/stop.js — emit per-tag rows; per-row body-canonical detection on terminal rows\n- hooks/listener-loop.js + lib/listener-loop.js — terminal-gated batch fire (today fires on any new row)\n- hooks/commands/review-mail.js — return multi-row batches in entries[]; cursor semantics across multi-row delivery\n- hooks/commands/read-member.js — multi-row return; back-compat with --peek / --since / --all\n- hooks/commands/peek-mailbox.js — preview semantics (earliest unread row? earliest unread terminal-batch? plan picks)\n- hooks/protocol/report-tags.js — schema for per-row reports; body field becomes required per-row\n- hooks/protocol/envelope.js — schema for outbox-row vs the legacy envelope wrapper (retire wrapper)\n- hooks/format-progress-tail.js — likely retire; the batch IS the progress tail + the terminal\n- hooks/protocol/review-gate.js, force-response logic — verify all mail-resolution checks operate over per-row batches\n- tools/*.js — CLI mirrors for review-mail, read-member, peek-mailbox\n\n## Stories suggestion (plan-with-ralph will refine)\n\n- US-001: parseTurnTags extracts per-tag blocks (parser-only change; no on-disk format change yet so the rest of the system is untouched)\n- US-002: Stop-hook writes per-tag rows to outbox; outbox-row schema; manifest lastSeq stays roll-forward\n- US-003: Listener-loop terminal-gated batch fire\n- US-004: review-mail / read-member / peek-mailbox multi-row delivery semantics + cursor advancement\n- US-005: Body-canonical per-row; mail-resolution per-row\n- US-006: format-progress-tail retirement; progressTail field removal from envelope shape\n- US-007: Test sweep — every existing test that asserts one-envelope-per-turn behavior is updated; ~30+ test files reference single-envelope assumptions\n- US-008: AGENTS.md (codexu crews-plugin invariants section), README, docs/protocol.md, CHANGELOG (v2.0.0)\n\nSerial within each story; US-001..US-003 must land in order (each builds on the previous). US-004..US-006 can land in parallel after US-003. US-007 + US-008 wrap.\n\n## Tests that MUST exist (current test suite has zero multi-tag coverage — that is why this bug survived)\n\nRegression: the exact failure case observed 2026-05-29.\n- Multi-tag turn (progress, progress, done) → outbox has 3 rows. Row 3 is kind=done with the done tag's summary. Row 2 is kind=progress with the second progress tag's summary. Row 1 is kind=progress with the first progress tag's summary. All three rows have distinct body slices (per-tag prose).\n\nPlus:\n- Single-tag turn (existing happy path): one row written.\n- Terminal-gated delivery: outbox with only progress rows → listener does NOT fire. Append a terminal row → listener fires once, delivers all rows as a batch.\n- Multiple terminal rows in one turn (rare but legal): listener delivers the batch through the LATEST terminal; cursor advances past all of them.\n- Per-row replyTo: row 1 replies to msg-A, row 2 (terminal) replies to msg-B. Both credited; both incoming messages marked resolved.\n- Body-canonical per-row: terminal row with overfull summary + underfull body → that row rejected; progress rows in same turn exempt.\n- /crews:read-member <name> --all returns all rows including unconsumed progress (the on-demand peek for progress-only members per D4).\n\n## Phase + reporting\n\nThis is the PLAN phase. Produce only a plan deliverable. Do NOT chain into impl.\n\nWORKTREE: per AGENTS.md worktree-placement convention, use a subdir of the toolkit repo:\n  git -C D:/ai-developer-toolkit worktree add D:/ai-developer-toolkit/.worktrees/crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox -b ralph/plan-crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox main\nPlan deliverables under .ralph/jobs/crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox/ inside the toolkit worktree (since this is a plugin-touching task, not a codexu task). Push the branch to origin. Report worktree path + branch + commit SHA in kind=done body.\n\nVERSION BUMP: this is a protocol-shape change — propose v2.0.0 (major). The plan must document the consumer-side breaking changes (envelope.kind/summary semantics change; envelope.progressTail removed; review-mail entries[] cardinality changes).\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.9.x body-canonical hard-block enforced): final turn ends with <|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>. Substantive content (commit SHA, branch, worktree, plan summary, story decomposition, version-bump rationale, open questions, anomalies including any Copilot engine quirks since this is also a smoke-test data point) in PROSE BODY. Summary > 200 chars + body < 50 chars triggers reject. Do NOT use reply-to=\\\"spawn\\\".\n\n## Reference\n\n- Parser bug source: D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/mailbox.js:761-767 (the === null first-wins guards)\n- Existing test: tests/protocol-report-tags.test.js — single-tag only; needs sibling tests/protocol-multi-tag-report.test.js\n- Operator conversation 2026-05-29 23:55Z – 2026-05-30 01:10Z where this design was locked\n- Today's outbox example: any member outbox JSONL under the crew-state dir (see hooks/paths.js for resolution)\""}},"mergeCommit":"af07987d,1be31195"},{"id":"crews-inner-pid-capture-not-firing","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T13:23:00Z","initialStage":"brainstorming","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-inner-pid-capture-not-firing","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Brainstorm-first.</strong> The v1.9.2 Node-side WMI inner-PID capture (commit f8cdcc90, designed to write the inner Copilot/Claude CLI PID to disk asynchronously so that <code>stop-member</code> can hard-terminate cleanly and let wt close the tab) is empirically NOT firing for Copilot-engine members on this dev box. Observed 2026-05-30 across 3 stop-member calls (G impl, plan-protocol-v2, others): every call returned <code>refusedReason: \"inner-pid-missing\"</code>, fell back to <code>force-killed-fallback</code> on the launcher pwsh, and likely left wt tabs as <code>[process exited]</code> placeholders. The v1.9.2 ship was operator-empirically-validated when it shipped, but those validations may have used a Claude-engine member; the regression is Copilot-specific. Brainstorm should pressure-test where the capture is failing (capture child never spawns? WMI lookup doesn't find Copilot CLI process? backoff times out? wrong CommandLine-marker pattern matched against Copilot's actual command line shape?), and propose a diagnostic + fix path.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Regression in v1.9.2 hard-terminate path for Copilot-engine members.</strong> The v1.9.2 inner-PID capture is foundational to the clean-tab-close UX — without it, hard-terminate silently regresses to v1.9.1's force-kill-fallback behavior. Brainstorm should reproduce on a dedicated test spawn (capture diagnostic output: was <code>spawnInnerPidCapture</code> called? did it find a child PID? what was the WMI Query that ran? what was Copilot CLI's actual CommandLine?), then propose either a marker-injection fix (stamp a unique env-var into the wt.exe new-tab command so WMI lookup can find the process by a guaranteed-unique substring) or an alternative mechanism (e.g., have the spawned shell write its own PID to disk on first turn via a SessionStart hook)."}],"prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"Why is the v1.9.2 Node-side inner-PID capture not firing for Copilot-engine members on this dev box?\n\n## Empirical evidence (operator-observed 2026-05-30)\n\nThree stop-member calls today, all targeting Copilot-engine members in crew ralph-pipeline:\n\n1. impl-ralph-overview-data-relocation-job2 (sessionId e88aa330) → terminationKind:'hard', innerCli:null, descendants:[7 PIDs], launcherPwsh:{pid:95612, fate:'force-killed-fallback'}, wtServerPid:32460, refusedReason:'inner-pid-missing'\n2. plan-crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox (sessionId 50060731) → terminationKind:'hard', innerCli:null, descendants:[7 PIDs], launcherPwsh:{pid:79620, fate:'force-killed-fallback'}, wtServerPid:32460, refusedReason:'inner-pid-missing'\n3. Earlier in-session smoke tests (sessions in conversation logs) had the same pattern.\n\nIn every case the WMI capture child wrote no inner.pid; stop-member fell back to force-killing the launcher pwsh; tabs likely persisted as '[process exited]' placeholders rather than closing cleanly.\n\nv1.9.2 (commit f8cdcc90) was supposed to fix this exact regression for v1.9.1. The fix uses a Node-side background PowerShell capture child that does parent-of process scanning to find Copilot CLI's PID asynchronously after spawnMember returns. The fact that it's failing for ALL Copilot-engine members today suggests the regression is Copilot-engine-specific (the v1.9.2 ship was validated, but possibly only against Claude-engine members).\n\n## Design axes\n\n### Axis 1 — Where is it failing?\n\nA. Capture child never spawned (spawnInnerPidCapture not invoked, or invoked but the spawn failed silently)\nB. Capture child spawned but the WMI query (Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | where ...) never matched any process\nC. Match logic looked for a Claude-CLI-specific CommandLine pattern that doesn't match Copilot CLI\nD. Backoff retried N times and timed out before Copilot CLI appeared in the process table\nE. The capture wrote inner.pid to the wrong path (race condition with manifest dir creation, wrong cwd, etc.) so stop-member's lookup didn't find it\n\nBrainstorm should propose a diagnostic plan that enumerates which of these is happening on this box.\n\n### Axis 2 — Diagnostic mechanism\n\nA. Add verbose logging to spawnInnerPidCapture + the WMI query and re-run a test spawn\nB. Capture the actual Copilot CLI CommandLine on this box (Get-WmiObject Win32_Process | Where {$_.Name -eq 'node.exe'} | Select-Object ProcessId, CommandLine | ConvertTo-Json) and compare against the v1.9.2 marker pattern\nC. Add an integration test that exercises the full v1.9.2 capture flow against a Copilot-engine spawn\nD. All of the above\n\n### Axis 3 — Fix strategy (depends on Axis 1 diagnosis)\n\nIf Axis 1 is C (wrong CommandLine pattern): update the WMI query to use a guaranteed-unique marker stamped into the wt.exe new-tab command (e.g., --title 'CREWS_CAPTURE_<uuid>') and pattern-match on title. Cross-engine, no engine-specific path.\nIf Axis 1 is D (timing/backoff): tune backoff, or have the inner CLI write its own PID via a SessionStart hook (more reliable than external WMI lookup).\nIf Axis 1 is A or B: investigate why the capture child isn't being invoked or its query isn't matching anything.\nIf Axis 1 is E: fix the path/race.\n\n### Axis 4 — UX impact mitigation in the meantime\n\nUntil this fix lands, every hard-terminate likely leaves a '[process exited]' tab placeholder. Should we surface this in the stop-member output so the operator knows to manually close the tab? E.g., add a 'tab may persist; close manually with Ctrl+W' note when refusedReason='inner-pid-missing'.\n\n### Axis 5 — Out of scope\n\n- Replacing wt.exe with a different terminal emulator (out of scope; tracked elsewhere).\n- Cross-platform support (Mac/Linux); this is Windows-only because wt is Windows-only.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nAt end of brainstorm Phase 5, produce .ralph/brainstorms/crews-inner-pid-capture-not-firing/selected-direction.md with:\n- Recommended diagnostic plan (which axis to investigate first, what data to collect)\n- Recommended fix strategy (depends on diagnosis, but pre-commit to a likely path so the planner has a starting point)\n- Recommended interim UX (Axis 4 — yes/no)\n- Open questions for the planner\n- Plugin version bump target (likely v1.9.3 if a small fix, v1.10.0 if it requires a structural change like SessionStart-hook PID-write)\n\nDo NOT chain into /plan-with-ralph. The bookkeeper spawns a separate plan member from selected-direction.md.\n\n## Reference\n\n- v1.9.2 ship: D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/CHANGELOG.md top entry; commit f8cdcc90\n- Capture implementation: D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/commands/spawn-member.js (spawnInnerPidCapture) and the script it spawns (likely under scripts/ or inline)\n- stop-member consumer: D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/commands/stop-member.js (applyHardStopMember)\n- The v1.9.2 research brief: .ralph/jobs/crews-v1.9.2-inner-pid-capture/research-brief.md (toolkit repo)\n\n## Phase + reporting\n\nThis is BRAINSTORM phase. Produce only selected-direction.md.\n\nWORKTREE: per AGENTS.md, plugin-only task → toolkit worktree at D:/ai-developer-toolkit/.worktrees/crews-inner-pid-capture-not-firing on ralph/brainstorm-crews-inner-pid-capture-not-firing. Push to origin.\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.9.x): final turn ends with <|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>. Body has commit SHA, branch, worktree, recommended direction, diagnostic plan, open questions, anomalies. Do NOT add reply-to=\\\"spawn\\\".\""}},"mergeCommit":"c6d08aba"},{"id":"crews-parser-multi-tag-stopgap","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T12:05:00Z","initialStage":"planning","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-parser-multi-tag-stopgap","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Plan-first stopgap.</strong> Short-term patch for the parseTurnTags first-tag-wins bug (D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/mailbox.js:761-767) that silently drops terminal kind tags when a Copilot member emits multiple <code>&lt;|report ...|&gt;</code> tags in one Stop-hook turn. The long-term fix is the v2.0.0 protocol bump (<code>crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox</code>) but that has 8 stories and will take days. This stopgap ships as v1.9.3 (or v1.9.x patch) and uses a minimally-invasive <strong>terminal-preference</strong> rule: pick the LAST terminal-kind (done/question/blocked) tag in the buffer; if none, pick the LAST progress; each tag's attributes (kind/summary/replyTo) stay atomic (no cross-tag attribute borrowing). This is option B from the operator brainstorm 2026-05-30 — explicitly NOT last-tag-wins (which has its own edge cases) and NOT severity-ordered. Lifetime: ships immediately, deprecated when v2.0.0 lands.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Coordinate with crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox.</strong> This stopgap modifies <code>parseTurnTags</code> in <code>hooks/mailbox.js</code> — the same function the v2 impl will rewrite end-to-end. Plan must explicitly handle the merge order: stopgap MUST land first; v2 impl rebases on stopgap and replaces the patched logic with the per-row implementation. Don't let the v2 impl skip past on the assumption stopgap hasn't landed yet — verify the v2 PRD references this stopgap's terminal-preference semantics as the baseline behavior its rewrite must preserve (in the multi-tag-turn migration test cases). Earlier-summary content is DISCARDED in this stopgap (atomic per-tag attributes); v2 will preserve them as per-row outbox rows."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Apply a stopgap patch to crews parseTurnTags (hooks/mailbox.js:761-767) that uses terminal-preference + atomic per-tag attributes to fix the silent-drop-of-terminal-kind bug observed 2026-05-30 with multi-tag Copilot turns.\n\n## Background\n\nObserved 2026-05-30 with impl-plan-with-ralph-auto-worktree: a Copilot member emitted 3 progress tags followed by 1 done tag in a single Stop-hook turn. Current parseTurnTags uses === null guards on kind/summary/replyTo (mailbox.js:761-767), so it locked onto the FIRST progress tag and silently discarded the done tag. The lead never saw the kind=done envelope; the member's commit SHAs + validation results were buried in seq 2's body marked kind=progress.\n\nLong-term fix is crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox (8-story v2.0.0 ship). This task is a stopgap to remove the silent-drop bug in production while v2 lands.\n\n## Locked design (operator picked 2026-05-30)\n\n### D1. Terminal-preference, last-wins-among-terminals\n\nScan all <|report ...|> tags in the buffer. Pick the canonical tag by:\n  1. Scan RIGHT-TO-LEFT, pick the FIRST tag whose kind ∈ {done, question, blocked}\n  2. If no terminal tags exist, scan right-to-left for the FIRST tag whose kind === 'progress'\n  3. If still no valid kind tag, leave kind=null (existing 'missing kind tag' Stop-hook block fires unchanged)\n\n### D2. Atomic per-tag attributes\n\nThe chosen tag's kind, summary, replyTo are taken ATOMICALLY from THAT tag's block — no cross-tag attribute borrowing. A done tag with no summary stays summary=null even if an earlier progress had summary=\\\"...\\\".\n\nThis is explicitly NOT last-tag-wins (which would pick a stray trailing progress over an earlier terminal). NOT severity-ordered (which complicates the picking rule). NOT body-merging from earlier tags (that comes with v2.0.0 per-row rows).\n\n### D3. ack / decision accumulation\n\nack/decision attributes continue to accumulate across ALL tags in the buffer (unchanged semantics — acks are about resolving incoming mail, not stating turn intent). Today's lines 768-778 in mailbox.js already iterate. Keep as-is.\n\n### D4. Earlier-tag content is DISCARDED\n\nEarlier progress tags' summaries do not appear in the envelope (no concatenation, no summaryHistory[]). The operator explicitly acknowledged this is acceptable for a stopgap; v2.0.0 preserves them as per-row outbox rows. Document this in the CHANGELOG.\n\n### D5. Version bump\n\nv1.9.3 (patch) — pure bug fix, no API change, no consumer-visible shape change (envelope still has scalar kind/summary/replyTo; just sourced from a different tag now).\n\n## Surface\n\n- hooks/mailbox.js — patch parseTurnTags (lines 761-767): replace === null guards with a two-pass scan (collect all tag blocks; pick winner per D1; extract winner's attributes per D2)\n- hooks/protocol/report-tags.js — no change\n- hooks/stop.js — no change (still consumes parsed kind/summary/replyTo as today)\n- tests/protocol-report-tags.test.js — add multi-tag test cases (the table from the operator brainstorm 2026-05-30):\n   - progress + progress → kind=progress, summary from SECOND progress\n   - progress + done → kind=done, summary from done\n   - done + progress → kind=done, summary from done (terminal wins over trailing progress)\n   - question + done → kind=done, summary from done (last terminal)\n   - done + question → kind=question, summary from question (last terminal)\n   - done (no summary) + progress (with summary) → kind=done, summary=null (no cross-borrow)\n   - <invalid-kind-tag> + done → kind=done, summary from done\n   - no tags → kind=null (existing behavior)\n- CHANGELOG.md — document the bug, the fix, the rationale for terminal-preference, the deprecation timeline (v2.0.0 will rewrite this whole path)\n- AGENTS.md (toolkit) — no change (briefing examples still show single-tag pattern; that remains valid)\n- codexu AGENTS.md crews-plugin-invariants section — note the v1.9.3 stopgap behavior alongside the v2.0.0 in-flight note\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- parseTurnTags test suite passes with the new multi-tag cases\n- All existing single-tag tests still pass unchanged\n- Manual test: spawn a Copilot member with a contrived multi-tag turn; verify envelope.kind === 'done' and envelope.summary is the done tag's summary\n- CHANGELOG v1.9.3 entry exists\n\n## Phase + reporting\n\nThis is PLAN phase. Produce only a plan deliverable. Do NOT chain into impl.\n\nWORKTREE: toolkit-only task → D:/ai-developer-toolkit/.worktrees/crews-parser-multi-tag-stopgap on ralph/plan-crews-parser-multi-tag-stopgap. Push to origin.\n\nVERSION BUMP: v1.9.3 (patch).\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.9.x body-canonical hard-block enforced): final turn ends with <|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>. Substantive content in PROSE BODY: commit SHA, branch, worktree, plan summary, story decomposition (likely just 2-3 small stories: code patch + tests + CHANGELOG), version-bump rationale, open questions, anomalies. Do NOT add reply-to=\\\"spawn\\\".\n\n## Coordination note\n\nThis stopgap MUST land BEFORE the impl phase of crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox (the v2 impl rebases on it and replaces the patched logic with the per-row implementation). The plan should explicitly call this out as a sequencing constraint for the bookkeeper.\n\n## Reference\n\n- Bug source: D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/mailbox.js:761-767 (the === null first-wins guards)\n- Operator conversation 2026-05-29 23:55Z – 2026-05-30 01:10Z where the design was locked (search for 'terminal-preference')\n- Long-term replacement: crews-protocol-v2-per-report-outbox (plan shipped bd9b0e8f / 6c53ae76)\""}},"mergeCommit":"superseded-by-v2.0.0"},{"id":"crews-orchestrator-tab-rename","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T14:55:30Z","initialStage":"planning","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-orchestrator-tab-rename","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Plan-first.</strong> When a crew lead role is assigned to an existing terminal session, rename the host wt.exe tab to <code>♔ &lt;name&gt; (&lt;crew&gt;)</code> — mirroring the existing member-side <code>♙ &lt;name&gt; (&lt;crew&gt;)</code> convention (set at spawn time via wt.exe <code>--title</code> + copilot <code>--name</code> + <code>COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE=1</code>). Creates a visually-coherent crew lineup (king + pawns) and makes the orchestrating tab immediately identifiable in a multi-tab Windows Terminal window. Plan must enumerate the OSC-vs-wt-rename-vs-both mechanism trade-offs (Copilot per-intent OSC writes can clobber OSC-only renames; wt.exe rename-tab persists at the OS level).","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Mechanism choice matters for Copilot sessions.</strong> Members get a sticky title because v1.8.12 stamps <code>COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE=1</code> on the launcher script BEFORE copilot starts. For an already-running lead session, that env-var can't be set retroactively — so OSC-emit alone may flicker as Copilot writes per-intent titles. The planner should either (A) accept the flicker, (B) use <code>wt.exe --window 0 rename-tab \"...\"</code> which takes precedence over inner-pane OSC writes, or (C) both. Empirical test on this dev box's Copilot setup is required, not a paper decision."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"When a crew lead is assigned to an existing terminal session (via /crews-assign-role lead --crew <X> --name <Y>, or on first /crews-status if the role is auto-detected), rename the host wt.exe tab to:\n\n    ♔ <name> (<crew>)\n\nMirroring the existing member-side convention (members get ♙ <name> (<crew>) stamped at wt.exe spawn time via --title, plus copilot --name + COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE=1 to keep it from being clobbered).\n\n## Background / why\n\nToday members are immediately identifiable by their tab title (♙ <name> (<crew>)) — operator can glance at the wt.exe window and see every crew member at a glance. The lead/orchestrator tab is whatever the operator launched (often just 'PowerShell' or the engine's per-intent OSC titles), making it hard to spot the orchestrating tab in a multi-tab Windows Terminal window. Naming the lead ♔ <name> (<crew>) closes that gap and creates a visually-coherent crew lineup (king + pawns).\n\n## Glyph choice\n\n♔ (white king, U+2654) for lead. Pairs thematically with ♙ (white pawn, U+2659) used for members today. Both are widely-rendered in Windows Terminal default fonts.\n\nFuture open question (NOT for this plan): if/when we add other crew roles (advisor, observer, etc.), what chess-piece glyphs map to them? Out of scope for v1.\n\n## Existing member-side machinery (reference)\n\n- D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/actors.js:1781 — sessionDisplayName template for new tabs (♙ <name> (<crew>))\n- actors.js:1950 — tabTitle stamped via wt.exe --title at spawn\n- actors.js:1822-1826 — comment chain on COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE=1 keeping the title stable for the tab lifetime\n- tests/engine-field.test.js, copilot-review-fixes.test.js — existing test coverage\n\n## Surface for THIS task (where lead-side rename happens)\n\n- D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/commands/assign-role.js — likely entry point; emit rename when role transitions to 'lead'\n- (Possibly) hooks/session-start.js or equivalent — emit rename on session start if role is already 'lead' from prior manifest\n\n## Mechanism options (planner should pick one with rationale)\n\n### Option A: OSC escape sequence at assign-time\n\nWhen assign-role transitions to lead, print to stdout:\n\n    \\x1b]0;♔ <name> (<crew>)\\x07\n\n(or the equivalent \\x1b]2;...\\x1b\\\\ ST-terminated form). Lightweight, no shelling out, works on any wt-compatible terminal.\n\nRisk: Copilot's per-intent OSC title updates (controlled by COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE) may clobber the title we set, the same way they did for members until v1.8.12 added the env-var workaround. But we can't stamp COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE on an already-running copilot — that's a startup env-var. So Option A alone may flicker.\n\n### Option B: wt.exe rename-tab subcommand\n\nShell out to:\n\n    wt.exe --window <current> rename-tab \"♔ <name> (<crew>)\"\n\nThis persists better because wt.exe owns the tab title at the OS level (overrides per-process OSC writes from inside the pane). Downside: requires identifying the current wt window-id (use --window 0 to target the active one), and the tab-rename API may not be available on older wt.exe versions.\n\n### Option C: Both A and B\n\nEmit OSC first (immediate update), then call wt-rename as a follow-up to lock it in. Belt-and-suspenders; small overhead.\n\n### Option D: Just document the limitation; don't auto-rename\n\nPunt. Operator manually renames their tab. (Rejected by operator-requested task scope, but plan should note this as the no-fix baseline.)\n\nRecommended: planner picks A, B, or C with empirical testing on this dev box's Copilot setup. C is likely most-robust.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- After /crews-assign-role lead --crew <X> --name <Y> the wt.exe tab title visibly changes to '♔ <name> (<crew>)' within ~1s\n- Title persists through subsequent Copilot tool calls (does not flicker back to per-intent titles)\n- /crews-assign-role none (clearing the role) restores the original title (best-effort; OK if it just leaves the last set title rather than the original)\n- Existing member-side tab renaming (♙ prefix) is untouched\n- One unit test confirms the rename emit-path is invoked when role transitions to lead\n- Manual smoke test recorded in CHANGELOG: spawn a fresh Copilot session, run /crews-assign-role lead, screenshot or transcript of the renamed tab\n\n## Edge cases for plan to address\n\n- Operator launches Copilot in a non-wt.exe terminal (PowerShell ISE, ConEmu, raw conhost). OSC may still work; wt-rename will silently fail. Plan should not crash in those cases.\n- Lead session is started via /crews-resume-crew or auto-detected from prior manifest on SessionStart — title should still be set then, not only on the assign-role command.\n- Operator renames the tab manually after assign — do we re-clobber on next heartbeat, or respect operator intent? Plan should pick a policy (likely: only emit on role transitions, not on every heartbeat).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Cross-platform (mac/Linux iTerm/gnome-terminal title sequences). Windows-only as members are.\n- New crew roles beyond lead/member. King-and-pawn is the v1 set.\n- Customizable glyph (operator override). Hard-coded ♔ for now.\n\n## Phase + reporting\n\nThis is PLAN phase. Produce only a plan deliverable. Do NOT chain into impl.\n\nWORKTREE: the /plan-with-ralph skill manages its own worktree at .ralph/jobs/crews-orchestrator-tab-rename/worktree/plan/ on branch ralph/plan-crews-orchestrator-tab-rename. Do NOT pre-create a worktree manually.\n\nVERSION BUMP: likely v1.10.0 (minor — new feature surfaces a new behavior in a stable command) OR v1.9.x patch if mechanism turns out to be a tiny OSC-emit. Let the plan justify.\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.9.x body-canonical hard-block enforced): final turn ends with <|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>. Substantive content in PROSE BODY: commit SHA, branch, worktree, plan summary, story decomposition, version-bump rationale, mechanism choice + rationale, open questions, anomalies. Do NOT add reply-to=\\\"spawn\\\".\n\n## Reference\n\n- Member-side equivalent: D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/actors.js:1781, :1950, :1822-1826\n- v1.8.12 COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE story: search CHANGELOG for 'COPILOT_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE'\n- Operator request: codexu session 37331928 conversation 2026-05-30T04:47Z ('when creating a crew I want us to rename the session of the orchestrator(lead) to: ♔ orchestrator (<team name>)')\""}},"mergeCommit":"b825e82c"},{"id":"ralph-orchestration-plan-with-ralph-auto-worktree","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T08:40:00Z","initialStage":"planning","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-orchestration-plan-with-ralph-auto-worktree","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Plan-first.</strong> Teach the <code>/plan-with-ralph</code> skill to auto-create a worktree at <code>.ralph/jobs/&lt;task-id&gt;/worktree/plan/</code> at the start of its flow, matching what <code>/implement-with-ralph</code> already does at <code>.ralph/jobs/&lt;task-id&gt;/worktree/</code>. Observed 2026-05-29 across three plan-spawn cycles that the skill does NOT manage its own worktree — each plan member had to be told via the bookkeeper-lead's spawn-prompt to create a worktree manually. That's bookkeeper toil per-spawn and a source of bugs (one cycle ended up with a leftover worktree at <code>D:/codexu-plan-data-relocation</code> — wrong path, outside the codexu repo, manually cleaned up later). Codifying skill-managed worktree auto-creation removes the toil and matches the existing codexu AGENTS.md worktree-placement convention (table in 'Branch + worktree discipline' section).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add auto-worktree management to the /plan-with-ralph skill.\n\n## Background\n\nObserved 2026-05-29 across three plan-spawn cycles (codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement, crews-stop-member-hard-terminate-no-envelope x2, ralph-overview-data-relocation-and-json-migration) that /plan-with-ralph does NOT manage its own worktree. Each plan member had to be instructed via the bookkeeper-lead's spawn-prompt to create a worktree manually, e.g.:\n\n```\nWORKTREE MANDATE: use `git worktree add ../codexu-plan-<slug> -b ralph/plan-<task-id> main` and do ALL work inside that worktree.\n```\n\nThat's bookkeeper-side toil per spawn AND a source of bugs:\n- Three sibling-of-repo worktrees were created in error (`../codexu-plan-wrapper-retirement`, `../codexu-plan-hard-terminate`, `../codexu-plan-hard-terminate-v2`) because the lead's spawn-prompt followed an outdated AGENTS.md pattern.\n- One leftover worktree ended up at `D:/codexu-plan-data-relocation` (root of D: drive) — definitely not where any documented convention says.\n\nMeanwhile /implement-with-ralph DOES auto-manage a worktree at `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree/` (verified from existing worktrees on codexu). The asymmetry is the bug.\n\n## Goal\n\nWhen /plan-with-ralph is invoked, the skill itself should:\n1. Determine task-id from the invocation (already in scope via the spawn prompt or --from-brainstorm path).\n2. Create a worktree at `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree/plan/` on a fresh topic branch `ralph/plan-<task-id>` (or `-v2`, `-v3` suffix if siblings already exist on origin — investigate edge case).\n3. Do all plan work INSIDE that worktree (commit deliverables there, push the topic branch, etc.).\n4. Report the worktree path + topic branch in its final kind=done message so the lead knows where to FF from and what to clean up.\n5. NOT touch the lead's primary working dir (which stays on main per AGENTS.md).\n\n## Design questions for the plan\n\n### Q1. Worktree path: subdir or no?\n\n   A. `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree/` (matches /implement-with-ralph's existing layout exactly)\n   B. `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree/plan/` (with a `plan/` subdir, so if both plan + impl are open at the same time they don't conflict)\n   C. `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree-plan/` (sibling-of-worktree, different basename)\n\n   Lean toward **B**: subdir lets the plan worktree coexist with a future impl worktree at `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree/impl/`. Plan should verify whether /implement-with-ralph would conflict with a same-task worktree dir already existing.\n\n### Q2. Topic branch naming and v2/v3 collision\n\nIf the lead re-spawns a plan member after the first one shipped (e.g., the v2 case observed 2026-05-29 where the plan was corrected based on operator feedback), the topic branch `ralph/plan-<task-id>` already exists on origin. Strategies:\n\n   A. Skill detects the collision and auto-suffixes `-v2`, `-v3`, etc. (what member B did manually for the hard-terminate task)\n   B. Skill fails fast and requires the lead to delete the old branch first\n   C. Skill always uses a UUID/timestamp suffix\n\n   Lean toward **A** (auto-suffix). Matches what already happened organically in the smoke-test session.\n\n### Q3. Cleanup story\n\nWho cleans up the plan worktree + topic branch after the lead FF's?\n\n   A. Lead does it manually (current state, post-FF cleanup is bookkeeper toil)\n   B. Skill provides a 'plan-complete' subcommand the lead can run after FF that nukes both\n   C. Skill auto-detects post-FF state on next invocation (e.g., the worktree's branch HEAD is reachable from origin/main → safe to remove)\n\n   Lean toward **B** (explicit subcommand) — cleanest API. The lead's post-FF flow becomes: `git merge --ff-only <sha> && git push origin main && /plan-with-ralph cleanup <task-id>`.\n\n### Q4. Sequencing with the existing /implement-with-ralph skill\n\n/implement-with-ralph uses `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree/`. If Q1 picks A (same path for plan and impl), there's a collision if both phases are active. If B (with `plan/` subdir), need to also rename impl's worktree to `impl/` subdir for symmetry — that's a breaking change for /implement-with-ralph. Plan should think about whether to:\n\n   - Migrate both skills together (synchronized rename)\n   - Keep /implement-with-ralph at `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree/` and put /plan-with-ralph at `.ralph/jobs/<task-id>/worktree-plan/` to avoid the symmetry burden\n   - Some other naming scheme\n\n## Reference\n\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/skills/plan-with-ralph/SKILL.md` (or wherever the plan-with-ralph skill body lives) — current behavior, no worktree auto-management.\n- `D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/skills/implement-with-ralph/SKILL.md` — reference for how the impl skill manages its worktree (the design to mirror).\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/AGENTS.md` 'Branch + worktree discipline' section — codified worktree-placement convention (2026-05-29 update); this task's deliverable should match.\n- Smoke-test session 2026-05-29: three plan-member spawns that each needed manual worktree mandates in the spawn prompt; one cleanup miss at `D:/codexu-plan-data-relocation`.\n\n## Phase discipline + reporting\n\nThis is the PLAN phase. Produce ONLY a plan deliverable. Do NOT chain into impl.\n\nCOMMIT TARGET: lead is on `main`. Create a worktree at `.ralph/jobs/ralph-orchestration-plan-with-ralph-auto-worktree/worktree/plan/` (eating our own dog food — use the convention this task is establishing). Commit on `ralph/plan-ralph-orchestration-plan-with-ralph-auto-worktree`, push to `origin`, report commit SHA + branch + worktree path in kind=done body.\n\nREPORTING (crews v1.8.x body-canonical hard-block): final turn ends with `<|report kind=\\\"done\\\" summary=\\\"<short>\\\"|>`. Substantive content (commit SHA, branch, worktree path, picked options Q1-Q4, open questions, anomalies) in PROSE BODY. DO NOT add `reply-to=\\\"spawn\\\"`.\""}},"mergeCommit":"6c53ae76,257cec66"},{"id":"ralph-overview-init-consumer-cross-engine-wrapper","scope":"ralph-overview","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","initialStage":"brainstorming","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-overview-init-consumer-cross-engine-wrapper","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Brainstorm-first.</strong> Push cross-engine plugin-path awareness UPSTREAM into the <code>ralph-overview</code> plugin's <code>scripts/init-consumer.mjs</code> so every consumer (codexu and future consumers alike) gets a working <code>pnpm sync-ralph-state</code> / <code>pnpm overview</code> under BOTH Claude Code and Copilot CLI without each consumer needing to hand-roll a per-repo resolver wrapper. Companion to the codexu-side fix <code>codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement</code> (plan @ commit <code>e527f83e</code>, impl shipped 2026-05-29) which un-ignored + committed codexu's existing wrapper as a SHORT-TERM measure — this task is the long-term ecosystem fix. Option C1 from the plan: <code>overview-init</code> emits a tiny consumer-side wrapper file at install time AND <code>package.json</code> scripts that point at <code>node bin/ralph-overview.mjs &lt;sub&gt;</code> instead of the current <code>$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT</code>-only form. The wrapper is plugin-versioned (consumers run <code>overview-init --force</code> after a plugin upgrade to refresh it) and the plugin can ship unit-test coverage of every install-layout (Claude versioned cache, Copilot single-copy, env-var override, local-dev fallback) — something codexu's hand-rolled wrapper currently lacks.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Cross-engine surface (Claude Code AND Copilot CLI install layouts). Brainstorm should pressure-test how consumers absorb the wrapper-bump on plugin upgrade — is <code>overview-init --force</code> idempotent? Does it preserve consumer-side customizations? What's the migration story for codexu (which today has a hand-committed wrapper that will conflict with the plugin-emitted one)? Target plugin version bump: v2.5.0+ minor. Coordinate with any in-flight ralph-overview impl work."}],"prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"Push cross-engine plugin-path resolution UPSTREAM into the ralph-overview plugin's overview-init skill so every consumer gets a working pnpm sync-ralph-state + pnpm overview under BOTH Claude Code AND Copilot CLI, without each consumer hand-rolling a per-repo resolver wrapper.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nThe ralph-overview plugin's current `scripts/init-consumer.mjs` (./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/init-consumer.mjs lines 10-15) emits PACKAGE_SCRIPTS of the form `node $CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/ralph-overview/bin/ralph-overview.mjs <sub> --repo .`. Those scripts break on Copilot CLI because Copilot exports NO equivalent env var (verified 2026-05-29 against https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-cli-reference/cli-plugin-reference — Copilot exposes only COPILOT_AGENT_SESSION_ID, COPILOT_CLI, COPILOT_CLI_BINARY_VERSION, COPILOT_LOADER_PID, COPILOT_RUN_APP, COPILOT_HOME).\n\nCodexu worked around this with a hand-rolled `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` wrapper that probes both engine install paths. That wrapper has now been committed (task `codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement`, plan @ commit `e527f83e`, impl shipped 2026-05-29) as a SHORT-TERM fix — but every NEW consumer of the plugin would have to author the same wrapper themselves. The long-term fix is to push the resolution UPSTREAM into the plugin.\n\nReference: codexu's `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` (now tracked in the codexu repo) is the working reference implementation of the cascade — it's ~130 LoC, self-contained, and has been battle-tested 2026-05-29 against Copilot CLI install + env-override + local-dev fallback (see `.ralph/jobs/codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement/smoke-test.md`). The plugin-side version would be ~identical but unit-tested.\n\n## Design axes to pressure-test\n\n### Axis 1 — Where does the wrapper live in the consumer repo?\n\nA. **`bin/ralph-overview.mjs` in the consumer repo** (matches codexu's current pattern). Pro: simple, discoverable, matches what package.json scripts already say. Con: consumer repo now ships a generated file that's tracked in git; regenerating on plugin upgrade requires explicit `overview-init --force` step.\nB. **`scripts/ralph-overview.mjs`** instead of `bin/`. Same trade-offs but avoids any `/bin/` gitignore-by-convention concerns (codexu had to un-ignore `/bin/` to make this work; other consumers may have similar global gitignore rules).\nC. **Inline in `package.json` scripts** via `node -e \"...resolver code...\" <sub>`. Pro: zero tracked files. Con: cross-shell quoting hell on Windows + cmd + PowerShell + bash; long inline strings are unreadable and hard to debug.\nD. **Install into `node_modules/.bin/ralph-overview`** as a postinstall step. Pro: matches npm/pnpm convention. Con: requires the plugin to ship as an npm package or have a postinstall hook that pnpm runs reliably across both engine installs.\n\nLean toward **A** (matches codexu's existing layout, minimal surprise). But brainstorm should weigh B vs A for new consumers without legacy.\n\n### Axis 2 — Cross-engine install-path cascade\n\nThe wrapper must probe (at least) these install layouts:\n1. `$RALPH_OVERVIEW_PLUGIN_ROOT` env override (escape hatch for plugin-dev)\n2. `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/<plugin>/` (Claude Code harness-set)\n3. `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT/cache/<marketplace>/<plugin>/<latest>/` (Claude Code cache)\n4. `~/.claude/plugins/cache/<marketplace>/<plugin>/<latest>/` (Claude Code user install)\n5. `~/.copilot/installed-plugins/<marketplace>/<plugin>/` (Copilot CLI user install, no per-version subdir)\n6. Local-dev fallback? (codexu has one at `./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/` — should the plugin-emitted wrapper include any local-dev escape, or rely on RALPH_OVERVIEW_PLUGIN_ROOT for plugin-author iteration?)\n\nBrainstorm should decide on the canonical cascade order + whether to expose any plugin-side configuration of additional probe paths.\n\n### Axis 3 — `overview-init` API for the new emit\n\nCurrent `initConsumer({addPackageJsonScripts: true})` writes scripts only. New shape options:\n\nA. **Additive flag:** `addPackageJsonScripts: true, addConsumerWrapper: true` (default both true).\nB. **Single flag rename:** rename to `addPackageJsonScriptsAndWrapper: true` to signal the new bundled emission.\nC. **Subcommand split:** `overview-init` is now always-bundled; add `overview-init --wrapper-only` and `overview-init --scripts-only` for advanced cases.\n\nLean toward **A** (additive flag, both default true). Existing consumers calling `addPackageJsonScripts: true` should opt in to `addConsumerWrapper: true` explicitly the first time, to avoid surprise file creation.\n\n### Axis 4 — Migration story for codexu (the current consumer with a hand-committed wrapper)\n\nCodexu's `bin/ralph-overview.mjs` is now tracked. When this plugin task ships v2.5.0+ with the auto-emit feature, codexu can:\n\nA. Re-run `overview-init --force` to replace its hand-committed wrapper with the plugin-emitted one (and stay in sync with future plugin upgrades automatically).\nB. Keep its hand-committed wrapper indefinitely; never opt into the auto-emit feature.\nC. Run `overview-init --diff` first to see what would change, then decide.\n\nPlan should document the recommended migration path for codexu + any other consumers that may exist by then.\n\n### Axis 5 — Test coverage of every install layout\n\nThe plugin-side wrapper SHOULD ship with unit tests that simulate each install layout. The codexu hand-rolled wrapper has zero tests today — this is one of the long-term wins of pushing it upstream. Test cases at minimum:\n\n- `RALPH_OVERVIEW_PLUGIN_ROOT` set to a valid path → resolves to it\n- `RALPH_OVERVIEW_PLUGIN_ROOT` set to an invalid path → warns + falls through\n- `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` set with direct subdir → resolves to it\n- `CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` set with cache versioned subdirs → picks latest by lexical sort\n- `~/.claude/plugins/cache/...` versioned subdirs → picks latest\n- `~/.copilot/installed-plugins/...` single dir → resolves to it\n- All cascade misses → exits 2 with the friendly install-instruction message\n- Found install missing the dispatcher file → exits 3\n- `--repo` arg present → preserved; absent → defaulted to `gitRepoRoot()`\n\n### Axis 6 — Out of scope for this brainstorm (separate concerns)\n\n- The Copilot CLI plugin-install dependency-installation gap (the 2026-05-29 smoke test found `~/.copilot/installed-plugins/.../ralph-overview/` lacks `node_modules/`). That's a Copilot CLI / plugin-publish-pipeline issue, NOT something the wrapper can fix — the wrapper successfully dispatches to the install path; it's the install that's incomplete. Surface as a separate plugin-author followup if not already tracked.\n- Option D from the codexu-side plan (Copilot CLI feature request for per-plugin env-var parity with `$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT`). Out of band; pursued via GitHub issue.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nAt end of brainstorm Phase 5, produce `.ralph/brainstorms/ralph-overview-init-consumer-cross-engine-wrapper/selected-direction.md` with:\n\n- Recommended wrapper location (A/B/C/D from Axis 1).\n- Final cascade order + whether to support plugin-side configurable additional probe paths.\n- Recommended `overview-init` API shape (A/B/C from Axis 3).\n- Recommended codexu migration path (Axis 4).\n- Test plan outline (Axis 5).\n- Plugin version bump target (v2.5.0 minor candidate, or other).\n- Open questions for the planner.\n\nDo NOT chain into /plan-with-ralph. The bookkeeper spawns a fresh plan-ralph-overview-init-consumer-cross-engine-wrapper member from the selected-direction.md per phase discipline.\n\n## Reference\n\n- `.ralph/jobs/codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement/plan.md` (commit `e527f83e`) — the parent plan that recommended this task as Option C1.\n- `.ralph/jobs/codexu-bin-ralph-overview-wrapper-retirement/smoke-test.md` — empirical evidence the codexu wrapper works under Copilot CLI.\n- `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/bin/ralph-overview.mjs` (committed during impl of the parent task) — the working reference implementation to port upstream.\n- `./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/init-consumer.mjs` — the plugin file to modify; current PACKAGE_SCRIPTS constant at lines 10-15.\n- `./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/bin/ralph-overview.mjs` — the plugin's own bin dispatcher (self-locating; reference for what env vars the plugin's runtime expects).\""}}},{"id":"crews-stop-tests-jsonparse-empty-2-tests","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T16:45:00Z","mergeCommit":"resolved-by-crews-v2.0.0-stop-hook-rewrite","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-stop-tests-jsonparse-empty-2-tests","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag</code> during Phase 5a verification on ai-developer-toolkit main (<code>2a22b20e</code>): two pre-existing tests fail with <code>JSON.parse('')</code> env-level errors — <code>tests/stop-circuit-breaker.test.js</code> + <code>tests/stop-hook-consumes-when-listener-exited.test.js</code>. Failures reproduce on plain main without any in-flight changes. Sibling task to <code>ralph-rubric-fixture-drift-3-tests</code> — pre-existing test flakiness that pollutes the green-baseline assertion.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Fix the 2 pre-existing crews-plugin test failures that fail with JSON.parse('') env-level errors on ai-developer-toolkit main.\n\n## Problem\n\nimpl-crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag confirmed two test files fail at base commit 2a22b20e without any in-flight changes: plugins/crews/tests/stop-circuit-breaker.test.js + plugins/crews/tests/stop-hook-consumes-when-listener-exited.test.js. Both fail with JSON.parse('') env-level errors. These failures pollute the 'tests remain green' baseline for every future Phase 5a reviewer.\n\n## Investigation\n\nRun each test in isolation, capture the JSON.parse stack trace, identify the empty-string source: missing test fixture, missing env var, or genuine Stop-hook code bug that doesn't gate input parsing.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Both tests pass on a fresh checkout of main.\n- If a code change was needed, regression test added.\n- CHANGELOG note (if code change) cites the failure pattern.\n\nVersion bump: crews v1.8.2 patch (test-only fix if no code change).\""}}},{"id":"crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T03:39:00Z","mergeCommit":"a7ad750a","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag","descriptionHtml":"Stop requiring the <code>&lt;|report kind=... summary=...|&gt;</code> footer tag for LEAD role turns. The tag was designed for member→lead orchestrator parsing; for lead→operator turns it adds visual noise to the operator's terminal without serving a parsing need. Today the Stop hook blocks turn-end on missing kind tag for BOTH roles, and the SessionStart briefing instructs both roles identically. Change: leads emit prose only; members keep the tag (members still report status to leads who DO parse it). Ships as crews v1.7.2 patch.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Need to keep lead-readable status visible to the orchestrator somewhere — but it's likely fine to drop entirely for leads since the lead's prose IS the operator-facing output (there's no further-up parser to satisfy). Verify by grepping crews plugin source for any code that consumes <code>lastKind</code> or <code>lastSummary</code> on lead actors; if any do, replace with prose-derived heuristic or accept null. Don't break the lead's <code>list-members</code> view."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Suppress the <|report kind=... summary=...|> footer-tag requirement for LEAD role turns in the crews plugin.\n\n## Motivation\n\nObserved 2026-05-28: the overview-bookkeeper lead session emits a `<|report kind='progress' summary='...'|>` tag at the end of every operator-facing turn. The operator finds this visually noisy and not useful — there is no orchestrator above the lead parsing the tag, so the kind value is never consumed.\n\nFor members, the tag IS useful: the lead's mailbox parser reads kind to route done/question/blocked envelopes correctly. So this change applies ONLY to leads.\n\n## Today's behavior\n\n- SessionStart hook briefing (claude-code AND copilot mirrors) instructs ALL actors to end every turn with the `<|report kind=...|>` footer.\n- Stop hook (hooks/stop.js) blocks turn-end when the kind tag is missing — for both roles.\n- Lead's manifest gets lastKind / lastSummary populated from the tag.\n\n## Target behavior\n\nFor `role === 'lead'`:\n- SessionStart briefing OMITS the kind-tag requirement (leads emit prose only).\n- Stop hook does NOT block turn-end on missing kind tag.\n- Lead's manifest lastSummary derived from the first line of the prose (Stop hook already has this fallback).\n- Lead's lastKind defaults to 'progress' when not specified (or null — plan decides whether null is OK for downstream consumers).\n\nFor `role === 'member'`: behavior unchanged. Members still emit + Stop still enforces.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/SessionStart.js` (and copilot mirror) — split the briefing text by role; lead briefing omits the kind-tag section.\n2. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js` — gate the kind-tag-missing check on `role === 'member'`.\n3. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/lib/decide-stop.js` (or wherever decideStopBlock lives) — same gate.\n4. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/CLAUDE.md` (plus copilot mirror) — document the role-asymmetric behavior.\n5. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tests/stop-decision.test.js` — add 2 fixtures: lead-no-tag (OK), member-no-tag (BLOCKED).\n6. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tests/listener-protocol.test.js` (if it asserts lead lastKind) — relax.\n7. Audit `tools/crews.js list-members` output — make sure lastKind=null for lead doesn't render as 'lead has no kind, broken'.\n8. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/CHANGELOG.md` + version bump (v1.7.2 patch).\n9. Bump plugin manifest version (5 manifest files per the standard release-stamp pattern).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- AC-1: A lead turn ending with prose-only (no kind tag) passes the Stop hook cleanly.\n- AC-2: A member turn ending without a kind tag still BLOCKS at Stop with the existing error message.\n- AC-3: Lead's manifest.lastSummary gets populated from the prose first-line fallback (existing Stop-hook behavior).\n- AC-4: `list-members` view shows lead's prose-derived summary without confusion (no 'broken' indicator).\n- AC-5: Members from a lead with no kind tag still process mailbox correctly (no downstream consumer requires lead.lastKind).\n- AC-6: Existing tests pass; 2 new fixtures added per US-002.\n- AC-7: Bookkeeper docs (CLAUDE.md) explicitly state the role-asymmetric behavior so future contributors don't 'fix' the lead path back to symmetric.\n\n## Workflow\n\nPlan-direct (single behavior change). Decomposes into 2-3 stories (hook gate + tests + docs).\n\nVersion bump: v1.7.2 patch (defensive UX improvement; no API change). Multi-remote push (Evyatar108 + gim-home). Member-driven direct merge authorized per recent precedent.\n\nAfter ship: bookkeeper-lead session experience improves immediately on next plugin cache refresh.\""}}},{"id":"crews-stop-listener-arm-gate","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-27T17:18:00Z","mergeCommit":"0a3464e4","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-stop-listener-arm-gate","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Stop hook gap.</strong> When a Stop-hook-forced synchronous <code>review-mail</code> satisfies the unreviewed-mail invariant and the next response is text-only, the turn ends cleanly with NO listener armed. The next inbound message goes unwatched until the agent's next tool-call attempt fires the PreToolUse re-arm block. The Stop hook should ALSO gate on listener-state (block turn-end when the listener isn't armed), parallel to the unreviewed-mail / missing-kind-tag invariants already enforced.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>crews plugin fix.</strong> Lives in <code>ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js</code>. Verify the existing Stop-hook invariants enumerate cleanly (missing kind, unreviewed mail, strict-ack unresolved). Add a listener-state check that reads the manifest's <code>listenerState</code> field and blocks turn-end when it's not <code>armed</code>. Provide the same self-healing exit message pattern the PreToolUse hook uses ('Run this Bash tool call: …arm…'). Bundle as crews v1.6.2 (patch) alongside whatever else lands."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add a listener-armed invariant check to the crews Stop hook.\n\n## Problem\n\nObserved 2026-05-27 in the overview-bookkeeper session: when the Stop hook forces a synchronous review-mail (because lastReviewRequiredSeq > lastReviewedSeq), the cursor advances and the unreviewed-mail gate clears. If the agent then ends its turn with a text-only response (no further tool calls), the Stop hook accepts the turn-end and the listener is NOT re-armed. The next inbound message arrives with no watcher attached. The PreToolUse hook only re-arms when the agent attempts a tool call, so the gap persists until the agent acts on its own initiative.\n\nThis is symmetric to the bug the crews-review-mid-turn-v160 task was fixing (mid-turn review-required not enforced) but on the Stop side: 'turn-end without armed listener' should be blocked, just like 'turn-end with unreviewed mail' is blocked today.\n\n## Fix\n\nIn `plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js`:\n\n1. Read the actor's manifest.json `listenerState` field (canonical source — same field the PreToolUse hook reads).\n2. If `listenerState !== 'armed'` (i.e. 'exited', 'unknown', missing, or stale per heartbeat), emit the same exit-code-2 blocking-error pattern used elsewhere:\n   ```\n   ReviewRequiredError: turn-end without armed listener for name \\\"<name>\\\" (crew=\\\"<crew>\\\").\n   \n   Run this Bash tool call now:\n     command: node '<plugin>/tools/crews.js' arm --name <name> --crew <crew> --cwd <cwd> --session-id <session-id>\n     run_in_background: true\n   ```\n   (Use the v1.6.0+ `crews.js arm` form, NOT the deprecated `wait-for-message.js`.)\n3. Honor the existing v1.6.0 progress-envelope exemption — Stop hook should NOT block when the actor has only emitted a `kind=progress` envelope and there's no inbound mail expected (otherwise every progress-only turn would forever require a listener). The check should fire when:\n   - The actor's role is `lead` OR `member` (any active actor).\n   - The kind tag is one of `done`, `question`, `blocked` (terminal-ish kinds expect a sender response).\n   - OR the actor has pending outbound messages awaiting reply (queueDepth > 0 in mailbox audit).\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js` — the fix.\n2. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/CLAUDE.md` (and copilot-plugin/agents-plugin mirrors) — document the new Stop-hook invariant.\n3. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tests/stop-decision.test.js` — extend with a 'unarmed-after-sync-review-mail' fixture asserting the block.\n4. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/.claude-plugin/plugin.json` + version bump (v1.6.2).\n5. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/CHANGELOG.md` — v1.6.2 entry.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- AC-1: After a synchronous review-mail that advances the cursor, if the agent attempts to end the turn WITHOUT an armed listener, the Stop hook emits the canonical block error containing the exact arm command.\n- AC-2: A turn that ends with `kind=progress` AND zero pending outbound replies passes the Stop hook without listener-state check (no false-positive nag for idle progress-only turns).\n- AC-3: A turn that ends with `kind=done` / `question` / `blocked` REQUIRES an armed listener — the actor is expected to await the orchestrator's response.\n- AC-4: stop-decision.test.js adds 3 fixtures: kind=done unarmed (BLOCKED), kind=progress unarmed (OK), kind=done unarmed + queueDepth=0 (BLOCKED).\n- AC-5: Existing Stop-hook tests (missing kind, unreviewed mail, strict-ack) still pass.\n- AC-6: Cross-plugin coexistence still OK (agent-peers + other plugins not affected).\n\n## Workflow\n\nConcrete enough to skip /brainstorm-with-ralph. Run /plan-with-ralph directly. Probably decomposes into 3 stories (hook check + tests + docs+version). Phase 5a/5b internal.\n\nVersion bump: v1.6.2 (patch — defensive invariant addition). Branch + multi-remote push (Evyatar108 + gim-home).\""}}},{"id":"ralph-orchestration-codex-exec-windows-spawn","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-27T14:38:00Z","mergeCommit":"686dd7ee","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-orchestration-codex-exec-windows-spawn","descriptionHtml":"Upstream fix in ai-developer-toolkit's <code>ralph-orchestration</code> plugin: v5.45.0's Node port <code>src/codex-exec.mjs</code> dropped <code>shell: true</code> from <code>spawn(&quot;codex&quot;, …)</code>. On Windows with the CVE-2024-27980 patch, every codex invocation fails with <code>ENOENT</code> in ~800ms. Workaround in place today: the impl-overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema member patched the file in-place + set <code>CODEX_EXEC_SCRIPT</code> env var to force the v5.44 bash wrapper. Auto-memory <code>feedback_codex_exec_v545_windows_spawn</code> records the workaround. This task ships the proper upstream fix as v5.45.1 (or v5.46) so future plugin installs work out-of-the-box on Windows.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Lives in <code>ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/</code>. Sibling repo, same multi-remote convention (push to Evyatar108 + gim-home). Verify the fix on this exact Windows host before claiming green — the bug is host-specific (CVE-2024-27980 patch behavior). Coordinate with <code>impl-overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema</code>'s in-place patch: don't trample the lead's running workaround mid-flight; spawn after that task ships."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Fix the codex-exec.mjs spawn-without-shell bug in ai-developer-toolkit's ralph-orchestration plugin.\n\n## Problem\n\n`ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/src/codex-exec.mjs` at v5.45.0 invokes:\n\n```js\nspawn('codex', args, { stdio: 'inherit', cwd, env })\n```\n\nMissing `shell: true`. On Windows with the Node.js CVE-2024-27980 patch (Node 18.20.5+, 20.18.1+, 22.11.0+), bare-name commands without shell context fail with ENOENT. The result: every codex iteration (and every review-changes Codex reviewer subagent) on Windows dies in ~800ms. v5.44.0 (the prior version) used a bash wrapper `codex-exec.sh` that called codex via Git Bash, so the issue didn't exist.\n\n## Workaround in place\n\nThe impl-overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema member (running 2026-05-27) discovered the bug mid-flight. Workaround applied:\n\n1. **Env var fallback:** Set `CODEX_EXEC_SCRIPT=C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/plugins/cache/ai-developer-toolkit/ralph-orchestration/5.44.0/codex-exec.sh` — the `resolveEngineScript` helper in v5.45 honors this and routes to the v5.44 bash wrapper.\n2. **In-place patch:** Edited the cached v5.45.0 `src/codex-exec.mjs` to add `shell: true` to the spawn call, so review-changes subagents (which spawn their own ralph-orchestration instances) also work.\n\nAuto-memory: `feedback_codex_exec_v545_windows_spawn` documents both.\n\n## Fix\n\nIn `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/src/codex-exec.mjs`:\n\n```diff\n- spawn('codex', args, { stdio: 'inherit', cwd, env })\n+ spawn('codex', args, { stdio: 'inherit', cwd, env, shell: true })\n```\n\nAlso audit `src/copilot-exec.mjs` and any other bare-name `spawn` calls in the Node port for the same bug. Likely applies to `codex-reviewer.mjs`, `copilot-reviewer.mjs`, etc. — anywhere v5.45 ported from a shell script to Node.\n\nSecondary consideration: `shell: true` opens command-injection risk if any argv element is attacker-controlled. Audit how `args` is constructed. For our use case args come from prompt strings written by the orchestrator — operator-trusted — so `shell: true` is acceptable. But document the threat model explicitly in a comment near the spawn call to prevent future regressions from introducing untrusted args.\n\nAlternative implementation: use `execFile` with the absolute path to codex (`spawnSync('where', ['codex']).stdout` resolved once at startup), avoiding shell entirely. More work but safer. Plan should evaluate both and recommend.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/src/codex-exec.mjs` — the fix (shell: true OR execFile-with-resolved-path).\n2. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/src/copilot-exec.mjs` — if same bug pattern, same fix.\n3. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/src/*.mjs` other spawn-bearing files — audit per `grep -rn spawn\\(` and apply fix where applicable.\n4. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/CHANGELOG.md` — entry for v5.45.1 (or v5.46) with the Windows-spawn-fix note.\n5. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/package.json` — version bump.\n6. Add a unit test (if the plugin has a test suite) that mocks spawn + asserts `shell: true` is set on Windows. If no test suite, skip.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Fix applied to all affected spawn calls in ralph-orchestration v5.45.x → v5.45.1 (or v5.46.0 if version bump policy is minor-on-fix).\n- A clean install of the new version on this Windows host successfully spawns codex without the CODEX_EXEC_SCRIPT workaround.\n- After fix lands and is installed, removing the workaround memory `feedback_codex_exec_v545_windows_spawn` is the lead's housekeeping job.\n- Existing cross-platform behavior unchanged (Mac/Linux still work).\n- Pushed to BOTH ai-developer-toolkit remotes (Evyatar108 + gim-home).\n\n## Workflow\n\nConcrete enough to skip /brainstorm-with-ralph. Run /plan-with-ralph directly. Probably decomposes into 2-3 stories (fix + audit + version bump + CHANGELOG). Phase 5a/5b internal to /implement-with-ralph.\n\nVersion bump: v5.45.1 (patch) — Windows-spawn-fix is strictly a bug fix, no API change.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-plan-with-ralph-copilot-cli-read-only-research","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-plan-with-ralph-copilot-cli-read-only-research","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>plan-crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag</code> (2d31eb4a) on 2026-05-28: the Copilot CLI invoked in research-mode by <code>/plan-with-ralph</code>'s Phase 2 (or Phase 4 review) overflowed and made 16 uncommitted edits to <code>D:/ai-developer-toolkit</code> working tree on main, including <code>plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js</code>, <code>plugins/crews/CHANGELOG.md</code>, version stamps, and tests. The Copilot CLI should treat plan-with-ralph research invocations as READ-ONLY — it must not write to source files. The diff happened to match the plan's design which is why it wasn't actively harmful, but on a different invocation could land random partial-implementation noise on a developer's checkout. Bookkeeper stashed the edits via 'git stash push' for recovery; impl member will redo the work cleanly per plan's Q1 default.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Could conflict with operator's other in-flight work</strong> on the affected repo. Fix should: (a) constrain Copilot CLI invocation flags to prevent file edits in research-mode, OR (b) wrap the Copilot research in a temp dir / read-only filesystem layer, OR (c) validate working-tree state pre+post and surface diffs as a kind=question instead of silently leaving them. Touches plugins/ralph/src/copilot-exec.mjs + the research-dispatching code in plan-with-ralph / brainstorm-with-ralph / review-changes."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Constrain Copilot CLI in research-mode invocations to read-only to prevent accidental file edits during plan-with-ralph / brainstorm-with-ralph / review-changes phases.\n\n## Problem\n\nObserved 2026-05-28 during plan-crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag: the Copilot CLI invoked for Phase 2 research (or Phase 4 review) on this Windows host overflowed beyond reading source and ACTUALLY EDITED 16 files in the operator's ./ai-developer-toolkit working tree, including plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js, CHANGELOG, version manifests, and test files. The edits happened to match the design the plan was researching, but on a different invocation could have landed random partial-implementation noise that compromises operator's working tree integrity.\n\n## Root cause hypotheses\n\n- Copilot CLI defaults to interactive file-edit mode unless explicitly constrained.\n- ralph-orchestration's research-invocation flags (in plugins/ralph/src/copilot-exec.mjs + agents/copilot-*-prompt.md) may pass arguments that allow file edits.\n- Copilot CLI doesn't differentiate research vs implementation modes — it always acts.\n\n## Fix options\n\n(a) Pass flags that constrain Copilot to read-only (--no-edit, --read-only, or equivalent — verify against Copilot CLI docs).\n(b) Run Copilot research inside a temp directory / read-only bind-mount so writes are sandboxed.\n(c) Snapshot working-tree state pre+post; if any source file changed, surface as a kind=question to the spawning agent and DISCARD the changes by default.\n\n## Investigation step (before picking option)\n\nRead Copilot CLI 1.0.55+ docs / --help for read-only mode flags. Test the chosen mechanism manually before proposing in plan.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- /plan-with-ralph research subprocess invoked against any plugin source CANNOT modify any file outside its job_dir / staging.\n- New test fixture demonstrates Copilot CLI running in research-mode + asserting zero source file diffs.\n- CHANGELOG entry cites the failure pattern.\n\nVersion bump: ralph v5.46.3 patch. Multi-remote push per operator pattern.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-plan-staging-unique-path-per-member","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"6a9da6a4","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T17:35:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-plan-staging-unique-path-per-member","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>plan-crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag</code> (2d31eb4a) on 2026-05-28: when two plan-phase members run concurrently in the same crew, they share a staging directory at <code>&lt;JOBS_BASE&gt;/.staging/&lt;session_id&gt;/</code> — but the session_id slug uses second-granularity timestamps, so two members spawning within the same second collide and overwrite each other's <code>feature-request.txt</code>. The concurrent member overwrote the staging mid-Phase-2; the affected member recovered by switching to a uniquely-suffixed staging path. Fix: include member-name in the staging-dir slug, OR use a millisecond/PID suffix.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Make ralph plan-with-ralph / brainstorm-with-ralph staging directories unique-per-member to prevent same-second collision when multiple plan members spawn concurrently in the same crew.\n\n## Problem\n\nObserved 2026-05-28 during 3 concurrent plan-member spawns in the overview-bookkeeper crew: plan-crews-envelope-body-canonical-for-all-kinds and plan-crews-lead-suppress-kind-tag both happened to spawn within the same wall-clock second and write to overlapping staging paths. The later member overwrote the earlier one's feature-request.txt mid-Phase-2, causing the earlier's Codex+Copilot research to receive corrupt content. The earlier member recovered by switching to a uniquely-suffixed path, but the recovery wasn't free.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe staging path is computed as <JOBS_BASE>/.staging/<session_id>/ where session_id is a bare timestamp slug at second-granularity. Two members spawning within the same second produce the same slug.\n\n## Fix options\n\n(a) Append member-name (kebab-case) to the slug: <JOBS_BASE>/.staging/<timestamp>-<member-name>/\n(b) Append PID: <JOBS_BASE>/.staging/<timestamp>-<pid>/\n(c) Append nanosecond timestamp: <JOBS_BASE>/.staging/<timestamp-ns>/\n(d) Add random suffix: <JOBS_BASE>/.staging/<timestamp>-<random4>/\n\nRecommended: (a) for human-readability + uniqueness.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Two plan-with-ralph members spawning within the same wall-clock second produce distinct staging paths.\n- Test fixture demonstrates the collision-free guarantee.\n- CHANGELOG cites the failure pattern.\n\nVersion bump: ralph v5.46.3 patch (could bundle with ralph-plan-with-ralph-copilot-cli-read-only-research). Multi-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"crews-review-mail-summary-payload-fallback","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T23:46:00Z","mergeCommit":"05ef339d","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-review-mail-summary-payload-fallback","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>investigate-crews-summary-drop</code> (79752a65). Single-line read-path fix in <code>plugins/crews/hooks/commands/review-mail.js:111</code>: read <code>row.summary ?? row.payload?.summary</code> instead of bare <code>row.summary</code>. The bug silently drops long summary attributes on system-routed envelopes (member-reply + proactive-report) emitted by <code>hooks/stop.js:831-842</code> + <code>:858-882</code>, which nest summary inside <code>payload</code>. Ships as crews v1.7.3 patch.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Fix the crews review-mail summary-attribute drop bug for system-routed envelopes.\n\n## Problem\n\nObserved 2026-05-28 in overview-bookkeeper session: a member's kind=question with a long summary= attribute returned summary: null when the lead called crews.js review-mail. Investigation .ralph/investigations/crews-summary-attribute-drop/INVESTIGATION-RESULT.md (commit 79752a65) confirmed: the kind-tag parser correctly captures the full summary into the outbox envelope's payload.summary; hooks/stop.js routes member-reply and proactive-report envelopes through appendSystemMailbox which writes to inbox-history.jsonl with summary nested in payload only (no top-level summary field); hooks/commands/review-mail.js:111 reads row.summary (top-level) which is undefined for these system-routed rows.\n\n## Fix\n\nOne-line read-path fallback in review-mail.js:111: change `summary: row.summary,` to `summary: row.summary ?? row.payload?.summary,`.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/commands/review-mail.js — the one-line fallback.\n2. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tests/review-mail-command.test.js — fixture seeding an inbox-history row shaped like a member-reply envelope (no top-level summary; populated payload.summary); assert returned entry has summary === payload.summary.\n3. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tests/member-reply-notify.test.js:88 — add an assertion that the surfaced envelope (after formatReviewMailEntry) returns the summary.\n4. CHANGELOG.md v1.7.3 entry citing the failing scenario.\n5. 5 release stamps to v1.7.3 across plugin manifests + marketplace indexes.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- formatReviewMailEntry returns summary === payload.summary for an inbox-history row with only nested summary. Regression test added.\n- member-reply-notify.test.js asserts the formatter-surfaced summary, not just the raw envelope payload.\n- CHANGELOG entry under v1.7.3 cites the failing scenario.\n- npm test green.\n- Manual repro from investigation §1 returns the long summary.\n\nVersion bump: v1.7.3 patch. Multi-remote push (Evyatar108 + gim-home). Local-main FF + push-main pattern per operator preference.\""}}},{"id":"crews-system-envelope-summary-top-level","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T23:46:00Z","mergeCommit":"05ef339d","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-system-envelope-summary-top-level","descriptionHtml":"Defense-in-depth companion to <code>crews-review-mail-summary-payload-fallback</code>. Mirror <code>effectiveSummary</code> to the top-level envelope key at the two <code>appendSystemMailbox</code> call sites in <code>hooks/stop.js</code> (member-reply L831-842; proactive-report L878-882). Keeps <code>payload.summary</code> for back-compat. Aligns system-routed envelope shape with direct-send envelope shape so future producers don't recreate the drift. Could bundle into v1.7.3 alongside the read-path fix OR ship as v1.7.4 separately.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Could be combined into the v1.7.3 fix as a single coordinated patch instead of a separate v1.7.4. Plan member should evaluate that bundling decision."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Lift summary to the top-level envelope key at the appendSystemMailbox call sites in crews hooks/stop.js — defense-in-depth companion to the read-path fallback fix.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe read-path fix (crews-review-mail-summary-payload-fallback v1.7.3) makes review-mail.js resilient to the nested payload.summary shape. But the underlying schema asymmetry remains: system-routed envelopes (member-reply, proactive-report) write summary ONLY inside payload while direct-sent envelopes write summary at the top level. Future consumers reading row.summary will hit the same drop.\n\n## Fix\n\nAt hooks/stop.js:831-842 (member-reply appendSystemMailbox call) and hooks/stop.js:858-882 (proactive-report appendSystemMailbox call), pass effectiveSummary to BOTH: payload.summary (preserves existing readers) and top-level summary key (new; matches direct-send shape).\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js — both appendSystemMailbox call sites; lift summary to top level.\n2. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tests/*.test.js — add at least one test asserting top-level summary on a member-reply envelope after stop.\n3. Update test goldens if any.\n4. CHANGELOG entry + version bump.\n\n## Sequencing decision (plan member's call)\n\nOption A: ship together with v1.7.3 as one coordinated v1.7.3 patch (read + write both fixed).\nOption B: ship as v1.7.4 patch separately.\n\nOption A is simpler (1 ship instead of 2); Option B is more granular for bisection.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- appendSystemMailbox envelopes for member-reply and proactive-report carry both summary and payload.summary.\n- Existing tests still pass; one new test asserts top-level summary on the system envelope.\n- CHANGELOG entry cites the schema-uniformity rationale.\n\nMulti-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"crews-envelope-body-canonical-for-all-kinds","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T02:19:00Z","mergeCommit":"2a22b20e","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-envelope-body-canonical-for-all-kinds","descriptionHtml":"Codify a protocol contract: for ALL review-surfacing kinds (<code>done</code>, <code>question</code>, <code>blocked</code> — and arguably <code>progress</code>), the substantive content MUST live in the prose <strong>body</strong>, NOT in the <code>summary</code> attribute. Summary is a quick-look ≤120 chars; body is canonical. The bookkeeper-lead's mailbox-history.jsonl audit trail is supposed to preserve the full member intent for forensics — today a member can stuff the actual question into the summary attribute (as happened with <code>impl-ralph-overview-watcher-consumer-workspace-root</code>'s rebase-auth question on 2026-05-28) and the body becomes an ack stub, defeating the audit. Combination of docs clarification + Stop-hook enforcement (warn or error when summary length exceeds N and body length is below M).","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Composite task with both docs + enforcement axes. Plan member should decide: (a) <strong>docs-only</strong> — clarify SessionStart briefing + CLAUDE.md without enforcement; (b) <strong>warn enforcement</strong> — Stop-hook emits a warning to the agent transcript when summary &gt; 200ch AND body &lt; 50ch; (c) <strong>hard enforcement</strong> — Stop-hook BLOCKS turn-end with a 'put your content in the body, not the summary attribute' message; (d) <strong>auto-promote</strong> — when summary &gt; N chars and body is shorter, copy summary to body automatically before envelope write. Option (c) is symmetric to the v1.6.2 listener-arm gate; option (a) is lightest-touch; option (d) is most forgiving but masks the protocol mistake."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Codify and enforce the crews protocol contract that the prose body carries canonical content for all review-surfacing kind tags (done, question, blocked, possibly progress) — not the summary attribute.\n\n## Why\n\nObserved 2026-05-28 in overview-bookkeeper coordination: a member (impl-ralph-overview-watcher-consumer-workspace-root) emitted kind=question with a 600-char summary attribute carrying the full question content + body = 'Adding the ack for the prior operator authorization message' (a 55-char ack stub). The bookkeeper lead received only the ack stub in review-mail because:\n\n1. The summary attribute should have been ≤120 chars per SessionStart briefing.\n2. The substantive content belongs in the prose body, not the summary attribute.\n3. The summary-drop bug (separately tracked at crews-review-mail-summary-payload-fallback) compounded the impact, but even without that bug, the audit trail in mailbox-history.jsonl would have only had the ack stub as the searchable / human-readable record of what the member was actually asking.\n\nThe operator's directive: ALL kind values that propagate to the lead's review queue (done, question, blocked, and arguably progress) should carry the substantive content in the body, mirroring the existing kind=done convention that members already follow. The summary attribute remains a quick-look for the manifest's lastSummary field.\n\n## Design space\n\n### Axis 1 — Documentation\n\nClarify in:\n- SessionStart briefing prose (the long instructions every new session reads at startup) — explicit 'body is canonical for X kinds' line.\n- crews/CLAUDE.md operator manual.\n- crews/docs/protocol.md if it exists; otherwise add one.\n- copilot-plugin mirrors.\n\n### Axis 2 — Enforcement (Stop-hook detection)\n\nWhen a member emits a kind tag with summary attribute > N chars AND body < M chars, the Stop hook can:\n\n**(a) docs-only** — no Stop-hook change; rely on operator + bookkeeper to flag misuse.\n**(b) warn** — Stop hook emits a transcript-visible warning ('your summary attribute is long but your body is short; the audit trail will only have the body. Consider moving the content into the body before the tag.') without blocking the turn. Member sees but may proceed.\n**(c) hard block** — Stop hook BLOCKS turn-end with an actionable message. Member must restructure prose before stop succeeds. Symmetric to the v1.6.2 listener-arm gate.\n**(d) auto-promote** — when summary > N and body < M, the envelope-writer copies summary to body before persistence. The member's intent is preserved without operator intervention; the protocol mistake is silently corrected.\n\nThresholds N (summary cap above which we suspect content-stuffing) and M (body floor below which we suspect ack-only). The audit member observed N=600 / M=55 in the reproducer; plausible defaults: N=200, M=50.\n\n### Axis 3 — Which kinds get enforcement?\n\n- All of {done, question, blocked} — these are the review-required kinds.\n- progress is more nuanced: idle progress envelopes legitimately have short bodies; enforcement here would create false positives.\n\nPlan should evaluate whether progress envelopes need the same treatment, given the v1.6.0 progress-envelope exemption from review-required.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js — if (b), (c), or (d): the kind-tag parser captures summary/body lengths and applies the chosen action.\n2. plugins/crews/hooks/lib/parse-report-tag.js (or wherever the parser lives) — capture both fields, expose for downstream check.\n3. plugins/crews/CLAUDE.md + .copilot-plugin/ mirror — protocol clarification.\n4. plugins/crews/hooks/SessionStart.js (or wherever the long briefing prose lives) — clarification on the body-canonical rule for {done, question, blocked}.\n5. plugins/crews/tests/stop-decision.test.js — fixtures for the (b)/(c)/(d) behavior on long-summary + short-body envelopes.\n6. CHANGELOG.md + version bump.\n\n## Operator-facing open questions\n\n1. Which option for axis 2: (a)/(b)/(c)/(d)?\n2. Threshold values N + M.\n3. Whether progress envelopes are in-scope or exempt.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- SessionStart briefing prose clearly states 'body is canonical for kind={done, question, blocked}'.\n- crews CLAUDE.md documents the rule + the thresholds.\n- Stop-hook behavior matches the chosen option from axis 2.\n- New tests cover the new behavior (lazy-summary detection, no false-positives on legitimate short-body progress envelopes).\n- Existing tests still pass (v1.6.2 listener-arm gate, v1.7.x sessionId pointer, etc).\n\nVersion bump: v1.8.0 minor (if (c) or (d) — behavioral change) OR v1.7.4 patch (if (a) or (b) — additive). Multi-remote push. Local-main FF + push-main pattern per operator preference.\n\nNote sequencing: this task can ship before, after, or in parallel with crews-review-mail-summary-payload-fallback (v1.7.3). They address different layers: this is producer-side discipline; that is consumer-side resilience.\""}}},{"id":"crews-ack-loop-fix","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"c514b97b","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T20:48:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-ack-loop-fix","descriptionHtml":"Critical fix for the silent-censor ack-loop bug observed on <code>impl-codex-upstream-rebase</code> 2026-05-30. <code>stop.js:898</code> unresolved-consumed gate fired BEFORE the <code>stop.js:937</code> outbox-write auto-derive — asymmetric ordering meant a member's new <code>kind=question</code> with reply-to never satisfied the gate, turn rolled back, content silently censored. Fix: implicit-replyTo pre-gate pass with <code>alreadyResolved</code> guard preserving explicit <code>decision=\"deferred\"/\"unable\"/\"ack-no-action\"</code> overrides. Research brief at <code>.ralph/jobs/crews-ack-loop-bug/research-brief.md</code> (81a5d9e1).","warnings":[],"prompts":{}}},{"id":"crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"6be4e3ce","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T20:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields","descriptionHtml":"D-001 from <code>brainstorm-crews-summary-rendering-audit</code> (c52c0360). Make <code>hooks/protocol/envelope.js</code>'s existing <code>validateEnvelope()</code>/<code>buildEnvelope()</code> framework load-bearing by defining canonical addresses per envelope field and enforcing via <code>CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1</code> on every producer. Today the schema framework exists but has ZERO production call-sites — dead code that allowed v1.7.2 summary-drop to ship. Plan defines canonical addresses (proposed: summary top-level, kind top-level, replyTo top-level on outbox + payload.replyToId on system-mailbox kept as asymmetry, progressTail payload-nested), CI round-trip test design, and migration steps.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Depends on (or is bundled with) two prerequisite tasks: <code>crews-protocol-buildenvelope-adoption</code> (route producers through buildEnvelope) + <code>crews-protocol-strict-schema-ci</code> (enable strict schema in CI). The brainstorm noted these prereqs are required for D-001's validation rules to have any teeth. Plan should decide whether to bundle all 3 into one ship or sequence."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Implement D-001 from crews-summary-rendering-audit brainstorm: make hooks/protocol/envelope.js's existing schema framework load-bearing, define canonical addresses per envelope field, and enforce via CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1 on every producer.\n\n## Why\n\nBrainstorm (c52c0360, .ralph/brainstorms/crews-summary-rendering-audit/selected-direction.md) discovered hooks/protocol/envelope.js ships a validateEnvelope/buildEnvelope framework that has ZERO production call-sites. The schema validator exists but is dead code. This is the root cause that allowed v1.7.2 summary-drop to ship: the schema framework was built but producers construct envelope objects ad-hoc.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Define the canonical envelope schema in hooks/protocol/envelope.js with explicit per-field addresses:\n   - summary: top-level (post-v1.7.3 normalization)\n   - kind: top-level (report kind, distinct from row.from.routingKind)\n   - replyTo: top-level on outbox + payload.replyToId on system-mailbox (intentional asymmetry; document it)\n   - progressTail: payload-nested\n   - decision, ack, bodyResolution, reviewedSeq: TBD per audit catalog (.ralph/brainstorms/.../selected-direction.md §Other envelope-field surveys)\n\n2. Update validateEnvelope() to enforce the schema with CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1.\n\n3. Add a node:test round-trip test: write an envelope via buildEnvelope() → read via review-mail formatter → assert all fields surface at their canonical addresses.\n\n4. Coordinate with prereq tasks crews-protocol-buildenvelope-adoption (route producers through buildEnvelope; otherwise validation has no teeth) and crews-protocol-strict-schema-ci (enable strict mode in CI workflow). Plan decides whether to bundle these or sequence.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/hooks/protocol/envelope.js — define canonical schema, harden validateEnvelope, add fixtures.\n2. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tests/protocol-envelope-canonical-fields.test.js — round-trip tests.\n3. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/CHANGELOG.md + version bump (v1.8.0 minor — additive schema enforcement).\n4. ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/CLAUDE.md — document the schema contract for future contributors.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- validateEnvelope rejects envelope with summary at unexpected address.\n- CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1 causes buildEnvelope to throw on schema violations.\n- Round-trip test passes for all 7 canonical fields (summary, kind, replyTo, decision, ack, bodyResolution, reviewedSeq).\n- Existing producer call sites that use the new buildEnvelope path don't regress.\n- Tests pass: cd plugins/crews && node tests/run.js\n\n## Workflow\n\nPlan-direct. v1.8.0 minor patch. Multi-remote push via local-main FF + push-main per operator pattern. May bundle with prereqs into single ship at plan's discretion.\""}}},{"id":"crews-protocol-strict-schema-ci","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"87e5dcc1","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T00:15:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-protocol-strict-schema-ci","descriptionHtml":"Fast-follow from <code>brainstorm-crews-summary-rendering-audit</code> (c52c0360). Enable <code>CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1</code> in the crews-plugin CI workflow. Smallest standalone follow-up; prerequisite for <code>crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields</code> (D-001).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Enable CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1 in crews-plugin CI workflow so the existing validateEnvelope() framework starts catching schema violations on every PR.\n\n## Why\n\nbrainstorm c52c0360 identified that hooks/protocol/envelope.js ships a validateEnvelope/buildEnvelope framework with CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1 support — but the env var is never set in CI. So no test ever exercises the strict-mode path, and schema-drift bugs like v1.7.2 summary-drop escape detection.\n\n## Pre-flight check (per brainstorm Disconfirming observation)\n\nIs CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1 ALREADY set in any crews-plugin CI workflow today? Check .github/workflows/*.yml at canonical crews source. If yes, audit why v1.7.2 summary-drop still escaped before doing more work. If no, proceed.\n\n## Fix\n\n1. Find the crews-plugin test invocation in CI (.github/workflows/*.yml or wherever the crews tests are run).\n2. Add CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1 to the env: block.\n3. Verify all existing tests still pass under strict mode (some may need adjustment if they depend on lax-mode behavior).\n4. CHANGELOG entry + version bump.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. ai-developer-toolkit/.github/workflows/*.yml — add CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1 env.\n2. plugins/crews/CHANGELOG.md + version bump (v1.7.4 patch — additive CI hardening).\n3. plugins/crews/CLAUDE.md — document the CI invariant.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- CI env block has CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA=1.\n- All existing crews tests pass under strict mode.\n- New synthetic test verifies strict-mode catches a bad envelope.\n\n## Workflow\n\nPlan-direct. Small standalone patch. v1.7.4 OR bundle with crews-protocol-buildenvelope-adoption + crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields into v1.8.0.\""}}},{"id":"crews-protocol-buildenvelope-adoption","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"bb7f1aff","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T21:44:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-protocol-buildenvelope-adoption","descriptionHtml":"Prerequisite for <code>crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields</code> (D-001) from brainstorm c52c0360. Route all producer call sites (<code>stop.js:782 appendOutbox</code>, <code>stop.js:831 appendSystemMailbox</code>, <code>send-to-member.js:95 appendMailbox</code>, <code>send-to-thread.js</code> writes) through the existing <code>buildEnvelope()</code> factory so D-001's validation rules have teeth.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Route all crews-plugin envelope writers through hooks/protocol/envelope.js's buildEnvelope() factory so the existing validation framework has teeth.\n\n## Why\n\nbrainstorm c52c0360 found 4 production call sites that construct envelopes ad-hoc instead of using buildEnvelope(): stop.js:782 (appendOutbox), stop.js:831 (appendSystemMailbox), send-to-member.js:95 (appendMailbox), send-to-thread.js writes. Without routing through buildEnvelope(), the validateEnvelope() framework is dead code — any future schema fix has no enforcement.\n\n## Fix\n\nFor each call site:\n1. Replace ad-hoc { kind, summary, body, ... } object literal with buildEnvelope({ ... }).\n2. Verify buildEnvelope() produces the same envelope shape as today (back-compat — should produce identical bytes for existing tests).\n3. If buildEnvelope() doesn't support all the fields currently written, extend it (this is part of the schema-formalization work).\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js — 2 call sites (lines 782 + 831 per brainstorm).\n2. plugins/crews/hooks/commands/send-to-member.js — 1 call site (~line 95).\n3. plugins/crews/hooks/commands/send-to-thread.js — 1+ call sites.\n4. plugins/crews/hooks/protocol/envelope.js — extend buildEnvelope to support all field combinations the existing ad-hoc writers use.\n5. plugins/crews/tests/ — existing tests should pass unchanged; add a test that asserts each producer routes through buildEnvelope.\n6. CHANGELOG + version bump.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- All 4+ producer call sites use buildEnvelope().\n- Existing tests pass: cd plugins/crews && node tests/run.js\n- New test confirms buildEnvelope is the only envelope-creation path from production code.\n- No envelope-shape changes (byte-identical envelopes for current behavior).\n\n## Workflow\n\nPlan-direct. v1.7.5 patch OR bundle with crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields into v1.8.0.\""}}},{"id":"crews-envelope-summary-canonical-cleanup","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"85a4d146","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T10:36:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-envelope-summary-canonical-cleanup","descriptionHtml":"Post-<code>crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields</code> cleanup from brainstorm c52c0360. Once D-001 picks a canonical address for <code>summary</code> (recommend: top-level), remove the OTHER half of v1.7.3's defensive fix. Either delete the consumer fallback in <code>review-mail.js:111</code> OR delete the producer mirror in <code>stop.js:840</code>. Not both. Owner-decision required.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Blocked on <code>crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields</code> shipping AND CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA validation being load-bearing across all producers (so removing one defensive layer doesn't reintroduce silent drop). Operator confirmed v1.7.3 keeps BOTH layers through v1.8.x; remove ONE in v1.9.x."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"After crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields ships D-001's canonical schema contract, remove one of v1.7.3's two defensive layers (read-fallback OR write-mirror) for summary so the canonical address is the only path.\n\n## Why\n\nv1.7.3 shipped Option A: read-fallback (review-mail.js:111: row.summary ?? row.payload?.summary) AND write-mirror (stop.js:840: lift summary to top-level). Both layers defend against schema-drift. After D-001 enforces a canonical address, one defensive layer becomes redundant.\n\nOperator preference (recorded 2026-05-28): keep BOTH through v1.8.x, remove ONE in v1.9.x.\n\n## Decision\n\nRecommended canonical address: top-level summary.\nRemove: consumer fallback OR producer mirror?\n\n- Remove consumer fallback (review-mail.js:111 reverts to row.summary): producers MUST emit summary at top-level OR the consumer drops it. Strict; producer-side enforcement via CREWS_STRICT_SCHEMA is the safety net.\n- Remove producer mirror (stop.js:840 stops lifting summary to top-level): summary stays nested in payload; consumer fallback handles it. This means top-level is ONLY for direct sends; system-routed keeps the nested form.\n\nThe first option is cleaner; the second preserves the producer's freedom to use the historical schema. Plan picks one with rationale.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- One of the two defensive layers removed.\n- v1.7.2 reproduce test (the original summary-drop fixture) still passes via the remaining defensive layer + validateEnvelope strict-mode.\n- CHANGELOG entry citing the canonical-address decision.\n- v1.9.0 minor (intentional behavior tightening).\n\n## Workflow\n\nBLOCKED on crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields + crews-protocol-buildenvelope-adoption shipping. Then plan-direct.\""}}},{"id":"crews-protocol-v200-envelope-renames","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"02af9994","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T11:45:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-protocol-v200-envelope-renames","descriptionHtml":"Two breaking-change renames bundled for v2.0.0 from brainstorm c52c0360. (a) Rename <code>row.from.kind</code> → <code>row.from.routingKind</code> so it's never confused with <code>row.kind</code> (report kind). (b) Rename <code>payload.replyToId</code> → <code>payload.replyTo</code> to match the outbox top-level field name. Both surface clarity wins; both require a coordinated migration of all consumers.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>BREAKING CHANGE → v2.0.0 major</strong>. Combined here because both have the same audience (envelope-schema awareness) + same migration profile + same major bump. Could split if needed but no clear benefit. Coordinate with the v2.0.0 release schedule (no rush; ship after D-001 stabilizes)."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Combine two breaking-change envelope-schema renames into a single v2.0.0 ship.\n\n## Why\n\nbrainstorm c52c0360 identified two surface-confusion bugs that need rename-class fixes:\n\n1. **row.from.kind vs row.kind**: row.from.kind is a routing kind (lead/member/etc.) that's syntactically identical to row.kind which is the report kind (done/question/blocked). Future maintainers confuse them. Rename row.from.kind → row.from.routingKind everywhere.\n\n2. **payload.replyToId vs replyTo**: outbox envelopes carry replyTo at top-level; system-mailbox envelopes carry payload.replyToId. Rename payload.replyToId → payload.replyTo to match the outbox name. (The asymmetry between top-level vs payload-nested is intentional per the brainstorm — only the field NAME unifies.)\n\nBoth are breaking changes that affect every consumer reading these fields. Bundle into v2.0.0 to ship once.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. plugins/crews/hooks/stop.js — both write sites.\n2. plugins/crews/hooks/commands/review-mail.js:85 — row.from.kind read.\n3. plugins/crews/hooks/lib/mailbox.js — wherever inbox-history writes the fields.\n4. plugins/crews/hooks/commands/send-to-member.js / send-to-thread.js — replyTo handling.\n5. All test files that reference either field — grep + update.\n6. CHANGELOG with prominent BREAKING CHANGE section.\n7. Bump major to v2.0.0 across all 6 stamps.\n\n## Migration story for external consumers\n\nIf any downstream code reads row.from.kind or payload.replyToId, document migration in CHANGELOG + provide a release-notes section. Probably also surface in the v2.0.0 announcement.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Zero references to row.from.kind or payload.replyToId in plugins/crews/ (excluding CHANGELOG historical refs).\n- All tests pass under new names.\n- CHANGELOG BREAKING CHANGE section enumerates the renames with from→to mappings.\n- Major bump to v2.0.0.\n\n## Workflow\n\nBLOCKED on crews-protocol-envelope-canonical-fields shipping (don't rename fields whose canonical addresses are still being decided). Then plan-direct. Multi-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"crews-summary-rendering-audit","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T00:13:00Z","mergeCommit":"c52c0360","initialStage":"brainstorming","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-summary-rendering-audit","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Brainstorm-first.</strong> Surfaced by <code>investigate-crews-summary-drop</code> as a forward-looking audit. Catalog every crews-plugin consumer that surfaces an envelope <code>summary</code> field, document which ones read top-level vs <code>payload</code>, and identify OTHER fields with similar producer/consumer drift (candidates: <code>kind</code>, <code>replyTo</code>, <code>progressTail</code>). Output is a markdown audit doc + optional follow-up tasks per drifted field.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"Audit every crews-plugin consumer that reads envelope summary and catalog where the producer/consumer schema may drift on other fields.\n\n## Why\n\nThe summary-drop bug (investigation 79752a65) was caused by a producer/consumer mismatch: hooks/stop.js writes envelopes with nested payload.summary while hooks/commands/review-mail.js reads top-level row.summary. The two were drifted apart silently. Same drift may exist on other fields (kind, replyTo, progressTail, etc).\n\n## Investigation scope\n\n### Phase 1 — enumerate consumers\n\nGrep all crews-plugin code that reads an envelope summary: review-mail.js (already known), format-progress-tail.js, briefing renderer, formatOutboxEntries, /list-members view code, spawn-launchers (do any echo summary?), copilot mirror hooks/commands.\n\nFor each: document file:line of the read, top-level vs payload, and what surface it renders to.\n\n### Phase 2 — enumerate producers\n\nGrep all crews-plugin code that WRITES envelope summary: hooks/stop.js (already known: appendSystemMailbox vs direct send), hooks/commands/send-to-member.js, hooks/commands/send-to-thread.js, format-report-tag.js, copilot mirror hook write paths.\n\nFor each: document file:line of the write, top-level / payload / both.\n\n### Phase 3 — find drift\n\nFor each (producer, consumer) pair: do they agree on schema? If not, that's a drift candidate.\n\nAlso check OTHER envelope fields (not just summary): kind, replyTo, decision, ack, progressTail, reviewedSeq, bodyResolution.\n\n### Lenses\n\nFeasibility (codex) — can the audit be automated via a one-time grep pass + diff?\nDevil's-advocate — is the schema asymmetry intentional (e.g. payload IS the canonical envelope shape; top-level is a flattened convenience)? If yes, the read-path fallback is the better fix than the write-path mirror.\nSimplification — could we replace the dual-path schema with a single canonical envelope type defined in TypeScript that all producers + consumers use?\n\n## Deliverable\n\n.ralph/brainstorms/crews-summary-rendering-audit/selected-direction.md with: catalog of (consumer file:line → reads top-level vs payload) pairs for summary; catalog of producer file:line write shapes; drift findings (which producer/consumer pairs disagree); other field surveys (kind, replyTo, progressTail, etc); recommended approach (read-fallback / write-mirror / canonical-type / hybrid); per-field follow-up tasks for the bookkeeper to add (one per drifted field).\""}}},{"id":"ralph-overview-watcher-copilot-cli-env-verify","scope":"ralph-overview","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"fea7520e","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T04:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-overview-watcher-copilot-cli-env-verify","descriptionHtml":"Deferred from <code>impl-ralph-overview-watcher-consumer-workspace-root</code> US-005 (the v2.4.1 source fix shipped; AC-1a/AC-1b end-to-end smoke deferred). The watcher's shared <code>repo-root.mjs</code> resolver provably works when <code>RALPH_OVERVIEW_REPO_ROOT</code> is set at the shell level — but <strong>Copilot CLI 1.0.55 does NOT propagate workspace <code>.mcp.json</code> env blocks to the launched MCP child process</strong>. The MCP child therefore never sees the env hint, the resolver's env-priority path never fires, and Copilot lands the owner-marker outside the consumer workspace. This task tracks: (a) monitor Copilot CLI for env-propagation fix, (b) design an alternative non-env consumer-root signal (e.g. CLI-emitted sidecar file beside <code>.mcp.json</code>), (c) re-run AC-1a/AC-1b smoke when either (a) lands or (b) ships.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Blocked on UPSTREAM Copilot CLI behavior. Two unblock paths: (a) Copilot CLI publishes a version that propagates workspace `.mcp.json` env blocks to MCP child processes — track GitHub Copilot CLI release notes; (b) ralph-overview adds an alternative consumer-root signal in v2.4.x+ (e.g. resolver looks for a sidecar file `_consumer-root` written by the workspace owner). Path (b) is in-scope here if (a) is slow to land. This is the symmetric task to <code>ralph-overview-copilot-smoke-deferred</code> on the same Copilot CLI 1.0.55 limitation surface."}],"prompts":{"impl":"/implement-with-ralph \"Verify the v2.4.1 watcher consumer-root fix end-to-end under Copilot once the env-propagation gap is resolved, OR ship an alternative resolver signal that doesn't depend on env propagation.\n\n## Pre-flight: determine which unblock path is viable\n\n1. **Check Copilot CLI version on host:** run `copilot --version`. If >1.0.55, run `copilot mcp list --json` against a workspace .mcp.json with an env block and confirm the env block is reachable by the MCP child process (set an obviously-distinctive env var, write a tiny MCP server that dumps its process.env, check the dump). If env propagation works, proceed to path (a) verification below.\n\n2. **Check ralph-overview version on main:** if main has shipped a non-env consumer-root signal (e.g. resolver-from-sidecar), proceed to path (b) verification.\n\n3. If neither path is unblocked: abort and notify operator. The task remains blocked.\n\n## Path (a) — Copilot CLI env propagation works\n\nRun the original AC-1a + AC-1b from .ralph/jobs/ralph-overview-watcher-consumer-workspace-root/plan.md:\n\n1. Scratch Copilot workspace with `.mcp.json` declaring `env: { RALPH_OVERVIEW_REPO_ROOT: '<consumer-root-path>' }` for the ralph-overview server.\n2. Invoke `copilot -p \\\"call overview.watcher_status\\\" --allow-all --silent --no-custom-instructions --disable-builtin-mcps` from that scratch workspace.\n3. Verify the watcher owner-marker file lands under `<consumer-root-path>/.ralph/overview-watcher.owner`, NOT under the plugin-cache `.ralph/`.\n4. Run a second probe without the env block; verify the fallback (walk-up from cwd) lands the marker correctly when cwd IS the consumer workspace.\n\n## Path (b) — alternative resolver signal landed in ralph-overview\n\nVerify the new signal's resolution: e.g. if the resolver now reads `_consumer-root` sidecar from the workspace, write one and confirm the watcher owner-marker lands under the path inside that sidecar.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nAt either path's completion, write `.ralph/jobs/<this-task>/copilot-watcher-smoke.log` with the command + assertion + PASS/FAIL row, then promote audit-report.md AC-1a/AC-1b rows from `deferred` to `PASS` in the original audit task's job dir, and ship a CHANGELOG patch note lifting the deferral.\n\n## If smoke FAILS despite the unblock path landing\n\nLikely a bug in v2.4.1's plugin-cache rejection layer or in the alternative signal's resolution. File a follow-up patch task with precise reproduction steps.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- AC-1a PASS row written.\n- AC-1b PASS row written.\n- Original audit task's audit-report.md updated.\n- CHANGELOG entry lifting the v2.4.1 'deferred US-005' deferral.\n\nVersion bump only if a patch ships from this task (e.g. ralph-overview v2.4.2 with the alternative resolver signal). Local-main FF + push main per operator pattern.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-rubric-fixture-drift-3-tests","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"empty-diff-on-6a9da6a4-rubric-drift-not-reproducible","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T18:50:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-rubric-fixture-drift-3-tests","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-ralph-exec-help-contract</code> during Phase 5a smoke-check verification on ai-developer-toolkit main (<code>b2e4913d</code>). Three pre-existing test failures exist on origin/main without any of today's Batch 3 changes — all rooted in the same rubric-fixture drift: <code>codex-exec-stdin.txt</code> fixture (and dependent regression-smoke fixtures) no longer match the current <code>docs/severity-rubric.md</code>. Affected tests: <code>test-codex-exec.mjs::'mixed body assembly pins assembled stdin byte shape'</code>, <code>test-regression-smoke-phase-3.mjs</code>, <code>test-regression-smoke-phase-4.mjs</code>. Regenerate the fixtures from the current rubric, OR back the rubric off to match the fixtures.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Test failures masquerade as 'pre-existing baseline noise' which causes future Phase 5a reviewers to spend cycles disentangling regression-from-baseline. Fix soon to keep the green-baseline assertion meaningful."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Fix the 3 pre-existing test failures on ralph plugin's ai-developer-toolkit main caused by rubric-fixture drift.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe impl-ralph-exec-help-contract member (Batch 3 Round 1) flagged 3 pre-existing test failures on ai-developer-toolkit origin/main b2e4913d:\n- plugins/ralph/tests/test-codex-exec.mjs::'mixed body assembly pins assembled stdin byte shape' — codex-exec-stdin.txt fixture stale vs current docs/severity-rubric.md.\n- plugins/ralph/tests/test-regression-smoke-phase-3.mjs — same root cause.\n- plugins/ralph/tests/test-regression-smoke-phase-4.mjs — same root cause.\n\nThe failures are unrelated to today's --help fix but they pollute the test-baseline assertion ('tests remain green'). Future Phase 5a reviewers will keep wasting cycles confirming the failures are pre-existing.\n\n## Investigation step (mandatory before fix)\n\nDetermine which side of the drift is canonical:\n- Read docs/severity-rubric.md current state.\n- Read codex-exec-stdin.txt fixture.\n- Run the failing tests + capture the actual diff between expected and observed.\n- Decide: regenerate fixture from rubric, OR roll rubric back to match the fixture (likely the former — rubric is the source of truth).\n\n## Fix\n\nIf the rubric is canonical:\n1. Run whatever fixture-generation script the plugin uses (search scripts/ for codex-exec-stdin generator).\n2. If no generator, hand-update the fixture to match the current rubric's byte-shape.\n3. Re-run the 3 failing tests + adjacent tests to confirm no regression.\n4. Verify regression-smoke-phase-3 and -phase-4 also pass after the fixture update (they cite the same root cause so a single fixture update should fix all 3).\n\nIf the rubric drifted accidentally:\n1. Identify which rubric edit broke fixture parity.\n2. Decide whether the rubric edit was intentional (then update fixtures) or accidental (then revert).\n\n## Files to touch\n\nLikely:\n- plugins/ralph/tests/fixtures/codex-exec-stdin.txt — regenerated.\n- plugins/ralph/docs/severity-rubric.md — possibly amended.\n- plugins/ralph/tests/fixtures/regression-smoke-phase-*/ — possibly regenerated.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- All 3 named tests pass on a fresh checkout of main.\n- No new test failures introduced.\n- CHANGELOG entry noting the fixture-baseline restoration.\n\nVersion bump: ralph v5.46.2 patch (test-only fix). Local main merge + push to both remotes per operator workflow.\""}}},{"id":"agent-memory-repo-migration","scope":"codexu|bookkeeping","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T13:00:00Z","mergeCommit":"78abec33,fec74989,f592aec4,8881263f,8a09c20a","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"agent-memory-repo-migration","descriptionHtml":"Migrate the 15+ <code>feedback_*.md</code> + project-memory files from <code>C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/projects/D--harness-efforts-codexu/memory/</code> (claude-code-specific, not git-tracked) into a repo-tracked location. <strong>Superseded 2026-05-29:</strong> the migration shipped at commits <code>78abec33,fec74989,f592aec4</code> (moved to <code>.agents/memory/</code> + NTFS junction), then auto-memory was <strong>retired entirely</strong> at commits <code>8881263f</code> (inline load-bearing entries into AGENTS.md) and <code>8a09c20a</code> (lead-stays-on-main rule). The <code>.agents/memory/</code> directory and the user-dir junction are removed. All operating invariants now live in AGENTS.md → \"Bookkeeper operating invariants\" section.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"🪦 Archived. Originally specced as a portability fix (move memories into the repo so copilot can read them). The migration succeeded but the post-migration design — runtime auto-load of repo-tracked memory files — was retired in favor of inlining the load-bearing rules into AGENTS.md directly. Cleaner outcome: zero indirection, no auto-load mechanism to maintain, no risk of stale memory entries drifting from current code reality."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Migrate the codexu auto-memory directory from claude-code-specific location to a repo-tracked location so memories travel with git + are readable by copilot sessions.\n\n## Today's state\n\n- Source: 15+ feedback_*.md + project_*.md files at C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/projects/D--harness-efforts-codexu/memory/ — index at MEMORY.md (also in that dir).\n- Auto-load mechanism: claude-code reads MEMORY.md as a session-global memory file.\n- Not in git. Not visible to copilot. Not portable across machines.\n\n## Target\n\nMove memories into the codexu repo at one of:\n- docs/agent-memory/ (matches docs/ convention)\n- .agents/memory/ (matches the .agents/ skill-pointer convention used elsewhere in codexu)\n- plans/agent-memory/ (matches the plans/ bookkeeper-data convention)\n\nPlan should pick one; suggest .agents/memory/ for parallel structure with .agents/skills/.\n\nIndex (MEMORY.md) lives at the chosen dir's root. Each feedback_*/project_* file moves alongside.\n\n## Auto-load wiring\n\n- AGENTS.md at codexu root gets an Auto-memory section that references the new dir + lists key entries. Copilot picks this up via its AGENTS.md auto-load.\n- CLAUDE.md (untracked, bookkeeper operating manual) gets the same section. Claude-code's auto-memory mechanism continues to read the user-dir copies UNTIL we remove them.\n- Optional: a small AGENTS.md `@docs/agent-memory/MEMORY.md` directive (if AGENTS.md spec supports it) auto-loads the index file directly.\n\n## Migration steps\n\n1. Decide target dir (recommend .agents/memory/).\n2. Copy all 15+ files from user-dir to the chosen repo dir.\n3. Update MEMORY.md links if they reference relative paths (likely just [[name]] wikilinks — those work cross-location).\n4. Add a section to AGENTS.md (and CLAUDE.md mirror) that points readers at the index.\n5. Commit + push.\n6. After verification, delete the user-dir copies OR convert them to symlinks pointing at the repo files (operator decides).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- All 15+ memories live under repo-tracked dir.\n- AGENTS.md has an Auto-memory section.\n- Copilot session in this repo can read the memories via standard file reads.\n- Claude-code's auto-load behavior is unchanged (until user-dir cleanup step).\n- README or contributor-onboarding doc mentions the auto-memory dir.\n\n## Workflow\n\nConcrete enough to skip /brainstorm-with-ralph. Plan-direct. Decomposes into 2-3 stories (file move + AGENTS.md wiring + user-dir cleanup as separate story so operator can defer).\""}}},{"id":"ralph-overview-copilot-smoke-deferred","scope":"ralph-overview","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-29T04:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-overview-copilot-smoke-deferred","descriptionHtml":"Deferred US-008 follow-up from ralph-overview v2.4.0 ship (<code>ad4938fc</code>). End-to-end Copilot smoke gate (marketplace install + <code>copilot mcp list</code> + 4 skill invocations) couldn't run pre-merge due to TWO external constraints: (a) marketplace pins published v2.3.0 — must wait for v2.4.0 publish; (b) Copilot CLI 1.0.55 doesn't expose plugin-manifest <code>mcpServers</code> in <code>copilot mcp list --json</code> — must wait for Copilot CLI version that does. Full brief at <code>.ralph/jobs/ralph-overview-copilot-mirror/deferred-us008-followup.md</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>Blocked on TWO external prerequisites:</strong> (a) v2.4.0 published to <code>ai-developer-toolkit</code> marketplace AND (b) Copilot CLI version that surfaces plugin-manifest <code>mcpServers</code> in <code>copilot mcp list --json</code>. Track Copilot CLI release notes; consider opening an upstream issue if (b) persists. Not actionable until BOTH conditions hold. If step 3 of the verification (mcp list) still misses the plugin MCP after CLI updates, the manifest may need to switch from <code>&quot;mcpServers&quot;: &quot;.mcp.json&quot;</code> to a Copilot-only inline form with a verified plugin-root token — treat that as part of the follow-up scope."}],"prompts":{"impl":"/implement-with-ralph \"Run the deferred US-008 end-to-end Copilot smoke gate for ralph-overview v2.4.0.\n\n## Pre-flight: verify both unblock conditions hold\n\n1. v2.4.0+ of ralph-overview is PUBLISHED to the ai-developer-toolkit marketplace (`copilot plugin install ralph-overview@ai-developer-toolkit` resolves to v2.4.0 or later — not v2.3.0). If still v2.3.0, abort and notify operator: the unblock condition (a) is not yet met.\n2. Copilot CLI version exposes plugin-manifest mcpServers in `copilot mcp list --json` output. Test by running `copilot mcp list --json` against a workspace with a plugin installed that declares mcpServers in its manifest. If plugin MCPs are still invisible (only workspace `.mcp.json` MCPs show), abort and notify operator: condition (b) is not yet met.\n\nIf either condition is unmet, file an updated status note at `.ralph/jobs/<this-job>/blocked-status.md` and report kind=blocked. Do NOT proceed with the smoke verification.\n\n## Verification checklist (run in fresh workspace when unblocked)\n\nPer `.ralph/jobs/ralph-overview-copilot-mirror/deferred-us008-followup.md` § What still needs to happen:\n\n1. `copilot plugin marketplace add gim-home/ai-developer-toolkit` (idempotent).\n2. `copilot plugin install ralph-overview@ai-developer-toolkit` — verify `copilot plugin list` shows ralph-overview at the current published version (≥ 2.4.0).\n3. `copilot mcp list --json` — verify the ralph-overview plugin-sourced MCP server is listed with a resolved, launchable command/args path pointing at an existing `launch.cjs`. No literal `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` token unless Copilot demonstrably expands it. If the launch path is unresolved, switch the manifest from `\\\"mcpServers\\\": \\\".mcp.json\\\"` to a Copilot-only inline object using Copilot's verified plugin-root token, then re-verify.\n4. Invoke `overview.parallel_ready_tasks` from a Copilot session and confirm the MCP server starts (capture transcript).\n5. Verify each of `/work-on`, `/triage`, `/blocker-report`, `/overview-init` is callable from a Copilot session.\n6. Verify at least one Copilot skill path runs `sync` or `cli derive-next-command` through `node <pluginRoot>/bin/ralph-overview.mjs` (no bare-`ralph-overview` PATH lookup).\n7. Capture command + assertion + PASS/FAIL into a refreshed `.ralph/jobs/<this-job>/copilot-skill-smoke.log` and require 4/4 PASS rows for the four skills.\n\n## If smoke fails\n\nIf any step fails despite both unblock conditions holding, the failure path likely points at a manifest-form bug (string-path vs inline-object mcpServers) — file a follow-up patch task against the ralph-overview plugin with the precise failing step and expected fix.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- 4/4 PASS rows in copilot-skill-smoke.log for the four skills.\n- overview.parallel_ready_tasks transcript captured showing successful MCP server invocation.\n- No remaining mention of US-008 as 'deferred' in CHANGELOG / README / migration docs (lift the deferred-status notes).\n- Optional: if a manifest patch was needed, ship that as a follow-on PR with its own version bump.\n\nNo code changes are anticipated unless step 3 surfaces a manifest-form bug. This task is verification-driven; if all steps pass cleanly, the only deliverable is the smoke log + lifting the deferred-status notes in v2.4.x docs (commit + PR).\""}}},{"id":"ralph-overview-copilot-mirror","scope":"ralph-overview","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T11:34:00Z","mergeCommit":"ad4938fc","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-overview-copilot-mirror","descriptionHtml":"Add a <code>.copilot-plugin/</code> mirror to <code>ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/</code> (currently absent — verified 2026-05-27 against main at <code>0493680c</code>). Sister plugins <code>crews</code> and <code>ralph</code> both have copilot mirrors; ralph-overview is the only marketplace plugin without one. This blocks copilot sessions from using <code>overview_parallel_ready_tasks</code> / <code>overview_dev_server_start</code> / <code>overview_init</code> / <code>overview_validate_data</code> MCP tools. Either regenerate via <code>generate-copilot-artifacts.mjs</code> (the pattern <code>ralph</code> uses) or hand-fork — plan decides.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ MCP server registration (<code>codexu-overview</code>) is part of this surface — the copilot mirror must register the same MCP server in copilot's settings format. Verify with a fresh copilot install on this Windows host that <code>overview_parallel_ready_tasks</code> shows up as an MCP tool."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add a .copilot-plugin/ mirror to plugins/ralph-overview/ in ai-developer-toolkit so copilot sessions can use ralph-overview MCP tools and skills.\n\n## Today's gap\n\nVerified 2026-05-27 against ai-developer-toolkit main at 0493680c:\n- plugins/crews/.copilot-plugin/ EXISTS.\n- plugins/ralph/.copilot-plugin/ EXISTS (regenerated in v5.46.0 via generate-copilot-artifacts.mjs).\n- plugins/ralph-overview/.copilot-plugin/ MISSING.\n\nWithout the copilot mirror:\n- Copilot sessions in this repo can't auto-register the codexu-overview MCP server.\n- The skills/work-on, skills/triage, skills/blocker-report skills don't have copilot variants.\n- The watcher's auto-start logic (Vite plugin) doesn't run under copilot.\n\n## Plan\n\nInvestigate whether ralph-overview can use the same auto-generation pattern that ralph v5.46.0 uses (`generate-copilot-artifacts.mjs`). If yes, port the script. If no (because ralph-overview has Vite-specific tooling that copilot doesn't run), hand-fork the copilot mirror and document the maintenance burden.\n\nMCP server registration in copilot: today the ralph-overview MCP is registered in .claude/settings.json. Copilot has its own MCP config (probably .copilot/config.json or similar). Plan must figure out the copilot MCP registration format + provide a one-time install script or document the manual steps.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. plugins/ralph-overview/.copilot-plugin/ — new mirror dir.\n2. plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/generate-copilot-artifacts.mjs — new script (mirrors ralph's pattern).\n3. plugins/ralph-overview/CHANGELOG.md — v2.4.0 entry for the additive copilot support.\n4. plugins/ralph-overview/package.json + .claude-plugin/plugin.json + new .copilot-plugin/plugin.json — version bumps.\n5. plugins/ralph-overview/README.md — document copilot install steps.\n6. Optional: scripts/install-copilot-mcp.mjs — one-time installer for copilot users.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A fresh copilot install on this Windows host shows ralph-overview as a usable plugin.\n- The 4 MCP tools (overview_parallel_ready_tasks, overview_dev_server_start, overview_init, overview_validate_data) are callable from copilot.\n- skills/work-on, skills/triage, skills/blocker-report have copilot variants.\n- Claude-code behavior unchanged (regression check).\n- Cross-tested: spawn one claude-code lead + one copilot member in the same crew, verify both see the same overview snapshot.\n\nVersion bump: v2.4.0 (minor — additive copilot support). Multi-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-exec-help-contract","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T18:13:00Z","mergeCommit":"d0682c89","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-exec-help-contract","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit</code> (f4d63067) — Batch 3 follow-up. Both <code>src/codex-exec.mjs</code> and <code>src/copilot-exec.mjs</code> return exit 1 + 'unknown argument: --help' when probed with <code>--help</code>. Audit/verifier scripts that exercise the help contract see a non-zero exit and can't trust the wrapper for parity checks. Add proper <code>--help</code> handling at both call sites (audit cites <code>codex-exec.mjs:148</code> and corresponding line in copilot-exec.mjs).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Fix the --help contract on ralph v5.46.0's codex-exec.mjs and copilot-exec.mjs Node wrappers.\n\n## Problem\n\nAudit plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit (f4d63067) found:\n- `node plugins/ralph/src/codex-exec.mjs --help` returns exit 1 + 'Error: unknown argument: --help'.\n- Same shape on `copilot-exec.mjs`.\n- Audit/verifier scripts that probe wrapper sanity via --help fail before they can check anything.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdd a `--help` handler in both files that prints usage + exits 0. Mirror whatever the v5.45.x bash wrappers did (or what the ralph plugin's other v5.46.0 ports do). Don't invent new help text — match the existing convention.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/src/codex-exec.mjs` — add --help handling (~codex-exec.mjs:148 per audit).\n2. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/src/copilot-exec.mjs` — same fix.\n3. Tests in `plugins/ralph/tests/` — add a test that `--help` exits 0 with non-empty stdout for each wrapper.\n4. `plugins/ralph/CHANGELOG.md` + version bump (v5.46.1 patch).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `node codex-exec.mjs --help` exits 0 with usage text.\n- `node copilot-exec.mjs --help` exits 0 with usage text.\n- New tests pass.\n- Existing tests still pass.\n\nVersion: v5.46.1 patch. Multi-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-exec-sh-wrapper-removal-changelog","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T18:36:00Z","mergeCommit":"2ed09a99,e8724534","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-exec-sh-wrapper-removal-changelog","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit</code> (f4d63067) — Batch 3 follow-up. v5.46.0 removed <code>codex-exec.sh</code> + <code>copilot-exec.sh</code> bash shims (Phase 4/5 of the all-Node migration), but the v5.46.0 CHANGELOG doesn't explicitly call out the breaking-change risk to external callers that reference the shims by path. Audit also flagged: need a caller-sweep across the broader fork ecosystem to confirm no consumer still expects them. Doc-only fix + audit sweep.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Document the v5.46.0 codex-exec.sh + copilot-exec.sh shim removal in CHANGELOG with explicit breaking-change advisory, plus run a caller-sweep audit across known consumers.\n\n## Problem\n\nv5.46.0 removed the codex-exec.sh + copilot-exec.sh bash entry-shims as part of the shell-to-Node migration. The change IS listed in the existing CHANGELOG but framed as a migration line item without an explicit 'BREAKING — external callers must migrate' advisory. Audit plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit (f4d63067) flagged: any caller invoking these shims by path will fail with 'no such file or directory'.\n\n## Fix\n\n1. Update plugins/ralph/CHANGELOG.md v5.46.0 entry: promote the shim-removal bullet to a 'BREAKING CHANGE' subsection with: (a) list of removed shim paths, (b) the migration alternative `node plugins/ralph/src/<name>.mjs ...`, (c) hermetic-test stub note (CODEX_EXEC_SCRIPT / COPILOT_EXEC_SCRIPT env vars still work for .sh stubs).\n\n2. Caller-sweep: grep across known consumers for 'codex-exec.sh' or 'copilot-exec.sh' string references:\n   - This codexu repo: search plans/, docs/, scripts/, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .ralph/jobs/*/plan.md, .agents/memory/, packages/.\n   - The ai-developer-toolkit repo: search other plugins (devui, seval, etc.) for cross-plugin references to ralph's shims.\n   - Document any findings as additional follow-up tasks.\n\n3. If the caller-sweep finds active consumers (other than the env-var-honored bash stubs), file each as a separate fix task.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/CHANGELOG.md` — promote shim-removal to BREAKING CHANGE section.\n2. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/src/codex-exec.mjs` (header comment) — add a one-line note pointing at the BREAKING CHANGE.\n3. (Optionally) `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph/README.md` — migration notes.\n4. Audit report at `.ralph/jobs/ralph-exec-sh-wrapper-removal-changelog/caller-sweep.md` — captures grep results.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- CHANGELOG has a clearly-labeled BREAKING CHANGE subsection for shim removal.\n- caller-sweep.md captures the search command + zero-or-more findings.\n- Each genuine caller (if found) has a follow-up task filed.\n- No version bump needed (doc-only); OR bump to v5.46.1 if combined with the --help contract fix above.\n\nMulti-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-overview-watcher-consumer-workspace-root","scope":"ralph-overview","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T21:38:00Z","mergeCommit":"ebd1fca2","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-overview-watcher-consumer-workspace-root","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit</code> (f4d63067) — Batch 3 follow-up. The ralph-overview watcher's owner-marker state roots inside the installed plugin cache instead of the consumer workspace, breaking the auto-start path under Copilot dev mode. A copilot session running <code>pnpm overview</code> via the workspace MCP launches against the wrong workspace root. Fix: have the watcher derive its repo root from the consumer workspace (cwd / nearest <code>.ralph/</code> ancestor), not from the plugin's own install dir.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Audit cites this as the primary blocker for Copilot lead-session migration (one of the 4 defer reasons). Fix unblocks (B) safe-with-impact path on the migration go/no-go."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Fix ralph-overview watcher to root its owner-marker state in the consumer workspace, not the plugin's installed cache directory.\n\n## Problem\n\nAudit plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit (f4d63067) audit-report.md row 'ralph-overview: dev-server-watcher-autostart' FAIL:\n\n> Watcher owner is rooted in plugin cache instead of consumer workspace.\n\nUnder Copilot dev mode, when the workspace MCP launches the ralph-overview server, the watcher derives its repo root from the plugin's installed directory (C:/Users/<user>/.copilot/.../ralph-overview/<ver>/) instead of the consumer workspace (D:/harness-efforts/codexu/). The owner-marker, sidecar writes, and dev-server hot-reload all anchor at the wrong root — making auto-start non-functional from Copilot.\n\n## Fix\n\nThe watcher's repo-root resolution should prefer (in order):\n1. Explicit env var (RALPH_OVERVIEW_REPO_ROOT or similar)\n2. Nearest .ralph/ ancestor walking up from process.cwd()\n3. Existing fallback (plugin install dir) — keep as last resort for non-workspace scenarios\n\nFiles likely affected (verify via grep):\n- `plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/sync-ralph-state.mjs` — owner-marker write path\n- `plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/lib/repo-root.mjs` (or wherever detectRepoRoot lives)\n- `plugins/ralph-overview/tools/overview-viewer/vite.config.ts` — dev server plugin auto-start integration\n- `plugins/ralph-overview/tools/overview-mcp/src/index.ts` — MCP server's working-dir handling\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- AC-1: Watcher launched from a Copilot session via the MCP server writes its owner-marker into the consumer workspace's .ralph/, NOT the plugin cache.\n- AC-2: `pnpm overview` from inside a codexu worktree continues to work (regression check for claude-code path).\n- AC-3: Audit's dev-server-watcher-autostart FAIL row flips to PASS when re-tested after this fix.\n- AC-4: New test fixture exercises the repo-root resolution priority order.\n\nVersion bump: ralph-overview v2.4.1 patch. Multi-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-implement-with-ralph-copilot-mirror-regenerate","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T20:38:00Z","mergeCommit":"c183326a","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-implement-with-ralph-copilot-mirror-regenerate","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit</code> (f4d63067) — Batch 3 follow-up. The Copilot mirror of <code>implement-with-ralph</code> SKILL.md still carries stale shell-shim guidance from before the v5.46.0 all-Node migration. Operators following the mirror's prose hit dead instructions referring to <code>bash codex-exec.sh</code> etc. Regenerate the mirror via <code>plugins/ralph/scripts/generate-copilot-artifacts.mjs --write</code>, or hand-edit if the generator doesn't cover this file.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Regenerate the implement-with-ralph Copilot skill mirror to drop stale .sh prose.\n\n## Problem\n\nAudit plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit (f4d63067) row 'ralph: copilot-skill-mirror-parity' FAIL:\n\n> Stale Copilot mirror prose can mislead operators during migration.\n\nThe v5.46.0 all-Node migration deleted codex-exec.sh + copilot-exec.sh bash shims but the implement-with-ralph Copilot mirror at plugins/ralph/.copilot-plugin/copilot-skills/implement-with-ralph/SKILL.md still references them in prose. Operators copying the mirror's example commands will hit 'no such file or directory'.\n\n## Fix\n\n1. Run plugins/ralph/scripts/generate-copilot-artifacts.mjs --write to regenerate from the canonical source.\n2. If the generator doesn't fully regenerate this mirror (it's flagged as a 'hand-fork' in the v5.46.0 CHANGELOG), hand-edit to drop bash prose and reference the .mjs ports instead.\n3. Verify by grepping the regenerated mirror for 'codex-exec.sh' / 'copilot-exec.sh' / 'bash <plugin>' — should be zero matches.\n4. Add a test or check-script that prevents future drift (similar to the audit's slash-commands-copilot check).\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. plugins/ralph/.copilot-plugin/copilot-skills/implement-with-ralph/SKILL.md — regenerated.\n2. plugins/ralph/scripts/generate-copilot-artifacts.mjs — if hand-fork pattern needs to change to auto-regenerable.\n3. plugins/ralph/tests/test-copilot-generator.mjs (or similar) — add staleness check.\n4. CHANGELOG entry + v5.46.x patch bump.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Regenerated mirror has zero references to .sh shims.\n- generator --check passes without manual fixup.\n- Audit's copilot-skill-mirror-parity FAIL row flips to PASS when re-tested.\n\nMulti-remote push.\""}}},{"id":"plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit","scope":"ralph-overview|crews|ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-28T15:47:00Z","mergeCommit":"f4d63067","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit","descriptionHtml":"Audit + verify that the 3 codexu-touching plugins (<code>crews</code> v1.7.1, <code>ralph</code> v5.46.0, <code>ralph-overview</code> current) work end-to-end under copilot, not just claude-code. Recent shipped fixes (v1.6.2 listener-arm gate, v5.45.1 shell:true) are landed in main; need to verify they fire correctly in copilot hooks too. Hook semantics (Stop, PreToolUse, PostToolUse) MAY differ slightly between engines — surface any gaps as separate plugin-fix tasks. Sister task to <code>ralph-overview-copilot-mirror</code> (which addresses the missing mirror).","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Investigation-heavy. Spawn smoke test members under copilot engine and validate: (a) Stop-hook listener-armed gate (crews v1.6.2) fires; (b) PostToolUse mid-turn review-required nag (crews v1.6.0) fires; (c) PreToolUse first-arm block fires; (d) ralph v5.45.1 codex/copilot CLI invocation succeeds on Windows; (e) ralph v5.46.0 all-Node migration didn't break copilot wrappers; (f) sessionId pointer index (v1.7.1) survives copilot SessionStart. File any gaps as separate plugin-fix tasks; do not in-line-fix here."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Audit and verify cross-engine parity for crews v1.7.1, ralph v5.46.0, and ralph-overview (current) under copilot, not just claude-code.\n\n## Why\n\nSeveral fixes shipped today and recently are claude-code-validated only:\n- crews v1.6.2 — Stop-hook listener-armed gate. Validated against claude-code Stop hook.\n- crews v1.6.0 — PostToolUse mid-turn review-required nag. Same.\n- ralph v5.45.1 — codex/copilot CLI shell:true on Windows. Same.\n- ralph v5.46.0 — all-Node migration with copilot mirror regenerated. Claims copilot parity; not verified end-to-end.\n- crews v1.7.1 — sessionId pointer index, mentioned COPILOT_AGENT_SESSION_ID support. Not e2e verified.\n\nOperator wants to migrate the bookkeeper lead session from claude-code to copilot — need confidence the coordination layer holds.\n\n## Audit scope\n\nFor EACH of the 3 plugins, verify under copilot engine:\n\n### crews v1.7.1\n- Spawn-member with --engine copilot creates a launcher that fires the copilot session.\n- SessionStart hook fires + reads COPILOT_AGENT_SESSION_ID correctly.\n- PreToolUse hook (must arm listener) blocks first tool call.\n- Stop hook (v1.6.2 listener-armed gate) fires when listener not armed + correct kind tag.\n- PostToolUse hook (v1.6.0 mid-turn nag) fires after 30s of unreviewed mail.\n- /review-mail, /send-to-member, /stop-member, etc. work via copilot's slash-command equivalent.\n- The kind tag <|report kind=...|> in member's prose is parsed.\n- v1.7.1 sessionId pointer survives engine restart.\n\n### ralph v5.46.0\n- /plan-with-ralph spawn works from copilot.\n- /implement-with-ralph spawn works.\n- src/codex-exec.mjs spawn(node) with shell:true (v5.45.1 fix) succeeds.\n- src/copilot-exec.mjs spawn(node) with shell:true succeeds.\n- Phase 4 + Phase 5 (review-changes) subagents work.\n- The all-Node migration's 30+ caller-site flips don't break copilot.\n\n### ralph-overview (current — pending copilot mirror task)\n- DEPENDS ON ralph-overview-copilot-mirror landing first. Audit happens after.\n- MCP tools (overview_parallel_ready_tasks etc.) callable from copilot.\n- watcher auto-start under copilot dev mode.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n.ralph/jobs/plugins-copilot-cross-engine-audit/audit-report.md with:\n- One section per plugin, one subsection per verified behavior.\n- PASS / FAIL / NOT-TESTED per check.\n- For each FAIL: file:line of the broken behavior + suggested follow-up task.\n- A summary table: 'safe to use under copilot' vs 'verified gaps blocking copilot use'.\n- Recommendations for the bookkeeper-lead engine migration (claude-code → copilot or claude-code-only-for-lead-with-copilot-members).\n\n## Workflow\n\nPlan-direct (well-defined audit scope). Decomposes into 3 stories (one per plugin) + 1 audit-report-aggregation story.\n\nNOT a fix task. Surface gaps as separate tasks; do not in-line-fix here.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-orchestration-followup-task-gatherer","scope":"ralph-orchestration","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"35ef177c","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T21:33:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-orchestration-followup-task-gatherer","descriptionHtml":"Add a Phase 5.5 retrospective subagent — sibling to <code>DSAT-analyst</code> and <code>skill-suggester</code> — that captures NEW follow-up tasks emerging from a ralph impl run. Inputs: iteration log, Phase 5a/5b findings (especially wont_fix + deferred), notepad.md Open Questions, and the diff against base_branch. Output: structured JSON at <code>.ralph/jobs/&lt;id&gt;/followup-suggestions.json</code> with proposed task entries (id, scope, lifecycle: tracked, prompts.{plan|brainstorm}, rationale, sourceFinding). The lead/bookkeeper consumes the suggestions when shipping the task to backlog-grow without losing surfaced threads. Mirrors today's manual bookkeeper pattern: after each ship we surface 'should we add follow-up tasks?' — automate it.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Lives in <code>ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/agents/</code> (or wherever DSAT-analyst lives). Phase 5.5 already runs DSAT + skill-suggester in parallel — add this as a third agent in the same parallel batch. Suggested tasks are advisory only — bookkeeper reviews + edits before adding to <code>.ralph-overview/data.json</code>. Do NOT auto-write to .ralph-overview/data.json; suggestion is operator-gated to preserve hand-curated invariant."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add a Phase 5.5 retrospective subagent that gathers follow-up task suggestions from a ralph impl run. Sibling to DSAT-analyst + skill-suggester (Phase 5.5 currently spawns those in parallel for post-impl retrospective).\n\n## Motivation\n\nObserved 2026-05-27 in the overview-bookkeeper session: after every impl ship, the bookkeeper manually scans the iteration artifacts for 'are there follow-up tasks worth tracking?' Today the bookkeeper found 3 such tasks from the just-shipped overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema + crews v1.6.2 work: (1) ralph-overview watcher parse-overview-data.mjs two-statement-preamble limitation (Phase 5b F-002 wont_fix); (2) 3 pre-existing Windows test-isolation failures in crews tests (documented in notepad.md but not captured as a task); (3) cross-platform smoke for v5.45.1's shell:true fix (deferred to Mac/Linux verification).\n\nThis manual scan is high-leverage but easy to forget. Automating it via a Phase 5.5 retrospective agent ensures every ship surfaces its loose ends.\n\n## Design\n\n### Agent location + naming\n\n- Lives at `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/agents/followup-task-gatherer/SKILL.md` (or wherever DSAT-analyst.md + skill-suggester.md live — match the existing convention).\n- Agent name: `followup-task-gatherer` (singular, matches existing kebab-case pattern).\n- Tools: read-only agent — Read, Grep, Glob. Does NOT write to disk except its single output artifact.\n\n### Inputs the agent reads\n\n1. `.ralph/jobs/<id>/job-state.json` — phase/story state.\n2. `.ralph/jobs/<id>/code-review-findings.json` + `docs-review-findings.json` — focus on `status: wont_fix` and `status: deferred` entries.\n3. `.ralph/jobs/<id>/notepad.md` — Open Questions section + Deferred Questions table.\n4. `.ralph/jobs/<id>/plan.md` — Open Questions for the planner section.\n5. `git diff <base_branch>..HEAD --name-only` — high-level file list of what changed.\n6. `git log <base_branch>..HEAD --format='%h %s'` — commit summaries.\n\n### Output artifact\n\n`.ralph/jobs/<id>/followup-suggestions.json` with schema:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"generatedAt\": \"2026-05-27T...\",\n  \"jobSlug\": \"...\",\n  \"suggestions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"suggested-task-slug\",\n      \"scope\": \"ralph-overview | crews | codexu | codex | etc\",\n      \"lifecycle\": \"tracked\",\n      \"rationale\": \"1-3 sentence why this is worth tracking\",\n      \"sourceFinding\": {\n        \"type\": \"wont_fix_finding | deferred_finding | open_question | notepad_entry | observed_gap\",\n        \"reference\": \"F-002 (Phase 5b docs review) / notepad.md line 47 / plan.md Open Questions #3\",\n        \"excerpt\": \"verbatim quote of the source\"\n      },\n      \"suggestedPrompts\": {\n        \"brainstorm\": \"...\",  // if fuzzy\n        \"plan\": \"...\"          // if concrete\n      },\n      \"recommendedPhase\": \"brainstorm | plan\",\n      \"estimatedSize\": \"S | M | L\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\"other-task-id\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### Phase 5.5 integration\n\nIn `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/skills/implement-with-ralph/SKILL.md` (or whatever file currently spawns DSAT + skill-suggester):\n\n- Find the Phase 5.5 step that launches `DSAT-analyst` + `skill-suggester` in parallel.\n- Add `followup-task-gatherer` to the same parallel batch (3 agents total, all read-only, all writing their own .json artifact).\n- Same retrospective semantics: Phase 5.5 is post-impl, doesn't gate kind=done, doesn't block Phase 6.\n\n### Operator-gated, NOT auto-write\n\n**CRITICAL invariant:** the agent writes to `.ralph/jobs/<id>/followup-suggestions.json` ONLY. It does NOT write to `.ralph-overview/data.json`. The bookkeeper lead reviews the suggestions when shipping and decides which to add. This preserves the 'hand-curated invariant' on .ralph-overview/data.json (per the codexu CLAUDE.md and the upcoming overview-data-ship-manifest task design).\n\nOptional v2: an MCP tool `overview.suggest_followups({jobId})` reads the .json + lets the bookkeeper bulk-add via an operator-confirm flow. Out of scope for v1.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n1. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/agents/followup-task-gatherer/SKILL.md` — new agent (mirror DSAT-analyst structure).\n2. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/skills/implement-with-ralph/SKILL.md` — Phase 5.5 batch addition.\n3. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/schemas/followup-suggestions-schema.json` — JSON schema for the output artifact.\n4. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/CHANGELOG.md` + version bump (v5.46.0 — minor since it's an additive feature, no API break).\n5. `ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-orchestration/package.json` + `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` version bumps.\n6. Test fixtures under `tests/` if the plugin has them.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- AC-1: After Phase 5.5 retrospective on a test impl run, `.ralph/jobs/<id>/followup-suggestions.json` exists and validates against the schema.\n- AC-2: When the impl has zero wont_fix/deferred findings AND zero notepad Open Questions, `suggestions[]` is empty (no false positives).\n- AC-3: When the impl has a Phase 5b wont_fix finding (sample fixture), `suggestions[]` contains at least one entry with `sourceFinding.type = 'wont_fix_finding'` and `sourceFinding.reference` pointing at the finding ID.\n- AC-4: When notepad.md Open Questions section contains an unanswered question, it appears as a suggestion with `sourceFinding.type = 'open_question'`.\n- AC-5: DSAT-analyst + skill-suggester continue to run unchanged (regression check).\n- AC-6: Phase 5.5 completes within +2 minutes of pre-feature baseline (the third agent runs in parallel; shouldn't extend wall time).\n- AC-7: `followup-suggestions.json` is gitignored at the .ralph/jobs/ level OR is intentionally tracked — the plan decides which. Default suggestion: track it (history is useful).\n\n## Workflow\n\nConcrete enough to skip /brainstorm-with-ralph. Run /plan-with-ralph directly. Probably decomposes into 3-4 stories (agent prompt + schema + Phase 5.5 integration + tests).\n\nVersion bump: v5.46.0 (minor) — additive retrospective agent, no breaking change. Multi-remote push (Evyatar108 + gim-home).\""}}},{"id":"codex-local-build","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-27T07:55:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-local-build","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Brainstorm-first.</strong> Make the codex submodule's Rust workspace compile on this Windows dev box (and on any contributor's machine) so impl members can verify <code>cargo build --release</code> green before pushing — eliminating today's reliance on &quot;CI is truth&quot; (per <code>feedback_codex_fork_no_local_cargo</code>). Today the only path to a clean codex build is the <code>/publish-sandbox-patch</code> release flow which installs xwin + LLVM + V8 lib on-demand for a release cut. Daily iteration defers verification to CI on push. This is a real safety gap: impl members can ship broken Rust changes that look fine in review but fail the publish workflow on tag, and the only feedback loop is a tagged release run.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Investigation-heavy. The brainstorm must first REPRODUCE the build failure (capture actual <code>cargo build --release</code> errors) and only THEN reason about fixes. Don't pre-commit to a solution before knowing which crates fail and why — likely suspects are V8/boa, windows-rs targets, OpenSSL, snmalloc, but verify. Cross-cuts: codex/.claude/commands/publish-sandbox-patch.md (already does the heavy install for releases — leverage or replicate?), codex's Cargo.toml feature flags, and the Windows MSVC toolchain expectations."}],"initialStage":"brainstorming","prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"How do we make the codex submodule's Rust workspace compile cleanly on this Windows dev box (and on any contributor's machine) so impl members can verify before push, instead of deferring to CI on tag-push?\n\n## Why this matters\n\nToday's reality (per auto-memory feedback_codex_fork_no_local_cargo):\n- Local cargo is unavailable on this Windows machine.\n- The publish path (codex/.claude/commands/publish-sandbox-patch.md) installs xwin + LLVM + V8 lib + other heavy deps ON-DEMAND for release cuts.\n- Daily impl iteration pushes to gim-home/codex and lets .github/workflows/invariant-check.yml verify.\n\nThis is a real safety gap. The impl-codex-sandbox-setup-release member (just shipped) made 8 file changes to codex including publish-npm.yml and got Phase 5a code-review consensus 'looks fine' — but no compile happened. If the cargo build line in the workflow has a typo or the bin name is slightly off, the failure surfaces only when a release tag triggers CI, possibly days later, with no clear assignment to the impl session that introduced it.\n\nWe want: impl members can run `cargo check` or `cargo build --release` locally as part of Phase 5a, catch compile errors before pushing, and only fall back to CI for the end-to-end packaging verification.\n\n## What we have today\n\n- Windows 11 Enterprise dev box.\n- Rust toolchain status: unknown — the brainstorm MUST verify (run `rustup show` and check what's installed).\n- codex submodule Cargo workspace: monorepo with ~30+ crates including codex (main bin), codex-core, codex-windows-sandbox-rs, codex-command-runner, and various sandbox/auth/plugin crates.\n- Known heavy dependencies (from publish-sandbox-patch.md): xwin (cross-compile shim for MSVC headers), LLVM (clang for some bindgen consumers), V8 lib (for the boa-engine or rusty_v8 JS interpreter inside codex's plugin/expression layer).\n- Existing build infrastructure: .github/workflows/publish-npm.yml does the full Windows release build on `windows-latest` runners.\n- CI invariant: .github/workflows/invariant-check.yml runs on push.\n\n## Investigation step (mandatory before recommending)\n\nBefore any solution evaluation, the brainstorm member must:\n\n1. **Reproduce the failure.** Run `cd D:/harness-efforts/codexu/codex && cargo build --release --bin codex 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codex-build-attempt.log`. Capture the FIRST error (subsequent errors often cascade from the first).\n2. **Categorize the failure** by error class:\n   - Missing native lib (linker error)?\n   - Missing system header (compile error in -sys crate)?\n   - Missing Rust toolchain feature (e.g., wasm32 target, MSVC target)?\n   - Missing tool (clang, llvm-config, lld)?\n   - Cargo registry/network issue (unlikely but check)?\n3. **Identify the failing crate(s).** Read the error context to determine which crate triggered the failure. Trace back through Cargo.toml to see which feature flag or platform-specific dep introduced it.\n4. **Check what publish-sandbox-patch.md installs.** That command is the canonical 'install everything needed for a release build.' Whatever it installs is by definition the answer to 'what's missing locally.' Reading that command's contents tells us 80% of the dependency map.\n\n## Approaches to lens against (after step 1)\n\n### A — Run publish-sandbox-patch's install steps once, then iterate normally\n\nIf publish-sandbox-patch.md already enumerates the install (xwin + LLVM + V8 + ...), run those steps ONCE on the dev machine. Subsequent cargo builds reuse the installed toolchain. Document the one-time install as a new contributor-setup script.\n\nPros: zero code change; minimum effort; matches the established release path.\nCons: heavy install (~5-10 GB for V8 lib alone); slow first build; may pollute the machine; multi-machine setup recurs.\n\n### B — WSL2 dev environment\n\nMove codex builds into WSL2 (Ubuntu). The Linux toolchain is simpler — install rustc + clang + standard libs via apt; no xwin/MSVC weirdness. Build target stays x86_64-pc-windows-msvc via cross-compile, OR build native Linux for development and cross-compile only for release.\n\nPros: faster install; easier reproduce; WSL2 is well-supported on Windows 11.\nCons: file-system perf (WSL2 mounts Windows files slowly); requires WSL2 already installed; pushes contributors to a Linux mental model even though the prod target is Windows.\n\n### C — Devcontainer / GitHub Codespaces\n\nDefine a .devcontainer/devcontainer.json in codex with all build deps. Open codex in a Codespace or local devcontainer. Build inside the container.\n\nPros: reproducible across machines; zero local install footprint; CI-like dev environment.\nCons: requires Docker; networking lag; volume mounts add another perf layer.\n\n### D — Minimize the dependency surface\n\nAudit codex's Cargo.toml + feature flags. Identify deps that are only needed for runtime (V8, snmalloc?) and gate them behind features. Default-build = lean (no V8 etc.); release-build = full features. impl members do default-build for compile-check; CI does release-build for end-to-end verification.\n\nPros: long-term value; faster CI; smaller release artifacts.\nCons: invasive surgery on codex codebase; needs upstream codex coordination if any features become non-default in our fork; risk of feature-cfg bugs.\n\n### E — Prebuilt binary cache + cargo-binstall pattern\n\nFor heavy deps (V8 lib), cache the build artifact in S3/GitHub Releases. cargo-binstall fetches the prebuilt instead of building from source.\n\nPros: skip the heaviest 80% of build time; viable if V8 is the actual bottleneck.\nCons: requires binary-cache infrastructure; not idiomatic Rust; brittle for security-sensitive crates.\n\n### F — Just-in-time install (current state, but documented)\n\nKeep the current model (CI is truth), but invest in: (1) faster CI feedback loop (push-to-pr-status in minutes), (2) better impl-member tooling that detects 'this change touches Rust' and prompts the operator to run /publish-sandbox-patch's verify mode before merging, (3) a 'cargo check on push' GitHub Actions workflow that runs in <2 minutes.\n\nPros: matches established workflow; minimum investment.\nCons: doesn't actually solve the safety gap; just makes the gap less risky.\n\n### G — Devil's-advocate: don't build locally at all; trust CI\n\nIs the safety gap real? How often does an impl member's Rust change actually break the build vs pass review + CI cleanly? Maybe the cost of local-build infra exceeds the cost of the occasional CI revert.\n\nMust be pressure-tested: count the number of Rust changes in the last N codex commits, count how many failed CI on first push, compute the actual-failure rate.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nAt the end of brainstorm Phase 5 (finalize), produce a `selected-direction.md` in `.ralph/brainstorms/codex-local-build/` capturing:\n\n- The actual reproduced build error (copy-paste from cargo output) — grounds the rest of the analysis.\n- Which crates / features are the actual bottleneck.\n- Recommended approach (one of A-G or hybrid).\n- Concrete next steps (one-time setup commands, doc edits, CI workflow additions, feature-flag changes — whatever the chosen approach needs).\n- Acceptance criteria for the eventual impl (e.g., 'after setup, cargo build --release --bin codex completes in <X minutes on a fresh checkout').\n- Open questions for operator that the planner should ask.\n\nDo NOT chain into /plan-with-ralph. The bookkeeper spawns a fresh plan-codex-local-build member from the selected-direction.md per phase discipline.\n\n## Reference reading + commands to run\n\n- `codex/.claude/commands/publish-sandbox-patch.md` — the canonical install path; READ FIRST.\n- `codex/.github/workflows/publish-npm.yml` — the CI build; what does it run before cargo build?\n- `codex/.github/workflows/invariant-check.yml` — the per-push CI; mirror this for local cargo check?\n- `codex/Cargo.toml` (workspace) + per-crate Cargo.toml files — feature flags + heavy deps.\n- `rustup show` to verify toolchain state.\n- `cargo metadata --manifest-path codex/Cargo.toml --format-version 1 | jq '.packages[] | select(.dependencies[] | .source == null and .kind != \\\"dev\\\")'` — inspect non-registry dependencies.\n\nLenses to deploy: feasibility (codex — actually try to run the build commands), product-reality (copilot — 'how often does the safety gap actually bite?'), simplification (does the chosen approach add more setup complexity than the gap costs?), devil's-advocate (is this premature optimization vs accepting the CI-is-truth model?). Plus a Claude general-purpose lens running the actual build attempt + dependency audit to produce the dependency map with file:line citations.\""}}},{"id":"ai-developer-toolkit-submodule","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[{"className":"cmd-info","html":"<strong>Plan shipped 2026-06-01</strong> (commit <code>ff08932f</code>). 7 user stories / 4 phases. Multi-model review: 13 findings, 7 fixed (1 Critical + 6 Highs), 6 Mediums in Open Questions."},{"className":"cmd-info","html":"<strong>Operator preflight approval recorded 2026-06-02T14:21Z</strong> (resolves US-001 pre-implementation gate): (1) <strong>Remote-sync DONE</strong> -- all 3 ai-developer-toolkit remotes now at d7e01874 (origin / personal FF-synced up to gim-home HEAD which was 1 commit AHEAD with crews v3.0.1 fix, not behind as the plan member assumed). Verified via git ls-remote on all 3 remotes. (2) <strong>Pin SHA selected: d7e01874385c13e7e833a6935d7de11ea2e565f7</strong> -- the now-aligned HEAD across all 3 remotes. (3) <strong>OVERRIDE: source remote URL = evmitran_microsoft, NOT gim-home.</strong> Impl must use <code>git submodule add https://github.com/evmitran_microsoft/ai-developer-toolkit.git ai-developer-toolkit</code> and the CI invariant script + AC #135 must verify that URL, not the gim-home URL. Rationale: evmitran_microsoft is the canonical source for codexu (its own origin remote points there); using the same org for the toolkit submodule keeps source-of-truth consistent."},{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"<strong>Impl member spawn-prompt MUST include the operator-decision-replay above</strong>: the plan.md still references gim-home throughout (26 hits in AC #135, US-001 submodule-add command, CI invariant verification, risk-mitigation prose). Impl must treat the kanban override as authoritative -- replace gim-home with evmitran_microsoft in (a) <code>.gitmodules url</code>, (b) AC #135 verification text, (c) <code>tools/check-toolkit-submodule-invariants.mjs</code> URL check, (d) any AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md cross-refs introduced. The \"gim-home is INTERNAL/private + GIM_HOME_PAT\" CI-auth subsection is now OBSOLETE -- evmitran_microsoft is also private but uses the existing repo CI credentials; CI invariant stays metadata-only by default."}],"command":{"name":"ai-developer-toolkit-submodule","descriptionHtml":"Integrate <code>ai-developer-toolkit</code> as a git submodule of codexu, mirroring the existing <code>codex/</code> submodule pattern. Today the toolkit lives at <code>D:/ai-developer-toolkit</code> (sibling repo, two remotes: Evyatar108 + gim-home) and is referenced from codexu via absolute paths in spawn-launcher scripts, CLAUDE.md instructions, bookkeeper memory, plan documents, and ad-hoc Bash commands. This is fragile (breaks on a different machine layout) and prevents codexu from version-pinning the toolkit commit it depends on. Add as submodule at <code>./ai-developer-toolkit/</code>, migrate absolute path references to relative, and add a CI invariant that the submodule pointer + toolkit's ralph-overview / crews plugin versions stay in sync with codexu's CLAUDE.md doc references.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>HIGH-COORDINATION change.</strong> Migration touches: (a) new submodule pointer commit; (b) <code>D:/ai-developer-toolkit/</code> → <code>./ai-developer-toolkit/</code> path rewrite across 30+ files (spawn-launchers, CLAUDE.md, codexu-roadmap.md, memory entries, plan documents, MCP server registration); (c) crew spawn pattern (members may need <code>--cwd</code> updates); (d) MCP server path in user <code>.claude/settings.json</code> may need update; (e) submodule URL: pick <strong>gim-home</strong> per codex/ precedent (codex submodule uses <code>https://github.com/gim-home/codex.git</code>). <strong>Land BEFORE the 4 overview-data-* tasks</strong> — every one of those references ai-developer-toolkit paths in their planPrompts; if we rebrand or restructure those after this lands, the path-rewrite is automatic. <strong>Or land LAST</strong> if the in-flight tasks need stable paths during their impl. Operator decides sequencing during plan phase. Must NOT touch ai-developer-toolkit's own git history — submodule add is one-way from codexu's perspective."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Integrate ai-developer-toolkit as a git submodule of codexu. Today the toolkit lives at ./ai-developer-toolkit as a sibling repo with two remotes (Evyatar108 origin + gim-home work). Codexu references it via absolute paths in spawn-launcher scripts, CLAUDE.md instructions, bookkeeper memory, plan documents, and ad-hoc commands. This is fragile (breaks on a different machine layout) and prevents codexu from version-pinning the toolkit commit it depends on.\n\n## Target structure\n\n```\nD:/harness-efforts/codexu/\n├── .gitmodules                  # add new submodule entry\n├── ai-developer-toolkit/        # NEW submodule (was: ./ai-developer-toolkit/)\n│   ├── plugins/\n│   │   ├── ralph-overview/\n│   │   ├── crews/\n│   │   └── ralph-orchestration/\n│   └── ...\n├── codex/                       # existing submodule (gim-home/codex)\n├── plans/\n├── tools/\n├── packages/\n└── ...\n```\n\n## Submodule remote URL\n\nUse `https://github.com/gim-home/ai-developer-toolkit.git` per the codex/ submodule precedent (already uses gim-home). The local checkout's `origin` (Evyatar108) becomes a non-default remote that the operator adds manually for the push-to-both convention.\n\n## Migration steps\n\n### Pre-flight\n\n1. **Verify upstream toolkit state.** Confirm both Evyatar108/ai-developer-toolkit and gim-home/ai-developer-toolkit point at the same main HEAD (the last merge commit was `e65e8161` per recent bookkeeping). If they diverge, sync first.\n2. **Snapshot the current absolute paths.** Run `grep -rn './ai-developer-toolkit\\|D:\\\\ai-developer-toolkit' D:/harness-efforts/codexu/ --include='*.md' --include='*.js' --include='*.mjs' --include='*.ts' --include='*.tsx' --include='*.ps1' --include='*.json' --include='*.txt'` to enumerate every reference. Save the list.\n3. **Check the user .claude/settings.json MCP server registration.** If `mcpServers.ralph-overview` references `./ai-developer-toolkit/` directly (vs the cached plugin location), it'll need update — but verify this is in scope; the published-plugin cache at `C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/plugins/cache/ai-developer-toolkit/ralph-overview/<ver>/` is separate from a dev-mode source reference.\n\n### Submodule add\n\n4. **Add submodule from inside codexu.** \n   ```\n   cd D:/harness-efforts/codexu\n   git submodule add https://github.com/gim-home/ai-developer-toolkit.git ai-developer-toolkit\n   git submodule update --init\n   ```\n   This creates `./ai-developer-toolkit/` checkout, adds `.gitmodules` entry, and stages a submodule pointer commit at the current main HEAD.\n\n5. **Add Evyatar108 remote to the local submodule checkout.** Without this, the operator can't push to the personal fork.\n   ```\n   cd ai-developer-toolkit\n   git remote add evyatar108 https://github.com/Evyatar108/ai-developer-toolkit.git\n   cd ..\n   ```\n\n6. **Commit the submodule pointer.** Single commit in codexu: `chore: add ai-developer-toolkit submodule at <SHA>`.\n\n### Path rewrite\n\n7. **Rewrite the absolute paths captured in step 2.** For each file in the snapshot:\n   - `./ai-developer-toolkit/...` → `./ai-developer-toolkit/...` (or relative to file location: `../ai-developer-toolkit/...` if needed).\n   - Backslash variants: `D:\\\\ai-developer-toolkit\\\\...` → forward-slash relative form.\n   - Spawn launchers under `.crews/spawn-launchers/*.ps1` contain `Set-Location './ai-developer-toolkit'`-style lines that pin the toolkit absolute path — update to `Set-Location '$PSScriptRoot/../../ai-developer-toolkit'` or equivalent.\n   - DO NOT rewrite paths inside `ai-developer-toolkit/` itself (those are the toolkit's own repo content, not codexu's).\n   - DO NOT rewrite paths inside cached plugin locations at `C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/plugins/cache/` (those are the published artifact, not source).\n   - DO update bookkeeper memory entries at `C:/Users/evmitran/.claude/projects/D--harness-efforts-codexu/memory/*.md` — these are codexu-scoped memories and reference toolkit paths.\n\n8. **Verify each rewrite still works.** Particular care for:\n   - `.crews/spawn-launchers/*.ps1` — these are auto-generated by crews plugin spawnMember; check that the next spawn-member call produces a launcher with the relative path.\n   - `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/CLAUDE.md` (untracked) — update the bookkeeper operating manual.\n   - `plans/codexu-roadmap.md` — update doc references.\n   - In-flight task plan.md files under `.ralph/jobs/*/plan.md` — leave as-is (those are in-flight committed state).\n\n### Crew spawn pattern\n\n9. **Spawn-member command in CLAUDE.md.** The current invocation is:\n   ```\n   node <plugin>/tools/crews.js spawn-member <name> --crew ralph-pipeline --cwd D:/harness-efforts/codexu --state-cwd D:/harness-efforts/codexu --as overview-bookkeeper -- <prompt>\n   ```\n   The `<plugin>` placeholder is the cached plugin path. With submodule integration, dev-mode (uncached) spawn-member calls could use `./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/crews/tools/crews.js`. Update the CLAUDE.md guidance to clarify when to use cached-plugin path vs in-tree submodule path — likely the cached path stays canonical for production use, while the submodule path is for dev-mode editing.\n\n10. **Verify member auto-resolution.** Spawned members rely on `--state-cwd` to find `.crews/`. Confirm members work with the submodule-integrated layout.\n\n### CI invariant\n\n11. **Add invariant check.** A pre-commit hook or CI workflow that checks codexu's CLAUDE.md / docs references to ralph-overview / crews plugin versions match the in-tree submodule version. Prevents drift between 'doc says version X' and 'submodule has version Y'.\n   - Specifically: `D:/harness-efforts/codexu/CLAUDE.md` mentions crews v1.6.0, ralph-overview v2.1.0. After submodule integration, the version is whatever's at `./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/<plugin>/package.json` or `plugin.json`. CI lints for the version-string match.\n\n### Bookkeeper memory updates\n\n12. **Update bookkeeper auto-memory:**\n   - `feedback_cross_repo_impl_worktree_mandate.md` — describes 'sibling repo' cross-repo coordination. With ai-developer-toolkit as a submodule, the 'sibling' framing changes. Update to reflect that ai-developer-toolkit lives INSIDE codexu now, and worktree mandates are scoped accordingly.\n   - `feedback_crews_spawn_state_cwd_override.md` — the --state-cwd flag rule. Update example paths.\n   - `feedback_plan_member_branch_cherry_pick.md` — if the cherry-pick worktree pattern changes for submodule edits, update.\n   - Any memory referencing `./ai-developer-toolkit/...` — sweep + update.\n\n### Acceptance\n\n- `.gitmodules` lists ai-developer-toolkit submodule at `./ai-developer-toolkit/` with gim-home URL.\n- `git submodule update --init` brings the toolkit in cleanly.\n- Zero `./ai-developer-toolkit` or `D:\\\\ai-developer-toolkit` references remain in tracked codexu files (excluding .ralph/jobs/*/plan.md historical artifacts and the cached plugin location).\n- Crew spawn-member works with the new layout (verify by spawning a test member).\n- MCP server registration unchanged (cache path still works) OR updated if dev-mode needs in-tree path.\n- Bookkeeper docs (CLAUDE.md, codexu-roadmap.md) describe the submodule integration; spawn-member examples use relative paths or the cached path with a note about the new option.\n- 4+ bookkeeper memory entries updated.\n- CI invariant check passes (versions match).\n- ai-developer-toolkit's own git history is untouched (no commits made inside the submodule as part of this task).\n\n## Sequencing relative to other in-flight work\n\nThe 4 overview-data-* tasks (dynamic-stages-schema, ship-manifest, context-scalability, naming-rebrand) all reference ./ai-developer-toolkit paths in their planPrompts. Two options the planner should evaluate:\n\n- **Land submodule FIRST** — then the 4 overview-data-* tasks naturally use relative paths in any new content they write. Path rewrite churn is concentrated in this task.\n- **Land submodule LAST** — keep absolute paths stable while the in-flight tasks finish. Single sweep at the end.\n\nLikely answer: depends on impl-overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema status. If it's not yet spawned, do submodule FIRST (cleaner). If it's mid-flight, do submodule LAST. Surface this decision to operator during plan phase.\n\n## Workflow\n\nConcrete enough to skip /brainstorm-with-ralph (operator has chosen 'submodule' approach). Run /plan-with-ralph directly. Probably decomposes into 4-6 user stories (submodule add + path rewrite + spawn launcher pattern + memory update + CI invariant + docs).\n\nNo version bump required for codexu (it's a structural change, not a feature release). Single PR to main. ai-developer-toolkit itself untouched.\"","impl":"/implement-with-ralph --from-plan .ralph/jobs/ai-developer-toolkit-submodule/plan.md --parallel --suggested-decomposition .ralph/jobs/ai-developer-toolkit-submodule/suggested-decomposition.json --autonomous"}}},{"id":"overview-naming-rebrand","scope":"codexu|ralph-overview","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"overview-naming-rebrand","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Brainstorm-first.</strong> Rename the &quot;overview&quot; concept (and all its derivatives) to something more descriptive. Candidates include <code>roadmap</code>, <code>board</code>, <code>tracker</code>, <code>pipeline</code>, <code>workspace</code>, <code>catalog</code>, <code>atlas</code>. The word &quot;overview&quot; is too generic — it doesn't convey what the system actually is (a hand-curated task roadmap with watcher-derived pipeline state). The rename touches ~30+ surfaces: file names (.ralph-overview/data.json, overview-snapshot.json, overview-ralph-state.{js,json}, overview-recommendations.json, overview-activity.jsonl, overview-dependency-graph.json, overview.html), packages (tools/overview-viewer/, tools/overview-mcp/), the <code>ralph-overview</code> plugin name in ai-developer-toolkit, MCP tool names (<code>overview_parallel_ready_tasks</code>, etc.), MCP server prefix (<code>mcp__ralph-overview__*</code>), bookkeeper lead role (<code>overview-bookkeeper</code>), TypeScript types (<code>OverviewTask</code>, <code>OverviewData</code>, <code>OverviewRalphState</code>, <code>OverviewPipelineState</code>), npm scripts (<code>pnpm overview</code>, <code>sync-ralph-state</code>), and dozens of doc references across CLAUDE.md / README.md / roadmap.md. Largest-surface rename in the project.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>BRAINSTORM FIRST.</strong> Both name-choice AND scope-of-rename are open questions. Brainstorm lenses must cover: (1) which new name best captures what the system does; (2) whether to rename EVERYTHING or only the user-facing surfaces (file names, npm scripts) while leaving types/plugin name alone; (3) migration strategy (one-shot vs deprecation-alias). <strong>SHOULD BE THE LAST OF THE 4 overview-data-* TASKS TO LAND</strong> — depends on overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema + overview-data-ship-manifest + overview-data-context-scalability all shipping first, otherwise the rebrand churns through changes those tasks haven't merged yet. Coordinate with the existing crew name <code>ralph-pipeline</code> (collision risk if &quot;pipeline&quot; is chosen)."}],"initialStage":"brainstorming","prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"Rename the 'overview' concept across the project. The word is too generic — it doesn't tell a new contributor what the system actually IS. Candidates include roadmap, board, tracker, pipeline, workspace, catalog, atlas, registry. This brainstorm picks the name AND scopes the rename.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nWalk a fresh contributor into the codebase and ask 'what is .ralph-overview/data.json?' — the word 'overview' could mean anything (executive summary doc, system architecture diagram, project status report). The actual system is more specific: a hand-curated task roadmap (operator-authored intent: planPrompts, kanban cards, scope, descriptions) FUSED with watcher-derived runtime pipeline state (RalphStage, crewSessions, storyCompletion). The current name buries that meaning.\n\nDownstream cost of the bad name: every doc that introduces the system has to spend a sentence explaining 'when we say overview we mean...'. Every new agent prompt that needs to reference it has to disambiguate. Operator-facing language ('the overview', 'overview viewer', 'overview bookkeeper') reads as a placeholder, not a system name.\n\n## Naming candidates to lens against\n\nThe brainstorm should pressure-test at least these and synthesize any others that emerge:\n\n- **roadmap** — captures the planning aspect; downside: doesn't convey runtime/pipeline state (a roadmap is static); collides slightly with plans/codexu-roadmap.md which IS a static doc.\n- **board** — kanban-y; matches the existing kanban surface in TaskCommand; downside: too narrow (board implies the kanban view is primary, but the task list is primary).\n- **tracker** — generic but accurate; downside: bland; collides with 'issue tracker' associations.\n- **pipeline** — captures the flow brainstorm→plan→impl→ship; COLLIDES with crew name 'ralph-pipeline' — would force a crew rename or accept dual meaning.\n- **workspace** — captures the meta-aspect; downside: VS Code workspace collision; doesn't convey 'roadmap of tasks'.\n- **catalog** — formal; captures the curated aspect; downside: implies read-only/reference.\n- **atlas** — evocative; conveys 'map of work'; downside: opaque without context.\n- **registry** — too database-y.\n- **directory** — too filesystem-y.\n- **scoreboard** — too gamified.\n- **manifest** — collides with package.json / crew manifest.\n- **plan** — too generic; collides with /plan-with-ralph.\n- **agenda** — captures intent; downside: implies meeting agenda.\n- **dashboard** — too web-UI-specific; loses the data-layer meaning.\n\nLenses should consider hybrid options (e.g., the data layer is 'roadmap' but the visual surface stays 'dashboard'; or the plugin keeps 'overview' as a legacy name but new files/types use the new name).\n\n## Scope-of-rename axis (separate from name choice)\n\nThe brainstorm MUST address: rename EVERYTHING, or rename only some layers?\n\n1. **Full rename:** every surface (files, packages, plugin, types, MCP tools, bookkeeper role name, npm scripts, all docs). Maximum cost; cleanest end-state.\n2. **Surface-only rename:** rename user-facing names (file names, npm scripts, plugin display name, docs) but keep internal types (OverviewTask, OverviewData) for back-compat. Lower cost; the type names become 'historical' and confuse future readers — but maybe acceptable.\n3. **Plugin-package keeps old name, new files use new name:** the `ralph-overview` plugin stays named that (npm dependency cost of renaming a published package); files inside use the new name. Mixed signal but pragmatic.\n4. **Add new alias, deprecate old:** ship the new name as additive; old names work via aliases for 1 minor; drop old names in v+2. Most expensive (twice as much code touched) but minimizes consumer breakage.\n\n## Constraints / invariants\n\n1. **Crew name 'ralph-pipeline' is OUT OF SCOPE for renaming.** The crew is named that for the ralph-pipeline crew (separate concept). If 'pipeline' is chosen as the new overview name, find a way to disambiguate or pick another name.\n2. **MCP tool prefix is a published contract.** Renaming `mcp__ralph-overview__*` → `mcp__ralph-<new>__*` requires every consuming Claude Code config to update. Need a migration path.\n3. **plans/overview.html is bookmarked + linked externally.** A rename of the published static viewer needs a redirect/alias.\n4. **Single-coordinated PR.** This rebrand should ship as ONE PR per repo (codexu + ai-developer-toolkit) to avoid half-renamed state.\n5. **Must land AFTER overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema + overview-data-ship-manifest + overview-data-context-scalability.** Otherwise the rebrand churns through merge conflicts with those in-flight schema changes. Sequence the 4 tasks in dependency order.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nAt the end of brainstorm Phase 5 (finalize), produce a `selected-direction.md` in `.ralph/brainstorms/overview-naming-rebrand/` capturing:\n\n- Recommended new name (the brainstorm picks one).\n- Recommended scope (1-4 from above).\n- Migration strategy (one-shot rename PR vs aliased deprecation).\n- Comprehensive surface inventory: every file path, type name, MCP tool, script name, doc reference, role name, etc. that needs renaming under the recommended scope. Use grep to enumerate — DO NOT rely on memory of the surfaces above.\n- Per-surface effort estimate (file rename = 1 unit; type rename + all call sites = 5 units; etc.).\n- Total LoC estimate.\n- Risk areas (external consumers, bookmarks, MCP-server registration in user .claude/settings.json, published-package consumers).\n- Sequencing recommendation: when in the 4-task chain does this land (last per the warnings, but maybe a subset can land earlier).\n\nDo NOT chain into /plan-with-ralph. The bookkeeper spawns a fresh plan-overview-naming-rebrand member from the selected-direction.md per phase discipline.\n\n## Reference reading + grep targets\n\n- Run `grep -r overview D:/harness-efforts/codexu/plans/ D:/harness-efforts/codexu/CLAUDE.md D:/harness-efforts/codexu/AGENTS.md ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/ ./ai-developer-toolkit/CHANGELOG.md` to produce the actual surface inventory. Do NOT trust the lens-prompt's list; verify by grep.\n- `grep -r OverviewTask\\|OverviewData\\|OverviewRalphState\\|OverviewPipelineState ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/` for type-rename surface.\n- `grep -r overview_ ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/tools/overview-mcp/` for MCP tool names.\n- `grep -r 'pnpm overview\\|overview:build\\|overview:dev\\|sync-ralph-state' D:/harness-efforts/codexu/package.json ./ai-developer-toolkit/` for script-name surface.\n- Cross-reference plans/codexu-roadmap.md, CLAUDE.md, README.md files for prose mentions that will need updating.\n\nLenses to deploy: feasibility (codex), product-reality (copilot — 'will users actually adopt the new name, or will they keep saying overview?'), devil's-advocate (challenge: 'a project-wide rename is the most thankless work in software; is there a 5%-effort version that captures 80% of the value, like adding a tagline to docs without renaming anything?'), simplification (does the new name actually make the system easier to explain to a new contributor, or just shifts the explanation burden?), and a Claude general-purpose lens that runs the actual greps and produces the surface inventory with file:line citations so the brainstorm output is grounded in real numbers.\""}}},{"id":"overview-data-context-scalability","scope":"codexu|ralph-overview","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"overview-data-context-scalability","descriptionHtml":"<strong>Brainstorm-first.</strong> Today <code>.ralph-overview/data.json</code> is one monolithic file (~50 entries, growing). The overview-bookkeeper lead loads the entire file via CLAUDE.md auto-context every session, AND every <code>overview_parallel_ready_tasks</code> / snapshot read pulls the full tasks[]. At ~50 tasks this is fine; at 200-500 it becomes untenable — context budget exhausts before any work begins, and recommendation tools degrade. This task brainstorms how to break the data down so bookkeeper sessions read only what they need (lazy MCP, per-task files, archive separation, etc.) without losing the single-source-of-truth and atomic-edit properties the current single file provides.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ <strong>BRAINSTORM FIRST.</strong> Multiple competing approaches (per-task files vs lazy MCP tools vs compression vs archive split vs hybrid). Spawn <code>brainstorm-overview-data-context-scalability</code> with <code>/brainstorm-with-ralph</code> BEFORE planning. Touches the same surface as <code>overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema</code> + <code>overview-data-ship-manifest</code>; impl phase needs to land after those to avoid churn. <strong>Plan + impl phases depend on schema-rename + ship-manifest both shipping first.</strong>"}],"initialStage":"brainstorming","prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"How should we break down .ralph-overview/data.json so the overview-bookkeeper lead does NOT need to load the full task list into context every session, while preserving the bookkeeper's hand-edit ergonomics + the watcher's single-source-of-truth model?\n\n## Why this matters\n\nToday .ralph-overview/data.json is ONE monolithic ESM file with `window.OVERVIEW_DATA = { tasks: [...], runs: [...], workstream: {...}, ... }`. At ~50 tasks (current state), it's ~1200 lines / ~80KB. Every overview-bookkeeper Claude Code session auto-loads it via CLAUDE.md context. Every dashboard render parses + ships all tasks[] to the browser. Every MCP tool reads from the merged snapshot which contains the full list.\n\nProjected scale: codexu's roadmap has Phase 1-6 with sub-tasks that could push this to 200-500 task entries within a year (shipped + in-flight + planned + closed). At 200+ entries the context-load alone becomes a significant fraction of an Opus context window, and the bookkeeper's reasoning quality degrades as it scans irrelevant entries to find the few that are actionable.\n\n## Constraints / non-negotiable invariants\n\n1. **Hand-edit ergonomics.** The bookkeeper edits .ralph-overview/data.json by hand (Edit tool, sometimes in a worktree). Whatever split must NOT require a complex multi-file write transaction per task. Atomic per-task edits.\n2. **Single source of truth for an agent.** When an agent queries `overview.get_task(id)` or reads `overview-snapshot.json`, the result must be deterministic and capture the full per-task state. No N-file merge ambiguity.\n3. **Watcher remains the sole writer of generated artifacts.** sync-ralph-state.mjs only writes overview-ralph-state.{js,json} / overview-snapshot.json / etc. It NEVER writes the hand-curated source(s).\n4. **Static-build still produces a single self-contained overview.html.** The published artifact inlines whatever data the renderer needs; the split is invisible to end users.\n5. **Backwards compatibility for at least 1 minor.** The current single-file shape continues to work; the split is opt-in.\n\n## Candidate approaches to lens against\n\nThe brainstorm should pressure-test at least these candidate directions (the lens doesn't need to pick one — synthesizing a hybrid is fine):\n\n### A — Per-task files under tasks/\n\nOne JSON (or markdown-frontmatter) file per task at `tasks/<id>.json` or `tasks/<id>.md`. The bookkeeper edits one small file per task; sync-core walks the directory to assemble the snapshot. CLAUDE.md auto-loads only an INDEX (slug + title + status + lastTouchedAt — maybe 5-10 lines per task) instead of the full content. Bookkeeper reads full task detail via Read on demand.\n\nPros: minimal context auto-load; atomic per-task edits; git history per task is cleaner.\nCons: needs a stable assembly script; loss of inter-task locality (currently bookkeepers see related tasks together in the file); risk of file-name collisions; needs new ESM bootstrap for the renderer.\n\n### B — Lazy MCP tools\n\nKeep the single file but never auto-load it into CLAUDE.md context. The bookkeeper queries via MCP tools (`overview.list_tasks({filter})`, `overview.get_task({id})`) for only the tasks it needs in the current turn. The full data lives on disk but is invisible to the model unless asked.\n\nPros: zero file-system restructuring; the existing single-file ergonomics stay; immediately deployable via MCP tool additions.\nCons: requires discipline (model has to remember to query); silently breaks when the model assumes 'it's all in context'; doesn't help dashboard load weight.\n\n### C — Lifecycle-bucketed files\n\nSplit by lifecycle: plans/overview-data-active.js (tracked/planning/implementing) + plans/overview-data-shipped.js (merged) + plans/overview-data-archive.js (archived). Bookkeeper auto-loads only -active.js. Shipped/archived loaded on demand.\n\nPros: simple split; matches the 'I only need to see in-flight work today' use case; merging into one source is straightforward.\nCons: bookkeeper has to remember to flip a task across files at ship time (a manual atomic-rename); cross-file references (spawnedFrom, dependencies) need stable IDs.\n\n### D — Compression / summary projection in CLAUDE.md\n\nKeep one file but auto-load only a SUMMARY projection in CLAUDE.md context: per task, drop the planPrompt/prompts text (often the biggest field) and keep only metadata (id, scope, lifecycle, lastTouchedAt, mergeCommit, descriptionHtml first line, warnings summary). The bookkeeper reads the full .ralph-overview/data.json on demand if it needs prompt detail.\n\nPros: zero structural change; the projection is a single script that the CLAUDE.md `@/plans/overview-summary.md` directive auto-loads; preserves the single-file edit model.\nCons: the projection has to be kept in sync (watcher-generated artifact); doesn't help the dashboard's full-tasks payload; just defers the problem (still O(n) per session).\n\n### E — Hybrid: per-task files + assembled snapshot for renderer\n\nBookkeeper hand-edits tasks/<id>.json or tasks/<id>.md (cleanest format TBD); the watcher assembles `.ralph-overview/generated/snapshot.json` which the React app reads. The hand-curated single file `.ralph-overview/data.json` becomes derivable from the per-task files. CLAUDE.md loads only an INDEX.md (auto-regenerated by the watcher) listing slug → title → status → 1-line summary.\n\nPros: combines A + D's benefits; renderer is unchanged (still reads a single snapshot); CLAUDE.md context is bounded regardless of task count.\nCons: most work; needs a clean format for the per-task source (markdown + frontmatter, JSON5, TOML?); needs migration tooling to convert today's .ralph-overview/data.json → tasks/<id>.* files.\n\n### F — The 'no, the model just deals with it' lens\n\nDevil's-advocate: 1M-context Opus + good attention means even 500 tasks at 200 LoC each (~100KB) fits in 5% of the window. The cost is real but not 'untenable'. Maybe the right answer is 'do nothing structural; let context cache handle it; revisit at 1000 tasks.'\n\nWorth pressure-testing — if the lenses converge on F, the bookkeeper effort budget is better spent elsewhere.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nAt the end of brainstorm Phase 5 (finalize), produce a `selected-direction.md` in `.ralph/brainstorms/overview-data-context-scalability/` capturing:\n\n- Recommended direction (one of A-F or a hybrid).\n- Concrete next steps (which approach gets a /plan-with-ralph follow-up; what work is deferred).\n- Migration story (how today's .ralph-overview/data.json converts to the new shape; one-shot or incremental).\n- Acceptance criteria for the eventual impl (how do we verify it works at 200+ tasks).\n- Open questions for the operator that the planner should ask.\n\nDo NOT chain into /plan-with-ralph from the brainstorm member. The bookkeeper spawns a fresh plan-overview-data-context-scalability member from the selected-direction.md per the phase-discipline rule.\n\n## Reference reading\n\n- D:/harness-efforts/codexu/.ralph-overview/data.json (the file under discussion).\n- D:/harness-efforts/codexu/plans/codexu-roadmap.md (downstream scale projection — what's coming in Phase 1-6 sub-tasks).\n- D:/harness-efforts/codexu/CLAUDE.md (untracked bookkeeper operating manual — describes the auto-load + edit contract).\n- ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/CLAUDE.md (sync/watcher contract).\n- ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/lib/sync-core.mjs (current assembly logic — where the split's assembly would slot in).\n- ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/scripts/lib/derive-next-command.mjs + crews-cross-walk.mjs (downstream consumers that read .ralph-overview/data.json).\n- ./ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/ralph-overview/tools/overview-viewer/src/types.ts (OverviewData shape).\n- Sibling overview-data tasks in flight: overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema (renames phase→lifecycle) + overview-data-ship-manifest (adds shipManifest field) — coordinate with both. Impl phase of this scalability work should land AFTER both, to avoid re-rebasing a per-task split mid-rename.\n\nLenses to deploy: feasibility (codex), product-reality (copilot), devil's-advocate (challenge: 'is the problem real today, or premature?'), simplification (does the proposed shape actually reduce cognitive load, or just spread the same complexity across more files?). Plus a Claude general-purpose lens reading the actual file sizes + token counts to ground the discussion in numbers.\""}}},{"id":"overview-data-ship-manifest","scope":"codexu|ralph-overview","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"746ef280,0d725e4d","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T20:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"overview-data-ship-manifest","descriptionHtml":"Replace the bare <code>OverviewTask.mergeCommit: string</code> with a richer <code>shipManifest</code> object capturing all commits that landed for a task ship plus a short human-readable summary of what shipped. Today the bookkeeper writes a single SHA (or a comma-separated string for multi-commit ships) and there's nowhere to record what each commit did or what the ship adds end-to-end. The dashboard chip can then surface a tooltip listing commit oneLines + a 1-3 paragraph summary, and agents querying the snapshot get structured ship provenance instead of an opaque SHA string.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Dependency on <code>overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema</code> (in flight). That task renames <code>phase</code> → <code>lifecycle</code> and restructures <code>command.planPrompt</code> → <code>command.prompts</code>; this task adds a sibling <code>shipManifest</code> field. <strong>Spawn the impl member only AFTER overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema lands on main</strong> to avoid a merge collision on <code>types.ts</code>. Plan phase can start anytime (read-only research)."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add structured ship provenance to OverviewTask. Today the bookkeeper writes a bare mergeCommit: string (sometimes comma-separated for multi-commit ships) and there's nowhere to record what each commit did or what the ship adds end-to-end. Operator wants the dashboard chip to surface a tooltip listing commit oneLines + a 1-3 paragraph summary, and agents querying the snapshot to get structured ship provenance.\n\n## Design\n\n**New OverviewTask field (additive):**\n\n```ts\nshipManifest?: {\n  shippedAt: string;                  // ISO 8601\n  summary: string;                    // 1-3 short paragraphs human-written; what shipped, key behaviors, anything operator-relevant\n  commits: Array<{\n    sha: string;                      // short (7-8 char) OR full; renderer accepts both\n    oneLine: string;                  // first line of commit message (verbatim)\n    repo?: 'codexu' | 'ai-developer-toolkit' | 'codex' | 'happy';  // omit for codexu (default)\n  }>;\n}\n```\n\n**Back-compat handling:**\n- Keep the existing `mergeCommit?: string` field as an alias. Read-side: when shipManifest is present, prefer it; when only mergeCommit is present, render the bare SHA as before (no commits[] or summary). When BOTH present, shipManifest wins; mergeCommit acts as a hint for the leading SHA only.\n- Writer-side: when the bookkeeper marks a task shipped, populate shipManifest (new path). Existing entries (the 8+ already shipped) can be left as-is with mergeCommit-only until backfilled.\n- Drop mergeCommit one minor later (after backfill of shipped entries is done in a follow-up task).\n\n**No watcher derivation in v1.** The summary is human-written — the bookkeeper composes it at ship time. Commits are also bookkeeper-captured (from the cherry-pick log + the impl member's final report). v1 does NOT try to derive commits/summary automatically; v2 could optionally derive commits via `git log <prevMergeCommit>..<newMergeCommit> --oneline` when given the SHA range. Out of scope for v1.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n**codexu repo:**\n\n1. .ralph-overview/data.json — no immediate rewrite of existing entries needed; the schema accepts both mergeCommit-only and shipManifest-shaped entries. Optionally backfill 1-2 recent ships (plugin-scope-agents, ralph-overview-multi-mcp-v210, crews-review-mid-turn-v160) as exemplars during impl phase so future bookkeepers see the shape in-repo.\n2. plans/codexu-roadmap.md — update 'Bookkeeper workflow at-a-glance' to capture shipManifest fields at the 'EDIT .ralph-overview/data.json' step.\n3. D:/harness-efforts/codexu/CLAUDE.md (untracked; bookkeeper operating manual) — same: add 'Capture commits + write summary' to the ship checklist.\n\n**ai-developer-toolkit repo (ralph-overview plugin):**\n\n4. tools/overview-viewer/src/types.ts — add the shipManifest field to OverviewTask interface; keep mergeCommit as @deprecated. Define the ShipManifest type (commits + summary + shippedAt).\n5. tools/overview-viewer/src/components/TaskCommand.tsx — when shipManifest is present, render its `summary` in the existing description area (or as an expandable section), and list `commits[]` in the chip tooltip (replacing or augmenting the current mergeCommit link rendering). Each commit oneLine + repo should be a clickable github URL when feasible (operator can hand-curate a known-repo-prefix map in .ralph-overview/data.json if needed; out-of-scope v1).\n6. tools/overview-viewer/src/components/Kanban.tsx — ship-stage card meta shows commit count badge (e.g. '4 commits' instead of '1 sha') when shipManifest present.\n7. tools/overview-viewer/src/utils/filters.ts — extend getTaskSearchHaystack to include shipManifest.summary + commits[].oneLine so search hits across both. Optional but high-value.\n8. tools/overview-viewer/src/__tests__/*.test.tsx — fixture updates: at least one shipped task with full shipManifest, at least one with mergeCommit-only (back-compat path).\n9. scripts/lib/sync-core.mjs — pass shipManifest through unchanged into the merged snapshot. No derivation.\n10. scripts/lib/emit-snapshot-schema.mjs — add the new field + nested shape to the emitted JSON Schema.\n11. tools/overview-mcp/src/tools/get_task.ts — surface shipManifest in the get_task result. list_tasks can include `shippedCommitsCount` as a derived summary field.\n12. README.md + CLAUDE.md in the ralph-overview plugin — document the field with one example.\n13. CHANGELOG.md — next minor entry: 'OverviewTask.shipManifest — structured ship provenance; mergeCommit deprecated.'\n\n## Bookkeeper workflow change\n\nWhen the lead marks a task shipped (the 'EDIT .ralph-overview/data.json' step), the change is:\n\nBEFORE (today):\n```js\n  phase: 'shipped',\n  mergeCommit: 'abc1234',\n  lastTouchedAt: '2026-05-27T...',\n```\n\nAFTER:\n```js\n  phase: 'shipped',  // or lifecycle: 'merged' after dynamic-stages-schema lands\n  shipManifest: {\n    shippedAt: '2026-05-27T...',\n    summary: 'Short 1-3 paragraph description of what shipped — operator-facing.\\n\\nKey behaviors, follow-ups, anything load-bearing.',\n    commits: [\n      { sha: 'abc1234', oneLine: 'feat: US-001 - story title' },\n      { sha: 'def5678', oneLine: 'fix: US-002 - other thing', repo: 'codex' },\n    ],\n  },\n  lastTouchedAt: '2026-05-27T...',\n```\n\nThe lead captures `commits[]` from the impl member's final report + `git log --oneline <prevMergeCommit>..<newMergeCommit>` after cherry-pick. The lead writes `summary` in the bookkeeping commit.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- types.ts has ShipManifest type + OverviewTask.shipManifest?: ShipManifest field.\n- React viewer renders summary + commits[] in tooltip/expander for shipped tasks with shipManifest; falls back to bare mergeCommit display when only mergeCommit is set.\n- At least 2 recently-shipped tasks in .ralph-overview/data.json backfilled with shipManifest as exemplars (suggested: plugin-scope-agents, ralph-overview-multi-mcp-v210).\n- Search haystack includes commit oneLines + summary text.\n- MCP get_task returns the new field; list_tasks includes derived shippedCommitsCount.\n- Schema JSON regenerated (`pnpm sync-ralph-state` produces a snapshot with the new shape).\n- Cross-package typecheck green; vitest green in ralph-overview.\n- README + CHANGELOG + bookkeeper docs updated.\n\n## Workflow\n\nConcrete enough to skip /brainstorm-with-ralph. Run /plan-with-ralph directly. Probably decomposes into 3-4 user stories (types + viewer + tests + backfill exemplars).\n\nVersion bump: ralph-overview v2.4.0 (after v2.3.0 schema rename lands). codexu commits to main as a single PR (.ralph-overview/data.json backfill + docs).\n\n**Dependency:** wait for overview-data-dynamic-stages-schema to ship on main before spawning impl. Plan phase can start anytime.\""}}},{"id":"codex-sandbox-setup-release","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"558cd1388,a36dd48c","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T10:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-sandbox-setup-release","descriptionHtml":"Bug — <code>codex-windows-sandbox-setup.exe</code> isn't published in the codex release tarball. Without it, non-<code>--yolo</code> runs fail because the sandbox installer can't be located. The binary builds from <code>windows-sandbox-rs/src/bin/setup_main.rs</code> but the publish workflow only builds <code>codex</code> + <code>codex-core</code> bins. Also includes a cross-platform investigation: today the fork's <code>.github/workflows/publish-npm.yml</code> is Windows-only (<code>windows-latest</code> runner, <code>x86_64-pc-windows-msvc</code> target); part of this task is to figure out what sandbox-setup-equivalent binaries are needed for Linux / macOS if/when the fork expands to those platforms.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ codex submodule change — touches <code>.github/workflows/publish-npm.yml</code> in <code>codex/</code> (gim-home/codex repo). Verify the binary target name with <code>cargo metadata</code> first; the workspace crate is <code>codex-windows-sandbox-rs</code> but the bin name may differ (likely <code>codex-windows-sandbox-setup</code>). Test by tagging a release and confirming the published tarball under GitHub Packages + GitHub Release bundle contains the new exe. Two-part task: (a) fix the Windows packaging gap; (b) document what's needed for Linux/macOS sandbox-setup parity."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Two-part task — Windows fix + cross-platform sandbox-setup investigation.\n\n## Part A — Windows fix\n\nThe gim-home/codex fork's publish workflow (.github/workflows/publish-npm.yml) builds and ships codex.exe + codex-core.exe + rg.exe but NOT codex-windows-sandbox-setup.exe. The sandbox-setup binary is built from external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/windows-sandbox-rs/src/bin/setup_main.rs (likely bin target name 'codex-windows-sandbox-setup', verify via cargo metadata). When users run codex without the --yolo flag (the default), the codex CLI tries to invoke the sandbox-setup binary, can't find it because it wasn't packaged, and fails.\n\nFix (3 edits to .github/workflows/publish-npm.yml in the codex submodule):\n\n1. **Build step** (~lines 126-128): add `--bin codex-windows-sandbox-setup` to the `cargo build --release` invocation alongside the existing `--bin codex --bin codex-core` (verify the exact bin name first).\n\n2. **Vendor layout assembly** (~lines 130-149): after the existing `cp $REL/codex-core.exe $VENDOR/codex/codex-core.exe`, add `cp $REL/codex-windows-sandbox-setup.exe $VENDOR/codex/codex-windows-sandbox-setup.exe`. Place it in the same $VENDOR/codex/ directory as codex.exe + codex-core.exe so the launcher's discovery logic finds it via the same path.\n\n3. **Release bundle** (~lines 224-248): the bundle tarball stage merges $GITHUB_WORKSPACE/stage-platform/vendor into the staging dir; verify the new exe gets carried through. Likely no edit needed there if step 2 placed it inside $VENDOR/codex/ — confirm by inspecting the produced tarball.\n\nAlso check whether the launcher (codex-rs-overlay/codex-copilot-launcher/) or the install scripts (external/repos/codex-patched/scripts/install/install.ps1) need to know about the new binary's path — they shouldn't if it's colocated with codex.exe, but verify.\n\nPrereq verification: run `cargo metadata --manifest-path external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/Cargo.toml --format-version 1 | jq -r '.packages[] | select(.name | test(\\\"windows-sandbox\\\")) | .targets[] | select(.kind | index(\\\"bin\\\")) | .name'` to confirm the bin target name.\n\nAcceptance for Part A: (1) tag a test release in gim-home/codex; (2) verify the published GitHub Packages tarball + GitHub Release bundle both contain codex-windows-sandbox-setup.exe under vendor/x86_64-pc-windows-msvc/codex/; (3) smoke-test by installing fresh from the release bundle and running codex without --yolo — should NOT fail with 'cannot find sandbox-setup binary'.\n\n## Part B — Cross-platform sandbox-setup investigation\n\nThe current publish workflow is Windows-only (runs-on: windows-latest, RELEASE_TARGET: x86_64-pc-windows-msvc). Investigate what's needed for parity on Linux + macOS so the fix doesn't have to be redone when those platforms are added.\n\nResearch deliverable: produce a doc at codex/docs/implementation/sandbox-setup-platform-matrix.md (or update an existing patch-surface section) covering:\n\n1. **Per-platform sandbox-setup binary inventory.** Grep external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/ for sandbox crates: `windows-sandbox-rs` is the Windows one. Look for `linux-sandbox-rs`, `macos-sandbox-rs`, `landlock-*`, `seccomp-*`, `sandbox-exec-*`, or similar. Inspect each crate's `src/bin/` directory and Cargo.toml `[[bin]]` entries to enumerate the bin targets that need to ship.\n\n2. **Upstream codex's release model.** Read openai/codex's .github/workflows/ for release.yml or publish-*.yml — they ship multi-platform tarballs (Linux x64/arm64, macOS x64/arm64, Windows x64). What binaries do they include per platform? Specifically: do they ship sandbox-setup-equivalent binaries on Linux/macOS, or do those platforms use a runtime-installed sandbox (Landlock kernel feature, macOS sandbox-exec system command)?\n\n3. **What our fork would need to do if/when we add Linux/macOS.** Concrete delta against the current publish-npm.yml:\n   - Add platform matrix to the `runs-on` and the cargo target triples (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu, x86_64-apple-darwin, aarch64-apple-darwin).\n   - Per-platform sandbox-setup bin builds (e.g., `--bin codex-linux-sandbox-setup` if such a bin exists).\n   - Per-platform vendor layout (vendor/<target-triple>/codex/<bins>).\n   - Per-platform install.ps1 → install.sh equivalents under scripts/install/.\n   - Per-platform npm package suffixes (codex-linux-x64, codex-darwin-arm64, etc.).\n\n4. **Recommendation.** Is the Linux/macOS publish expansion a separate task to spawn later, or should it be bundled with Part A as a 2-PR sequence (PR 1 = Windows fix, PR 2 = Linux/macOS expansion)? If recommendation is 'separate task', file a follow-up task in codexu's .ralph-overview/data.json as `codex-cross-platform-publish` (plan-ready, plan exists in the doc this task produces).\n\nPart B acceptance: doc lands in codex/docs/implementation/sandbox-setup-platform-matrix.md (or chosen filename); enumerates every sandbox-setup binary across crates; cites upstream codex's per-platform publish strategy with file:line; concrete delta written against current publish-npm.yml; recommendation on bundling vs splitting the cross-platform expansion.\n\nCommit policy: Part A is a single small commit to .github/workflows/publish-npm.yml in the codex submodule. Part B is a single docs commit (could be same commit if both small enough; separate commits OK). CI on gim-home/codex runs invariant-check.yml on push (no local cargo build needed per fork's no-local-cargo convention). The acceptance gate is the publish workflow producing the right artifacts, which only manifests on tag push — flag this in the spawn prompt so the impl member tags a test release and verifies.\""}}},{"id":"ralph-overview-multi-mcp-v210","scope":"ralph-overview","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-26T10:29:34Z","mergeCommit":"d0a700d0","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"ralph-overview-multi-mcp-v210","descriptionHtml":"ralph-overview v2.1.0 — cooperative-lease watcher (N concurrent MCPs in same repo → 1 active watcher + N-1 passive consumers); dev server moved out of MCP (operator launches via <code>pnpm overview:dev</code>); new <code>overview_watcher_status</code> MCP tool; hostname cross-machine guard. ai-developer-toolkit branch <code>ralph/ralph-overview-multi-mcp-v210</code> HEAD <code>42bdb8b7</code> merged to ai-developer-toolkit/main via merge commit <code>e65e8161</code> (pushed to both Evyatar108 fork and gim-home). codexu side cherry-picked onto main as <code>d0a700d0</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"(shipped — see plans/ralph-overview-multi-mcp-coordination-brainstorm.md and .ralph/jobs/ralph-overview-multi-mcp-v210/plan.md)"}}},{"id":"crews-review-mid-turn-v160","scope":"crews","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-26T11:06:47Z","mergeCommit":"bd94fc67","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"crews-review-mid-turn-v160","descriptionHtml":"crews v1.6.0 — PostToolUse advisory injection closes the mid-turn review-required gap. Fires after 30s grace, once per <code>lastReviewRequiredSeq</code> bump; <code>progress</code> dropped from <code>DEFAULT_REVIEW_KINDS</code> (Stop no longer blocks on unread progress envelopes). Uniform lead+member. Cross-plugin coexistence test passes (agent-peers verified safe). ai-developer-toolkit branch <code>ralph/crews-review-mid-turn-v160</code> HEAD <code>bd94fc67</code> merged to ai-developer-toolkit/main via merge commit <code>e65e8161</code> (pushed to both Evyatar108 fork and gim-home). 22 commits = 9 stories + 7 Phase 5a code fixes + 6 Phase 5b docs fixes.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"(shipped — see plans/crews-review-required-mid-turn-brainstorm.md and plans/crews-review-mid-turn-v160-plan.md)"}}},{"id":"plugin-scope-agents","scope":"codex|codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-26T10:14:44Z","mergeCommit":"9d89ca43","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 2c — Plugin scoping (host vs agent)</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\"><strong>Upstream codex patch.</strong> Add <code>scope: \"host\" | \"agent\" | \"both\"</code> to plugin manifest; loader checks <code>next_thread_spawn_depth</code>. Lives in <code>codex/</code> submodule, not codexu.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">upstream codex</span>\n        <span>separate repo</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":50}],"command":{"name":"plugin-scope-agents","descriptionHtml":"Plugin scoping with top-level-only plugins + agent-spawner pattern · unblocked (agent-view-research landed 2026-05-14)","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Read <code>plans/agent-view-research.md</code> first — its findings define the spawner-pattern model this task implements. Also coordinate with <code>codex-child-spawn-tools</code> (sibling spike): the agent-tree-rpc agent surfaced that pinned codex submodule child agents lack <code>spawn_agent</code>/<code>wait_agent</code> in their tool surface. If we keep that restriction (top-level-only spawning), the agent-spawner becomes the canonical path; if we want child-spawns-child, the codex-side tool surface needs an overlay or submodule bump. The spike's recommendation should land BEFORE this task scopes the spawner pattern."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Extend codex's plugin-scoping (Phase 2c — host vs agent context) with a third dimension: 'top-level-agent-only' plugins. Today's spec: scope=host|agent|both. New spec: scope=top-level|subagent|both (or equivalent — see plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 2c for current shape). Some plugins like ralph-orchestration must be available only to TOP-LEVEL agents (operator-spawned sessions, or sessions spawned by a designated 'agent-spawner' agent) — never to sub-agents spawned via codex's multi_agents_v2/spawn.rs (recursion + context bloat). Build the spawner agent (similar to Claude Code's agent-view if the research from agent-view-research clarifies the model): it can SPAWN top-level sessions on behalf of the operator, those spawned sessions inherit top-level scope and have ralph-orchestration + similar host-tier plugins. Read plans/agent-view-research.md (output of agent-view-research) before scoping. Read codex/codex-rs-overlay/* for divergence-crate precedent (changes should land as overlay if they touch codex; happy-cli changes are fine). Plugin manifest schema lives in codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core-plugins/src/manifest.rs (READ-ONLY). Acceptance: ralph-orchestration plugin manifest declares scope=top-level; spawning a subagent that tries to load it fails or silently no-ops; spawning via the new agent-spawner succeeds and the new session sees ralph-orchestration. Tests: codex integration test with mock plugin in both scopes. Cross-package typecheck green. Surface to operator before merging — this changes the plugin-loading contract.\""}}},{"id":"agent-comms","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"blocked","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T03:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">BOOX validation Phases 2–6</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Chat round-trip, refresh-per-request, token revocation, multi-device fan-out, signed-APK release. Manual hardware. Will feel slow before realtime-sync-perf lands.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">manual</span>\n        <span>operator only</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":10},{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 4 — Coexistence verification (4a–4m)</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">13 end-to-end integration tests across codexu × codex × ralph × phone. Gated on Phase 3 landing; failures file back into the appropriate phase.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">integration</span>\n        <span>post-Phase-3</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":70}],"command":{"name":"agent-comms","descriptionHtml":"Top-level agent ↔ top-level agent comms across 3 scopes (cross-tunnel / same-daemon / parent-child)","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Blocked on <code>plugin-scope-agents</code> + <code>agent-view-research</code> + <code>channels-research</code> landing. The communication channel design depends on the spawner pattern's chosen model AND on whether MCP \"channels\" (2-way) are available as the transport."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Build top-level-agent ↔ top-level-agent communication across THREE distinct scopes — design has to decide whether to unify them under one transport or distinguish them with different mechanisms.\n\nScope A — Cross-tunnel / cross-machine (codexu-specific). Two top-level sessions live on different daemons (e.g., laptop daemon + remote machine daemon, both paired to the same operator's GitHub identity). Today daemons are silo'd (single-user-per-process embedded happy-server per Sprint A); no cross-daemon routing exists. This is the hardest scope because there's no existing routing layer. Options: (i) re-introduce a relay (we explicitly deleted this — operator policy); (ii) use Microsoft Dev Tunnels as a P2P channel between daemons; (iii) operator's mobile app acts as the rendezvous broker. Surface this choice to operator BEFORE designing the wire shape.\n\nScope B — Same-machine, daemon-managed. Two top-level sessions on ONE daemon. Daemon already coordinates via Socket.IO event-routing (packages/happy-server/sources/app/events/eventRouter.ts). The cross-session message-pass would extend that with a per-session inbox + a tool the agent invokes. This is the easiest scope.\n\nScope C — Parent-spawned-child relationship. An agent-spawner (from plugin-scope-agents) spawned the child top-level session; the parent retains a 'session reference' or capability. Communication has implicit trust + a known channel from spawn time. Could be a strict subset of B/A but with stronger invariants (e.g., parent can read child's logs/state; child can post back to parent's inbox without explicit handshake).\n\nThe design must answer: are these three separate mechanisms (different MCP servers / RPC surfaces per scope), one unified transport with scope-aware routing (single MCP with a 'scope' parameter), or a spectrum (parent-child piggybacks on B; B uses daemon Socket.IO; A needs the explicit cross-daemon design)?\n\nTwo consumer-facing channels to cover regardless of scope: (1) SPAWNING — request another top-level agent be spawned with role/cwd/plugins; (2) MESSAGE PASSING — request-response or pub-sub between live sessions.\n\nRead plans/agent-view-research.md, plans/channels-research.md, plans/agent-view-followups.md if exists, codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/multi_agents_v2/ for codex's existing inter-agent plumbing (READ-ONLY per minimize-conflict-surface). Read packages/happy-cli/src/api/apiMachine.ts + apiSession.ts for how spawn-happy-session is currently exposed; the agent-comms MCP likely lives in happy-cli. Read packages/happy-server/sources/app/events/eventRouter.ts for the existing fan-out primitive that Scope B would extend.\n\nAcceptance: design doc + per-scope working fixture: (B) two sessions on the same daemon exchange a message; (C) a spawned child reports back to its parent without re-handshake; (A) the design names a concrete transport choice for cross-daemon (no full implementation required for the first pass). Tests: vitest fixture for each scope. Pitfall: cycle-prevention applies across scopes (A spawns B spawns A → loop); need hop counter + operator-approval gate. Surface ARCHITECTURAL CHOICES (relay-vs-P2P-vs-broker, unified-vs-separate transports) to operator BEFORE landing code.\""}}},{"id":"channels-research","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T09:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"channels-research","descriptionHtml":"Research Claude Code's `channels` (MCP 2-way) + codex implementation plan · doc <a href=\"channels-research.md\">channels-research.md</a> · 2 follow-ups (mcp-server-notifications, codex-channels)","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Research Claude Code's 'channels' concept and design a codex equivalent if it doesn't exist there. Goal: 2-way communication between agents and MCP servers (today MCP is largely request-response from the agent; we want the MCP to be able to push messages back / stream state changes / interrupt the agent). Output: plans/channels-research.md covering: (1) What Claude Code calls 'channels' — read the relevant Claude Code source (C:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/ if accessible) + Anthropic's docs/changelog. Capture the wire shape, lifecycle (open/close), back-pressure model, how an MCP server discovers/registers a channel, how the agent subscribes. (2) Codex's current MCP support — `codex` ships an MCP client; what does it support today? Read codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/ (READ-ONLY per minimize-conflict-surface tenet) for mcp_tool_call.rs / mcp_client / rmcp_client modules. Identify whether codex's MCP transport (stdio? sse? streamable-http? something else?) admits server-initiated messages. (3) Gap analysis: if codex doesn't support 2-way channels, what's the minimal patch? An overlay crate in codex/codex-rs-overlay/ that extends mcp_client with channel semantics? A new tool type? A protocol upgrade? (4) Implementation sketch: file:line refs for where the patches would land, effort estimate, risk areas. (5) Cross-reference Phase 2d (ask_user_question primitive) and the MCP spec evolution (https://spec.modelcontextprotocol.io/) — channels might already be standardized. NO CODE — research + design only. Add follow-up ralph task entries to plans/parallel-assignments.md if the gap analysis surfaces concrete next steps. Surface to operator before committing the doc.\""}},"planOnly":true},{"id":"async-events-design","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"blocked","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T13:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"async-events-design","descriptionHtml":"Design async event listening for agents — re-blocked 2026-05-14 to wait for channels primitive before locking v1 transport","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Re-blocked 2026-05-14. Was briefly run — its draft (working tree only at <code>plans/async-events-design.md</code>) recommends long-poll MCP for v1 and channels-research's notification bridge as fast-follow. Operator decided the design should be informed by an actually-landed channels primitive (Stage A — <code>mcp-server-notifications</code>) before locking the v1 transport. Resume after that ships. Working-tree draft is preserved as a starting point."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Design how an agent listens to async events — e.g., 'wake me when a commit lands on main', 'notify me when a periodic task fires', 'tell me when another agent finishes its turn'. Today an agent's only async-trigger model is: exit and be re-spawned (e.g., a periodic background task that exits to wake the operator/agent). That's not ideal — loses context, restart latency, can't subscribe to fine-grained events. Output: plans/async-events-design.md covering: (1) The use cases — at least these 4: git events (commit on main / branch updated / push), periodic-task firings, inter-agent notifications (sibling-agent completion / sibling-agent question), file-system events (file changed under cwd). (2) Current options in Claude Code — is there a 'wait for X' tool? hooks? streamable MCP? A 'send-when-idle' channel? Read C:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/ if accessible. (3) Current options in codex — codex's hook system (config/src/hook_config.rs), inter-agent fast path in multi_agents_v2/, MCP support. READ-ONLY on codex/external/repos/codex-patched/ per minimize-conflict-surface. (4) Design options for codexu — compare at least: (a) MCP with 2-way channels (depends on channels-research outcome); (b) periodic background task that exits to wake agent (current model — note limits); (c) long-poll MCP tool (block until event); (d) a 'subscription' RPC on the codex app-server side that streams events to the agent's session; (e) hybrid (use codex hooks for in-session triggers, channels for cross-session). (5) Recommendation with rationale; identify the smallest-viable subset that covers the 4 use cases. (6) Surface to operator before committing follow-up implementation tasks. NO CODE — design only. The output doc IS the deliverable.\""}}},{"id":"native-agent-parity","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T08:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"native-agent-parity","descriptionHtml":"Research codex parity with Claude Code's native subagents · doc <a href=\"native-agent-parity.md\">native-agent-parity.md</a> · 3 follow-ups recommended (explorer, plan, verification)","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Research whether (and how) we want codex parity with Claude Code's native subagent palette — e.g., Explore (fast codebase exploration), Plan (architect), claude-code-guide, statusline-setup, etc. Today codex's [agents.<role>] TOML system is operator-defined; Claude Code ships preset subagent types with curated system prompts. Output: plans/native-agent-parity.md covering: (1) Enumerate Claude Code's preset subagents — use the Agent tool yourself if accessible to list them, or grep C:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/ for the subagent registry. For each: name, purpose, tool allowlist, model preference, system prompt. (2) For each, assess whether it makes sense for codex too: high-value port (e.g., Explore — every developer benefits from a fast codebase-explorer subagent) vs Claude-specific (e.g., claude-code-guide — references Claude Code features that may not apply). (3) Implementation packaging — should codex parity ship as: (a) a plugin in packages/codexu-plugin/ that registers default [agents.<role>] entries; (b) an overlay crate in codex/codex-rs-overlay/ that bakes them into the binary; (c) a one-time `codex agent install --preset claude-code-equivalents` migration. Discuss tradeoffs. (4) Should we LEVERAGE Claude Code's system prompts as inspiration? Yes — they're battle-tested. Should we copy verbatim (license / attribution question — check Claude Code's license) or paraphrase? Recommend. (5) Cross-reference Phase 3b-i (subagents → [agents.<role>] for ralph's 12 internal subagents) — that's the analogous port but operator-private; this is the public-facing 'native palette' port. (6) Pick top 3 most-valuable to port first. NO CODE — research + decision doc. Surface recommendations to operator before opening follow-up implementation tasks.\""}},"planOnly":true},{"id":"roadmap-plugin","scope":"bookkeeping|codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T05:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 1c residual — TUI plugin install verification</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Operator-side smoke test: codex TUI → plugins picker → enable codexu-plugin → verify <code>hello-world</code> in /skills.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">manual</span>\n        <span>~15 min</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":120}],"command":{"name":"roadmap-plugin","descriptionHtml":"🛠 Plugin: agents manage roadmap/overview.html via skill or MCP (add/update/take tasks)","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Build a plugin that lets agents manage the codexu roadmap (.ralph-overview/data.json + plans/parallel-assignments.md + plans/codexu-roadmap.md) programmatically. Plugin home: packages/codexu-plugin/.\n\nTWO INTERFACE SURFACES — surface design choice to operator before scoping the build:\n\n1. Skill commands (lower overhead, easier first version). Slash commands like /roadmap-add-task, /roadmap-update-status, /roadmap-record-run, /roadmap-take-task. Implementation: SKILL.md files under packages/codexu-plugin/skills/roadmap/. Each skill is a procedure the agent follows that reads/edits .ralph-overview/data.json (OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[], OVERVIEW_DATA.runs[], OVERVIEW_DATA.lastTouched[id], etc.) + plans/parallel-assignments.md's status table. Reuses the existing .agents/skills/roadmap-and-overview/SKILL.md as the data-model reference (it documents the data schema + update procedures exhaustively).\n\n2. MCP server (richer, agent-callable without slash commands). Stdio MCP server registered via packages/codexu-plugin/.codex-plugin/plugin.json's mcp_servers field. Tools exposed: roadmap.list, roadmap.addTask, roadmap.updateStatus, roadmap.recordRun, roadmap.takeTask. Server lives in packages/codexu-plugin/mcp-roadmap/ (new Node/TS subdir; can reuse @slopus/happy-wire for any shared types).\n\nCORE OPERATIONS (regardless of surface):\n- add-task: takes task metadata (id, status, workstream, sizeBucket, risk, effort, cadence?, ralphCommand string, optional blockedOn list). Appends a new task object to OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[] in .ralph-overview/data.json + new lettered section in plans/parallel-assignments.md. Validates against the data model documented in .agents/skills/roadmap-and-overview/SKILL.md.\n- update-status: takes (taskId, newStatus, optional commit sha + summary if flipping to closed). Finds the task by id in OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[] and updates its status field; updates OVERVIEW_DATA.lastTouched[taskId]; updates parallel-assignments.md status table cell; if status='closed' appends a run record to OVERVIEW_DATA.runs[].\n- record-run: takes (taskId, ranAt ISO, outcome, commits[], summary). Appends to OVERVIEW_DATA.runs[]. If task is periodic, recomputes OVERVIEW_DATA.periodic[taskId].lastRunId + nextDueAt.\n- take-task: spawns a top-level agent session running the task's ralph command, then calls update-status to flip the task to in-progress. Spawn integration: call happy-cli's existing spawn-happy-session RPC. The MCP server (which runs as a stdio child of codex) needs to reach the daemon — via daemon's HTTP control surface at 127.0.0.1:<httpPort> (read from ~/.happy/daemon.state.json) OR via the daemon's existing socket. Surface this integration choice to operator.\n\nDEPENDENCIES / CROSS-REFERENCES:\n- READ .agents/skills/roadmap-and-overview/SKILL.md FIRST — it's the canonical data-model + per-operation procedure documentation. The plugin should automate exactly what that skill describes manually.\n- Read packages/codexu-plugin/README.md + Phase 1c scaffolding for the plugin layout.\n- For MCP transport: stdio MCP. Codex's MCP client docs in codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/mcp_tool_call.rs (READ-ONLY per minimize-conflict-surface).\n- For task-taking spawn: packages/happy-cli/src/daemon/run.ts:spawnSession (daemon-side handler) + packages/happy-cli/src/api/apiMachine.ts (apiMachineClient with spawn-happy-session RPC method registration).\n- OVERLAPS WITH agent-comms: agent-comms's Scope B (same-daemon spawn/message) covers similar ground. Coordinate to avoid duplicating the spawn surface — this plugin can ship v1 using existing happy-cli RPCs without waiting for agent-comms; agent-comms can later subsume or refactor the spawn integration.\n\nACCEPTANCE v1 (skill-only):\n- /roadmap-add-task with full metadata appends a new task to .ralph-overview/data.json and the operator sees it on reload.\n- /roadmap-update-status flips a task's status in OVERVIEW_DATA.tasks[] + updates parallel-assignments.md status table cell + OVERVIEW_DATA.lastTouched.\n- /roadmap-record-run appends a run to OVERVIEW_DATA.runs[] + updates periodic metadata if applicable.\n- /roadmap-take-task spawns a happy session with the ralph command and flips the task to in-progress.\n- The 4 skills are documented in packages/codexu-plugin/skills/roadmap/*/SKILL.md.\n\nACCEPTANCE v1.5 (MCP add-on):\n- Same operations exposed as MCP tools.\n- An agent in a codex session with codexu-plugin enabled can call roadmap.addTask etc. without slash commands.\n\nSurface scope choice (skill-only vs MCP-only vs both for v1) to operator BEFORE writing code. NO CODE until operator agrees on scope.\""}}},{"id":"agent-tree-rpc","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T19:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"agent-tree-rpc","descriptionHtml":"App-server RPC exposing codex's live spawn tree (sessionGetAgentTree + agent-tree-update events). Spawned by <code>agent-view-research</code>. Branch <code>agent-tree-rpc</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Expose codex's live spawn tree as a queryable + streamable app-server RPC. Per plans/agent-view-research.md §6 task `agent-tree-rpc`. Today codex already has the data — AgentRegistry.agent_tree (codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/agent/registry.rs), control.rs:832-840 subscribe_status, and CollabAgentSpawnBegin/EndEvent are emitted (spawn.rs:71-205) — but nothing exposes the live tree as a single queryable surface that mobile clients can render. Minimize-conflict-surface tenet per plans/codexu-roadmap.md: codex source READ-ONLY; add the RPC bridge on the happy-cli side (packages/happy-cli/src/codex/codexAppServerClient.ts + runCodex.ts) by subscribing to codex's existing event stream and re-emitting structured spawn-tree updates over happy-server's Socket.IO. Add two RPC shapes: (1) sessionGetAgentTree(sessionId) → { nodes: AgentTreeNode[], edges: { parent, child }[] } where AgentTreeNode = { threadId, agentRole, nickname, status, lastTaskMessage, spawnedAt }; (2) socket subscription event 'agent-tree-update' emitting incremental deltas (node-added, node-status-changed, node-removed). Read codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/agent/registry.rs + control.rs (READ-ONLY) for the data shape. Read packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts for the existing codex event subscription pattern. Read packages/happy-server/sources/app/events/eventRouter.ts for the fan-out primitive. Acceptance: codex spawns 2 sub-agents via spawn_agent → happy-cli detects + emits agent-tree-update events → happy-server fans out → vitest assertion that subscribed clients receive both spawn-begin and spawn-end deltas in correct order with parent linkage. Cross-package typecheck green. Single commit on main, NO codex submodule edits. Pitfall: subscribe_status returns a tokio watch::Receiver that lives in codex's process — happy-cli's bridge must hold the receiver in a task that survives across the agent's lifetime, not just for one tool call.\""}}},{"id":"session-parent-link","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T15:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"session-parent-link","descriptionHtml":"Added parentSessionId + spawnedChildren[] to Session metadata · commit <code>11c3eafb</code> · unblocks mobile-tree-view + spawn-from-app. Spawned by <code>agent-view-research</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add parentSessionId + spawnedChildren tracking to Session metadata end-to-end. Per plans/agent-view-research.md §6 task `session-parent-link`. Today Session has no concept of parent-child (packages/happy-app/sources/sync/storageTypes.ts:130-163) — the mobile app shows a flat list. Add: Metadata.parentSessionId?: string and Metadata.spawnedChildren?: string[] (composite session IDs). Plumb through: (1) happy-server schema/wire shape — if backend tracks the link, add columns; if metadata-only, just extend the JSON contract; surface choice to operator. (2) happy-app storageTypes.ts Session/Metadata types. (3) storage.ts:395-570 applySessions reducer — preserve the new fields on update merges. (4) New helper getSessionChildren(sid) and getSessionParent(sid) in sources/sync/storage.ts. NO UI changes yet — that's mobile-tree-view's job. Acceptance: writing a Session with parentSessionId='m1:abc' and spawnedChildren=['m1:def','m1:ghi'] round-trips through happy-server → app → MMKV → re-fetch unchanged; helpers return correct neighbours; cross-package typecheck green. Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants — composite-id (machineSessionId.ts) helpers must be respected for parent refs. Single commit on main. Pitfall: existing sessions have no parent — null parentSessionId means top-level; don't backfill spawnedChildren=[] on every existing session (use undefined/null to distinguish missing-field from empty-children).\""}},"mergeCommit":"11c3eafb"},{"id":"mobile-tree-view","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-25T13:55:00Z","mergeCommit":"5fea5583","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"mobile-tree-view","descriptionHtml":"Tree-style happy-app session list with depth indentation + expand/collapse — unblocked 2026-05-14 (session-parent-link shipped <code>11c3eafb</code>). Spawned by <code>agent-view-research</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Convert the happy-app session list from flat-with-date-groups to a tree with depth indentation + expand/collapse. Per plans/agent-view-research.md §6 task `mobile-tree-view`. PREREQUISITE: session-parent-link must have landed so Session.metadata.parentSessionId + spawnedChildren are queryable. Files: packages/happy-app/sources/sync/storage.ts:250-343 buildSessionListViewData — change from flat SessionListViewItem[] to depth-tagged flattened-tree (each item carries `depth: number` and `hasChildren: boolean`). packages/happy-app/sources/components/SessionsList.tsx:193-333 FlatList — keep FlatList but consume the new depth-tagged data; SessionsList.tsx:342-463 SessionItem memo — render depth-based left indent (e.g., depth × 20px) + chevron icon when hasChildren; toggle expanded state in a new MMKV-persisted Map<sid, boolean>. Active-vs-inactive grouping: when a parent is in the active group, render its children inline beneath it (still in the active group); otherwise children appear inside their date group, indented under parent if the parent is in the SAME date group, otherwise as orphans (rare). Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants — renderWindow + seq-based pagination must not be touched. Acceptance: vitest snapshot of buildSessionListViewData with mocked parent + 2 children → correct depth ordering; manual smoke: cold-launch with synthetic parentSessionId data → tree renders, chevron toggles expand/collapse, persists across refresh. Cross-package typecheck green. Single commit on main. Pitfall: SessionItem memo must include depth + expanded in its keyExtractor / memo deps, or stale rows will render at wrong depth on tree mutations.\""}}},{"id":"session-role-pill","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T14:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"session-role-pill","descriptionHtml":"Surface flavor + model + permission-mode pills inline in session-list row · merge <code>7e9f724c</code>. Spawned by <code>agent-view-research</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Surface agent flavor + currentModelCode + currentPermissionModeCode inline in the session-list row. Per plans/agent-view-research.md §6 task `session-role-pill`. Today these fields live in Session.metadata (packages/happy-app/sources/sync/storageTypes.ts:130-163) and are visible only inside SessionContextDrawer — never in the list row. The Claude Code 'teammate view' shows agent role + model on every spinner-tree line; this brings the same visual affordance to the mobile session list. File: packages/happy-app/sources/components/SessionsList.tsx:342-463 SessionItem memo — add a small horizontal pill row beneath the subtitle showing: (a) flavor icon (codex/claude/...), (b) abbreviated model code (e.g., 'gpt-5-codex' → 'codex' or full code based on width budget), (c) permission-mode badge (plan / default / acceptEdits, color-coded). Reuse existing components from sources/components/ if a Pill / Badge primitive exists. Acceptance: vitest snapshot of SessionItem with metadata.flavor='codex' + currentModelCode='gpt-5-codex' + currentPermissionModeCode='plan' → renders the three pills; existing list-row tests stay green. Cross-package typecheck green. Single commit on main. Pitfall: shallow-copy of SessionRowData might already include these fields — confirm before adding to keyExtractor / memo deps. Independent of mobile-tree-view (no schema changes); land first as a quick UX win.\""}},"mergeCommit":"7e9f724c"},{"id":"spawn-from-app","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-26T03:55:00Z","mergeCommit":"e7d297ca,0ed8d958,45347d40,f8a082fb","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"spawn-from-app","descriptionHtml":"\"Spawn child session\" affordance in app + spawnSessionFromSession RPC end-to-end — unblocked 2026-05-14 (session-parent-link shipped <code>11c3eafb</code>). Spawned by <code>agent-view-research</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add a 'spawn child session' affordance to the happy-app session detail view + new spawnSessionFromSession RPC end-to-end. Per plans/agent-view-research.md §6 task `spawn-from-app`. PREREQUISITE: session-parent-link must have landed so the new child can carry parentSessionId. New RPC shape: spawnSessionFromSession(parentSid: compositeSessionId, config: { agent, path?, permissionMode?, model?, initialMessage? }) → newSid. happy-cli side (packages/happy-cli/src/api/apiSession.ts or apiMachine.ts): register the handler; resolve parent's machineId from compositeSessionId; call existing machineSpawnNewSession-equivalent path with auto-set Metadata.parentSessionId = parentSid; also update parent's metadata.spawnedChildren via sessionUpdateMetadata. happy-app side (packages/happy-app/sources/sync/ops.ts): add machineSpawnSessionFromSession wrapper; in session detail view (sources/app/(app)/session/[id].tsx or wherever the detail screen lives), add a 'Spawn child agent' action button that opens a sheet with agent flavor/permission/model pickers and an optional initial-message field. Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants. Acceptance: vitest fixture in happy-cli — call spawnSessionFromSession against a mocked parent; assert new session created with parentSessionId set + parent's spawnedChildren contains new sid. Manual smoke from app: tap 'spawn child' on a session → new child appears in list with parent linkage visible (if mobile-tree-view has landed, indented under parent; otherwise just present in flat list with correct metadata). Cross-package typecheck green. Single commit on main per side (happy-cli commit, happy-app commit). Pitfall: cycle prevention — if a user tries to spawn under a session that's already a child of the new child (cycle), the handler must reject; document the limit (max-depth 1? unlimited with hop counter?). Coordinate with the agent-comms task — spawnSessionFromSession is the same RPC Scope C will need; this lands the wire shape first.\""}}},{"id":"agent-status-stream","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T19:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 6 — Long-lived teammates</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Deferred polish. Per-role <code>keepalive</code>, workflow conventions, codexu phone UI for active teammates. Un-defer when re-spawning friction hits 3+ times/week.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex</span>\n        <span>deferred</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":90}],"command":{"name":"agent-status-stream","descriptionHtml":"Live \"active teammates\" overlay in app — bridges shipped agent-tree-update events through happy-cli/server to mobile. Spawned by <code>agent-view-research</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Bridge codex's per-session spawn-tree updates from agent-tree-rpc through to a live 'active teammates' overlay in the happy-app session detail view. Per plans/agent-view-research.md §6 task `agent-status-stream`. PREREQUISITE: agent-tree-rpc must have landed so happy-server emits 'agent-tree-update' events with structured deltas. happy-app side: (1) sources/sync/storage.ts — add agentTreeBySession: Record<sid, AgentTreeNode[]> reducer state; subscribe to agent-tree-update events via the existing socket plumbing; apply deltas with a per-session monotonic guard using the shipped delta.seq field (server frames are { sessionId, delta }). (2) sources/app/(app)/session/[id].tsx — add a collapsible 'Active teammates' overlay panel showing the spawn tree (parent thread + spawned sub-agents) with live status indicators ('running' / 'idle' / 'completed' / 'failed' pulsed dot); tap a teammate row to peek its last-task-message + lastN messages (separate RPC, follow-up). Reuse mobile-tree-view's depth-indentation pattern for the teammate sub-tree rendering. Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants — the new reducer state is in-memory only (ephemeral; don't persist to MMKV); reconnect should re-fetch full tree via sessionGetAgentTree(sid) once and then resume incremental deltas. Acceptance: integration test — happy-cli emits 'agent-tree-update' with 2 spawn events, app receives both, panel shows 2 teammates with correct status; status transition to 'completed' updates the panel pulsed dot. Cross-package typecheck green. ONE commit per side (happy-app commit; happy-cli changes minimal since agent-tree-rpc already lands the wire shape). Pitfall: subscribe_status's watch::Receiver fires aggressively (every internal state tick); happy-cli's bridge must coalesce to status-changed deltas only — verify the bridge dedupes idempotent re-emissions before fanning out to clients. Surface UX choice to operator BEFORE building: should the 'Active teammates' overlay auto-show when a session has > 0 children, or only after user opens it from a menu?\""}}},{"id":"codex-wire-spike","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T18:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"soon","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 2a Test 3 — execpolicy sandbox denial</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Deferred 2026-05-03. Local config is <code>danger-full-access</code>; needs a non-trusted dir or config flip. Verify codex sandbox enforces denial.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-low\">low</span>\n        <span>~30 min</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":110}],"command":{"name":"codex-wire-spike","descriptionHtml":"Wire-acceptance spike — resolved Gaps 2/3/5 against codex 0.125 · commit <code>06e0025b</code> · unblocks codex-attachments. Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"30-minute pre-flight spike against a real codex app-server to resolve wire-acceptance questions blocking Gaps 2, 3, 5 from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md. Spike 3 questions: (1) does codex-core accept InputItem `{ type: 'image', url: 'data:image/png;base64,...' }` or does it require `{ type: 'localImage', path }` (Gap 3)? (2) does codex-core honor `config: { project_doc_fallback_filenames: ['CLAUDE.md', 'AGENTS.md'] }` in NewConversationParams (Gap 2)? (3) what's the behavior of `compactPrompt` in NewConversationParams (Gap 5)? Method: spawn a real `codex app-server` and send hand-crafted JSON-RPC requests via netcat / node script. Read packages/happy-cli/src/codex/codexAppServerTypes.ts for the wire shape; read codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/protocol/src/lib.rs for the deserializer behavior. Output: new section 'Wire spike results' appended to plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md with concrete answers and any follow-up steps. NO production code changes — research/spike only. Acceptance: each of the 3 questions has a definitive answer in the doc (works / requires X / unsupported), with evidence (request + response payload or referenced source-line). This unblocks codex-attachments (Gap 3) and informs codex-claude-md-autoload (Gap 2) + future codex-slash-commands (Gap 5).\""}},"mergeCommit":"06e0025b"},{"id":"codex-claude-md-autoload","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-26T04:58:00Z","mergeCommit":"cb37724d","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-claude-md-autoload","descriptionHtml":"Gap 2 — project CLAUDE.md auto-load on codex path (NewConversationParams.config). Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Conflicts with <code>codex-system-prompts</code> and <code>codex-hooks-parity</code> (all touch <code>runCodex.ts</code> + <code>codexAppServerClient.ts</code>). <code>mcp-discovery</code> has landed; rebase this task before firing."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Gap 2 from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md — codex project CLAUDE.md auto-load. Today happy-cli passes `config: null` in NewConversationParams / ResumeConversationParams (codexAppServerTypes.ts:26,56) and the codex app-server therefore uses codex's default project-doc filename (AGENTS.md only). Fix: pass `config: { project_doc_fallback_filenames: ['CLAUDE.md', 'AGENTS.md'] }` in BOTH client.startThread (runCodex.ts:794) AND resumeExistingThread (runCodex.ts:725). Add a happy CLI flag `--codex-project-doc <name>` (repeatable) to packages/happy-cli/src/codex/cliArgs.ts so power users can override the dual fallback; default unset → use ['CLAUDE.md', 'AGENTS.md'] in that order (CLAUDE.md wins per codex-core's first-non-empty semantics). PREREQ DECISION: optionally consume codex-wire-spike's confirmation that codex honors project_doc_fallback_filenames in NewConversationParams; if spike not done, ship anyway — the wire field is documented in codexAppServerTypes.ts. CONFLICT WARNING: same files as mcp-discovery (currently in progress); sequence after mcp-discovery lands. Acceptance: smoke test with only CLAUDE.md present in cwd → fresh codex session reports awareness of the project doc; with both CLAUDE.md + AGENTS.md present, CLAUDE.md wins. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-claude-md.log. Single commit referencing audit Gap 2. Update plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md Gap 2 section to mark shipped.\""}}},{"id":"codex-attachments","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"paused","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-26T06:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-attachments","descriptionHtml":"Gap 3 — image attachments on codex turn input · unblocked 2026-05-14 (codex-wire-spike commit <code>06e0025b</code> resolved the wire-shape question). Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Read the \"Wire spike results\" section in <code>plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md</code> first — it documents the chosen InputItem shape for images per the codex 0.125 spike."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Gap 3 from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md — image attachments never reach codex turn input. Today packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts:321 calls messageQueue.push(message.content.text, ...) — message.content.attachments is read NOWHERE on the codex path, and sendTurnAndWait (codexAppServerClient.ts:1338-1341) hardcodes input as `[{ type: 'text', text: prompt }]`. The wire InputItem (codexAppServerTypes.ts:134-137) DOES support image inputs — happy-cli just never produces them. PREREQUISITE: codex-wire-spike must have landed and answered whether codex accepts InputItem `{ type: 'image', url: 'data:image/png;base64,...' }` (preferred — no tmpfile management) or requires `{ type: 'localImage', path }` (needs tmpfile write + cleanup). Fix: plumb MessageQueueAttachment[] through MessageQueue2's payload to runCodex.ts's main loop (mirror the Claude path's claudeRemote.ts:65-90 `toClaudeUserContent`), synthesize InputItem[] in sendTurnAndWait based on the spike's chosen wire shape. Support PNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP (same set as Claude); skip + log unsupported MIME types. Acceptance: integration test sends a PNG attachment end-to-end against a mocked codex app-server; codex agent acknowledges the image content. Cross-package typecheck green. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-attachments.log. Single commit; update plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md Gap 3 section to mark shipped.\""}}},{"id":"codex-system-prompts","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"dcb0dc8a","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T12:42:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-system-prompts","descriptionHtml":"Gap 7 — customSystemPrompt + appendSystemPrompt parity (baseInstructions/developerInstructions on codex). Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Conflicts with <code>codex-claude-md-autoload</code> and <code>codex-hooks-parity</code> (all touch <code>runCodex.ts</code> + <code>codexAppServerClient.ts</code>). <code>mcp-discovery</code> has landed; sequence after the earlier ones land."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Gap 7 from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md — customSystemPrompt + appendSystemPrompt parity on codex path. Today the codex EnhancedMode (runCodex.ts:96-100) is restricted to { permissionMode, model, thinkingLevel }; customSystemPrompt + appendSystemPrompt are tracked on the Claude side (runClaude.ts:376-377, 490-518) but NOT on codex. The codex wire protocol has NewConversationParams.baseInstructions + developerInstructions (codexAppServerTypes.ts:27-28) and ResumeConversationParams equivalents (lines 57-58) — happy-cli never sets them. Fix: extend codex EnhancedMode in runCodex.ts:96-100 with `customSystemPrompt?: string; appendSystemPrompt?: string`. Mirror Claude's per-message tracking pattern (runClaude.ts:490-518) — store currentCustomSystemPrompt + currentAppendSystemPrompt at the codex session scope; pass through to client.startThread on first turn (baseInstructions = customSystemPrompt, developerInstructions = appendSystemPrompt) AND resumeExistingThread on resume. Mid-session changes deferred to next thread start (matches Claude's 'next turn only' semantics). CONFLICT WARNING: same files as mcp-discovery / codex-claude-md-autoload; sequence after those land. Acceptance: vitest fixture in runCodex.test.ts — send a message with meta.customSystemPrompt='You are a pirate' to a fresh codex session; assert startThread called with baseInstructions='You are a pirate'; resume the thread, assert resumeExistingThread also called with the persisted baseInstructions. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-sysprompts.log. Single commit; update plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md Gap 7 section to mark shipped.\""}}},{"id":"codex-hooks-parity","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T05:16:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"className":"cmd-info","html":"<strong>Plan shipped 2026-06-01</strong> (commit <code>6e61b5b4</code> via cherry-pick from <code>2175860d</code>). 4 deliverables at <code>.ralph/jobs/codex-hooks-parity/</code>. 2 rubber-duck passes; second pass caught 4 blocking issues before commit (package-name <code>happy</code> not <code>happy-cli</code>, existing permissionHandler test mock breakage, impossible self-SHA-in-same-commit, malformed AC7 grep). Single-commit ship; Stories A (turn-lifecycle) + B (permission-prompt push) + C (audit-doc rewrite) inside. Deferred-switch protocol on codex stays out of scope per AGENTS.md Codex exclusion. Renderable permission-prompt boundary (new wire-protocol discriminator) also deferred with rationale."}],"command":{"name":"codex-hooks-parity","descriptionHtml":"Gap 4 — fan codex JSON-RPC events to happy turn-lifecycle handlers (autocompact context-boundary, permission-prompt notification). Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>. <strong>PARTIAL SHIP 2026-05-30 (commit 34cc5238/codexu 620d31a8):</strong> autocompact half wired — codex <code>thread/compacted</code> v2 + legacy <code>context_compacted</code> events both fan to <code>session.sendContextBoundary({kind:'autocompact', triggeredBy:'system'})</code>. Tests 203/203 in src/codex/. <strong>Still deferred:</strong> turn-lifecycle (<code>onTurnStarted</code>/<code>onTurnCompleted</code>) per the deferred-switch-protocol operator open item, and permission-prompt <code>Notification</code> half per the codex-exclusion documented in packages/happy-cli/AGENTS.md (codex has no Session/local-launcher/remote-launcher split, and sessionContextBoundaryKindSchema has no <code>permission-prompt</code> kind without a wire-protocol change).","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Larger task (~6-10h). Conflicts with other runCodex.ts changes — sequence after <code>codex-claude-md-autoload</code> / <code>codex-system-prompts</code> have landed so the codex path is otherwise feature-complete before adding the hook-equivalent plumbing. <code>mcp-discovery</code> has landed."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Gap 4 from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md — fan codex JSON-RPC events to the same downstream handlers Claude hooks drive (onTurnStarted, onTurnCompleted, onNotification, sendContextBoundary). Today happy-cli wires 6 Claude hooks (SessionStart/PreCompact/PostCompact/Stop/UserPromptSubmit/Notification) via generateHookSettings.ts:42-49 + runClaude.ts:265-314 to drive turn lifecycle, auto-compact boundaries, idle detection. The codex path infers turn lifecycle from task_started/task_complete/turn_aborted (runCodex.ts:605-642) but does NOT call onTurnStarted/onTurnCompleted/sendContextBoundary({ kind:'autocompact' }). Fix: wire 3 codex events to the equivalent handlers — (1) task_started → call session.onTurnStarted() (currently only toggles thinking=true at runCodex.ts:628-633); (2) task_complete/turn_aborted → call session.onTurnCompleted() — defer the deferred-switch-protocol question (operator open item, see .ralph/jobs/preserve-turn-on-mode-switch/plan.md); (3) codex's setApprovalHandler permission-request path (runCodex.ts:563-583) → emit a session.onNotification()-equivalent envelope. Auto-compact (codex-internal compaction signal) is the hardest piece — investigate whether codex emits a turn_diff or compact event we can detect; if not, defer to a follow-up. CONFLICT WARNING: sequence after mcp-discovery + codex-claude-md-autoload + codex-system-prompts have landed. Acceptance: a codex session that hits codex's internal compaction threshold emits sendContextBoundary({ kind:'autocompact', ... }) visible in the wire log; permission-prompt events fire a notification path equivalent to Claude's. Cross-package typecheck green. Tests: vitest fixtures for each of the 3 event paths. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-hooks.log. Single commit; update plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md Gap 4 section to mark shipped.\"","impl":"/implement-with-ralph --from-plan .ralph/jobs/codex-hooks-parity/plan.md --autonomous"}}},{"id":"port-explorer-prompt","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T20:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"port-explorer-prompt","descriptionHtml":"Filled empty <code>explorer.toml</code> stub with paraphrased Explore prompt · codex commit <code>e9fa64a0</code> · codexu pointer-bump <code>d279d49d</code>. Spawned by <code>native-agent-parity</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Fill the empty explorer.toml stub at codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/agent/builtins/explorer.toml with a paraphrased Explore prompt — smallest delta from plans/native-agent-parity.md §2.1. Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md 'minimize-conflict-surface' tenet, this is the only built-in role port that requires NO core/src/agent/role.rs match-arm change (file is already wired in role.rs's config_file_contents() switch). Read the inspiration source at D:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/exploreAgent.ts — paraphrase the prose per the license posture in plans/native-agent-parity.md §4 (keep structural patterns like READ-ONLY enforcement; rewrite all prose; cite inspiration in a comment header). Replace any 'Claude Code, Anthropic's CLI' references with codex-functional descriptions ('the codex agent'). Workflow: create a worktree of the codex submodule at .ralph/jobs/<job-name>/codex-worktree/ pointed at gim-home/codex's main; do edits there; commit on a topic branch in the submodule; push to gim-home/codex; then bump the codexu submodule pointer as a separate commit on codexu main. Acceptance: cargo build --workspace from codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/ stays green; the role parses (check via existing agent_roles.rs tests or a tiny new test); a 1-minute smoke 'codex exec' against the new explorer role returns a sensible exploration of a small target. Surface to operator before committing — license-paraphrase review is gating. No happy-cli or happy-app changes; pure codex-submodule edit + submodule pointer bump.\""}},"mergeCommit":"e9fa64a0,d279d49d"},{"id":"port-plan-and-verification-roles","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"8d2ae3ec6,3ff71f000,e0397548b,16a5bcb5","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T08:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"port-plan-and-verification-roles","descriptionHtml":"Add <code>plan.toml</code> + <code>verification.toml</code> built-ins + their <code>role.rs</code> match arms. Spawned by <code>native-agent-parity</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Operator must resolve <code>plans/native-agent-parity.md</code> §6 open questions 1 (role.rs edit policy — direct edits vs registration seam), 4 (workspace-write tmpdir scope for verification), and 5 (test baseline location) before this opens. Question 3 (browser-MCP availability) affects the verification prompt's content but isn't gating."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Add plan + verification built-in roles to codex per plans/native-agent-parity.md §2.2 + §2.3. New files: codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/agent/builtins/plan.toml (read-only architect role; ends each turn with 'Critical Files for Implementation' list) + codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/agent/builtins/verification.toml (workspace-write adversarial reviewer; VERDICT: PASS|FAIL|PARTIAL final-line contract). Edit codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/agent/role.rs to add two new match arms in config_file_contents() + extend the built-ins table. License posture per native-agent-parity.md §4: paraphrase all prose from D:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/planAgent.ts + verificationAgent.ts; keep structural patterns; cite inspiration in TOML comment headers. PREREQUISITES (operator-gated, must be resolved before opening): §6 Q1 role.rs edit policy (3 small match-arm edits vs design a registration seam first); §6 Q4 workspace-write tmpdir scope (verification needs $TMPDIR writes outside project tree); §6 Q5 test baseline location (codex-invariant-tests overlay vs piggyback on agent_roles.rs tests). §6 Q3 browser-MCP availability affects verification's frontend-strategy section but isn't gating — investigate codex's mcp__ namespace before writing that section. Workflow: codex-submodule worktree at .ralph/jobs/<job-name>/codex-worktree/; commit on a topic branch in gim-home/codex; bump codexu submodule pointer in a separate commit. Acceptance: cargo build --workspace green; new test (codex-invariant-tests or core agent_roles.rs test) validates both new TOMLs parse + have non-blank developer_instructions + valid permission_profile; smoke 'codex exec --agent plan' returns a 'Critical Files for Implementation' list; smoke 'codex exec --agent verification' returns a VERDICT: line. Surface to operator before merging.\""}}},{"id":"audit-general-purpose-vs-worker","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-14T08:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"audit-general-purpose-vs-worker","descriptionHtml":"Prompt-diff finding: worker role is description-only (no developer_instructions); real gaps live in the base prompt at a different layer. Spawned by <code>native-agent-parity</code>. Spawned follow-up: <code>codex-base-prompt-safety-rails</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"30-minute prompt-diff task per plans/native-agent-parity.md §2.4. Compare D:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/generalPurposeAgent.ts (or whichever file exposes Claude's general-purpose system prompt) against codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/agent/builtins/worker.toml's developer_instructions. Identify guidance present in general-purpose that's missing from worker.toml — likely candidates per the research: 'NEVER create files unless absolutely necessary', 'NEVER create documentation files unless explicitly requested', other safety-rail rules. If worker.toml is sparser, fold the missing rules into worker.toml as a small targeted change (NOT a wholesale rewrite); keep the change minimal so codex's worker behavior shifts only at the margins. If worker.toml already covers the same ground (likely — codex's worker is mature), document this in plans/native-agent-parity.md §2.4 as a no-op finding. License posture: paraphrase any rule text from Claude's general-purpose; do NOT copy verbatim. Workflow: codex-submodule worktree; commit on topic branch; bump codexu submodule pointer. Acceptance: either (a) worker.toml updated with N paraphrased rule additions referenced in commit body, OR (b) plans/native-agent-parity.md §2.4 amended with a 'no merge needed, worker.toml already covers X/Y/Z' note. Surface to operator before merging. No happy-cli/happy-app changes.\""}},"planOnly":true},{"id":"3h-tail","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"24a222825,516d2cd96,38d6e175,6acae155","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T13:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 2d — ask_user_question sibling tool</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\"><strong>Upstream codex patch.</strong> Clone/adapt <code>request_user_input</code> for structured choices. Lives in codex submodule.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">upstream codex</span>\n        <span>separate repo</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":60}],"command":{"name":"3h-tail","descriptionHtml":"Phase 3h-tail — two codex-submodule follow-ups deferred during 3h-options: (1) TUI statusline plugin slot, (2) request_user_input pre_tool_use_payload override. Spawned by <code>3h-options</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Phase 3h-tail — two codex-submodule follow-ups deferred during the 3h-options-mode plugin migration (commit 756d4290 + merge e71497eb). Per plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 3h closure. Read the deferred-items log in packages/codexu-options-mode-plugin/README.md (or the 3h-options run record in .ralph-overview/data.json — OVERVIEW_DATA.runs[] filtered to taskId='3h-options') for what's outstanding. Two distinct deferrals — surface to operator whether to ship together or split:\n\nITEM 1 — codex TUI statusline plugin slot. The codex TUI's status_line_setup.rs currently exposes NO plugin slot for plugins to contribute statusline content; the options-mode plugin had to fall back to an in-band 'options-mode: <mode>' prefix injected via SessionStart additionalContext. The proper fix is upstream-style: add a plugin-slot registration seam in codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/tui/src/status_line_setup.rs (or wherever the TUI status line is composed) that plugins can call to contribute a segment. Prefer an overlay-crate approach (codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-statusline-slots/?) over editing status_line_setup.rs directly to honor the minimize-conflict-surface tenet (plans/codexu-roadmap.md §'Codex changes — minimize upstream conflict surface'). If overlay isn't viable, fall back to a minimal sandbox-patch seam in status_line_setup.rs.\n\nITEM 2 — codex request_user_input handler override for auto-mode AskUserQuestion. The options-mode plugin's auto-mode wants to intercept AskUserQuestion (or codex's request_user_input equivalent) and respond with a default-continuation envelope so an unattended session doesn't stall. Codex currently has no override seam for request_user_input handling. Probe: codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/ for request_user_input call sites + the handler trait; design a pre_tool_use_payload() override seam that plugins can register.\n\nWorkflow (codex submodule): work in a codex-submodule worktree at .ralph/jobs/<job-name>/codex-worktree/ pointed at gim-home/codex's main; do edits there on a topic branch; push to gim-home/codex; bump the codexu submodule pointer as a separate commit on codexu main. Cargo build --workspace from codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/ must stay green. For each item: surface design choice to operator BEFORE landing — overlay-crate vs sandbox-patch seam, registration shape, default behavior. Acceptance: (1) options-mode plugin can register a TUI statusline segment via the new slot, and the statusline-script fallback in packages/codexu-options-mode-plugin/apps/statusline/ is reduced or eliminated; (2) options-mode plugin's auto-mode hook can override request_user_input to inject a default-continuation envelope without the existing transcript-scan workaround. Tests under codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-invariant-tests/ if a new overlay crate is created. NO happy-cli or happy-app changes — purely codex-submodule + the options-mode plugin's hook code consuming the new seams.\""}}},{"id":"session-parent-link-writer","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-27T07:47:00Z","mergeCommit":"cc32aa13,86562252","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"session-parent-link-writer","descriptionHtml":"CLI writer wiring for parentSessionId — populate the metadata fields the read path (session-parent-link) now reads. Spawned by <code>session-parent-link</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Wire the CLI writer side for the parentSessionId + spawnedChildren[] metadata fields shipped by session-parent-link (commit 11c3eafb, read path only). Today the schema accepts those fields and the helpers (getSessionParent, getSessionChildren) work end-to-end — but no code populates them on the wire, so the storage round-trips empty/null defaults forever. Per the session-parent-link plan's Open Questions, the writer side was explicitly deferred to this task. Touch points (per the plan + agent-view-research.md §6): (1) packages/happy-app/sources/sync/ops.ts createSessionMetadata — when the app spawns a new session that has a logical parent (e.g., via spawn-from-app), set parentSessionId on creation; (2) packages/happy-cli/src/daemon/forkSession.ts — when a session is forked via HAPPY_FORKED_FROM_SESSION_ID, set parentSessionId on the child + add the child's sid to the parent's spawnedChildren via apiSession.updateMetadata; (3) packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts — when codex spawns a sub-agent (via multi_agents_v2/spawn.rs) the new thread that gets a happy session should carry the parent's compositeSessionId; (4) packages/happy-cli/src/api/apiSession.ts updateMetadata — ensure the path can write parentSessionId/spawnedChildren without overwriting other fields. Cycle prevention: when setting parentSessionId, walk up the parent chain (max-depth hop counter, e.g., 16) to reject cycles; document the limit. Read packages/happy-app/AGENTS.md sync invariants (composite-id rules via machineSessionId.ts must be respected). Read the session-parent-link run record in .ralph-overview/data.json — OVERVIEW_DATA.runs[] filtered to taskId='session-parent-link' for the existing read-path landing's scope. Workflow: codexu worktree at .worktrees/session-parent-link-writer/ on a topic branch ralph/session-parent-link-writer off origin/main. Acceptance: integration test in happy-cli — fork a session via HAPPY_FORKED_FROM_SESSION_ID, assert new session has parentSessionId set + parent's metadata.spawnedChildren includes the new sid; cycle test — try to set parent on a session that's already a transitive ancestor, assert rejection. Cross-package typecheck stays green. Single commit on topic branch; push; surface for merge.\""}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-05-27T07:47:00Z","summary":"Wired fork-session parent-link metadata so child sessions record their logical parent and parent sessions can track spawned children. Added daemon documentation for the parent-link postconditions that future fork/spawn work relies on.","commits":[{"sha":"cc32aa13","oneLine":"feat: [US-001..US-004] - Wire forkSession parent-link metadata","repo":"codexu"},{"sha":"86562252","oneLine":"docs(happy-cli/daemon): surface fork-into-worktree parent-link postconditions","repo":"codexu"}]}},{"id":"codex-base-prompt-safety-rails","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-25T12:00:00Z","mergeCommit":"9fe9b81,b0e55a72","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 7 — Claude model via Copilot adapter</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Deferred, spike-first. <code>codex login</code> + <code>curl</code> Copilot <code>/models</code> + branch table. Wide effort range pending spike.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex</span>\n        <span>spike pending</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":100}],"command":{"name":"codex-base-prompt-safety-rails","descriptionHtml":"Inject Claude's safety rails ('NEVER create files...' + 'NEVER proactively create docs...') into codex base prompt via launcher additional_instructions. Spawned by <code>audit-general-purpose-vs-worker</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Inject Claude's two safety rails into codex's main-agent base prompt as launcher-injected additional_instructions (overlay path per AGENTS.override.md tenet 1). Per audit-general-purpose-vs-worker finding (plans/native-agent-parity.md §2.4). Gap: Claude's general-purpose prompt has 'NEVER create files unless absolutely necessary' + 'NEVER proactively create *.md / README files' (D:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/src/tools/AgentTool/built-in/generalPurposeAgent.ts:15-16); codex's main-agent base prompt at codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/prompts/gpt_5_2_prompt.md:156,158 has only weak abstract analogues ('surgical precision', 'don't gold-plate'). The audit established the correct fix-site is launcher-injected additional_instructions (NOT a worker-only TOML — that would skip the main-agent flow). Implementation: investigate the codex launcher seam — likely codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-copilot-launcher/ (the precedent overlay crate) is the right place to add a `--additional-instructions` or equivalent flag that codex-core threads into the base prompt. Paraphrase the rule text (do NOT copy verbatim — license posture per plans/native-agent-parity.md §4); cite inspiration in the launcher's docs/source comment. Workflow (codex submodule): worktree at .ralph/jobs/<job-name>/codex-worktree/ pointed at gim-home/codex's main; commit on topic branch; push to gim-home/codex; bump the codexu submodule pointer as a separate commit on codexu main. PREREQUISITE INVESTIGATION: confirm whether codex already exposes an additional_instructions plumbing point, or whether the overlay crate needs to add one. Surface the choice (overlay-vs-upstream-patch) to operator BEFORE landing. Acceptance: cargo build --workspace green; new test asserting the additional_instructions reach the model's system prompt at session start; smoke 'codex exec' with a prompt asking to create a doc verifies the agent declines or asks first. NO happy-cli or happy-app changes — purely codex-submodule + the launcher overlay.\""}}},{"id":"codex-child-spawn-tools","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-26T04:25:00Z","mergeCommit":"23d43f93","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-child-spawn-tools","descriptionHtml":"Spike: do codex child agents get <code>spawn_agent</code>/<code>wait_agent</code> in their tool surface? Decision input for plugin-scope-agents + agent-comms. Spawned by <code>agent-tree-rpc</code> (US-006 AC4 gap).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Investigation spike — surfaced by agent-tree-rpc's unmet US-006 AC4 (commit 11bc1ff4; see .ralph-overview/data.json OVERVIEW_DATA.runs[] filtered to taskId='agent-tree-rpc'). The agent-tree-rpc agent tried to test nested A→B spawn (codex top-level spawns child, child spawns grandchild) and discovered the pinned codex submodule does NOT expose spawn_agent / wait_agent in child agents' tool surface. Investigate: (1) Is this an upstream codex design choice or an artifact of the pinned version? Look in codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/multi_agents_v2/ for the tool-registration site (spawn.rs + the parent/child role distinction). (2) If upstream HEAD exposes these tools to children, recommend a submodule bump (and surface scope to operator). If not, design an overlay crate (codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-child-spawn-tools/?) that enables them — sketch the registration seam + permission-profile implications. (3) Decision context: does codexu's roadmap actually need child→grandchild spawning, or is top-level-only spawning sufficient? Cross-reference plans/agent-view-research.md §6 (the agent-view-research follow-ups assume parent→child works) and plans/codexu-roadmap.md §Phase 2c (plugin-scope-agents) — if plugin-scope-agents adopts the agent-spawner pattern (one designated top-level agent spawns top-level sessions for the operator), child-spawn-tools may be unnecessary; if agent-comms Scope C (parent-spawned-child) needs in-session spawning, child-spawn-tools is required. Output: new doc plans/codex-child-spawn-tools.md with (a) factual findings (what upstream codex does, what pinned codex does, where the divergence is), (b) decision matrix for our roadmap (top-level-only vs child-can-spawn — affects plugin-scope-agents AND agent-comms scope), (c) recommended path (bump, overlay, or accept). Surface to operator BEFORE plugin-scope-agents fires — plugin-scope-agents is currently parallel-safe but its scoping model depends on this. NO code changes — research only. The ralph job for this spike should NOT require codex-submodule edits; reading is fine. ~2-4h.\""}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-05-26T04:25:00Z","summary":"Documented the child-agent spawn-tool research spike and decision matrix for whether codex child agents need their own spawn_agent/wait_agent surface. The shipped artifact informs plugin-scope-agents and agent-comms planning without changing runtime code.","commits":[{"sha":"23d43f93","oneLine":"docs(plans): codex-child-spawn-tools research spike","repo":"codexu"}]}},{"id":"codex-slash-commands","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"a3f3d60e","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T10:40:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Phase 5 — Drop Claude Code (5a–5f)</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">Dual-stack dogfood buffer, audit remaining flows, archive configs, uninstall, update personal docs. Gated on Phase 4 passing.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-multi\">cutover</span>\n        <span>post-Phase-4</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":80}],"command":{"name":"codex-slash-commands","descriptionHtml":"Gap 5 — `/clear` and `/compact` parity on the codex path. Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Blocked on <code>codex-hooks-parity</code> — Gap 5's <code>/compact</code> handling depends on Gap 4's auto-compact context-boundary emission so the manual <code>/compact</code> also fires <code>sendContextBoundary({ kind:'compact', triggeredBy:'user' })</code>."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Gap 5 from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md — /clear and /compact parity on the codex path. Today packages/happy-cli/src/codex/runCodex.ts:280-334 pushes the user message text into messageQueue with NO slash-command parsing — parseSpecialCommand (shared with the Claude path at runClaude.ts:541-577) is never imported on the codex side. Even if a user types /compact on a codex session, codex-core sees the literal string and codex-core's own slash-command handling may or may not fire. Codex's wire surface supports compact via NewConversationParams.compactPrompt (codexAppServerTypes.ts:29) but client.startThread (codexAppServerClient.ts) never sets it. PREREQUISITE: codex-hooks-parity (Gap 4) must have landed so the auto-compact context-boundary emission is in place — manual /compact should also fire sendContextBoundary({ kind:'compact', triggeredBy:'user' }) to keep the UI signal consistent. STEP 1 spike (~30 min): does codex-core's own slash-command handling fire when the user message is /clear or /compact? If YES → document + add a smoke test; pure happy-cli passthrough is fine. If NO → STEP 2: implement happy-cli-side interception via parseSpecialCommand (shared with Claude) — on /clear call client.disconnect() + start a fresh thread (verify with codex maintainer that this is intended UX); on /compact plumb a codex-side compact RPC if one exists, OR set compactPrompt on the NEXT startThread / similar. Also handle the wrapped form <command-name>/clear</command-name> in sessionProtocolMapper.ts (matches the Claude path's detectWrappedSlashCommandBoundary at packages/happy-cli/AGENTS.md 'Wrapped-slash-command detection (F-012 / F-013)'). Acceptance: typing /clear on a happy codex session results in a fresh thread; typing /compact triggers codex's compact path AND emits sendContextBoundary({ kind:'compact', triggeredBy:'user' }). Workflow: codexu worktree at .worktrees/codex-slash-commands/ on topic branch ralph/codex-slash-commands. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-slash.log. Single commit; update plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md Gap 5 section to mark shipped.\""}}},{"id":"codex-plan-mode-defensive","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"48fac386","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T13:20:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-plan-mode-defensive","descriptionHtml":"Gap 6 — defensive plan-mode mapping on codex path (v1, ~2h). V2 overlay-crate deferred. Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Operator-gated. Per <code>plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md</code> Gap 6 — two-tier choice: (a) defensive parity now (map <code>plan</code> → codex <code>read-only</code> sandbox + UX hint that ExitPlanMode isn't available, ~2h) OR (b) full parity via a new overlay crate <code>codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-plan-mode/</code> that injects an <code>exit_plan_mode</code> tool (Medium-to-Large, spike upstream codex's tool-registration seam first). Decide before scoping. Audit recommended (a) for v1."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Gap 6 from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md — defensive plan-mode mapping on codex path. Today packages/happy-cli/src/codex/cliArgs.ts VALID_CODEX_REMOTE_PERMISSION_MODES (re-exported as VALID_REMOTE_PERMISSION_MODES in runCodex.ts:251) is restricted to default, read-only, safe-yolo, yolo — `plan` arriving from the mobile UI is silently ignored (runCodex.ts:287-294); the local CLI flag path falls into the defensive branch in executionPolicy.ts:24,39 returning untrusted+workspace-write. There is no ExitPlanMode codex tool. PREREQUISITE (operator-gated): confirm v1 scope is option (a) DEFENSIVE — map `plan` → codex `read-only` sandbox in executionPolicy.ts, AND emit a UI hint (session-event 'message') on first plan-mode entry: 'Plan mode on codex is approximated as read-only; ExitPlanMode tool is not available.' If operator wants option (b) overlay-crate full parity instead, defer this task and open a separate `codex-plan-mode-overlay` ralph command that lands an overlay crate codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-plan-mode/ providing real plan-mode parity (model gets an exit_plan_mode tool, mode transitions on tool call) — spike upstream codex's tool-registration seam first. Acceptance for v1 (a): switching a codex session to plan mode results in sandbox: read-only, approvalPolicy: never, and the user sees the explanatory message; the existing mobile-UI plan-mode badge still renders correctly. Cross-package typecheck green. Test command: pnpm --filter '{packages/happy-cli}' exec vitest run 2>&1 | tee /tmp/codexu-plan-mode.log. Single commit referencing audit Gap 6. Update plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md Gap 6 section to mark v1-defensive shipped; flag v2-overlay as still deferred. Workflow: codexu worktree at .worktrees/codex-plan-mode-defensive/ on topic branch ralph/codex-plan-mode-defensive off origin/main.\""}}},{"id":"codex-polish-lows","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"a7107e44,60f770c7,d79186e1","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T13:42:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-polish-lows","descriptionHtml":"Bundle of Gaps 8/9/11/12 (tool gating partial + codex-args passthrough + statusline + SDK init-metadata mirror). Spawned by <code>codex-parity-audit</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Best to land after the bigger codex-* parity follow-ups (mcp-discovery, codex-claude-md-autoload, codex-system-prompts, codex-hooks-parity) so this bundles cleanly without rebase churn. Conflicts with anything else touching <code>runCodex.ts</code>."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Bundle the 4 Low-severity codex-parity gaps from plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md into a single polish PR. The audit recommended this packaging explicitly: '8. Gaps 8, 9, 11, 12 are all small and can land in a single polish PR if desired.' One commit per gap so each can be reverted independently.\n\nGAP 8 — codex-tool-gating-partial. Track allowedTools/disallowedTools in codex EnhancedMode (runCodex.ts:96-100, currently restricted to { permissionMode, model, thinkingLevel }); at startThread, filter the mcpServers entries (whole-server granularity — drop entries whose tools are entirely disallowed). True per-tool-within-server filtering deferred to a follow-up overlay crate.\n\nGAP 9 — codex-args-passthrough. Add repeatable --codex-arg <flag> to packages/happy-cli/src/codex/cliArgs.ts (alongside existing --effort, --model, --permission-mode, --codex-transport, --resume). Forward to the `codex app-server` spawn argv. Power-user escape hatch — document in packages/happy-cli/AGENTS.md that structured flags are preferred.\n\nGAP 11 — codex-statusline-metadata-mirror. Extend the metadata-merge in both runCodex.ts:796-800 AND resumeExistingThread.ts to include model, modelProvider, approvalPolicy, sandbox, reasoningEffort from NewConversationResponse / ResumeConversationResponse (codexAppServerTypes.ts:35-47) — currently only codexThreadId is written. Match the Claude side's mergeSDKInitMetadata semantics.\n\nGAP 12 — codex-sdk-init-metadata-mirror. Investigate the codex initialize RPC's full response shape (server capabilities, available tools, slash commands) and propagate those into Session.metadata so the mobile app can render them. Likely a small extension of the metadata-merge from Gap 11.\n\nCONFLICT WARNING: all 4 gaps touch runCodex.ts and/or resumeExistingThread.ts — sequence after mcp-discovery / codex-claude-md-autoload / codex-system-prompts / codex-hooks-parity have landed to avoid rebase churn. Workflow: codexu worktree at .worktrees/codex-polish-lows/ on topic branch ralph/codex-polish-lows off origin/main. 4 commits (one per gap), all referencing the corresponding audit-doc section. Cross-package typecheck green. Each gap has its own acceptance test. Update plans/codex-agent-parity-audit.md Gap 8/9/11/12 sections to mark shipped.\""}}},{"id":"mcp-server-notifications","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"bd98f77,7a51ef8c,2d5a4864","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T17:31:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"mcp-server-notifications","descriptionHtml":"Stage A: bridge rmcp notification handlers + sampling into codex agent event loop, feature-gated. Spawned by <code>channels-research</code>.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Plumb MCP server-initiated notifications (progress, cancelled, resources/updated, resources/list_changed, tools/list_changed, prompts/list_changed, message) AND sampling/createMessage into the codex agent event loop. Today codex's rmcp-client (codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/rmcp-client/) decodes every one of these but the handlers in src/logging_client_handler.rs:49-135 are tracing-only — they never reach the agent. The elicitation path at codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/codex-mcp/src/elicitation.rs:103-231 is the working precedent: emit Event { msg: EventMsg::… } over the tx_event: Sender<Event> sink that's already wired in at codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/codex-mcp/src/connection_manager.rs:175. READ-ONLY on codex/external/repos/codex-patched/ per minimize-conflict-surface tenet (plans/codexu-roadmap.md:190-228); prefer a new overlay crate at codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-mcp-bridge/ for the NotificationBridge struct + EventMsg additions; accept minimal sandbox-patch seams in logging_client_handler.rs (~12 lines additive) and elicitation_client_service.rs (~30 lines for sampling/createMessage). Add a new Feature::McpServerNotifications gate (default off). Add an invariant test under codex/codex-rs-overlay/codex-invariant-tests/ asserting the bridge is off by default. Bump codex-protocol with new EventMsg variants (McpServerNotification, McpSamplingRequest). Effort estimate per plans/channels-research.md §6.5: ~3-5 d wall. Read plans/channels-research.md FIRST — it has the full file:line map, the overlay-vs-inline decision, and the risk surface. Surface the overlay-vs-inline-patch shape choice to operator BEFORE landing code.\""}}},{"id":"codex-channels","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"deferred-stage-b-2026-06-01-option-B-chosen-instead","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T13:32:00Z","regressionReason":"Option B chosen 2026-06-01 (plan 635248c6/bbc49ceb). Stage A mcp-server-notifications (bd98f77/7a51ef8c/2d5a4864) already delivers state-change push for the named use cases (file watcher, dynamic tools) via EventMsg::McpServerNotification. Channels framing is specifically for external-user-message ingress (Slack/Telegram/SMS bridge) — narrower applicability, no concrete chat-bridge proposal on roadmap. Reopen if such use case lands. Replacement: codex-channels-option-b (notifications-to-prompt-queue on Stage A, happy-cli only, zero codex Rust touches).","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-channels","descriptionHtml":"Stage B: Claude-Code-parity <code>experimental[\"codex/channel\"]</code> envelope + prompt-queue policy. Spawned by <code>channels-research</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 Deferred. Blocked on <code>mcp-server-notifications</code> landing AND on operator deciding whether channels (external-user-message envelope) are the right primitive vs raw notifications (which cover state-change push perfectly without the channels framing)."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Design + implement Claude-Code-parity 'channels' on top of mcp-server-notifications: capability key experimental['codex/channel'] advertised by participating MCP servers, notification methods notifications/codex/channel and notifications/codex/channel/permission mirroring Claude Code's wire shape at D:/harness-efforts/claude-code/worktrees/main/src/services/mcp/channelNotification.ts:37-316. Agent-loop policy: when a channel message arrives between turns, enqueue as a prompt with priority:'next' semantics; when one arrives during an LLM call, buffer until the call completes (matches Claude Code's non-preemption behaviour). Permission half is out of scope for first cut — codex doesn't have a real-time permission dialog with race resolution to relay to. Use codex/ namespace, not claude/ (cross-vendor experimental keys aren't coordinated). READ plans/channels-research.md §6.2 FIRST — it has the full design. READ-ONLY on codex/external/repos/codex-patched/ per minimize-conflict-surface tenet. Effort estimate per plans/channels-research.md §6.5: ~1.5-2 d on top of mcp-server-notifications. SURFACE TO OPERATOR FIRST whether channels (as opposed to plain notifications) are wanted for the intended use cases — channels are specifically the 'external-user pipes messages into prompt queue' shape and may be narrower than the operator needs.\""}}},{"id":"codexu-mcp-config-regenerate-with-env-block","scope":"bookkeeping|codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"46d3fe52","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T15:15:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codexu-mcp-config-regenerate-with-env-block","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-ralph-overview-watcher-copilot-cli-env-verify</code> (2026-05-30, fea7520e). Codexu's own <code>D:/harness-efforts/codexu/.mcp.json</code> did NOT carry the <code>env.RALPH_OVERVIEW_REPO_ROOT</code> block that v2.4.1+ ralph-overview requires for AC-1a to work. Every Copilot session start logged <code>Failed to start MCP client for ralph-overview</code>. <strong>Shipped 2026-05-31 as <code>46d3fe52</code></strong> — added <code>env.RALPH_OVERVIEW_REPO_ROOT=D:/harness-efforts/codexu</code> to the ralph-overview server entry in codexu's .mcp.json.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Run the overview.init MCP tool (or the equivalent CLI command per ralph-overview v2.6.0 docs) in D:/harness-efforts/codexu. Verify .mcp.json now has env.RALPH_OVERVIEW_REPO_ROOT=D:/harness-efforts/codexu in the ralph-overview server block. Restart Copilot CLI session to verify MCP server boots clean and overview.parallel_ready_tasks responds. Commit + push the .mcp.json update."}}},{"id":"task-expansion-skill","scope":"ralph-overview|bookkeeping","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"63a347f4,0c8047f3","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T16:58:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"task-expansion-skill","descriptionHtml":"Operator-favored task-overview format proven useful 2026-05-31. Bookkeeper produced an ad-hoc expansion of <code>impl-mcp-server-notifications</code> + <code>plan-port-plan-and-verification-roles</code> using a 4-section template: <strong>What it is</strong> (concrete scope), <strong>Why it matters</strong> (downstream impact), <strong>Upstream-conflict surface</strong> (per-file/per-seam rebase liability with mitigation), <strong>What unblocks it / what it unblocks</strong> (prerequisite work + downstream dependents). Operator response: \"I really like this overview... we should make it into a skill.\" Build as a reusable skill so bookkeeper sessions (and other agents picking tasks) emit consistent task-expansion analyses for any backlog item. Likely lives in ralph-overview plugin or a new dedicated skill under D:/ai-developer-toolkit/plugins/.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"brainstorm":"/brainstorm-with-ralph \"Design a reusable 'task-expansion' skill that, given a tracked task id (or set of ids), produces a structured Markdown overview with these required sections: (1) What it is — concrete user-facing + technical scope, cite source files where relevant; (2) Why it matters — downstream impact, user-perceived value, what gets unblocked once shipped; (3) Upstream-conflict surface — for fork-relevant code (codex/, packages/happy-{cli,app,server,wire}/), per-file rebase liability, overlay-vs-canonical mitigation, recent rebase pain points if any; (4) Prerequisites — what previous work unblocked this task (look at lifecycle=merged tasks with shipped commits that touched relevant files); (5) Unblocks — what downstream tasks become spawn-ready once this lands.\\n\\nData sources to consult: .ralph-overview/data.json (task descriptionHtml, warnings, prompts.brainstorm/plan/impl, kanbanCards.column), .ralph/jobs/<id>/plan.md if it exists, git log --grep='<task-id>' across codexu + ai-developer-toolkit + codex submodule, codex/CLAUDE.md for the minimize-conflict-surface tenet, and operator preferences from .agents/memory or AGENTS.md.\\n\\nKey design questions to surface: (a) where does the skill live — new plugin vs extend ralph-overview vs extend crews? (b) is it an MCP tool (callable by any agent) or a CLI/skill invocation? (c) how does it auto-discover prerequisites — git log heuristic, plan-doc reference scan, or operator-curated 'unblocks' field in data.json schema? (d) does it write a sidecar (e.g. .ralph-overview/generated/task-expansions/<id>.md) or only emit on-demand? (e) skill output format: pure Markdown for terminal/chat vs structured JSON for programmatic consumers vs both?\\n\\nProduce selected-direction.md with the chosen approach and the operator-mandate 'always show 5 sections' contract.\""}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-rusty-v8-archive-fetch","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T03:05:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-rusty-v8-archive-fetch","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-codex-upstream-rebase-v2</code> (2026-05-30). Upstream <code>rust-v0.135.0</code> bumped <code>v8 = \"=147.4.0\"</code> from <code>146.4.0</code>. Local cache at <code>~/.cargo/.rusty_v8/</code> has only <code>v146.4.0.lib</code>; <code>v147.4.0</code> missing. Blocks <code>cargo build --release -p codex-cli</code> (publish path). Per operator: fetch into <strong>D: drive cache</strong>, not C:.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Fetch rusty_v8_release_x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.lib.gz from https://github.com/denoland/rusty_v8/releases/download/v147.4.0/rusty_v8_release_x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.lib.gz — decompress to ~/.cargo/.rusty_v8/rusty_v8_release_x86_64-pc-windows-msvc_v147.4.0.lib. If CARGO_HOME is on C: but space-constrained, redirect to D:/.cargo via env. Single 1-command task; can be done by operator directly or scripted into codexu fork-setup scripts."}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-02T03:05:00Z","summary":"Effectively-done on arrival: the rusty_v8_release_x86_64-pc-windows-msvc_v147.4.0.lib artifact was already present at ~/.cargo/.rusty_v8/ (193.8 MB, dated 2026-05-31) -- prior r6 build that produced the first working codex.exe pre-fetched the v147 pin during its setup. Verified by lead 2026-06-01T20:05Z via Get-ChildItem. No code commits required; task was overtaken by events. v146.4.0 archive also present (192.6 MB, dated 2026-05-30) -- safe to retain as fallback or delete once the v0.135.0 pin is fully stable.","commits":[]}},{"id":"codex-rebase-rustup-1-95-install-d-drive","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"resolved-rustup-1-95-already-installed-locally","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T21:45:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-rustup-1-95-install-d-drive","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-codex-upstream-rebase-v2</code> (2026-05-30). <code>codex-rs/rust-toolchain.toml</code> now pins <code>channel = \"1.95.0\"</code> (post-v0.135 rebase). <code>rustup toolchain install 1.95.0-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc</code> failed with <em>Insufficient system resources (os error 1450)</em> mid-rename — C: drive had 14.8 GB free. Blocks <code>cargo check --workspace</code> + <code>/publish-sandbox-patch</code> local verify. Falling back to 1.93 fails because <code>sqlx 0.9.0</code> requires rustc ≥1.94.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Set RUSTUP_HOME=D:\\.rustup + CARGO_HOME=D:\\.cargo (preserving any existing local toolchains via copy if needed). Then: rustup toolchain install 1.95.0-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc. Verify with rustup show. If CARGO_HOME relocation is too invasive, just symlink ~/.rustup to a D: path. Document the env var pinning in scripts/fork-setup/."}}},{"id":"escalate-gim-home-actions-policy","scope":"codex|ops","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"blocked","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T12:25:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Unblock gim-home/codex GitHub Actions (org policy escalation)</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">100/100 recent CI runs exit startup_failure since at least 2026-04-07. Diagnosed as org-level Actions policy refusal — not code. Operator/org-admin action only.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-ops\">ops</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-high\">blocked on org-admin</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":5},{"column":"blocked","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">On unblock: run rebase skill Sunset checklist</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">workflow_dispatch invariant-check on gim-home/codex@main HEAD, then amend codex/.claude/commands/rebase-upstream.md Phase 5b interim subsection per its <code>### Sunset checklist (self-triggering)</code>. Cross-check every rebase task that closed under the interim path.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-ops\">ops</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-med\">post-unblock</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":6}],"command":{"name":"escalate-gim-home-actions-policy","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-06-01 by <code>diagnose-codex-ci-startup-failure-r2</code>. <strong>Root cause</strong>: org-level GitHub Actions policy on <code>gim-home</code> blocks ALL workflow dispatches on <code>gim-home/codex</code> repo. 100/100 recent runs are zero-job non-rerequestable startup_failure (the canonical fingerprint of org-policy rejection at dispatch time, NOT YAML/runner/secrets issues). Pattern goes back to at least 2026-04-07 (last successful run was 2026-04-07 'blob-size-policy' — likely pre-policy-tightening). <strong>Fix is ops-only</strong>; no code change possible. Three hypotheses (likelihood order): (1) allowed-actions allowlist excludes third-party actions like <code>dtolnay/rust-toolchain</code> + <code>Swatinem/rust-cache</code>; (2) selected-repos allowlist doesn't include <code>gim-home/codex</code>; (3) repo-level Actions disabled (<code>has_actions:null</code> is suspicious). <strong>Blocks</strong>: every CI workflow on gim-home/codex including <code>publish-npm</code> (means no new codex.exe releases ship from this fork until fixed) + <code>invariant-check</code> + Dependabot.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"🔒 <strong>Blocked on org admin</strong>. Operator must visit <code>https://github.com/organizations/gim-home/settings/actions</code> to either: (a) update the allowed-actions allowlist to include <code>dtolnay/rust-toolchain@*, Swatinem/rust-cache@*, actions/*</code> etc., OR (b) add <code>gim-home/codex</code> to selected-repos allowlist, OR (c) enable repo-level Actions via Settings → Actions → General. If org won't move: workaround is relocating publish workflow to a non-gim-home repo."}],"prompts":{"impl":"OPS-ONLY task — no impl member to spawn. Operator escalates to gim-home org admin to fix the GitHub Actions policy per the description. After fix, verify with: gh run list --repo gim-home/codex --limit 3 (should show queued/in_progress/success status instead of startup_failure for the next workflow trigger). Then mark this task merged with mergeCommit: org-policy-fix-applied-YYYY-MM-DD."}}},{"id":"codex-rs-build-time-and-cache-resilience","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"2f2f402a1,edb6ef2e","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T12:35:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rs-build-time-and-cache-resilience","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-06-01 by <code>build-codex-release-exe-r6</code> after 2h 47m cold-cache build of codex.exe. Build-iteration pain: env-var changes (LTO=thin→off, jobs=2→1) invalidate cargo cache wholesale. Ranked highest-leverage fixes per member: (1) install + wire <code>sccache</code> as <code>RUSTC_WRAPPER</code> with cache dir on D: — would have made r6 a ~3-min rebuild instead of 2h 47m; (2) codify a frozen 'iteration profile' in codex/CLAUDE.md (LTO + codegen-units + jobs values that don't change round-to-round); (3) raise jobs back to 4-6 with LTO=off confirmed not to OOM; (4) Windows pagefile ≥32 GB; (5) <code>RUSTFLAGS=\"-C debuginfo=0\"</code> for try-it builds (~30% less RAM, ~20% less wall-time).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Improve codex-rs local build time + cache resilience after the build-codex-release-exe-r6 retrospective (2h 47m cold build burned by env-var-induced cache invalidation). 5 ranked fixes per the r6 member: (1) sccache install + RUSTC_WRAPPER wire-up with cache dir on D: drive (highest-leverage); (2) frozen iteration profile in codex/CLAUDE.md so LTO/codegen-units/jobs don't drift round-to-round; (3) jobs=4-6 with LTO=off (currently jobs=1 leaves 15 cores idle); (4) Windows pagefile ≥32 GB; (5) RUSTFLAGS=-C debuginfo=0 for local 'try-it' builds. Plan should decide bundle-vs-split, verify sccache supports the LLVM cross-toolchain we use, and pin a benchmark methodology (build A: before fixes, build B: after; measure both cold and warm cache).\""}}},{"id":"codex-rs-iteration-env-sccache-race-and-jobs-default","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T19:14:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rs-iteration-env-sccache-race-and-jobs-default","descriptionHtml":"Combined fix from F-5 + jobs-sweep winner (benchmark-codex-rs-jobs-sweep-with-defender, artifacts at .ralph/jobs/codex-rs-build-perf-jobs-sweep-with-defender/results.md). Two changes to <code>codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh</code>: (1) <strong>F-5 sccache race</strong>: current <code>sccache --stop-server && --start-server</code> reconciliation leaves the server transiently unreachable for ~5m of build time; jobs=6/12 cold runs registered only 21 sccache requests vs 1705 in jobs=4/8 same setup. Fix: poll <code>sccache --show-stats</code> until ready (server-version line present, exit 0) before returning from reconciliation. (2) <strong>jobs default 4 → 8</strong>: benchmark winner at 50:12 cold (vs 56:26 at jobs=4) with sccache wrapper active and 8.2 GB peak RSS (56 GB headroom on 64 GB box).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Two changes to D:/harness-efforts/codexu/codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh: (1) F-5 sccache race fix — after the existing sccache --stop-server / --start-server reconciliation, add a poll loop: `for i in {1..30}; do sccache --show-stats >/dev/null 2>&1 && break; sleep 0.5; done`. Time-box to 15s. If still not ready after timeout, emit a WARNING (don't fail — fall back to no-sccache build). (2) Change CARGO_BUILD_JOBS default from 4 to 8 — update both the export line and the FROZEN ITERATION PROFILE ACTIVE banner. Single docs-tier commit on wrapper repo (gim-home/codex). Lead bumps codexu submodule pointer. Phase 5b docs convergence."}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-01T19:14:00Z","summary":"Fixed two issues found in jobs-sweep benchmark: (1) F-5 sccache --start-server race where server was transiently unreachable post-restart, causing jobs=6/jobs=12 cold runs to register only 21 sccache requests vs 1705 for jobs=4/jobs=8 — added a 15s poll loop (30 × 0.5s) that waits for sccache --show-stats to respond, with WARN-not-fail fallback. (2) Bumped CARGO_BUILD_JOBS default 4→8 to match the jobs-sweep winner (50:12 cold, 8.2 GB peak RSS — 56 GB headroom on the 64 GB box). Banner already interpolated  dynamically so no banner-text edit needed; header comment + inline rationale updated. Single-file wrapper-only edit on codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh (+21 −4). Phase 5b docs verified: zero remaining jobs=4 references in script. Codexu submodule pointer bumped to d8e4dad15. Expected impact: ~5m cold-build savings (50m → ~45m) once the new defaults exercise on the next cold cycle, plus elimination of the non-deterministic sccache-bypass flake at higher jobs counts.","commits":[{"sha":"d8e4dad151dbe8e9be9f11cce05b6f93ea648111","oneLine":"fix(iteration-env): poll sccache server post-restart + jobs default 4->8","repo":"gim-home/codex"},{"sha":"7142353e","oneLine":"chore(codex): bump pointer to d8e4dad15","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"}]}},{"id":"codex-rs-defender-exclusions-procmon-verify","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T19:03:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rs-defender-exclusions-procmon-verify","descriptionHtml":"F-6 finding from benchmark-codex-rs-jobs-sweep-with-defender. Defender exclusions (path: target/, sccache cache, .cargo, .rustup, .xwin, .rusty_v8, codex wrapper; process: rustc.exe cargo.exe sccache.exe link.exe lld-link.exe clang-cl.exe) delivered NO measurable build-perf benefit at jobs=4 (56:26 with vs 53:00 without — within noise, actually slightly slower). Per benchmark recommendation: run Procmon on jobs=8 cold to verify MpEng.exe isn't still touching D:\\codex-sccache despite exclusions. If genuinely no-op, drop exclusions to reduce machine-state divergence. If exclusions ARE being applied but other Defender component (real-time AMSI, cloud-protection, etc.) is the culprit, document the root cause.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Operator-driven Procmon capture. (1) Launch Procmon, set filters: Path contains 'codex-sccache' OR 'codex-rs/target' OR 'sccache.exe', AND Process Name is MpEng.exe (or any Defender process). (2) Run scripts/measure-build.ps1 with the jobs=8 frozen iteration profile for ~5-10 min (don't need full cold; partial run is sufficient to capture pattern). (3) Stop Procmon, save .pml. (4) Analyze: are MpEng.exe operations on the excluded paths zero (exclusions working) or non-zero (exclusions ignored)? (5) If zero: file followup to remove the Defender exclusions from publish-sandbox-patch.md OPTIONAL preconditions since they're no-op on this hardware. If non-zero: investigate why exclusions are ignored (admin context? GPO override? cloud-delivered protection?)."}}},{"id":"codex-rs-feature-pruning-for-sub-45m-cold","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T05:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"className":"cmd-info","html":"<strong>Plan shipped 2026-06-01</strong> (commit <code>bf2c89be</code> via cherry-pick from <code>6fe40c3c</code>). 5 user stories in 4 clusters / 3 phases. Multi-model review (Claude+Codex+Copilot) found 15 findings; all 6 Highs fixed in plan; 8 Mediums + 1 Low deferred as Open Questions. <strong>Key correction:</strong> seed prompt assumed wrapper-only CARGO_FEATURES_TO_DISABLE env would work; cargo --no-default-features -p codex-cli does NOT propagate to codex-tui/codex-utils-image defaults. Plan now requires per-package [features] blocks + dep-edge default-features=false forwarding through cli/Cargo.toml + core/Cargo.toml. F-7 lalrpop lever dropped (not present in checkout). Recommended impl flow: <code>/implement-with-ralph --from-plan ... --parallel --suggested-decomposition ... --autonomous</code>."}],"command":{"name":"codex-rs-feature-pruning-for-sub-45m-cold","descriptionHtml":"F-7 finding from benchmark-codex-rs-jobs-sweep-with-defender. Sub-45m cold target requires reducing TOTAL compile work, not more concurrency — 4×→12× concurrency only bought 7% (56:26 → 49:22). Bottleneck is the late-stage critical path: codex-tui + codex-app-server + codex-core LTO link. Candidate: prune unused codex-rs cargo features (image, lalrpop, aws-lc-rs dep trees). Survey + measure before/after. <strong>Touches upstream-canonical Cargo.toml files</strong> — minimize-conflict-surface mandate applies; prefer disabling features via cargo flags over editing default-features in workspace Cargo.toml.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"⚠️ Touches upstream-canonical Cargo.toml. Per minimize-conflict-surface mandate: prefer disabling features via cargo flags (CARGO_FEATURES_TO_DISABLE=...) over editing default-features in workspace Cargo.toml. If feature toggles require workspace-level Cargo.toml edits, mark with SANDBOX PATCH + §15 replant recipe."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Survey codex-rs workspace for cargo features that can be disabled without breaking the 4 produced bins (codex, codex-core, codex-windows-sandbox-setup, codex-command-runner). Candidates per F-7 finding: image, lalrpop, aws-lc-rs dep trees. Measurement methodology: cold-cache build per scripts/measure-build.ps1, before vs after feature-disable. Goal: cold-cache ≤45m AC-5 target. Per minimize-conflict-surface mandate: prefer CARGO_FEATURES_TO_DISABLE env or --no-default-features + explicit features list over editing workspace Cargo.toml default-features. If Cargo.toml edits required, mark with SANDBOX PATCH + §15 replant recipe. Plan should identify which features the 4 bins actually need (cargo tree --depth 1 + grep for #[cfg(feature)] gates), propose disable set, estimate the saved compile work, and gate impl on a verification build proving no functional regression.\"","impl":"/implement-with-ralph --from-plan .ralph/jobs/codex-rs-feature-pruning-for-sub-45m-cold/plan.md --parallel --suggested-decomposition .ralph/jobs/codex-rs-feature-pruning-for-sub-45m-cold/suggested-decomposition.json --autonomous"}}},{"id":"codex-rs-measure-build-includes-launcher","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T20:06:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rs-measure-build-includes-launcher","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-06-01 by operator: after the jobs-sweep + sccache-race benchmark passes the <code>codex.exe</code> launcher was gone from <code>target/release/</code>, leaving only <code>codex-core.exe</code> (the main 294 MB binary). Cause: <code>codex/scripts/measure-build.ps1:151</code> invokes <code>cargo build --release -p codex-cli --bin codex-core --timings</code> — it explicitly builds ONLY the codex-core bin, never the small <code>codex</code> launcher. Every cold-cache scenario also runs <code>cargo clean</code> beforehand, which wipes the launcher and never regenerates it. Net effect: a successful benchmark run leaves the workspace in a \"core binary exists but cannot be invoked\" state, which is misleading both for users following docs and for follow-up smoke tests. Two-line fix: change line 151 to <code>cargo build --release -p codex-cli --bin codex-core --bin codex --timings</code> (or build the whole codex-cli package). Wall-time delta should be near-zero (launcher is small + cheap once dependencies are built). Also note in <code>build-perf.md</code> that the benchmark builds the full launcher artifact, not just the heavy bin.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"/implement-with-ralph --autonomous \"Tiny wrapper-only fix in codex/scripts/measure-build.ps1 (and brief docs callout in codex/docs/implementation/build-perf.md). Surfaced 2026-06-01: benchmark script only builds codex-core, never the codex launcher, so every cold-cache scenario leaves the launcher missing. Fix:\\n\\n1. codex/scripts/measure-build.ps1 line 151: change\\n     & cargo build --release -p codex-cli --bin codex-core --timings\\n   to\\n     & cargo build --release -p codex-cli --bin codex-core --bin codex --timings\\n   (Verify the launcher target name with: cd codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs && cargo metadata --no-deps --format-version 1 | jq \\\".packages[] | select(.name==\\\"codex-cli\\\") | .targets[] | select(.kind[0]==\\\"bin\\\") | .name\\\" — adjust the second --bin arg to match.) Also update the .SYNOPSIS / first-line .DESCRIPTION block at the top of the script to note the launcher is included.\\n\\n2. codex/docs/implementation/build-perf.md: add a short note under §Methodology that benchmark scenarios build the full launcher artifact (so the workspace state after a benchmark run is invocable, not just core-bin-only).\\n\\nValidation: after edit, source iteration-env.sh and run the script with -ParseOnly to confirm syntax still parses. No actual build needed for the fix — wall-time impact is negligible (launcher builds in <30s once core is fresh).\\n\\nWrapper-only commit on gim-home/codex. Lead bumps codexu submodule pointer separately. Single commit. Topic branch ralph/measure-build-includes-launcher. Body-canonical report.\""}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-01T20:06:00Z","summary":"Wrapper-only +22/-6 across 4 files: measure-build.ps1:157 cargo invocation now builds both shipped bins (-p codex-cli --bin codex-core -p codex-copilot-launcher --bin codex --timings) so cold-cache scenarios no longer leave the workspace launcher-less; script .SYNOPSIS/.DESCRIPTION updated to explain the launcher bin lives in a separate package; build-perf.md Methodology gained a bullet noting both bins are built per scenario with <30s wall-time impact. Bonus scope (added mid-flight via mailbox msg): CLAUDE.md + AGENTS.override.md confusion-point bullet corrected -- previously claimed -p codex-cli --bin codex --bin codex-core works, but cargo rejects --bin codex under that -p because codex-copilot-launcher owns the launcher bin target. Both files now show the correct two-package invocation. Verified by lead via cargo metadata + actual 7m55s rebuild producing both binaries (codex.exe 763 KB + codex-core.exe 309 MB, codex-cli 0.135.0-copilot-api.1 runnable).","commits":[{"sha":"e78e29e3","oneLine":"docs+fix(measure-build): build launcher too + correct cargo-cmd bullet in CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.override.md","repo":"gim-home/codex"},{"sha":"ac331dfe","oneLine":"chore(codex): bump pointer to e78e29e3","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"}]}},{"id":"codex-stream-cut-diagnostics","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T02:49:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-stream-cut-diagnostics","descriptionHtml":"User report 2026-06-01: response sometimes stops mid-stream in the codex fork. Current logs (tracing INFO + session JSONL) tell us \"the stream ended\" but not WHY -- silent-end branches are indistinguishable from clean termination, no HTTP transport classification, no Job Object close attribution, no last-events ring buffer for postmortem. Per codex/CLAUDE.md tenants 1 (minimize upstream-canonical conflict surface) and 3 (test invariants in-tree), add three opt-in diagnostic hooks gated by <code>CODEX_STREAM_TRACE=1</code>: (1) stream-end classifier wrapping the Copilot SSE consumer that logs <code>[copilot-stream-end] cause=&lt;EOF|RST|ServerError|ClientDrop|IoErr|Timeout&gt; bytes_streamed=&lt;n&gt; events_streamed=&lt;n&gt; last_event_kind=&lt;variant-name-only&gt; turn_id=&lt;...&gt;</code>; (2) Job Object close trace behind <code>CODEX_SHUTDOWN_TRACE=1</code> in <code>core/src/spawn.rs::spawn_child_async</code>; (3) metadata-only last-N (N=64) ring buffer dumped to <code>~/.codex/sessions/&lt;id&gt;/stream-cut-dump.jsonl</code> on abnormal termination. <strong>HARD PRIVACY CONSTRAINT</strong>: NO customer data ever logged or persisted -- not model output text, not tool arguments, not tool results, not file paths, not prompt content. Ring buffer captures variant name + byte length + timestamp + seq ONLY; never event payload. Tracing events carry counts + variant names only. New patch-surface invariant: stream-diagnostics output must round-trip the secret-redaction audit (same gate the existing secret-redaction invariant uses, extended to cover stream sidecar files). Overlay-crate eligible: new crate <code>codex-rs-overlay/codex-stream-diagnostics/</code> with public <code>StreamEndClassifier</code> + <code>MetadataRingBuffer</code> + payload-redaction helpers, called from 1-3 line edits at the SSE consumer seam + spawn.rs. Off-by-default, zero perf impact when disabled.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"HARD PRIVACY CONSTRAINT: this diagnostic feature MUST NOT log or persist customer data. No model output text, no tool arguments, no tool results, no file paths, no prompt content. Metadata only (variant names, byte counts, timestamps, internal IDs). The ring-buffer sidecar file lives in the user home dir and may be attached to bug reports -- treat it as adversarially-readable output."}],"prompts":{"impl":"/implement-with-ralph --autonomous \"Build opt-in stream-cut diagnostics for the codex Copilot SSE response path. All hooks gated by CODEX_STREAM_TRACE=1 env (off by default; zero perf impact for normal users). Overlay-crate-first per codex/CLAUDE.md tenant 1.\\n\\n*** HARD PRIVACY CONSTRAINT (read first, never violate) ***\\n\\nThis feature MUST NOT log or persist customer data. The Copilot SSE stream carries model output text, tool-call arguments, tool-call results, and user prompt content -- all of which are PII / customer-confidential / potentially-secret. None of that may appear in tracing output, the ring-buffer sidecar, or any file this feature creates.\\n\\nConcrete rules:\\n  - Ring buffer stores METADATA ONLY per event: { variant_name: \"OutputItemAdded\", byte_length: 247, seq: 12, timestamp: \"...\" }. Never the payload. Never a deserialized struct that contains payload fields.\\n  - Tracing events emit counts + variant names + IDs only. Never an event payload field.\\n  - Sidecar file ~/.codex/sessions/<id>/stream-cut-dump.jsonl contains classifier output + metadata-only ring buffer dump. Designed to be safe-to-attach to a bug report.\\n  - last_event_kind is the variant NAME (e.g. \"OutputItemAdded\"), not the variant payload.\\n  - cause classification looks at the Result variant + Io error kind + HTTP status code only. Never the response body bytes (those may contain partial customer data even on error).\\n  - If a field could ever contain a free-form string from the user, the model, or a tool, it is OFF-LIMITS. When in doubt, omit.\\n  - Add a privacy-audit test: round-trip a stub Stream that emits events with KNOWN canary strings as payload (e.g. \"CANARY_PAYLOAD_XYZ_123\") and assert grep -r \"CANARY\" ~/.codex/sessions/ AND grep \"CANARY\" $(captured tracing output) both return zero matches. This test gates the feature -- if it ever fails, the build fails.\\n  - New patch-surface §14 invariant: \"Stream diagnostics output is metadata-only\" with the canary test as the enforcing test. Add to §4 secret-redaction inventory cross-reference.\\n\\n*** END PRIVACY CONSTRAINT ***\\n\\nBACKGROUND READING (before touching code):\\n  - codex/CLAUDE.md core engineering tenants (especially 1 minimize-conflict-surface, 3 test-invariants-in-tree)\\n  - codex/docs/implementation/patch-surface.md §4 (existing secret-redaction invariant -- your privacy constraint extends this), §14 (invariants), §15 (replant recipes)\\n  - codex/docs/implementation/architecture.md fork-strategy section\\n\\nSTEP 0 (seam verification per CLAUDE.md tenant 2):\\n  Grep-survey to identify the EXACT seam where the Copilot SSE stream is consumed in this fork. Candidates:\\n    - external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/model-provider/src/copilot.rs\\n    - external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/model-provider/src/copilot_responses.rs (if exists)\\n    - external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/client.rs (the main agent loop that consumes ResponseStream)\\n    - external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/codex-rs-overlay/codex-copilot/\\n  Look for the Stream::next() loop or while-let that drives ResponseEvent items. THAT is the seam. Record file:line in your plan output.\\n  Also verify: external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/core/src/spawn.rs::spawn_child_async + core/src/windows_job.rs exist at the lines CLAUDE.md cites.\\n\\nSTEP 1 (placement -- propose then commit):\\n  Per CLAUDE.md tenant 1 ranking:\\n    Preferred: new overlay crate codex-rs-overlay/codex-stream-diagnostics/ with public APIs:\\n      pub struct StreamEndClassifier -- wraps the SSE consumer Future; classifies termination cause from inner Result + HTTP status; emits [copilot-stream-end] tracing event with metadata-only fields\\n      pub struct MetadataRingBuffer -- fixed-capacity (N=64) ring of EventMetadata { variant_name, byte_length, seq, timestamp }. NEVER stores payload. dump_on_abnormal(&self, session_dir, turn_id, classifier_output) writes JSONL sidecar\\n      pub fn extract_metadata(event: &ResponseEvent) -> EventMetadata -- the ONLY function that touches event payloads; returns metadata only. Use std::mem::size_of_val + variant-name extraction. Be careful: a serde-based byte_length is fine; a Debug-format byte_length leaks payload via Debug impl.\\n      pub fn trace_job_object_close(pid, job_handle) -- internal helper for spawn.rs\\n      pub fn enabled() -> bool -- reads CODEX_STREAM_TRACE env once via OnceLock<bool>\\n    Call sites in upstream-canonical files (allowed because each is 1-3 lines per tenant 1 option 2):\\n      - SSE consumer loop: wrap existing Stream::next() / while-let in StreamEndClassifier::new(stream).run().await -- single SANDBOX PATCH edit\\n      - core/src/spawn.rs::spawn_child_async: emit trace_job_object_close() at the point the watcher closes the job (behind CODEX_SHUTDOWN_TRACE=1)\\n  EVERY upstream-canonical edit MUST carry // SANDBOX PATCH: comment + entry in patch-surface.md §14 (invariant + enforcing test) and §15 (replant recipe).\\n\\nSTEP 2 (gating + perf):\\n  All hooks gated by CODEX_STREAM_TRACE=1 env. Read once via OnceLock<bool>. enabled() returns false when env is unset/empty/\"0\"/\"false\". When disabled, wrapping should be a near-zero-cost passthrough -- compiler-elidable. Verify with cargo bloat or by reasoning about the inline boundary.\\n\\nSTEP 3 (sidecar file format):\\n  ~/.codex/sessions/<id>/stream-cut-dump.jsonl (JSONL -- one record per line):\\n    First line: { \"kind\": \"stream-end\", \"cause\": \"EOF|RST|ServerError|ClientDrop|IoErr|Timeout\", \"bytes_streamed\": N, \"events_streamed\": N, \"turn_id\": \"...\", \"session_id\": \"...\", \"ts\": \"ISO-8601\", \"http_status\": <optional>, \"io_error_kind\": <optional> }\\n    Subsequent lines: ring buffer dump as { \"kind\": \"event-metadata\", \"seq\": N, \"variant_name\": \"OutputItemAdded\", \"byte_length\": 247, \"timestamp\": \"...\" }\\n    NEVER includes event payload, free-form strings from user/model/tool, or stack traces with captured values.\\n  Only written when (a) CODEX_STREAM_TRACE=1 AND (b) stream terminated abnormally (cause != EOF, OR EOF without explicit done-event).\\n\\nSTEP 4 (tests per CLAUDE.md tenant 3):\\n  Required tests in codex-stream-diagnostics/tests/:\\n    a) Cause-classifier round-trip: every Cause variant through the formatter (exhaustive enum match).\\n    b) PRIVACY canary test: stub Stream emits events with payload containing the canary string \"CANARY_PAYLOAD_XYZ_123_DO_NOT_LOG\". After running through StreamEndClassifier with CODEX_STREAM_TRACE=1, assert:\\n         - The sidecar JSONL file does NOT contain \"CANARY\" (grep -r the temp session dir)\\n         - The captured tracing output does NOT contain \"CANARY\"\\n         - The MetadataRingBuffer dump does NOT contain \"CANARY\"\\n       This test is a HARD GATE -- if it fails, the build fails. Mark it #[test] (no #[ignore]).\\n    c) Metadata-only ring-buffer test: insert 100 events, dump, verify N=64 most recent are present, and verify each entry has only the four metadata fields (no payload-shaped data leakage via Debug or Display).\\n\\nPLACEMENT VERIFICATION (CLAUDE.md tenant 1 plan-deliverables):\\n  In your plan / commit output, record:\\n    - Seams considered (overlay-only vs thin-call-site vs inline)\\n    - Chosen option (expect overlay + thin call sites)\\n    - Edit-budget estimate (LoC for overlay + LoC for upstream-canonical call sites -- aim for LoC_upstream <= 15)\\n    - Re-conflict probability for each call site (low if well-anchored seams)\\n\\nFILES TO TOUCH (anticipated):\\n  New: codex-rs-overlay/codex-stream-diagnostics/Cargo.toml + src/lib.rs (overlay crate)\\n  New: codex-rs-overlay/codex-stream-diagnostics/tests/classifier.rs + tests/privacy_canary.rs + tests/ring_buffer.rs\\n  Edit: external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/Cargo.toml (workspace member entry)\\n  Edit: SSE consumer seam (TBD per STEP 0) + // SANDBOX PATCH: marker\\n  Edit: core/src/spawn.rs (Job Object close trace) + // SANDBOX PATCH: marker\\n  Edit: docs/implementation/patch-surface.md §4 cross-reference + §14 invariants (incl. metadata-only canary test as gate) + §15 replant recipes\\n  Edit: codex/CLAUDE.md confusion-points (add Stream-cut diagnostics bullet pointing to CODEX_STREAM_TRACE=1 + privacy constraint)\\n\\nBUILD ENV:\\n  Local iteration: source codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh; cargo check --workspace (~6 min) per CLAUDE.md. Do NOT run cargo test --workspace (90+ min, CI-only).\\n  Verify your tests with cargo test -p codex-stream-diagnostics specifically. Privacy canary test MUST pass.\\n  Optional release build: cargo build --release -p codex-cli --bin codex-core -p codex-copilot-launcher --bin codex (per the just-fixed measure-build invocation) -- diagnostic-only feature, runtime smoke not strictly required.\\n\\nBRANCH/WORKTREE:\\n  Inner submodule (Evyatar108/codex-openai-fork sandbox-patches branch). Topic branch ralph/stream-cut-diagnostics on the inner submodule.\\n  Worktree under .ralph/jobs/codex-stream-cut-diagnostics/codex-worktree/ pointing at the INNER submodule.\\n  Push to codex-patched remote (the Evyatar108 fork) on sandbox-patches.\\n  Lead bumps wrapper (gim-home/codex) submodule gitlink + codexu submodule gitlink as separate commits after FF.\\n\\nREPORT KIND=DONE WITH:\\n  - Inner-submodule SHA on sandbox-patches\\n  - Files touched (overlay-LoC vs upstream-canonical-LoC counts)\\n  - Confirmed seam line numbers\\n  - cargo check --workspace status\\n  - Privacy canary test status (MUST be passing)\\n  - Patch-surface §4 + §14 + §15 entry text drafted for lead review\\n\\nBody-canonical: prose body substantive, summary <= 200 chars.\""}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-02T02:49:00Z","summary":"Shipped opt-in stream-cut diagnostics gated by CODEX_STREAM_TRACE=1, off-by-default zero-perf-impact. ~600 LoC overlay crate codex-rs-overlay/codex-stream-diagnostics/ (8 src modules + 4 test files including the HARD privacy-canary gate + source_invariants static-grep test banning serialization of event payloads). ~36 LoC upstream-canonical SANDBOX PATCH at three confirmed seams: (1) SSE consumer at core/src/session/turn.rs:1745 wrapping all 5 terminal exits (Cancellation->ClientCancel, Some(Err)->ProviderError, None->EofBeforeCompleted, Completed->Completed, preempt-mailbox->Completed); (2) Job Object close breadcrumb at core/src/windows_job.rs::close_job_on_child_exit:205 behind CODEX_SHUTDOWN_TRACE=1; (3) watcher-installed breadcrumb at core/src/spawn.rs::attach_windows_job:187. Metadata-only ring buffer (variant_name + byte_length + seq + timestamp; NEVER payload) dumped to ~/.codex/sessions/<id>/stream-cut-dump.jsonl on abnormal termination. Privacy canary 5/5 PASSING: caller-supplied session_id/turn_id hashed via FNV-1a before persistence (caught a verbatim-ID leak in first canary run, fixed); RAII Drop guard emits cause=\"unfinalized\" for forgotten code paths instead of silent disappearance; sink injection enables test verification without env mutation; per-variant canary coverage across every payload-bearing ResponseEvent variant. cargo check green for all touched crates; one pre-existing unrelated rebase-debt failure in codex-thread-manager-sample (missing additional_instructions field, same pattern as 590b50759) noted but not in scope. Patch-surface §4 cross-ref + §14 Invariant 30 + §15 replant subsection drafted and committed.","commits":[{"sha":"69a2108a7","oneLine":"feat(stream-diagnostics): codex-stream-diagnostics overlay + SSE seam + Job Object close trace (SANDBOX PATCH)","repo":"Evyatar108/codex-openai-fork (inner submodule, sandbox-patches)"},{"sha":"7b6387340","oneLine":"feat(stream-diagnostics): add CODEX_STREAM_TRACE-gated overlay crate + docs","repo":"gim-home/codex (wrapper)"},{"sha":"28f78ca0","oneLine":"chore(codex): bump pointer to 7b6387340","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"}]}},{"id":"codex-rs-build-perf-jobs-sweep-with-defender","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T17:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-01T17:00:00Z","summary":"CARGO_BUILD_JOBS sweep (4/6/8/12) under Defender exclusions to identify the cold-cache wall-time optimum on the 16-thread / 64 GB dev box. Results: jobs=4 56:26, jobs=6 51:16, jobs=8 50:12 (WINNER), jobs=12 49:22. Peak RSS scaled 8.1->8.3 GB (negligible). Defender exclusions delivered NO measurable benefit at jobs=4 (56:26 vs prior 53:00 baseline -- within noise; F-6). Two HIGH findings: F-5 sccache --start-server race causes jobs=6/jobs=12 to register only 21 cache requests vs 1705 for jobs=4/jobs=8 (transient post-restart unreachability); F-7 sub-45m floor needs feature pruning, not more concurrency (4x->12x only bought 7%). Artifacts at .ralph/jobs/codex-rs-build-perf-jobs-sweep-with-defender/ (master.log + per-scenario csv/log/sccache-stats + results.md).","commits":[{"sha":"a0a64939","oneLine":"chore(perf): commit jobs-sweep + defender benchmark artifacts","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"},{"sha":"e59e4303","oneLine":"chore(overview): file 3 followups from jobs-sweep findings","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"},{"sha":"df85850b","oneLine":"chore(overview): add remaining 2 followups (defender-procmon + feature-pruning)","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"}]},"command":{"name":"codex-rs-build-perf-jobs-sweep-with-defender","descriptionHtml":"Benchmark task -- completed 2026-06-01. Sweep CARGO_BUILD_JOBS 4/6/8/12 under Defender exclusions to find the cold-cache wall-time optimum. Winner: jobs=8 at 50:12 (vs jobs=4 baseline 56:26). See shipManifest.summary for full findings. Surfaced 3 followups: <code>codex-rs-iteration-env-sccache-race-and-jobs-default</code> (F-5, now MERGED), <code>codex-rs-defender-exclusions-procmon-verify</code> (F-6), <code>codex-rs-feature-pruning-for-sub-45m-cold</code> (F-7).","warnings":[],"prompts":{}}},{"id":"codex-rs-iteration-profile-lto-bypass-doc-fix","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T19:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rs-iteration-profile-lto-bypass-doc-fix","descriptionHtml":"Consolidated docs-fix bundle for the 2026-06-01 build-perf benchmarks (baseline-measurement + jobs-sweep-with-defender). Multiple stale claims in codex-build documentation contradicted by measured findings: <strong>F-1 (HIGH):</strong> sccache wrapper BYPASSES LTO=thin entirely (0 cache requests in lto-change scenario despite cache growth -- losing benefit on EVERY future LTO=thin build, not just first). <strong>F-3/F-4 (LOW):</strong> warm-cache + jobs-change scenarios as written are pure relink floors (cargo does not recompile because only the bin was deleted). <strong>F-5 (HIGH, code-fixed already at d8e4dad15):</strong> sccache --start-server race at jobs=6/12 caused 0 cache hits on those runs. <strong>F-6 (MED):</strong> Defender exclusions delivered no measurable benefit -- downgrade publish-sandbox-patch.md recommendation. <strong>Jobs default 4-&gt;8:</strong> sweep winner. Files to fix: <code>codex/CLAUDE.md:160</code> + <code>codex/AGENTS.override.md:127</code> (LTO mechanism wording), <code>codex/.claude/commands/publish-sandbox-patch.md:28-30,85</code> (Defender downgrade + jobs=4-&gt;8), <code>codex/docs/implementation/build-perf.md</code> (rewrite scenarios per F-3/F-4 + fill treatment results table with baseline + sweep + sccache-race-fix numbers + F-1 LTO bypass section + F-6 Defender callout).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Consolidated docs-only convergence for 2026-06-01 build-perf benchmarks. Wrapper-only edits on gim-home/codex (codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh + codex/docs/implementation/*.md + codex/CLAUDE.md + codex/AGENTS.override.md + codex/.claude/commands/publish-sandbox-patch.md). Lead bumps codexu submodule pointer separately. NO inner submodule changes, NO codex Rust source changes.\n\nFix scope:\n\n1. codex/CLAUDE.md:160 + codex/AGENTS.override.md:127 -- UPDATE the \"only lever is LTO=off/thin/fat\" sentence to also note: sccache wrapper BYPASSES rustc invocations entirely under LTO=thin (F-1 finding 2026-06-01) -- switching to LTO=thin loses sccache benefit on every future build until switched back, not just the first. LTO=off is the only profile under which sccache actually wraps rustc on this workspace.\n\n2. codex/.claude/commands/publish-sandbox-patch.md:\n   - Lines 28-30 (Defender exclusion): downgrade from \"OPTIONAL Defender exclusion\" recommendation to a no-measurable-benefit note. Cite F-6 (2026-06-01 jobs-sweep benchmark, 56:26 with exclusions vs 53:00 baseline -- within noise; even slightly slower).\n   - Line 85: change \"CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 (safe to override -- NOT in cache key)\" to reflect the new jobs=8 default after 2026-06-01 jobs-sweep (winner 50:12 cold vs 56:26 at jobs=4 on this 16-thread / 64 GB box). Also any other lingering jobs=4 mentions in step 2a or the frozen-env block.\n\n3. codex/docs/implementation/build-perf.md -- substantial rewrite:\n   - LTO mechanism section: rewrite per F-1 (sccache wrapper bypass under thin; LTO=off is the only sccache-effective profile).\n   - Scenarios table: rewrite jobs-change row (remove 4 to 2 framing; explain why warm-cache + jobs-change as written are pure relink floors per F-3/F-4 -- only bin was deleted, cargo does not recompile).\n   - Treatment results table (currently empty): fill with three rows -- (a) baseline 2026-06-01 cold 53m / warm 1m08s / lto-change 58m (from baseline-measurement); (b) jobs-sweep 2026-06-01 jobs=4 56:26 / jobs=6 51:16 / jobs=8 50:12 WINNER / jobs=12 49:22 (from jobs-sweep-with-defender); (c) post-sccache-race-fix expected ~45m at jobs=8 (per shipped d8e4dad15 + winner combo, awaiting measurement on next cold cycle).\n   - Add Defender exclusion subsection: F-6 callout (tested 2026-06-01 -- no measurable benefit at jobs=4; listed in publish-sandbox-patch.md preconditions but downgraded to non-recommended).\n   - Add sccache + LTO interaction subsection: F-1 callout standalone with cross-link to CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.override.md.\n   - Reset cold-cache target from <=45m to ~50m per F-2 (we just landed jobs=8 winner -- measure once before adjusting further).\n\n4. codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh -- banner already updated by d8e4dad15 for jobs=8 + sccache poll. Confirm no further changes needed; if banner still mentions any stale value, fix it.\n\nPhase 5b docs convergence: after edits, reviewer round should verify zero remaining \"jobs=4\" references in the script, every doc cross-link is consistent (CLAUDE.md <-> AGENTS.override.md <-> publish-sandbox-patch.md <-> build-perf.md), and the build-perf.md treatment table actually matches the .ralph/jobs/ benchmark artifacts.\n\nSingle commit OK (docs-only, focused). Topic branch ralph/iteration-profile-lto-bypass-doc-fix off gim-home/codex main. Push to gim-home/codex. Body-canonical report: prose body substantive, summary <= 200 chars."}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-01T19:30:00Z","summary":"Consolidated docs-only convergence for 2026-06-01 build-perf benchmarks. Single commit on gim-home/codex (+156/-36 across 4 files): CLAUDE.md + AGENTS.override.md sccache-cache-key bullet extended with F-1 trapdoor note (sccache wrapper inspects -Clto and bypasses caching entirely under any non-off LTO; LTO=off is the only sccache-effective profile; 939 crates / 57m 57s / 0 sccache requests cited); publish-sandbox-patch.md Defender preconditions downgraded from OPTIONAL-recommended to NOT-recommended per F-6 (56:26 with exclusions vs 53:00 baseline -- within noise), CARGO_BUILD_JOBS default 4->8 per jobs-sweep winner; build-perf.md substantial rewrite (background + scenarios table per F-3/F-4 + filled treatment results table with 10 rows from both benchmark passes + new sccache+LTO interaction section + new Defender no-measurable-benefit section + cold-cache target reset 45m->50m per F-2); iteration-env.sh confirmed already correct from d8e4dad15. Phase 5b cross-link verification passed.","commits":[{"sha":"712d3d369","oneLine":"docs(build-perf): consolidate 2026-06-01 benchmark findings","repo":"gim-home/codex"},{"sha":"a5c9b064","oneLine":"chore(codex): bump pointer to 712d3d369","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"}]}},{"id":"codex-rs-build-perf-baseline-measurement","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"ea4b852c","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T14:52:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rs-build-perf-baseline-measurement","descriptionHtml":"Operator-driven follow-up to <code>codex-rs-build-time-and-cache-resilience</code> (which shipped the methodology + tooling but not the recorded numbers). Run the four scenarios documented in <code>codex/docs/implementation/build-perf.md</code> — <code>cold-cache</code>, <code>warm-cache</code>, <code>jobs-change</code>, <code>lto-change</code> — using <code>codex/scripts/measure-build.ps1</code> after sourcing <code>codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh</code>. Each scenario takes 30 min to a few hours; the full matrix is multi-hour and requires a clean machine state (kill running codex/codex-core processes; optionally apply the Defender exclusion from the publish-sandbox-patch.md Preconditions). Copy the resulting CSV rows from <code>codex/docs/implementation/build-perf-artifacts/&lt;runId&gt;.csv</code> into the empty 'Recorded measurements → Treatment' table in <code>build-perf.md</code> and commit. Confirms AC-2 (warm-cache ≤ 5 min target), AC-3 (jobs-change ≤ 6 min target), AC-4 (LTO-change documented as expected miss), AC-5 (treatment cold-cache row recorded).","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"Operator-only — autonomous impl members cannot reliably finish multi-hour release builds. Best run on a quiet day with the dev box not doing other heavy work."}],"prompts":{"impl":"Operator-driven benchmark recording. Step 1: kill any running codex / codex-core processes (powershell `Get-Process | Where-Object { $_.ProcessName -match 'codex' } | Stop-Process -Force`). Step 2 (optional, recommended): from elevated PowerShell, `Add-MpPreference -ExclusionPath D:\\codex-sccache` and `Add-MpPreference -ExclusionPath D:\\harness-efforts\\codexu\\codex\\external\\repos\\codex-patched\\codex-rs\\target`. Step 3: open Git Bash, `cd D:/harness-efforts/codexu` and `source codex/scripts/iteration-env.sh` — confirm the FROZEN ITERATION PROFILE ACTIVE banner. Step 4: for each scenario in `codex/docs/implementation/build-perf.md` (cold-cache, warm-cache, jobs-change, lto-change) run `sccache --zero-stats` then `pwsh -NoProfile -File codex/scripts/measure-build.ps1 -Scenario <name>`. Use a fresh `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per scenario per the methodology to keep cache resets unambiguous. Step 5: open the resulting CSV rows under `codex/docs/implementation/build-perf-artifacts/` and copy the relevant numbers (wallSeconds, peakRssMB, sccache hits/misses/stores) into the empty 'Recorded measurements → Treatment' table in `build-perf.md`. Step 6: commit `docs(codex): record build-perf baseline measurements` on a topic branch and push for lead FF-merge."}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-debt-fix-tui-and-exec","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"ec91d8da6,769bc4d59,590b50759,6b371e2b1,2d2e9bce","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T01:12:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-debt-fix-tui-and-exec","descriptionHtml":"Bundled fix for 3 v0.135.0 rebase-debt issues in codex-tui + codex-exec surfaced by build-codex-release-exe-r4: (1) duplicate <code>history_cell</code> module file (both .rs and mod.rs existed — kept upstream's mod.rs structure + forward-ported the 1 fork-side patch); (2) unresolved git merge marker <code>>>>>>>> f4a628f40</code> committed mid-merge into <code>tui/src/style.rs:255</code>, resolved 4 conflict regions per the original rebase commit's documented intent; (3) <code>codex-exec/lib.rs:403</code> ConfigOverrides initializer missing new required <code>additional_instructions</code> field. cargo check -p codex-tui -p codex-exec GREEN. Audit-grep confirmed no other unresolved merge markers anywhere in codex-rs/. <strong>Subsumes followup task</strong> codex-rebase-history-cell-forward-port.","warnings":[],"prompts":{}}},{"id":"codex-mcp-server-eventmsg-match-arms-fix","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"22eceaab1,8a54ce213,f5a97ab4","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-01T00:04:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-mcp-server-eventmsg-match-arms-fix","descriptionHtml":"Fork-introduced regression from today's Stage A mcp-server-notifications ship (bd98f77). Stage A added new <code>EventMsg::McpServerNotification</code> + <code>McpSamplingRequest</code> variants and fanned out the exhaustive-match update to <code>rollout/src/policy.rs</code> + <code>rollout-trace/src/protocol_event.rs</code>, but missed the 3rd match site at <code>mcp-server/src/codex_tool_runner.rs:224</code>. Shipped + cargo check -p codex-mcp-server clean + audit confirmed no other missed match sites.","warnings":[],"prompts":{}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-debt-fix-app-server-client-brace","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"1560e120b,f2350375d,0c3e47ec","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T23:32:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-debt-fix-app-server-client-brace","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-05-31 by <code>build-codex-release-exe-r2</code> Phase 5 cargo build. v0.135.0 rebase merge <code>03fe64287</code> dropped 1 closing brace from <code>app-server-client/src/remote.rs::pub async fn connect()</code> — match arms closed but fn body never closed before next pub fn started. Pure syntax error, +1 brace fix. Shipped + cargo check -p codex-app-server-client clean + no sibling brace issues found in the same rebase commit.","warnings":[],"prompts":{}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-debt-fix-app-server-plugin-share-context","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"652db37de,22435d0d0,39b2ca72","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T22:40:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-debt-fix-app-server-plugin-share-context","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-05-31 by <code>build-codex-release-exe</code> Phase 5 cargo build. Yet another v0.135.0 rebase-debt dangling reference: <code>app-server/src/request_processors/plugins.rs:919</code> calls <code>codex_core_plugins::remote::fetch_remote_plugin_share_context(&cfg, auth, &id).await</code> — async, network-fetch from chatgpt backend with auth. Function never defined in workspace (only sync local-only sibling <code>remote_plugin_share_context</code> exists with different signature). Shipped as SANDBOX-PATCH stub returning <code>Ok(None)</code> mirroring the existing <code>fetch_remote_installed_plugins</code> pattern + the prior <code>494219ed4 group_remote_installed_plugins_by_marketplaces</code> fix. Preserves network-suppression invariant per codex CLAUDE.md §1; <code>Ok(None)</code> drives caller's existing fallback path (locally-computed PluginShareContext + warn log).","warnings":[],"prompts":{}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-resume-proper","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"e423b8c7e,13558dfb9,9a2753ed7,4e08dabb2,09e3adfb","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T18:35:00Z","kanbanCards":[{"column":"ready","cardClass":null,"inlineStyle":null,"html":"\n      <div class=\"card-title\">Resume the v0.135.0 codex rebase properly</div>\n      <div class=\"sub\">v0.135.0 squash silently dropped producer-side modules (PlannedTools type + add_collaboration_tools fn + likely more). impl-codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation surfaced this after 3 fix commits; cargo check workspace remains broken on 30 errors all rooted in deeper rebase incompleteness. Multi-day scope.</div>\n      <div class=\"card-meta\">\n        <span class=\"pill area-codex\">codex submodule</span>\n        <span class=\"pill p-high\">high effort</span>\n        <span>~1-2 days</span>\n      </div>\n    ","order":10}],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-resume-proper","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-05-31 by <code>impl-codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation</code> after 3 fix commits unblocked partial workspace compilation. The v0.135.0 codex upstream rebase (shipped 2026-05-30) silently dropped producer-side modules during the squash: <code>PlannedTools</code> type + <code>add_collaboration_tools</code> fn are referenced throughout <code>spec_plan.rs</code> but defined NOWHERE in the workspace. Plus 4 fork/upstream files need trait migration (old <code>ToolHandler::handle()</code> shape vs new <code>CoreToolRuntime</code> trait). Full inventory + recommended <code>/rebase-upstream rust-v0.135.0</code> resume-discipline approach + base SHAs documented at <code>.ralph/jobs/codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation/handoff.md</code>. <strong>Multi-day scope</strong> (~1-2 days per the prior member's estimate). Build on top of 3 fix commits already pushed at <code>Evyatar108/codex-openai-fork@ralph/rebase-debt-reconciliation-impl</code> tip <code>5726e6f08</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"🚧 Multi-day scope. Read handoff.md FIRST. Recommended approach: /rebase-upstream rust-v0.135.0 discipline → git diff bd98f7751..rust-v0.135.0 to identify ALL dropped files → import → re-apply the 3 existing fix commits on top → verify cargo check --workspace GREEN."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Resume the v0.135.0 codex upstream rebase to make cargo check --workspace GREEN. The prior reconciliation member (impl-codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation) shipped 3 valuable fix commits on Evyatar108/codex-openai-fork@ralph/rebase-debt-reconciliation-impl (tip 5726e6f08) and surfaced that the squash silently dropped producer-side modules. Full inventory + recommended approach at .ralph/jobs/codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation/handoff.md. Plan: (a) survey the 30 remaining errors in codex-core per the handoff Z/C/A categories; (b) decide between /rebase-upstream rust-v0.135.0 discipline (fresh fetch + diff against pre-rebase 93d915e66 to identify ALL dropped files) vs surgical recovery of just PlannedTools + add_collaboration_tools; (c) base the new work on the existing ralph/rebase-debt-reconciliation-impl tip 5726e6f08 (don't rebase or revert the 3 fix commits); (d) plan for Z3 + C1 + C2 ToolHandler-to-CoreToolRuntime trait migration; (e) plan for A1-A4 dropped definitions (mod.rs re-exports, config_profile orphan, truncation_policy field). Workflow: codex worktree + topic branch + push to gim-home/codex + wrapper FF + codexu submodule bump after lead approval per AGENTS.md only-lead-merges discipline.\""}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"subsumed-by-codex-rebase-resume-proper-9a2753ed7","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T18:28:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation","descriptionHtml":"Coordinated reconciliation of all pre-existing rebase debt from the v0.135.0 codex upstream rebase (shipped 2026-05-30). Multiple impl members have hit per-crate blockers; this task is a single coordinated effort to make <code>cargo check --workspace</code> GREEN. Survey-categorize-fix approach: (a) mechanical fixes applied autonomously with <code>rebase-debt-fix-followup:</code> commit tags, (b) fork-architecture calls surfaced as <code>kind=question</code> with options, (c) security-sensitive items (network-suppression invariant per CLAUDE.md §1) require pause-points. Subsumes (or partially subsumes) the 2 existing followup tasks <code>codex-rebase-core-plugins-manager-dangling-call</code> + <code>codex-rebase-spec-plan-malformed-function</code>.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"🔒 Touches security-sensitive surfaces (network-suppression invariant in core-plugins/remote.rs). Expect 1+ kind=question pause-points during the fix phase."}],"prompts":{"impl":"See spawn-prompt for impl-codex-rebase-debt-reconciliation (2026-05-31). Survey-categorize-fix approach: cargo check --workspace baseline → categorize each error as (a) mechanical / (b) fork-architecture / (c) security-sensitive → fix all (a) autonomously, surface (b)/(c) as kind=question → re-verify workspace GREEN → push topic branch to gim-home/codex (lead does wrapper FF + codexu submodule bump). Every edit gets a // SANDBOX PATCH: rebase-debt-fix v0.135.0 marker + §15 replant recipe entry."}}},{"id":"codex-channels-option-b","scope":"codexu","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T03:59:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-channels-option-b","descriptionHtml":"Replacement for <code>codex-channels</code> after operator chose Option B 2026-06-01. Thin notifications-to-prompt-queue impl on Stage A's existing <code>EventMsg::McpServerNotification</code> surface. happy-cli only; ZERO codex/ Rust touches. 3 stories, ~150-250 LoC TS. Plan at <code>.ralph/jobs/codex-channels-option-b/plan.md</code> (commit bbc49ceb). Includes mcp_sampling_request log-and-ignore handling.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Read .ralph/jobs/codex-channels-option-b/plan.md. 3-story decomposition: (1) subscribe happy-cli's EventMsg consumer to McpServerNotification + McpSamplingRequest events; (2) routing config that maps notification kinds to display-only vs enqueue-as-prompt-input; (3) integration with existing input queue (priority handling, mid-LLM-call buffering). Tests: vitest fixtures per kind. Single commit OK on ralph/codex-channels-option-b off codexu origin/main. Push to origin + personal. Phase 5a+5b convergence."}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-02T03:59:00Z","summary":"Shipped Option B notifications-to-prompt-queue consumer in happy-cli (TS-only, zero codex/ Rust touches). 8 files / +1418 / -2 LoC. Three stories: (1) routing config mcpNotificationRouting.ts with 7-kind default table + per-server overrides + opaque Settings.mcpNotificationRouting field + unknown-kind warn-and-drop; (2) generic consumer mcpNotificationConsumer.ts with trailing-edge debounce per ${server}|${uri ?? kind} key + lazy currentMode() accessor + mcp_sampling_request log-once-per-server per plan §4; (3) wiring in runCodex.ts with consumer built right after currentDisallowedTools so closure captures live EnhancedMode + handle(msg) at top of setEventHandler + dispose() in BOTH handleKillSession AND the main-loop finally (the latter added after Phase 5a code-review caught a real Medium bug: dispose() missing from normal-exit path meant a queued debounce timer could fire post-session-close, leak event-loop lifetime, throw on closed queue -- the exact failure mode the kill-path comment was defending against). 34 new vitest tests all green; 202 codex/* tests pass; typecheck clean. Phase 5b doc-review caught roadmap staleness on rows 34 + 211, fixed in same commit. Deferred (out of scope per plan): reply path for mcp_sampling_request -- future codex-sampling-handler task.","commits":[{"sha":"c3075b6c","oneLine":"feat(codex): Option B notifications-to-prompt-queue consumer (member commit on topic branch)","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu (topic)"},{"sha":"e845ffc5","oneLine":"feat(codex): Option B notifications-to-prompt-queue consumer (cherry-picked to main)","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu (main)"}]}},{"id":"codex-mcp-server-notifications-patch-surface-docs","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"3f7d53e35,cb9bc687","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T17:32:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-mcp-server-notifications-patch-surface-docs","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-mcp-server-notifications</code> ship (2026-05-31). The 7 <code>// SANDBOX PATCH: invariant 25</code> markers are in place across 5 functional seams in the codex inner submodule (bd98f77), but the corresponding <code>codex/docs/implementation/patch-surface.md</code> §14/§15 entries for invariants 25, 25a, 25b, 25c, 25d were NOT added during the ship. Small docs-only follow-up; reference table of intended invariants + enforcing tests is in the impl member's done report.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Add 5 invariant rows to codex/docs/implementation/patch-surface.md §14 (invariants 25, 25a, 25b, 25c, 25d per the impl-mcp-server-notifications done-report reference table) + add §15 replant recipe entry for the SANDBOX PATCH: invariant 25 markers across rmcp-client/{logging_client_handler.rs, elicitation_client_service.rs, rmcp_client.rs}, codex-mcp/{rmcp_client.rs, connection_manager.rs}. Docs-only commit on a small topic branch in gim-home/codex; FF to wrapper main; codexu submodule pointer bump in a sibling commit on codexu main."}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-spec-plan-malformed-function","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"partially-addressed-by-5726e6f08","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T18:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-spec-plan-malformed-function","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-05-31 by <code>impl-mcp-server-notifications</code> Phase 5a workspace gate. <code>core/src/tools/spec_plan.rs:658-714</code> has ~57 lines of stray code that reference <code>config</code> and <code>builder</code> (vars not in scope) sitting BELOW the closing brace of <code>add_core_utility_tools</code> and ABOVE <code>add_mcp_runtime_tools</code> — clearly a mid-merge artifact from the v0.135.0 rebase that left <code>add_core_utility_tools</code> malformed. <strong>Fork-architecture call:</strong> deleting the stray block requires understanding which collab-tools / agent-jobs / spawn-top-level-session handler registrations are still wanted.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Reconcile the spec_plan.rs:658-714 stray block left by the v0.135.0 rebase. Survey: which collab-tools / agent-jobs / spawn-top-level-session handler registrations does the fork still want vs which were upstream-removed? The stray block references config and builder vars that are no longer in scope, but the registration intent may still be load-bearing. Plan should: (a) identify each registration in the stray block; (b) cross-reference upstream's current spec_plan.rs to see whether the registrations moved elsewhere; (c) decide per-registration whether to delete (upstream-canonical) or re-implant in the correct scope (fork-canonical); (d) verify codex-core builds after fix.\""}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-core-plugins-manager-dangling-call","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"partially-addressed-by-494219ed4","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-31T18:30:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-core-plugins-manager-dangling-call","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced 2026-05-31 by <code>impl-port-plan-and-verification-roles</code>. v0.135.0 rebase accepted upstream's new caller in <code>core-plugins/src/manager.rs</code> (<code>crate::remote::group_remote_installed_plugins_by_marketplaces(...)</code>, 2 call sites) but kept HEAD's network-suppressed <code>remote.rs</code> (empty stubs per the rebase commit's documented decision). Result: dangling reference blocking <code>cargo check --workspace</code>. <strong>Security-sensitive</strong> — per codex CLAUDE.md §1 network-suppression invariant, <code>/ps/plugins/*</code> HTTP must stay disabled. 3 options: (i) stub the function locally in remote.rs to preserve invariant, (ii) re-port upstream's fn with network-suppression treatment applied, (iii) modify manager.rs to not call it. Each needs review against patch-surface §1.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn","html":"🔒 Security-sensitive: touches the codex network-suppression invariant. Plan member must review patch-surface §1 + the existing 4-conflict-region resolution in remote.rs before recommending an option."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"core-plugins/src/manager.rs has a dangling call to crate::remote::group_remote_installed_plugins_by_marketplaces (introduced by v0.135.0 upstream rebase, dropped by the HEAD-side network-suppression treatment of remote.rs). Workspace cargo check fails on this. Survey the 3 options: (i) stub the function locally in remote.rs to preserve the network-suppression invariant, (ii) re-port upstream's function body with the network-suppression treatment applied (return Vec::new() / AuthRequired equivalents), (iii) modify manager.rs's 2 call sites to not call it. Read codex/CLAUDE.md §1 (network suppression) + the existing 4-conflict-region resolution that the rebase commit message describes (kept HEAD's empty Vec::new() / AuthRequired stubs). Per minimize-conflict-surface tenet: SANDBOX PATCH with §15 replant recipe is acceptable here since remote.rs is already a heavy patch surface; overlay crate is unlikely viable for this seam. Recommend the safest option that preserves invariant + minimizes future-rebase churn. Plan should also probe whether there are OTHER similar dangling-caller cases beyond this one that the rebase missed.\""}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-history-cell-forward-port","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"archived","status":"ok","mergeCommit":"subsumed-by-codex-rebase-debt-fix-tui-and-exec-ec91d8da6+769bc4d59","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T21:45:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-history-cell-forward-port","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-codex-upstream-rebase-v2</code> (2026-05-30) Phase 2b. We kept HEAD's <code>tui/src/history_cell.rs</code> file against upstream's deletion in <code>rust-v0.135.0</code> (still referenced by <code>tui/src/lib.rs:135 mod history_cell;</code>). Upstream moved this content to PR #21278 (<em>Move message history out of core</em>). Next rebase will hit the same deletion conflict unless we forward-port now. Patch is 'no-op when Style::default() carries no bg' per CLAUDE.md, so low-risk migration — but cleanest to migrate when upstream's new layout stabilizes. Per minimize-conflict-surface tenet: prefer overlay crate over sandbox-patch if upstream's new home allows it.","warnings":[],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Forward-port the SANDBOX PATCH content of codex-rs/tui/src/history_cell.rs to upstream PR #21278's new home (Move message history out of core). Read codex/CLAUDE.md history_cell entry first. Survey upstream PR #21278 to find where message-history rendering moved. Choose overlay-crate path (preferred per minimize-conflict-surface) or sandbox-patch on the new file. Test in codex/external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs/ — cargo check + tui smoke. Lead-FF-then-cherry-pick the wrapper gitlink bump to codex/main.\""}}},{"id":"codex-rebase-phase-5a-5b-ci-closeout","scope":"codex","lifecycle":"merged","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-06-02T05:00:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"codex-rebase-phase-5a-5b-ci-closeout","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>impl-codex-upstream-rebase-v2</code> (2026-05-30). The Phase 2 rebase (codexu <code>5d26cc93</code> / wrapper <code>f4551c95</code> / codex-patched <code>03fe64287</code>) shipped without local Phase 5a/5b convergence because <code>cargo check --workspace</code> couldn't run on this box (1.95 toolchain blocker). CI (<code>.github/workflows/invariant-check.yml</code> on ubuntu-latest + windows-latest) is the canonical workspace-test gate. <strong>Action:</strong> watch first post-push CI run on <code>sandbox-patches</code> and <code>codex/main</code>; if type-drift fixes needed, force-push fix on top.","warnings":[{"className":"cmd-warn blocked","html":"⏸️ <strong>Phase 5b is currently gated on <code>escalate-gim-home-actions-policy</code></strong> — every invariant-check run on <code>gim-home/codex@main</code> returns startup_failure with zero jobs (100/100 since 2026-04-07). Interim closeout path (local cargo check --workspace + release build + pre-tag runtime smoke) is documented in <code>codex/.claude/commands/rebase-upstream.md</code> <code># Phase 5b → Interim closeout when CI is org-policy-blocked</code>. Sunset: when <code>escalate-gim-home-actions-policy</code> ships, the bookkeeper runs the <code>### Sunset checklist (self-triggering)</code> in the rebase skill."}],"prompts":{"plan":"/plan-with-ralph \"Monitor CI on the post-rebase commits (codex-patched 03fe64287 on sandbox-patches, codex wrapper f4551c95 on gim-home/codex@main). If CI is green, mark this task merged with no follow-up. If CI surfaces type-drift fixes (compiler errors in HEAD-side code triggered by upstream's API changes in v0.135.0), apply the fix in a force-push to sandbox-patches per the skill convention (sandbox-patches lives through force-with-lease resets). Re-bump the wrapper gitlink + codexu submodule pointer on a small follow-up commit.\"","impl":"/implement-with-ralph --from-plan .ralph/jobs/codex-rebase-phase-5a-5b-ci-closeout/plan.md --autonomous\n\nCRITICAL HANDOFF NOTES (from plan author):\n  - Worktree: codex/.worktrees/<task-id> (INSIDE the codex submodule, per fork-level AGENTS.md worktree convention table).\n  - Commit pattern: ONE codex submodule commit on ralph/<task-id>, then ONE codexu commit on ralph/<task-id> that bumps the codex gitlink + codexu-side AGENTS.md + .ralph-overview/data.json together.\n  - DO NOT touch plans/codexu-roadmap.md (bookkeeper post-ship responsibility).\n  - DO NOT git add CLAUDE.md from the codexu worktree (gitignored per fork pattern).\n  - After data.json edit, run `pnpm sync-ralph-state` (or `node bin/ralph-overview.mjs sync`) to regenerate sidecars.\n  - Push to ralph/<task-id> topic branches on BOTH the codex submodule and codexu, NEVER directly to either repo main. Lead FF-merges.\n\nPlan covers: Phase 5a HARD local cargo check --workspace gate + Phase 5b post-push invariant-check CI watch + interim closeout for the org-policy-blocked state (escalate-gim-home-actions-policy) + self-triggering sunset checklist that flips Phase 5b back to real CI watch once the org block lifts. Doc-only, no Rust changes."}},"shipManifest":{"shippedAt":"2026-06-02T05:00:00Z","summary":"Plan + impl both shipped 2026-06-01. Plan commit 2ad507ef (17 ACs, multi-model-reviewed, all 16 of 17 findings fixed in v2). Impl: codex submodule 166c8ec07b0c (+167/-3 across rebase-upstream.md Phase 5a/5b protocol + step 5/6 inline amends + CLAUDE.md + AGENTS.override.md mirror bullet byte-identical per AC7 lockstep) and codexu 71660e1f (codex gitlink bump + AGENTS.md cross-ref + data.json warnings row + Sunset checklist kanban row on escalate-gim-home-actions-policy + regenerated ralph-overview sidecars + tasks/INDEX.md regen). Codifies the session lesson as procedural protocol: future codex rebases MUST run cargo check --workspace as a HARD local gate before sandbox-patches force-push; explicit (incomplete) / (workspace green) split-commit fallback if needed; wrapper-side REFUSE: gitlink target safety check prevents bumping the gitlink to an (incomplete) SHA. Phase 5b post-push invariant-check CI watch + self-triggering sunset checklist (bookkeeper runs when escalate-gim-home-actions-policy flips merged: workflow_dispatch + gh run watch + amend the interim subsection). All 17 ACs verified PASS. Workspace-parse preflight cargo metadata --no-deps exit 0 (699 KB output at .ralph/jobs/.../cargo-metadata.out).","commits":[{"sha":"2ad507ef","oneLine":"plan: codex-rebase-phase-5a-5b-ci-closeout (17 ACs, multi-model reviewed)","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"},{"sha":"166c8ec07b0c","oneLine":"docs(rebase): add Phase 5a (workspace check) and Phase 5b (CI closeout) protocol","repo":"gim-home/codex"},{"sha":"71660e1f","oneLine":"chore(codex): bump pointer to 166c8ec07b0c (Phase 5a/5b rebase protocol)","repo":"Evyatar108/codexu"}]}},{"id":"happy-skill-md-roadmap-path-correction","scope":"bookkeeping","lifecycle":"tracked","status":"ok","lastTouchedAt":"2026-05-30T21:45:00Z","kanbanCards":[],"command":{"name":"happy-skill-md-roadmap-path-correction","descriptionHtml":"Surfaced by <code>plan-happy-upstream-sync</code> (2026-05-30). The <code>.agents/skills/happy-upstream-sync/SKILL.md</code> references <code>plans/codexu-roadmap.md</code> when bumping the upstream-merge bullet, but the happy fork's correct path is <code>docs/fork-roadmap.md</code>. Small doc-only fix; surfaced post-postponement of the actual happy-sync work (operator chose to diverge from upstream, so sync is paused — but the SKILL.md correction stands regardless if/when sync resumes).","warnings":[],"prompts":{"impl":"Edit .agents/skills/happy-upstream-sync/SKILL.md in the happy repo (D:/harness-efforts/happy) — replace all references to plans/codexu-roadmap.md with docs/fork-roadmap.md (the happy fork's actual file). Single commit on a topic branch off main; push to happy-fork remote; PR or direct push per fork conventions. NOT urgent since happy-sync is postponed."}}}],"phaseTree":[{"id":"phase-1","title":"Phase 1 — Foundations","headerHtml":"Phase 1 — Foundations <span class=\"ptag\">parallel</span>","nodes":[{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name open\">1a Codex fork strategy commit</span> — docs-only, open"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name donefade\">1b.1 Transport refactor stdio→ws</span>"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name donefade\">1b.2 Discovery + reattach</span>"},{"kind":"task-ref","taskId":"1b-multidev","visibleText":"1b.3 Multi-device discoverability hint","state":"open","trailingHtml":" — unblocked, re-read"},{"kind":"task-ref","taskId":"1b-multidev","visibleText":"1b.4 Multi-client approval fan-out","state":"open","trailingHtml":" — unblocked, re-read"},{"kind":"task-ref","taskId":"1b-multidev","visibleText":"1b.5 Walkthrough verification","state":"open","trailingHtml":" — manual"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name donefade\">1c Personal codex plugin scaffolding</span> — 90%, TUI smoke residual"}]},{"id":"phase-2","title":"Phase 2 — Codex divergences","nodes":[{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name donefade\">2a Verify upstream features</span> — 2/3 tests, T3 deferred"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name donefade\">2b .claude/skills discovery via junctions</span>"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">2c Plugin scoping (host vs agent)</span> — upstream codex patch"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">2d ask_user_question sibling tool</span> — upstream codex patch"}]},{"id":"phase-3","title":"Phase 3 — Plugin migrations","headerHtml":"Phase 3 — Plugin migrations <span class=\"ptag\">Claude Code → codex</span>","nodes":[{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name\" style=\"color: var(--warn);\">🟡 3a Skills port</span> — 13 skills, agent in pre-code discovery"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">3b-i Subagents → [agents.&lt;role&gt;]</span> — blocked on 3a discovery"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">3b-ii Tool permission audit</span> — blocked on 3a discovery"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name open\">3c Hooks port</span> — likely zero, verify (parallel-safe)"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">3d Codex-based workers (native spawn)</span> — blocked on 3a + 3b"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name donefade\">3e Cross-vendor workers</span> — stays as shell scripts"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">3f Asset migration</span> — blocked on 3a discovery"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">3g Plugin packaging</span> — blocked on 3a discovery"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name open\">3h options-mode plugin migration</span> — 575 LOC hooks (separate plugin, parallel-safe)"}]},{"id":"phase-4","title":"Phase 4 — Coexistence verification","headerHtml":"Phase 4 — Coexistence verification <span class=\"ptag\">gated on Phase 3</span>","collapsible":true,"collapsibleSummary":"13 sub-items (all blocked) — click to expand","nodes":[{"kind":"raw","html":"4a codex from codexu works (smoke)"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4b ralph from codex works"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4c ralph + codexu combined (laptop + phone)"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4d Plugin scoping works (host vs agent)"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4e Cross-vendor fan-out"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4f AskUserQuestion fan-out"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4g App-server lifecycle (reattach)"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4h End-to-end trace IDs"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4i Pre-Phase-3 ralph state migrates"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4j Reviewer fan-out determinism"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4k Output-file contract migration"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4l Sandbox-bypass parity"},{"kind":"raw","html":"4m Stop-hook tag enforcement post-port"}]},{"id":"phase-5","title":"Phase 5 — Drop Claude Code","headerHtml":"Phase 5 — Drop Claude Code <span class=\"ptag\">gated on Phase 4</span>","collapsible":true,"collapsibleSummary":"6 sub-items (all gated) — click to expand","nodes":[{"kind":"raw","html":"5a Dual-stack dogfood (2-week buffer)"},{"kind":"raw","html":"5b Audit remaining Claude Code workflows"},{"kind":"raw","html":"5c Archive Claude Code configs"},{"kind":"raw","html":"5d Uninstall Claude Code binary"},{"kind":"raw","html":"5e Update personal docs"},{"kind":"raw","html":"5f Mark migration complete"}]},{"id":"phase-6-7","title":"Phase 6 + 7 — Deferred polish","nodes":[{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">6 Long-lived teammates</span> — un-defer when respawn friction ≥3×/week"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">7 Claude model via Copilot adapter</span> — spike first, wide effort range"}]},{"id":"devtunnels-follow-on","title":"Devtunnels follow-on / open gaps","headerHtml":"Devtunnels follow-on / open gaps <span class=\"ptag\">post-Sprint-E</span>","nodes":[{"kind":"task-ref","taskId":"perf-WS3","visibleText":"Realtime sync perf WS1 / WS2 / WS3","state":"open","trailingHtml":" — see <a href=\"realtime-sync-perf.md\">plan</a>"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">Realtime sync perf WS4</span> — conditional on WS3 outcome"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name donefade\">Codex agent project-.mcp.json parity</span> — shipped 462776df"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">Drop userId scoping (happy-server cleanup)</span> — ~140 refs, blocked on perf-WS3"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name closed\">Polish PR — F-013 closed</span> · <span class=\"item-name open\">F-015 / F-017</span> — low-risk isolated"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">F-014</span> — needs server redeploy"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">F-016</span> — BOOX hardware quirk"},{"kind":"task-ref","taskId":"polish-Fs","visibleText":"F-001/F-002 (Med) + F-003-F-007 (Low)","state":"open","trailingHtml":" — security polish PR"},{"kind":"raw","html":"<span class=\"item-name deferred\">BOOX Phases 2–6</span> — operator manual validation"}]}],"lastTouched":{"perf-WS1":"2026-05-13T22:30:00Z","perf-WS3":"2026-05-13T22:00:00Z","3a-skills":"2026-06-02T15:31:32Z","F-013-perms":"2026-05-13T21:00:00Z","F-015-toast":"2026-05-14T07:00:00Z","mcp-discovery":"2026-05-13T12:39:11-07:00","co